        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Input and Output",
            "explanation": "Now that you know how to store data using variables, the next step is learning how programs communicate with users.\nA program usually receives some information, processes it, and then displays a result. This process is called **Input and Output**.\n\n**Input** is any information given to a program. This information can come from a user, a file, a database, or an API. In Python, we commonly use the `input()` function to receive information from the user.\n\n**Output** is the information that a program displays after processing the input. In Python, we use the `print()` function to display output on the screen.\n\nAlmost every application follows the same pattern:\n`Input → Processing → Output`\n\nFor example, when you search on Google, your search query is the input. Google processes your request and shows search results as output.\n\n**Real-World Examples:**\n• User enters their name in a registration form.\n• User submits a question to an AI chatbot.\n• User uploads a photo to a social media application.\n• Application displays the result back to the user.\n\nUnderstanding input and output is important because every software application interacts with users through these two mechanisms.",
            "example": "name = input('Enter your name: ')\nprint('Hello', name)",
            "tip": "**Remember:** `input()` receives data, while `print()` displays data."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Type Conversion",
            "explanation": "In the previous lesson, you learned that variables can store different types of data such as numbers, text, and Boolean values. Sometimes we need to change a value from one data type to another. This process is called **Type Conversion**.\n\nA common situation occurs when using the `input()` function. Even if a user enters a number, Python receives it as text (`str`). If you want to perform calculations, you must first convert that text into a number.\n\nPython provides several built-in functions for type conversion:\n\n• `int()` → Converts a value into an integer.\n• `float()` → Converts a value into a decimal number.\n• `str()` → Converts a value into text.\n• `bool()` → Converts a value into a Boolean value.\n\nFor example, if a user enters their age as `20`, Python initially stores it as text. By using `int()`, you can convert it into a number and perform mathematical operations.\n\n**Real-World Example:**\nAn online form may ask for a user's age. Since the value arrives as text, it must be converted into a number before calculations or validations can be performed.\n\nType conversion helps ensure that data is processed correctly and prevents many common programming errors.",
            "example": "age = input('Enter age: ')\nage = int(age)\nprint(age + 5)",
            "tip": "**Important:** Values returned by `input()` are always strings unless converted."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Basic Debugging",
            "explanation": "As you start writing programs, you will occasionally encounter errors. This is completely normal and happens to every programmer. The process of finding and fixing these errors is called **Debugging**.\n\nDebugging is one of the most important programming skills because it helps you understand why a program is not working as expected.\n\nThere are three common types of errors:\n\n• **Syntax Errors** → Occur when Python rules are not followed.\nExample: Missing quotes, brackets, or incorrect indentation.\n\n• **Runtime Errors** → Occur while the program is running.\nExample: Trying to divide a number by zero.\n\n• **Logical Errors** → The program runs successfully but produces the wrong result.\nExample: Using the wrong formula in a calculation.\n\nOne simple debugging technique is using the `print()` function to display variable values and check whether the program is behaving correctly.\n\nWhenever an error occurs, do not panic. Follow these steps:\n\n1. Read the error message carefully.\n2. Identify the line causing the problem.\n3. Understand why the error happened.\n4. Fix the code.\n5. Run the program again.\n\nEvery developer spends time debugging code. The more you practice debugging, the faster you become at solving problems and building reliable programs.",
            "example": "age = '20'\nprint(type(age))\nage = int(age)\nprint(type(age))",
            "tip": "**Pro Tip:** Error messages are your friend. Read them carefully before making changes."
          }
        },
