            "question": "Complete the program by following the instructions below:\n\n1. Create a list named `numbers` with values `[5, 2, 8]`.\n2. Add `10` using `append()`.\n3. Add `[15, 20]` using `extend()`.\n4. Insert `1` at the beginning using `insert()`.\n5. Remove the value `8` using `remove()`.\n6. Sort the list in ascending order.\n7. Reverse the list.\n8. Print the final list.\n9. Print how many times `10` appears using `count()`.\n10. Print the index of `15` using `index()`.\n11. Remove and print the last element using `pop()`.",
            "starterCode": "# Step 1: Create the list\n\n\n# Step 2: Add 10\n\n\n# Step 3: Add 15 and 20\n\n\n# Step 4: Insert 1 at index 0\n\n\n# Step 5: Remove 8\n\n\n# Step 6: Sort the list\n\n\n# Step 7: Reverse the list\n\n\n# Step 8: Print the list\n\n\n# Step 9: Print count of 10\n\n\n# Step 10: Print index of 15\n\n\n# Step 11: Pop and print the last element\n",
            "solution": "numbers = [5, 2, 8]\n\nnumbers.append(10)\nnumbers.extend([15, 20])\nnumbers.insert(0, 1)\nnumbers.remove(8)\nnumbers.sort()\nnumbers.reverse()\n\nprint(numbers)\nprint(numbers.count(10))\nprint(numbers.index(15))\nprint(numbers.pop())",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use the list methods in this order: append(), extend(), insert(), remove(), sort(), reverse(), count(), index(), and pop()."
          }
        },
