          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is Indexing and Slicing",
            "explanation": "Now that you've learned about Python data structures like **List, Tuple, Dictionary, and Set**, let's learn how to access the data stored inside them.\n\nPython provides two powerful techniques:\n\n**1. Indexing** → Used to access a single element.\n\n**2. Slicing** → Used to access multiple elements from a sequence.\n\n### What is Indexing?\n\nEvery element in a sequence has an **index (position)**.\n\nPython starts counting from **0**.\n\nExample List:\n\n```python\nfruits = ['Apple', 'Banana', 'Orange', 'Mango']\n\nprint(fruits[0])   # Apple\nprint(fruits[2])   # Orange\nprint(fruits[-1])  # Mango\n```\n\n• **0** → First element\n• **1** → Second element\n• **-1** → Last element\n• **-2** → Second last element\n\n### What is Slicing?\n\nSlicing allows us to retrieve a portion of a sequence.\n\nSyntax:\n\n```python\nsequence[start:stop:step]\n```\n\n• **start** → Starting index (included)\n\n• **stop** → Ending index (excluded)\n\n• **step** → Number of positions to skip (optional)\n\nExample:\n\n```python\nnumbers = [10,20,30,40,50,60]\n\nprint(numbers[1:4])   # [20, 30, 40]\nprint(numbers[:3])    # [10, 20, 30]\nprint(numbers[3:])    # [40, 50, 60]\nprint(numbers[::2])   # [10, 30, 50]\nprint(numbers[::-1])  # [60, 50, 40, 30, 20, 10]\n```\n\nDictionary Example:\n\n```python\nstudent = {\n    'Name': 'Emma',\n    'Age': 22\n}\n\nprint(student['Name'])\n# Emma\n```\n\nSet Example:\n\n```python\ncolors = {'Red', 'Blue', 'Green'}\n\n# Sets are unordered\n# colors[0]  ❌ Error\n```\n\n### Key Takeaway\n\nUse **Indexing** when you want **one element**, and use **Slicing** when you want **multiple elements**. Lists, Tuples, and Strings support both indexing and slicing, Dictionaries support indexing using **keys**, while Sets do not support either because they are unordered collections."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Practice Indexing and Slicing",
            "question": "Create a list named `colors` containing the values **'Red'**, **'Blue'**, **'Green'**, **'Yellow'**, and **'Purple'**.\n\nThen complete the following tasks:\n\n1. Print the first color.\n2. Print the last color using negative indexing.\n3. Print the second and third colors using slicing.\n4. Print the first three colors.\n5. Print all colors except the first one.\n6. Print the list in reverse order using slicing.",
            "starterCode": "# Create the list\n\n\n# 1. Print the first color\n\n\n# 2. Print the last color\n\n\n# 3. Print the second and third colors\n\n\n# 4. Print the first three colors\n\n\n# 5. Print all colors except the first one\n\n\n# 6. Print the list in reverse order\n",
            "solution": "colors = ['Red', 'Blue', 'Green', 'Yellow', 'Purple']\n\n# 1. Print the first color\nprint(colors[0])\n\n# 2. Print the last color\nprint(colors[-1])\n\n# 3. Print the second and third colors\nprint(colors[1:3])\n\n# 4. Print the first three colors\nprint(colors[:3])\n\n# 5. Print all colors except the first one\nprint(colors[1:])\n\n# 6. Print the list in reverse order\nprint(colors[::-1])",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use positive indexing (0, 1, 2...), negative indexing (-1), and slicing with the syntax `list[start:stop:step]`."
          }
        },
