      "id": "M001-5-oop",
      "title": "Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Classes and Objects",
            "explanation": "Until now, you have learned how to use variables, conditions, loops, and functions. These work well for small programs. But as programs become larger, managing everything with separate variables and functions can become difficult.\n\nPython solves this problem using **Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)**. The main idea of OOP is to keep related data and related actions together in one place.\n\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/python/oop1.png]\n\nOOP is based on two main concepts: **Class** and **Object**.\n\n**1. Class (Blueprint):**\nA class is like a blueprint or design for creating objects. It defines the properties (data) and behaviors (actions) that every object created from it will have.\n\nIn the image above, the blueprint represents a **Car** class. It describes that every car will have properties such as a color, steering wheel, wheels, and fuel level, along with behaviors such as starting, stopping, or driving. However, the blueprint itself is not a real car.\n\n**2. Object (Real Instance):**\nAn object is a real instance created from a class.\n\nIn the image, the **red car** and the **blue car** are two different objects created from the same **Car** class. Both cars have the same structure because they were created from the same blueprint, but each object stores its own values. For example, one car is red while the other is blue, and each can have a different fuel level or other property values.\n\n**Why Do We Need OOP?**\nWithout OOP, we would need many separate variables and functions to manage related information. OOP helps us organize everything into objects, making programs easier to understand, maintain, and reuse.\n\nObject-Oriented Programming is widely used in websites, mobile apps, games, banking systems, desktop software, and AI applications. As your projects become larger, OOP helps you write cleaner, more organized, and reusable code.",
            "example": "class Car:\n    color = 'Red'\n    wheels = 4\n\ncar1 = Car()\ncar2 = Car()\n\ncar2.color = 'Blue'\n\nprint(car1.color)\nprint(car2.color)\nprint(car1.wheels)"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Class vs Object",
            "question": "Drag and drop the correct term into each blank.",
