              "Class",
              "Object"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Constructors (__init__) and 'self'",
            "explanation": "Now that you know what classes and objects are, let's learn how objects receive their initial values.\nWhen a new object is created, we often want to give it some information immediately. For example, when creating a student object, we may want to store the student's name and age.\nTo do this, Python provides a special method called `__init__()`, also known as the constructor.\n\nThe constructor runs automatically whenever an object is created. Its job is to initialize the object's data.\nYou will also notice a special keyword called `self`. The `self` keyword refers to the current object. It allows each object to store and access its own data.\n\nFor example, if we create two students, John and Jhon, `self` ensures that John's data stays separate from Jhon's data.\nWhenever you create a method inside a class, `self` should be the first parameter.",
            "example": "class Student:\n    def __init__(self, name, age):\n        self.name = name\n        self.age = age\n\nstudent1 = Student('John', 21)\nprint(student1.name)",
            "warning": "Never forget the double underscores on `__init__` (called 'dunder' init) and always include `self` as the first parameter in class methods!"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Create a Student class with an __init__ method that takes name and age as parameters. Then, create an instance called student1 with the name 'John' and age 20.",
            "starterCode": "class Student:\n    # Write the __init__ method here\n    \n\n# Create student1 here\n",
            "solution": "class Student:\n    def __init__(self, name, age):\n        self.name = name\n        self.age = age\n\nstudent1 = Student('John', 20)",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use def __init__(self, name, age): and assign them to self.name and self.age."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Inheritance",
            "explanation": "Sometimes different classes share common features. Instead of writing the same code again and again, Python allows one class to inherit features from another class.\n\nThis concept is called **Inheritance**.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/python/oop3.png]\nImagine we have a `Person` class with a name and a method to introduce themselves. A `Student` is also a person, so instead of creating everything from scratch, the Student class can inherit from the Person class.\n\nThe class being inherited from is called the **Parent Class**, and the class that inherits is called the **Child Class**.\n\nInheritance helps reduce code duplication and makes programs easier to maintain.\n\nOne of the biggest advantages of inheritance is that the child class automatically gets the features of the parent class while also being able to add its own unique features.",
            "example": "class Person:\n    def greet(self):\n        print('Hello')\n\nclass Student(Person):\n    pass\n\nstudent = Student()\nstudent.greet()"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Types of Inheritance",
            "explanation": "**1. Single Inheritance**\n•One child inherits from one parent.\n•Example: Animal → Dog\n•(A Dog gets Animal features like eat() and sleep()).\n**2. Multiple Inheritance**\n•One child inherits from multiple parents.\n•Example: Camera + Phone → Smartphone\n•(A Smartphone can take photos and make calls.)\n**3. Multilevel Inheritance**\n•Inheritance happens in a chain.\n•Example: Animal → Mammal → Dog\n•(Dog inherits from Mammal, which inherits from Animal.)\n**4. Hierarchical Inheritance**\n•Multiple children inherit from the same parent.\n•Example: Vehicle → Car, Bike, Bus\n•(All share common Vehicle features.)\n**5. Hybrid Inheritance**\n•Combination of multiple inheritance types.\n•Example: Vehicle → Car, Aircraft → Plane, Car + Plane → FlyingCar\n•(FlyingCar can drive and fly.)\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/python/oop4.png]\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Create a parent class called `Animal` with a method `speak()` that prints 'Animal Sound'. Then create a child class called `Dog` that inherits from `Animal`. Create an object of `Dog` and call the `speak()` method.",
            "starterCode": "# Write your code below:\n",
            "solution": "class Animal:\n    def speak(self):\n        print('Animal Sound')\n\nclass Dog(Animal):\n    pass\n\ndog = Dog()\ndog.speak()",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use `class Dog(Animal):` to inherit from the parent class."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Encapsulation",
            "explanation": "Imagine you are using an ATM. You can check your balance, deposit money, and withdraw money, but you cannot directly access or modify the bank's internal database. This is done to keep your account information safe.\nThis idea is called **Encapsulation**.\nEncapsulation means keeping important data protected and allowing access to it only through controlled methods. Instead of directly changing data, users interact with the object using methods provided by the class.\n\nIn Python, data and methods are grouped together inside a class. Some data can be marked as private using double underscores (`__`). Private data should not be accessed directly from outside the class.\n\nFor example, in a bank account, the balance should not be modified directly because it could lead to invalid values. Instead, methods such as `deposit()`, `withdraw()`, and `get_balance()` are used to safely access and update the balance.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/python/oop5.png]\nEncapsulation provides several benefits:\n• Protects important data from accidental changes.\n• Improves security by restricting direct access.\n• Reduces bugs by controlling how data is modified.\n• Makes programs easier to maintain and understand.\n\nIn simple words, Encapsulation means hiding important details inside a class and providing safe ways to interact with them.",
            "example": "class BankAccount:\n    def __init__(self, balance):\n        self.__balance = balance # Private variable\n\n    def get_balance(self): # Public getter method\n        return self.__balance\n\n    def deposit(self, amount):\n        if amount > 0:\n            self.__balance += amount\n\naccount = BankAccount(1000)\n# print(account.__balance) # This will crash! It's private.\nprint(account.get_balance()) # This is the correct, safe way."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Create a class called `Student` with a private variable `__grade` set to 85. Add a method called `display_grade()` that prints the grade. Create an object and call the method.",
            "starterCode": "# Write your code below:\n",
            "solution": "class Student:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.__grade = 85\n\n    def display_grade(self):\n        print(self.__grade)\n\nstudent = Student()\nstudent.display_grade()",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Private variables start with double underscores (`__`)."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Polymorphism",
            "explanation": "\nNow that you understand inheritance, let's learn another powerful OOP concept called **Polymorphism**. The word polymorphism means 'many forms'.\nPolymorphism allows the same method name to behave differently depending on which object is using it.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/python/oop6.png]\nFor example, both a Dog and a Cat can have a method called `sound()`. Even though the method name is the same, each object can produce a different result.\nThis makes programs more flexible because the same interface can work with different types of objects.\n\nPolymorphism is commonly used in large applications to make code reusable and easier to extend.",
            "example": "class Dog:\n    def sound(self):\n        print('Bark')\n\nclass Cat:\n    def sound(self):\n        print('Meow')\n\ndog = Dog()\ncat = Cat()\n\ndog.sound()\ncat.sound()"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Create two classes, `Bike` and `Car`, each with a method called `move()`. The Bike should print 'Bike is moving' and the Car should print 'Car is moving'. Create objects of both classes and call their `move()` methods.",
            "starterCode": "# Write your code below:\n",
            "solution": "class Bike:\n    def move(self):\n        print('Bike is moving')\n\nclass Car:\n    def move(self):\n        print('Car is moving')\n\nbike = Bike()\ncar = Car()\n\nbike.move()\ncar.move()",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Polymorphism allows different classes to use the same method name."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Understanding Polymorphism",
            "question": "Fill in the blank with the correct concept.",
            "template": [
              "Polymorphism means 'many ____'.",
              "",
              "The same method can behave differently for different objects."
            ],
            "options": [
              "forms",
              "classes",
              "variables",
              "loops"
            ],
            "answers": {
              "blank_1": "forms"
            },
            "explanation": "Polymorphism means many forms. Different objects can use the same method name but produce different results.",
            "hint": "Think about the meaning of the word polymorphism."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Abstraction",
            "explanation": "\nAs programs become more complex, users do not need to know every implementation detail. This is where **Abstraction** helps.\nAbstraction means hiding complex internal details and showing only the essential features.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/python/oop7.png]\nThink about driving a car. You use the steering wheel, brake, and accelerator without needing to understand how the engine works internally.\nSimilarly, abstraction allows programmers to focus on what an object does rather than how it does it.\n\nThis makes code easier to use, understand, and maintain.\n`from abc import ABC`, abstractmethod imports Python's Abstract Base Class (ABC) and abstractmethod decorator, which are used to create classes that define a common structure and require child classes to implement specific methods.",
            "example": "from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n\nclass Vehicle(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    def start(self):\n        pass\n\nclass Car(Vehicle):\n    def start(self):\n        print('Car Started')\n\ncar = Car()\ncar.start()"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Method Overriding",
            "explanation": "\n`Method Overriding` happens when a child class provides its own version of a method that already exists in the parent class.\n\nThis allows child classes to customize behavior while still inheriting features from the parent class.\n\nFor example, an Animal class may have a `sound()` method, but a Dog class can override it to provide its own implementation.\n\nMethod Overriding is commonly used with inheritance to create specialized behavior.",
            "example": "class Animal:\n    def sound(self):\n        print('Animal Sound')\n\nclass Dog(Animal):\n    def sound(self):\n        print('Bark')\n\ndog = Dog()\ndog.sound()",
            "tip": "When a child class defines a method with the same name as the parent class, the child version is executed."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Create an abstract class called `Shape` with an abstract method `draw()`. Then create a class `Circle` that inherits from `Shape` and implements `draw()` to print 'Drawing Circle'. Create a Circle object and call the method.",
            "starterCode": "from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n\n# Write your code below:\n",
            "solution": "from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n\nclass Shape(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    def draw(self):\n        pass\n\nclass Circle(Shape):\n    def draw(self):\n        print('Drawing Circle')\n\ncircle = Circle()\ncircle.draw()",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Abstract methods must be implemented by child classes."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
