    {
      "id": "M001-file-handling-data",
      "title": "File Handling & Data Processing",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "File Operations: Reading and Writing",
            "explanation": "Until now, all the data we worked with existed only while the program was running. Once the program stopped, that data was lost.\n\nBut in real-world applications, we often need to save information permanently. For example, a notes application saves your notes, a banking application stores transaction records, and a school management system stores student details.\nTo save and retrieve information, Python provides **File Handling**.\n**The `with` Statement:**\nWhen working with files, Python needs to open the file before using it and close it when the work is finished.\nInstead of manually closing files, Python provides the `with` statement. It automatically closes the file after the work is completed, making the code safer and easier to write.\n\nBecause of this, `with open()` is the recommended way to work with files in Python.\n**File Modes:**\n• `'r'` (Read Mode):** Used to read data from an existing file.\nExample: Reading notes from a text file.\n\n• `'w'` (Write Mode):** Used to write data to a file.\nIf the file already contains data, the old data will be removed and replaced with the new data.\n• `'a'` (Append Mode):** Used to add new data at the end of a file without deleting the existing content.\n\n**Real-World Example:**\nImagine a diary application. Every time you write a new diary entry, the application can save it in a file. Later, you can open the file and read all your previous entries.\nFile handling is an important skill because most applications need to store data permanently so it can be used again in the future.",
            "example": "with open('notes.txt', 'w') as file:\n    file.write('Learning Python File Handling')\n\nwith open('notes.txt', 'r') as file:\n    content = file.read()\n    print(content)"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Use a `with` statement to open a file named `status.txt` in write mode (`'w'`) and write the string 'All systems nominal.' to it.",
            "starterCode": "# Write your file operation below:\n",
            "solution": "with open('status.txt', 'w') as file:\n    file.write('All systems nominal.')",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use `with open('filename', 'mode') as file:` followed by `file.write()`."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "CSV Processing",
            "explanation": "Now that you know how to read and write files, let's learn about one of the most commonly used file formats in programming and data analysis: the **CSV file**.\nCSV stands for **Comma-Separated Values**. It is a simple file format used to store data in rows and columns, similar to a spreadsheet.\nFor example, a CSV file can store student records, employee details, product information, or sales data.\nPython provides a built-in module called `csv` that makes it easy to work with CSV files.\n\nThere are two common ways to read CSV data:\n`csv.reader() :`This reads each row as a list. To access values, you use index numbers.\nExample:\nIf a row contains `['John', '21', 'Pune']`, then:\n• `row[0]` gives `'John'`\n• `row[1]` gives `'21'`\n\n`csv.DictReader() :`This reads each row as a dictionary using the column names as keys.\nExample:\nIf a row contains `{'Name': 'John', 'Age': '21', 'City': 'Pune'}`, then:\n• `row['Name']` gives `'John'`\n• `row['Age']` gives `'21'`\n\nMany developers prefer `DictReader()` because the code is easier to read and understand. Instead of remembering column positions, you can directly use the column names.\n\nCSV files are widely used because they are simple, lightweight, and supported by tools such as Excel, Google Sheets, databases, and data analysis applications.",
            "example": "import csv\n\n# Reading a CSV into dictionaries\nwith open('users.csv', 'r') as file:\n    reader = csv.DictReader(file)\n    for row in reader:\n        print(f\"{row['Name']} is {row['Age']} years old.\")",
            "warning": "Data from a CSV is always read as a String. If the age is 25, Python sees it as '25'. You must cast it to an `int()` before doing math."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Read `employees.csv` using `csv.DictReader()`. Print a sentence in the format: '<Name> works in <Department>' for each row.",
            "starterCode": "import csv\n\n# Write your code below:\n",
            "solution": "import csv\n\nwith open('employees.csv', 'r') as file:\n    reader = csv.DictReader(file)\n    for row in reader:\n        print(f\"{row['Name']} works in {row['Department']}\")",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Access values using the column names as dictionary keys."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "JSON Processing (File I/O)",
            "explanation": "Now that you know how to work with files, let's learn how to store and read data using **JSON files**.\nLet's discuss first what is JSON (JavaScript Object Notation_? JSON is one of the most popular formats for storing and exchanging data because it is simple, readable, and supported by almost every programming language.\nA JSON file stores data in a structured format using key-value pairs, similar to a Python dictionary.\nFor example:\n`{\"name\": \"John\", \"age\": 21}`\nMany real-world applications use JSON files to save settings, application configurations, user preferences, API responses, and cached data.\n\n**Common Use Cases:**\n• Saving application settings such as theme, language, and preferences.\n• Storing user information.\n• Saving API responses for later use.\n• Configuration files used by websites and applications.\n• Data exchange between different systems.\n\nPython provides a built-in module called `json` to work with JSON files.\n\n`json.dump(data, file)`\nThis function is used to write a Python dictionary into a JSON file.\nThink of it as saving your Python data permanently into a file.\n\n`json.load(file)`\nThis function is used to read data from a JSON file and convert it back into a Python dictionary.\nThink of it as loading previously saved data back into your program.\nWhen writing JSON files, you may see the parameter `indent=4`. This simply formats the JSON file neatly, making it easier for humans to read.",
            "example": "import json\n\nconfig = {\"theme\": \"dark\", \"max_retries\": 5}\n\n# Writing to a JSON file\nwith open('config.json', 'w') as f:\n    json.dump(config, f, indent=4) # indent makes it readable!\n\n# Reading from a JSON file\nwith open('config.json', 'r') as f:\n    loaded_config = json.load(f)\n    print(loaded_config['theme'])",
            "tip": "Remember: `dump()` and `load()` are used with files, while `dumps()` and `loads()` are used with strings."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "teacher_vis",
          "content": {
            "title": "The JSON Function Matrix",
            "sections": [
              {
                "label": "String to Dict",
                "text": "Converts a JSON string into a Python dictionary.",
                "code": "import json\n\njson_string = '{\"name\": \"John\", \"age\": 25}'\ndata = json.loads(json_string)\nprint(data)"
              },
              {
                "label": "Dict to String",
                "text": "Converts a Python dictionary into a JSON string.",
                "code": "import json\n\ndata = {\"name\": \"John\", \"age\": 25}\njson_string = json.dumps(data)\nprint(json_string)"
              },
              {
                "label": "File to Dict",
                "text": "Reads JSON data from a file and converts it into a Python dictionary.",
                "code": "import json\n\nwith open('data.json', 'r') as f:\n    data = json.load(f)\n\nprint(data)"
              },
              {
                "label": "Dict to File",
                "text": "Writes a Python dictionary into a JSON file.",
                "code": "import json\n\ndata = {\"name\": \"John\", \"age\": 25}\n\nwith open('data.json', 'w') as f:\n    json.dump(data, f, indent=4)"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Create a dictionary called `user` with keys `name` and `age` and values `Alice` and `25`. Write the dictionary to a file called `user.json` using `json.dump()`. Then read the file using `json.load()` and print the user's name.",
            "starterCode": "import json\n\n# Write your code below:\n",
            "solution": "import json\n\nuser = {'name': 'Alice', 'age': 25}\n\nwith open('user.json', 'w') as f:\n    json.dump(user, f, indent=4)\n\nwith open('user.json', 'r') as f:\n    loaded_user = json.load(f)\n    print(loaded_user['name'])",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use `json.dump()` to save the dictionary and `json.load()` to read it back."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Data Validation & Exception Handling",
            "explanation": "While working with files, user input, or external data, we cannot always assume that the data is correct. Sometimes a file may contain missing values, invalid information, or unexpected data.\nFor example, if a program expects a number but receives the text `'ten'`, Python will generate an error because text cannot be directly converted into a number.\nIf errors are not handled properly, the program may stop running and crash. To prevent this, Python provides **Exception Handling**.\nException Handling allows us to detect errors and respond to them safely without stopping the entire program.\n\nThe most common way to handle errors is by using `try` and `except` blocks.\n\n**1. `try` Block:**\nThe code that might cause an error is placed inside the `try` block.\nPython attempts to execute this code normally.\n**2. `except` Block:**\nIf an error occurs inside the `try` block, Python immediately jumps to the `except` block.\nInstead of crashing the program, we can display a helpful message or take another action.\n\n**Real-World Example:**\nImagine a user enters their age in an application. If they enter `25`, everything works correctly. However, if they enter `'twenty-five'`, Python cannot convert that text into a number.\nUsing `try` and `except`, we can catch the error and inform the user to enter a valid number.",
            "example": "data_points = ['100', '250', 'N/A', '300']\nclean_data = []\n\nfor item in data_points:\n    try:\n        # Risky operation\n        value = int(item)\n        clean_data.append(value)\n    except ValueError:\n        # Graceful recovery\n        print(f\"Warning: Could not parse '{item}'. Skipping.\")\n\nprint(clean_data) # Output: [100, 250, 300]",
            "tip": "Never use a bare `except:`. Always specify the exact error you are anticipating, like `except ValueError:` or `except FileNotFoundError:`. Catching everything masks deeper bugs."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Ask the user to enter their age. Use a `try` and `except` block to convert the input to an integer. If the conversion succeeds, print the age. If the user enters invalid data, print 'Please enter a valid number.'",
            "starterCode": "# Write your code below:\n",
            "solution": "try:\n    age = int(input('Enter your age: '))\n    print(age)\nexcept ValueError:\n    print('Please enter a valid number.')",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "The `int()` conversion may raise a `ValueError` if the input is not a number."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "title": "The Corrupt Sensor Log",
          "description": "Your IoT weather sensor is writing temperatures to a CSV. You are writing a script to calculate the daily average. While testing, the script crashes on row 402 because the sensor briefly lost power and wrote the word 'ERROR' instead of a number. How do you fix the script?",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "Manually open the CSV in Excel, delete row 402, and run the script again.",
              "outcome": "Terrible idea. What if it happens tomorrow? You can't manually edit logs every day. Automation is key.",
              "score": 0
            },
            {
              "text": "Use an if-statement: `if row['temp'] != 'ERROR':` before converting to float.",
              "outcome": "Better, but brittle. What if the next glitch writes 'NULL' or 'TIMEOUT'? You'd have to account for every possible weird word.",
              "score": 50
            },
            {
              "text": "Wrap the `float(row['temp'])` conversion in a `try...except ValueError:` block to skip any un-mathable text.",
              "outcome": "Perfect Engineering! This guarantees the script survives NO MATTER WHAT weird text the sensor accidentally outputs.",
              "score": 100
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "File Handling and Data Processing Review",
            "question": "Drag and drop the correct term into each blank.",
            "template": [
