    {
      "id": "M001-pandas-introduction",
      "title": "Introduction to Pandas",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is Pandas?",
            "explanation": "In the previous lessons, you learned Python programming. Now it's time to work with real-world data.\n\n**Pandas** is one of the most popular Python libraries used for working with structured data. It helps us load, organize, clean, analyze, and manipulate data efficiently.\n\nImagine a company has thousands of customer records stored in an Excel or CSV file. Reading and processing this data manually would take a lot of time. With Pandas, we can load the entire dataset and perform operations such as filtering, sorting, updating, and analyzing the data using just a few lines of code.\n\n### Why Do We Use Pandas?\n\n• Read data from CSV, Excel, JSON, SQL databases, and more.\n• Clean missing or incorrect data.\n• Filter and sort records.\n• Perform calculations and statistical analysis.\n• Prepare data for Machine Learning and AI models.\n• Save processed data back into files.\n\n### Real-World Applications\n\n• Data Analysis\n• Machine Learning\n• Business Intelligence\n• Financial Analysis\n• Healthcare Analytics\n• Sales Reporting\n• Data Preprocessing for AI",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"font-family:Segoe UI,sans-serif;background:#0f172a;padding:24px;border-radius:12px;color:white;\"><h2 style=\"text-align:center;color:#60a5fa;\">Why Pandas?</h2><div style=\"display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;gap:12px;margin-top:30px;\"><div style=\"flex:1;background:#1e293b;padding:18px;border-radius:10px;text-align:center;\"><h3>📄 CSV / Excel</h3><p>Raw Data</p></div><div style=\"font-size:32px;color:#38bdf8;\">→</div><div style=\"flex:1;background:#1e293b;padding:18px;border-radius:10px;text-align:center;\"><h3>🐼 Pandas</h3><p>Clean & Analyze</p></div><div style=\"font-size:32px;color:#38bdf8;\">→</div><div style=\"flex:1;background:#1e293b;padding:18px;border-radius:10px;text-align:center;\"><h3>📊 Insights</h3><p>Charts, Reports & AI</p></div></div></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Pandas Data Structures",
            "explanation": "Now that you know how Pandas works with data, let's learn how it stores that data.\n\nPandas mainly provides **two data structures**: **Series** and **DataFrame**.\n\n### 1. Series\nA **Series** is a one-dimensional collection of data. You can think of it as a **single column** in a table.\n\n<div style=\"background:#0f172a;padding:18px;border-radius:10px;margin:12px 0;\"><div style=\"max-width:220px;margin:auto;background:#1e293b;border-radius:8px;padding:12px;\"><h4 style=\"text-align:center;color:#60a5fa;margin-top:0;\">Series</h4><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:white;text-align:center;\"><tr style=\"background:#334155;\"><th>Age</th></tr><tr><td>24</td></tr><tr><td>31</td></tr><tr><td>27</td></tr><tr><td>29</td></tr></table></div></div>\n\n### 2. DataFrame\nA **DataFrame** is a two-dimensional table made up of **rows and columns**. It is similar to an Excel spreadsheet or a SQL table.\n\n<div style=\"background:#0f172a;padding:18px;border-radius:10px;margin:12px 0;\"><div style=\"background:#1e293b;border-radius:8px;padding:12px;\"><h4 style=\"text-align:center;color:#60a5fa;margin-top:0;\">DataFrame</h4><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:white;text-align:center;\"><tr style=\"background:#334155;\"><th>Name</th><th>Age</th><th>City</th></tr><tr><td>Emma</td><td>24</td><td>London</td></tr><tr><td>Liam</td><td>31</td><td>New York</td></tr><tr><td>Olivia</td><td>27</td><td>Sydney</td></tr></table></div></div>\n\nA **Series** stores data in a single column, while a **DataFrame** stores data in multiple rows and columns. In real-world projects, you'll mostly work with **DataFrames** because datasets usually contain many columns such as names, ages, salaries, and departments."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Creating Series and DataFrames",
            "explanation": "Now that you know the two data structures, let's see how to create them.Before working with Pandas, you first need to install it using `pip install pandas`.\n\nA **Series** is created from a single list.\n\nA **DataFrame** is created using a dictionary where each key becomes a column.\n\nIn real-world projects, you usually don't create DataFrames manually. Instead, you load them from files such as CSV, Excel, or databases.",
            "example": "import pandas as pd\n\n# Create Series\nages = pd.Series([25,30,22,28])\nprint(ages)\n\n# Create DataFrame\nstudents = pd.DataFrame({\n    'Name':['John','Sara','Tom'],\n    'Age':[25,30,22],\n    'City':['Delhi','Mumbai','Pune']\n})\n\nprint(students)",
            "html_code": "<div style='background:#111;padding:10px 15px;border-top:1px solid #333;color:#4ec9b0;font-weight:bold;'>Terminal</div><pre style='margin:0;padding:15px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;color:#dcdcdc;'>0    25\n1    30\n2    22\n3    28\ndtype: int64\n\n   Name  Age    City\n0  John   25   Delhi\n1  Sara   30  Mumbai\n2   Tom   22    Pune</pre></div>"
