          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Common Pandas Functions",
            "explanation": "Pandas provides many built-in functions to quickly understand your dataset.\n\n### Frequently Used Functions\n\n**head()** → Shows the first 5 rows.\n\n**tail()** → Shows the last 5 rows.\n\n**shape** → Returns the number of rows and columns.\n\n**columns** → Displays all column names.\n\n**dtypes** → Shows the data type of each column.\n\n**info()** → Provides a summary of the dataset.\n\n**describe()** → Displays statistical information for numeric columns.\n\nThese functions are usually the first ones developers use after loading a dataset.",
            "example": "import pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.read_csv('students.csv')\n\nprint(df.head())\nprint(df.shape)\nprint(df.info())\nprint(df.describe())"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Loading Data into Pandas",
            "explanation": "In real-world projects, data is usually stored in files or databases rather than being created manually.\n\nPandas can load data from many different sources.\n\n### Common Data Sources\n\n• CSV Files (.csv)\n• Excel Files (.xlsx)\n• JSON Files (.json)\n• SQL Databases\n\nEach source stores data in a different format, but Pandas converts all of them into a DataFrame, making them easy to analyze.\n\nIn the next lesson, we'll learn how these file formats look and how to read them using Pandas.",
            "example": "import pandas as pd\nimport sqlite3\n\n# Load CSV file\ncsv_df = pd.read_csv('students.csv')\n\n# Load Excel file\nexcel_df = pd.read_excel('students.xlsx')\n\n# Load JSON file\njson_df = pd.read_json('students.json')\n\n# Load data from a SQL database\nconn = sqlite3.connect('students.db')\nsql_df = pd.read_sql('SELECT * FROM students', conn)\nconn.close()"
          }
