              "text": "They forgot to create a virtual environment and install the required packages on their specific machine.",
              "outcome": "Exactly! The Python code was downloaded, but the external libraries (which live in the un-versioned venv) were not.",
              "score": 100
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Package Management (pip & requirements.txt)",
            "explanation": "Now that you know how to create a virtual environment, let's learn how to add extra features to our Python projects.Python comes with many built-in features, but sometimes we need additional functionality that is not included by default. For example, we may want to work with data, create charts, build websites, or interact with APIs.\nTo add these capabilities, developers use **packages**.\n\nA **package** is a collection of pre-written Python code created by other developers. Instead of writing everything from scratch, you can install a package and use its functionality in your own project.\n**For Example**: Need to work with tables of data? Install the pandas package using **pip install pandas** instead of building data-processing tools from scratch.\n\nTo install and manage packages, Python provides a tool called **pip**.\n**pip** (Pip Installs Packages) is Python's official package manager. It allows you to download, install, update, and remove packages.\nWhen a virtual environment is active, packages installed with pip are available only to that project. But how can others know which packages your project requires?This is where **requirements.txt** becomes useful.\n\nA **requirements.txt** file is a simple text file that contains a list of all the packages required by a project, along with their versions.\n**Create it (Freezing):**\n• `pip freeze > requirements.txt`\nThis command checks all packages currently installed in the virtual environment and saves them into a requirements.txt file.\n**Use it (Installing):**\n• `pip install -r requirements.txt`\nThis command reads the requirements.txt file and installs all the listed packages automatically.\nUsing `pip` and `requirements.txt` helps keep projects organized and ensures that everyone working on the project uses the same package versions.",
            "example": "# What a requirements.txt file looks like:\nnumpy==1.26.4\npandas==2.2.0\nfastapi==0.109.2\nuvicorn==0.27.0.post1",
            "warning": "Always run `pip freeze > requirements.txt` before pushing your code to GitHub so others know what to install!"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Your project uses the `pandas` package. Write the pip command to install `pandas` version 2.2.0, then write the command to save all installed packages to a `requirements.txt` file.",
            "starterCode": "# Write the commands below:\n",
            "solution": "pip install pandas==2.2.0\npip freeze > requirements.txt",
            "language": "bash",
            "hint": "Use `pip install` to install a package and `pip freeze` to generate a requirements file."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "pip and Requirements Review",
            "question": "Fill in the blanks using the correct package management concepts.",
