        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Async Programming Deep Dive",
            "explanation": "Let's Discuss about **Asynchronous Programming** in a very simple way.\n\nImagine you order food from a restaurant. After placing the order, you do not stand at the counter doing nothing until the food arrives. Instead, you sit with your friends, talk, check your phone, or do other activities while the food is being prepared.\nThis is exactly the idea behind asynchronous programming.\nNormally, Python works step by step. It starts one task and waits for it to finish before moving to the next task.\n\nFor example, if a program is downloading data from the internet and it takes 10 seconds, the program simply waits during those 10 seconds.\nAsynchronous programming allows Python to use that waiting time more effectively. Instead of doing nothing, Python can work on other tasks and return when the waiting task is finished.\n**Real World Use Cases :**\n• **Downloading Files: ** Download multiple files simultaneously instead of waiting for each file to finish before starting the next.\n• **Chat Applications : ** Send and receive messages in real time without making the app freeze.\n• **Online Shopping Websites:** Load product details, reviews, images, and recommendations at the same time for a faster experience.\n• **AI Applications:** Handle other user requests while waiting for the AI model to generate a response.\n\nPython provides three important keywords and tools:\n• `async def`Used to create a special function that can pause and continue later.\n• `await`Used when a task may take some time. It tells Python, 'While waiting for this task, do something else.'\n• `asyncio`A built-in Python library that helps manage these tasks.\nThe main benefit of asynchronous programming is that programs feel faster and more responsive because they do not spend all their time waiting.",
            "example": "import asyncio\n\nasync def fetch_api_data():\n    print(\"Starting API call...\")\n    await asyncio.sleep(3) # Simulates waiting 3 seconds for a network\n    print(\"Data received!\")\n    return {\"status\": 200}\n\n# To run an async function, you need an event loop:\n# asyncio.run(fetch_api_data())",
            "tip": "You cannot use `await` outside of an `async def` function. They are a matching pair!"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Async Programming Review",
            "question": "Fill in the blanks using the correct asynchronous programming concepts.",
            "template": [
              "Asynchronous programming helps a program do other work while ____ for a task to finish.",
              "The `____ def` keyword is used to create an asynchronous function.",
              "The `____` keyword tells Python to wait for a task while allowing other work to continue.",
              "The `____` library is used to manage asynchronous tasks in Python.",
              "Asynchronous programming is commonly used when working with APIs, databases, and ____."
            ],
            "options": [
              "waiting",
              "async",
              "await",
              "asyncio",
              "files",
              "loops",
              "classes"
            ],
            "answers": {
              "blank_1": "waiting",
              "blank_2": "async",
              "blank_3": "await",
              "blank_4": "asyncio",
              "blank_5": "files"
            },
            "explanation": "Async programming helps Python perform other tasks while waiting. `async` creates asynchronous functions, `await` pauses execution, and `asyncio` manages asynchronous tasks.",
            "hint": "Think about async, await, and asyncio."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "interactive_simulation",
          "title": "Synchronous vs Asynchronous Timeline",
          "description": "Watch how a server handles two users making AI requests that take 2 seconds each.",
          "steps": [
            {
              "label": "Synchronous (Blocking)",
              "action": "eval",
              "description": "User 1 requests. Server waits 2 seconds. User 1 done. User 2 requests. Server waits 2 seconds. User 2 done. Total: 4 seconds."
            },
            {
              "label": "Asynchronous (Non-Blocking)",
              "action": "branch",
              "description": "User 1 requests. Server starts task, hits 'await'. Server pauses User 1."
            },
            {
              "label": "Context Switch",
              "action": "eval",
              "description": "Server immediately accepts User 2's request, starts task, hits 'await'. Both tasks process in the background."
            },
            {
              "label": "Resolution",
              "action": "store",
              "description": "Both tasks finish almost simultaneously. Total: ~2 seconds. Server is 2x faster!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Import the `asyncio` library. Write an async function named `countdown` that prints '3', waits 1 second using `await asyncio.sleep()`, and then prints 'Done!'.",
            "starterCode": "# Write your async code below:\n\n\n",
            "solution": "import asyncio\n\nasync def countdown():\n    print('3')\n    await asyncio.sleep(1)\n    print('Done!')",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use `async def countdown():`. Remember to `import asyncio` at the top and use `await asyncio.sleep(1)` inside the function."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "teacher_vis",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Golden Rule of Async",
            "sections": [
              {
                "label": "I/O Bound vs CPU Bound",
                "text": "Async is magic for I/O Bound tasks (waiting for APIs, reading files). It does NOTHING for CPU Bound tasks (training a Neural Network, complex math)."
              },
              {
                "label": "The Analogy",
                "text": "Async is like a Chef. The Chef puts a pizza in the oven (await). The Chef doesn't stare at the oven for 15 minutes (Synchronous). The Chef goes and chops onions while the pizza bakes (Asynchronous)."
              },
              {
                "label": "Warning",
                "text": "If the Chef is chopping 1,000 onions (CPU Bound math), they can't do anything else until the chopping is done. Async cannot bypass hard CPU math."
              }
            ]
          }
        },
