    {
      "id": "M001-final-project",
      "title": "Practice Project: Student Information System",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Project Overview",
            "explanation": "Congratulations! You have completed the core Python concepts. Now it's time to combine everything you have learned into a small project.\n\nIn this project, you will build a simple Student Information System. The program will allow users to enter student details, display information, and interact with the program through a menu.\n\nThis project helps you practice variables, input/output, conditions, loops, functions, and basic data structures in a real-world scenario.\n\nBy the end of this project, you will understand how multiple Python concepts work together to build a complete application."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "How the Project Works",
            "explanation": "The program will continuously display a menu and wait for the user's choice.\n\nThe user can:\n\n1. Add a student name.\n2. View all student names.\n3. Exit the application.\n\nThe program uses a list to store student names, a loop to keep the application running, and conditions to perform different actions based on the user's choice.\n\nThis is similar to how many real-world applications work by repeatedly accepting user input and performing actions."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Build a Student Information System that allows users to add student names, view all students, and exit the program.",
            "starterCode": "students = []\n\n# Your code here",
            "solution": "students = []\n\nwhile True:\n    print('\\n1. Add Student')\n    print('2. View Students')\n    print('3. Exit')\n\n    choice = input('Enter your choice: ')\n\n    if choice == '1':\n        name = input('Enter student name: ')\n        students.append(name)\n        print('Student added successfully!')\n\n    elif choice == '2':\n        print('\\nStudent List:')\n        for student in students:\n            print(student)\n\n    elif choice == '3':\n        print('Goodbye!')\n        break\n\n    else:\n        print('Invalid choice. Please try again.')",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use a list to store student names, a while loop to keep the program running, and if-elif-else statements to handle user choices."
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "M002-ml-comprehensive-lesson-1",
      "title": "Introduction to Machine Learning & Core Paradigms",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#0f766e",
        "accent": "#115e59"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is Artificial Intelligence (AI)?",
            "explanation": "Welcome! Before we start learning **Machine Learning**, let's first understand **Artificial Intelligence (AI)** because Machine Learning is a part of AI.\n\n**Artificial Intelligence (AI)** is the ability of a computer or machine to perform tasks that normally require human intelligence. These tasks include learning, reasoning, understanding language, recognizing images, making decisions, and solving problems.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/ml/ai.png]\nThink about how humans work. When we see a picture, we can recognize objects. When someone asks us a question, we understand it and give an answer. We can also learn from our past experiences and improve over time. AI aims to give computers similar intelligent abilities.\n\nToday, AI is used almost everywhere around us.\n\n• Voice assistants like Siri, Alexa, and Google Assistant understand and answer our questions.\n\n• Google Maps finds the fastest route by analyzing traffic.\n\n• Netflix and YouTube recommend movies and videos based on our interests.\n\n• ChatGPT can answer questions, write code, summarize documents, and generate content.\n\n• Self-driving cars use AI to recognize roads, traffic signs, and nearby vehicles.\n\nHowever, AI itself is a very broad field. There are many techniques used to build intelligent systems, and one of the most important techniques is **Machine Learning (ML)**.\n\nIn the next topic, we will understand **what Machine Learning is** and how it fits inside the world of Artificial Intelligence."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is Machine Learning?",
            "explanation": "Have you ever wondered how YouTube recommends videos, how Netflix suggests movies, or how Gmail detects spam emails? These applications are able to make smart decisions because they use **Machine Learning**.\n\nBefore understanding Machine Learning, let's look at how traditional programming works.\n\nSuppose a program needs to check whether a person is eligible to vote. The rule can be written like this:\n\n```python\nage = 20\n\nif age >= 18:\n    print(\"Allow Voting\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Not Eligible to Vote\")\n```\n\nHere, the programmer writes the rule, and the computer simply follows it. This is called **Traditional Programming**.\n\nHowever, some problems are much more difficult. For example, how do we write thousands of rules to identify spam emails, predict house prices, or recommend videos? Writing every rule manually would be almost impossible.\n\nThis is where **Machine Learning (ML)** helps.Instead of giving the computer all the rules, we provide many examples and their correct answers. The computer studies these examples, finds patterns, and learns the rules by itself.\n\nThink about how a child learns to recognize cats. We don't explain every rule about ears, eyes, fur, and tail. Instead, we show many pictures and say, 'This is a cat.' After seeing enough examples, the child learns to recognize cats on their own.\n\nMachine Learning works in the same way. We give the computer data and correct answers, and it learns the rules automatically.\n\n**For example:**\nStudy Hours Data → Pass/Fail Data → Answers.\n\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/ml/in.png]\n\nAfter looking at many students' records, the computer learns the relationship between study hours and exam results. It can then predict whether a new student is likely to pass or fail. Refer to the diagram.\nIn the next topic, we'll explore the **Different Levels of AI** and understand how **Artificial Intelligence (AI)**, **Machine Learning (ML)**, and **Deep Learning (DL)** are connected, along with the role each of them plays in building intelligent systems.",
            "example": "• Traditional Programming: Data + Rules ➔ Answers\n• Machine Learning: Data + Answers ➔ Rules"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Different Levels of AI",
            "explanation": "Before we start building Machine Learning models, it's important to understand the relationship between `Artificial Intelligence (AI)`, `Machine Learning (ML)`, and `Deep Learning (DL)`. Many beginners think they are three completely different technologies, but they are actually connected.\n\nThink of them like three circles. `AI` is the biggest circle, `Machine Learning` is inside AI, and `Deep Learning` is inside Machine Learning. As we move from AI to ML and then to DL, the systems become more specialized in how they learn and solve problems.\n\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/ml/in1.png]\n\nLet's start with the biggest circle.\n\n**Artificial Intelligence (AI)**\n\nBefore learning Machine Learning, we first need to understand what Artificial Intelligence actually is.\n\nHave you ever used **ChatGPT**, **Google Maps**, **Siri**, or **Alexa**? These applications can answer questions, recognize speech, recommend videos, and even find the fastest route. They are able to perform these intelligent tasks because they use AI.\n\nIn simple words, `Artificial Intelligence (AI)` is the broad field of building computer systems that can perform tasks which normally require human intelligence, such as understanding language, recognizing images, making decisions, solving problems, and learning from experience.\n\n`Applications:` Chatbots, Virtual Assistants, Self-Driving Cars, Recommendation Systems, Smart Home Devices.\n\nNow, you might be wondering...\n\n**If AI can perform all these intelligent tasks, then what exactly is Machine Learning?**\n\nLet's understand that.\n\n**Machine Learning (ML)**\n\n`Machine Learning` is a part of Artificial Intelligence.\n\nInstead of writing every rule manually, we provide the computer with data and examples. The computer studies this data, finds patterns, and learns how to make predictions or decisions on its own.\n\nFor example, instead of writing thousands of rules to detect spam emails, we can train a Machine Learning model using thousands of spam and non-spam emails. After learning from these examples, the model can automatically identify whether a new email is spam.\n\n`Applications:` Spam Detection, House Price Prediction, Fraud Detection, Sales Forecasting, Product Recommendations.\n\nNow another question naturally comes to mind...\n\n**If Machine Learning can learn from data, then why do we need Deep Learning?**\n\nLet's find out.\n\n**Deep Learning (DL)**\n\n`Deep Learning` is a specialized part of Machine Learning.\n\nTraditional Machine Learning works well for many problems, but tasks such as recognizing faces, understanding speech, translating languages, or generating images are much more complex.\n\nTo solve these problems, we use Deep Learning.\n\nDeep Learning uses `Artificial Neural Networks` with many layers to automatically learn complex patterns from very large amounts of data. Because of this, it performs exceptionally well on images, audio, videos, and text.\n\n`Applications:` Face Recognition, Speech Recognition, Language Translation, Medical Image Analysis, Generative AI (ChatGPT, Image Generation).\n\n**Key Takeaway**\n\n`Artificial Intelligence` is the broad field of building intelligent systems.\n\n`Machine Learning` is a part of AI that enables computers to learn from data.\n\n`Deep Learning` is a specialized part of Machine Learning that uses deep neural networks to solve highly complex problems.\nIn the next topic, we'll explore some of the most common **Real-World Applications of Machine Learning** and see how businesses use ML to solve everyday problems. "
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Real-World Applications of Machine Learning",
            "explanation": "Now that we know what **Machine Learning (ML)** is, let's explore where it is used in our daily lives. Machine Learning is one of the most widely used technologies today because it helps computers learn patterns from data and make intelligent decisions without being explicitly programmed.\n\nHere are some common real-world applications of Machine Learning:\n\n**Email Spam Detection**\nEmail services like Gmail use Machine Learning to identify spam emails. By learning from millions of emails, the model can automatically move unwanted messages to the Spam folder.\n\n**Recommendation Systems**\nPlatforms like Netflix, YouTube, Spotify, and Amazon analyze your viewing or shopping history to recommend movies, songs, or products that match your interests.\n\n**Online Shopping**\nE-commerce websites recommend products based on your browsing history, previous purchases, and items viewed by similar customers.\n\n**Fraud Detection**\nBanks and payment systems use Machine Learning to detect unusual transactions. If a transaction looks suspicious, the system can block it or ask for additional verification.\n\n**Healthcare**\nMachine Learning helps doctors detect diseases, analyze X-rays and MRI scans, predict health risks, and recommend suitable treatments.\n\n**Self-Driving Cars**\nAutonomous vehicles use Machine Learning to recognize roads, traffic signs, pedestrians, and nearby vehicles, helping them make driving decisions.\n\n**Voice Assistants**\nApplications like Siri, Alexa, and Google Assistant use Machine Learning to understand spoken language and provide accurate responses.\n\n**Language Translation**\nServices such as Google Translate use Machine Learning to translate text between different languages while preserving the meaning.\n\n**Face Recognition**\nSmartphones and security systems use Machine Learning to recognize faces for unlocking devices, verifying identity, and improving security.\n\n**Chatbots and AI Assistants**\nCustomer support chatbots and AI assistants like ChatGPT use Machine Learning to understand user questions, generate responses, summarize documents, and assist with various tasks.\n\n**Key Idea:** Machine Learning is used in almost every industry today, including healthcare, finance, education, transportation, entertainment, cybersecurity, manufacturing, and e-commerce. It enables computers to learn from data, make predictions, automate tasks, and continuously improve their performance over time.\nIn the next topic, we'll explore the Workflow of Machine Learning."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Core Machine Learning Workflow",
            "explanation": "Now that we know what Machine Learning is, let's understand the steps involved in building a Machine Learning model. Every Machine Learning project follows a similar workflow. Think of it as a step-by-step process where each step prepares the model to make accurate predictions.\n\n**1. Data Collection**\nThe first step is to collect data. The quality and quantity of data directly affect how well the model learns.\n\n• Example: House prices, customer reviews, student marks, medical records, or sales data.\n\n**2. Data Preprocessing**\nReal-world data is often incomplete, duplicated, or contains errors. Before training the model, we clean and prepare the data.\n\n• Example: Remove duplicate records, fill missing values, and correct incorrect data.\n\n**3. Feature Selection**\nNot every piece of data is useful. We select only the important features that help the model make better predictions.\n\n• Example: To predict house prices, features like area, number of bedrooms, and location are useful, while the house ID is usually not.\n\n**4. Model Training**\nNow we provide the prepared data to a Machine Learning algorithm. The model studies the data, learns patterns, and understands the relationship between inputs and outputs.\n\n**5. Model Evaluation**\nAfter training, we test the model using new data that it has never seen before. This helps us measure how well the model performs.\n\n• Example: If the model predicts house prices accurately on new houses, it is considered a good model.\n\n**6. Model Deployment**\nOnce the model performs well, it is deployed so real users or applications can use it.\n\n• Example: A house price prediction model can be deployed as a website or mobile application where users enter house details and instantly receive the predicted price.\n\n**Key Idea:** Every successful Machine Learning project follows these six steps: **Collect Data → Prepare Data → Select Features → Train Model → Evaluate Model → Deploy Model**. Skipping any step can reduce the model's performance.\nThe next question is: Does every Machine Learning model learn the same way? Let's find out by exploring the three main types of Machine Learning in next topic."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Supervised vs. Unsupervised Learning",
            "explanation": "Machine Learning can be divided into three main types: `Supervised Learning`, `Unsupervised Learning`, and `Reinforcement Learning`. The main difference between them is `how the model learns from data`. Let's understand the first two in a simple way.\n\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/ml/in2.png]\n\n**Supervised Learning (Learning with a Teacher)**\n\nImagine you are preparing for an exam and your teacher gives you both the questions `and the correct answers`. By practicing these examples, you learn how to solve similar questions on your own.\n\nSupervised Learning works in the same way. The model is trained using `input data` along with the `correct output (labels)`. By learning from these examples, it can predict the correct output for new, unseen data.\n\n• `Goal:` Learn the relationship between inputs (features) and outputs (labels) to make accurate predictions.\n\n• `Real-World Example:` Predicting house prices using features such as area, number of bedrooms, and location with previously known house prices.\n\n• `Technical:` Uses `Labeled Data` for `Classification` and `Regression` tasks.\n\n**Unsupervised Learning (Learning without a Teacher)**\n\nNow imagine your teacher gives you a box of different fruits but doesn't tell you their names. You naturally start grouping similar fruits together based on their color, shape, or size.\n\nUnsupervised Learning works the same way. The model receives only the input data without any correct answers. It analyzes the data and discovers hidden patterns, similarities, or groups by itself.\n\n• `Goal:` Discover hidden structures, patterns, or clusters within the data.\n\n• `Real-World Example:` An e-commerce company automatically groups customers based on their shopping behavior for personalized marketing.\n\n• `Technical:` Uses `Unlabeled Data` for `Clustering`, `Dimensionality Reduction`, and `Anomaly Detection`.\nIn the next lesson, we'll learn about next types of machine learning."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Reinforcement Learning (RL)",
            "explanation": "Have you ever trained a pet dog? When the dog performs the correct action, you give it a treat. When it does something wrong, you do not reward it. Over time, the dog learns which actions lead to rewards and starts repeating those actions.\n**Reinforcement Learning** works in a very similar way.\nIn Reinforcement Learning, we have an **Agent** and an **Environment**.\n• **Agent:** The learner or decision-maker.\n• **Environment:** The world in which the agent operates.\nThe agent starts by observing its environment and then takes an action.\nAfter taking the action, the environment responds and provides feedback.\nThis feedback can be:\n• **Reward (+):** Given when the agent makes a good decision.\n• **Penalty (-):** Given when the agent makes a bad decision.\n\nThe goal of the agent is simple: earn as many rewards as possible and avoid penalties.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/ml/in3.png]\nLet's understand this with a simple example.\nImagine a robot is trying to find the exit of a maze.\n\n• The robot is the **Agent**.\n• The maze is the **Environment**.\n• Moving left, right, forward, or backward are **Actions**.\n• Reaching the exit gives a **Reward**.\n• Hitting a wall gives a **Penalty**.\n\nAt first, the robot does not know the correct path. It tries different actions, makes mistakes, receives feedback, and gradually learns the best route.\n\nAfter many attempts, it discovers the path that gives the highest reward.\nThis learning process is called **Reinforcement Learning**.\n\nReinforcement Learning is commonly used in:\n• Self-driving cars.\n• Game-playing AI.\n• Robots.\n• Recommendation systems.\n• Route optimization systems.\n\nThe key idea is that the agent learns by trying actions, receiving feedback, and improving its decisions over time.\nExcellent! We've now completed the introduction to Machine Learning. In the next lesson, we'll learn about Data Preprocessing and Feature Engineering, where we'll prepare raw data so that it is ready for training Machine Learning models."
          }
        },
