    {
      "id": "M002-ml-lesson-3",
      "title": "Data Preprocessing & Feature Engineering",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#f59e0b",
        "accent": "#d97706"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Anatomy of Data: Features, Labels & Types ",
            "explanation": "Excellent! Before we train a Machine Learning model, we first need to understand the data we are working with.\n\nThink of a dataset as an Excel spreadsheet. Each **row** represents one record, and each **column** stores a specific piece of information about that record.\n\nFor example, suppose we have a dataset of houses. Each row represents one house, while the columns contain information such as the house area, number of bedrooms, location, and selling price.\n\nNow, you might be wondering, **how does a Machine Learning model know which columns to learn from and which column to predict?**\n\nTo answer that, let's understand two important concepts that is `Features` and `Labels`.\n\n**What are Features (X)?**\n\n`Features` are the input values that describe a record. They provide the information that the model uses to learn patterns.\n\nFor our house dataset, the features could be:\n\n• House Area\n\n• Number of Bedrooms\n\n• Number of Bathrooms\n\n• House Location\n\n• Age of the House\n\n**What is a Label (Y)?**\n\nThe `Label` is the value that we want the model to predict.\n\nFor the same dataset:\n\nFeatures → House Area, Bedrooms, Bathrooms, Location\n\nLabel → House Price\n\nDuring training, the model learns the relationship between the Features and the Label so it can predict the price of a new house.\n\nNow that we understand Features and Labels, let's look at the types of data we commonly use.\n\n**Structured Data**\n\nStructured data is organized into rows and columns, making it easy to store and analyze.\n\nExamples include:\n\n• Student Records\n\n• Customer Details\n\n• Sales Reports\n\n• Bank Transactions\n\n**Unstructured Data**\n\nUnstructured data does not follow a table format. It includes information such as:\n\n• Images\n\n• Text Documents\n\n• Emails\n\n• Audio\n\n• Videos\n\nThis type of data usually requires additional preprocessing before it can be used for Machine Learning.\n\n**Example**\n\nImagine a hospital wants to predict whether a patient has a high risk of heart disease.\n\nFeatures:\n\n• Age\n\n• Blood Pressure\n\n• Cholesterol Level\n\n• Heart Rate\n\nLabel:\n\n• Heart Disease Risk (High / Low)\n\n**Key Takeaway**\n\nWhenever you work with a new dataset, ask yourself:\n\n• `What information is available?` → Features\n\n• `What am I trying to predict?` → Label\n\n• `Is the data organized in rows and columns or is it text, images, audio, or videos?` → Structured or Unstructured Data."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Data Quality & Bias",
            "explanation": "Machine Learning models learn from data. If the data is incorrect, incomplete, or unfair, the model may also produce incorrect or unfair results.\nThis is why **Data Quality** is very important in Machine Learning.\n\nAnother important concept is **Bias**. Bias occurs when a model learns unfair patterns from the training data and produces unfair predictions.\n**Historical Bias:**\nImagine a company has mostly hired experienced employees in the past. If a model is trained only on those records, it may incorrectly assume that new candidates without experience are never suitable.\n**Sampling Bias:**\nImagine you want to understand students' opinions about a school, but you only ask students from one classroom. The results may not represent all students. Similarly, if a model is trained on limited or unbalanced data, it may perform poorly in real-world situations.\n\nTo build reliable AI systems, we must use high-quality data that is accurate, complete, and fairly represents different situations.\n\nAlways remember: A Machine Learning model can only be as good as the data used to train it.\nIn the next lesson, we'll learn about what are Outlier and how to handle it.",
            "warning": "Never blindly trust your data. Always audit your datasets for demographic imbalances before training begins."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What are Outliers & How to Handle Them?",
            "explanation": "Great! Before training a Machine Learning model, there is another important step we should perform—`detecting and handling Outliers`.\n\nImagine you are analyzing the salaries of employees in a company. Most employees earn between `₹30,000 and ₹80,000` per month, but one record shows a salary of `₹5 Crore`. This value is very different from the rest of the data.\n\nSuch unusually large or unusually small values are called `Outliers`.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/ml/dp2.png]\n### What is an Outlier?\n\nAn `Outlier` is a data point that is significantly different from the majority of the data.\n\nFor example:\n\nStudent Marks:\n\n```text\n72, 75, 78, 80, 81, 79, 76, 250\n```\n\nHere, `250` is an outlier because exam marks cannot realistically be 250.\n\n### Why are Outliers a Problem?\n\nOutliers can negatively affect Machine Learning models because they may distort the actual pattern in the data.\n\nFor example:\n\n• They can increase prediction errors.\n\n• They can shift the average (mean).\n\n• They may reduce model accuracy.\n\n• Some algorithms, such as `Linear Regression`, are highly sensitive to outliers.\n\nHowever, not every outlier should be removed. Sometimes an outlier represents a genuine real-world event.\n\nFor example:\n\n• A customer purchasing products worth ₹10 lakh.\n\n• A luxury house priced much higher than normal houses.\n\n• A patient with an extremely high heart rate.\n\nIf the value is genuine, removing it may lead to loss of important information.\n\nBut Important thing is how do we detect **Outliers** ?\n\nLet's discuss one of the most commonly used techniques is the `Interquartile Range (IQR)` method.\n\nThe idea is simple:\n\n• Calculate the 25th percentile (Q1).\n\n• Calculate the 75th percentile (Q3).\n\n• Compute the IQR = Q3 − Q1.\n\nAny value smaller than `Q1 − 1.5 × IQR` or greater than `Q3 + 1.5 × IQR` is considered an outlier.\n\n### Remove Outliers using IQR\n\n```python\nQ1 = df['Salary'].quantile(0.25)\nQ3 = df['Salary'].quantile(0.75)\n\nIQR = Q3 - Q1\n\nlower = Q1 - 1.5 * IQR\nupper = Q3 + 1.5 * IQR\n\n# Remove Outliers\n\ndf = df[(df['Salary'] >= lower) &\n        (df['Salary'] <= upper)]\n```\n\n**Example Output**\n\n```text\nRows Before : 1000\nRows After  : 982\n```\n\nThis means `18 records` were identified as outliers and removed.\n\n### Should We Always Remove Outliers?\n\nThe answer is `No.` Before removing outliers, always understand why they exist.\n\n• Remove them if they are caused by data entry mistakes, sensor errors, or incorrect measurements.\n\n• Keep them if they represent real and important business cases.\n\n### Key Takeaway\n\nOutliers are unusual values that differ significantly from the rest of the dataset. They can reduce the performance of some Machine Learning models if they are caused by errors. Always analyze outliers carefully before removing them, because genuine outliers may contain valuable information."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Data Cleaning & Handling Missing Values",
            "explanation": "Excellent! Before training any Machine Learning model, we must make sure our dataset is clean.\n\nIn real-world projects, data is rarely perfect. Sometimes users forget to fill out forms, sensors fail to record measurements, or data gets lost while transferring between systems. As a result, some cells in the dataset remain empty. These empty values are called `Missing Values` (also known as `Null` or `NaN` values).\n\nFor example, imagine a customer dataset:\n\n```text\nName    Age    City\nJohn     25    Mumbai\nAlice   NaN    Pune\nDavid    32    NaN\n```\n\nHere, Alice's `Age` and David's `City` are missing.\n\nIf we try to train a Machine Learning model without handling these missing values, many algorithms will either produce incorrect results or fail to train.\n\nTherefore, handling missing values is one of the most important steps in `Data Preprocessing`.\n\n### Method 1: Remove Missing Values (Dropping)\n\nIf only a small number of records contain missing values, we can simply remove those rows.\n\nFor example, if only `1% or 2%` of the dataset has missing values, removing them usually does not affect the overall data.\n\n```python\n# Remove rows containing missing values\n\ndf = df.dropna()\n```\n\n**When should we use it?**\n\n• Very few records contain missing values.\n\n• Removing them will not significantly reduce the dataset size.\n\n**Disadvantage:**\n\nIf many rows are removed, we may lose valuable information and reduce the model's learning ability.\n\n---\n\n### Method 2: Fill Missing Values (Imputation)\n\nInstead of deleting the data, we can replace the missing values with appropriate estimates. This process is called `Imputation`.\n\nDifferent types of data require different techniques.\n\n`For Numerical Columns:`\n\n• Mean → Replace with the average value.\n\n• Median → Replace with the middle value (preferred when outliers exist).\n\n```python\n# Fill missing Age using the median\n\ndf['Age'] = df['Age'].fillna(df['Age'].median())\n```\n\n`For Categorical Columns:`\n\nReplace missing values with the `Mode`, which is the most frequently occurring value.\n\n```python\n# Fill missing City using the most common city\n\ndf['City'] = df['City'].fillna(df['City'].mode()[0])\n```\n\n### Which Method Should We Choose?\n\nThere is no single method that works for every dataset.\n\n• Use `Dropping` when only a few records are missing.\n\n• Use `Imputation` when the dataset contains many missing values and removing them would result in a significant loss of data.\n\n### Key Takeaway\n\nMissing values are very common in real-world datasets. Before training any Machine Learning model, always check for missing values and handle them appropriately. Choosing the correct technique helps improve the quality of the data and allows the model to learn more effectively."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "A pandas DataFrame contains a column named 'Salary' with some missing (NaN) values. Write code to replace all missing salaries with the average salary of the column.",
            "starterCode": "import pandas as pd\n\n# DataFrame already exists as df\n\n# Write your code below\n",
            "solution": "df['Salary'] = df['Salary'].fillna(df['Salary'].mean())",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use fillna() along with mean() on the 'Salary' column."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Encoding: Converting Categories into Numbers",
            "explanation": "Excellent! So far, we have cleaned our data and handled missing values. However, there is one more challenge before we can train a Machine Learning model.\n\nMany datasets contain **categorical data**, such as gender, city, education, or product category. These values are represented as text, but Machine Learning algorithms can only work with numerical data.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nColor\n-----\nRed\nBlue\nGreen\n```\n\nA computer cannot perform mathematical calculations on words like `Red` or `Blue`. Therefore, we need to convert these categories into numbers. This process is called `Encoding`.\n\nThere are two commonly used encoding techniques.\n\n### 1. Label Encoding\n\n`Label Encoding` replaces each category with a unique numerical value.\n\nIt is mainly used when the categories have a natural order or ranking.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nEducation Level\n\nHigh School\nBachelor\nMaster\nPhD\n```\n\nAfter Label Encoding:\n\n```text\nHigh School → 0\nBachelor     → 1\nMaster       → 2\nPhD          → 3\n```\n\nSince these categories have a logical order, Label Encoding works well.\n\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/ml/dp.png]\n\n### 2. One-Hot Encoding\n\nSometimes categories do **not** have any order.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nColor\n\nRed\nBlue\nGreen\n```\n\nIf we assign:\n\n```text\nRed   → 0\nBlue  → 1\nGreen → 2\n```\n\nthe algorithm may incorrectly assume that **Green is greater than Blue**, or **Blue is greater than Red**, even though colors have no ranking.\n\nTo solve this problem, we use `One-Hot Encoding`.\n\nInstead of assigning one number, we create a separate column for each category.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nColor   Is_Red   Is_Blue   Is_Green\nRed         1         0          0\nBlue        0         1          0\nGreen       0         0          1\n```\n\nThis representation tells the model exactly which category is present without introducing any incorrect order.\n\n### Practical Example\n\n```python\n# Label Encoding\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import LabelEncoder\n\nsizes = ['Small', 'Medium', 'Large', 'Small']\n\nencoder = LabelEncoder()\nencoded_sizes = encoder.fit_transform(sizes)\n\nprint(encoded_sizes)\n\n# Output\n# [2 1 0 2]\n```\n\n```python\n# One-Hot Encoding\nimport pandas as pd\n\ncolors = pd.DataFrame({\n    'Color': ['Red', 'Blue', 'Green', 'Red']\n})\n\nencoded_colors = pd.get_dummies(colors['Color'])\n\nprint(encoded_colors)\n\n# Output\n#\n#    Blue  Green  Red\n# 0     0      0    1\n# 1     1      0    0\n# 2     0      1    0\n# 3     0      0    1\n```\n\n### When Should We Use Each Technique?\n\nUse `Label Encoding` when the categories have a meaningful order.\n\nExamples:\n\n• Education Level\n\n• Customer Rating\n\n• T-Shirt Size (Small, Medium, Large)\n\nUse `One-Hot Encoding` when the categories do **not** have any natural order.\n\nExamples:\n\n• Color\n\n• Country\n\n• City\n\n• Product Category\n\n• Department\n\n### Key Takeaway\n\nEncoding is the process of converting categorical values into numbers so that Machine Learning algorithms can understand them. Use `Label Encoding` for ordered categories and `One-Hot Encoding` for unordered categories to avoid introducing incorrect relationships between the values."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "A dataset contains an ordinal feature called 'Size' with values Low, Medium, and High. Use Label Encoding to convert them into numerical values.",
            "starterCode": "from sklearn.preprocessing import LabelEncoder\n\nsizes = ['Low', 'Medium', 'High']\n\n# Write your code below\n",
            "solution": "from sklearn.preprocessing import LabelEncoder\n\nsizes = ['Low', 'Medium', 'High']\n\nencoder = LabelEncoder()\nencoded_sizes = encoder.fit_transform(sizes)\n\nprint(encoded_sizes)",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Create a LabelEncoder object and use fit_transform()."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Scaling: Leveling the Playing Field",
            "explanation": "Imagine predicting a house price using 'Number of Bedrooms' (range: 1-5) and 'Square Footage' (range: 500-5000).\nLarger Square Footage values can dominate the model, reducing the impact of Bedrooms.!\n**Scaling fixes this by squashing all numbers into the same range:**\n• **Normalization (Min-Max):** Squashes all values to exist strictly between 0.0 and 1.0.\n• **Standardization (Z-Score):** Centers the data around a mean of 0, measuring how many standard deviations a value is from the average. Handles outliers better.\n`Example` After Min-Max scaling:\nBedrooms: 3 ➔ 0.6\nSq Footage: 2500 ➔ 0.5\nNow the algorithm can compare them equally!\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/ml/dp11.png]\nWhy Scaling required :\n• Balances all features so none gets extra importance.\n• Improves prediction accuracy.\n• Reduces training time.\n• Required for distance-based algorithms like KNN, SVM, K-Means, and PCA.",
            "example": "# First install Scikit-learn\npip install scikit-learn\n\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler, MinMaxScaler\nimport numpy as np\n\nX = np.array([[1500], [2000], [2500], [3000]])\n\n# Standardization (Mean = 0, Std = 1)\nstandard_scaler = StandardScaler()\nX_standard = standard_scaler.fit_transform(X)\nprint(X_standard)\n\n# Normalization (Scale between 0 and 1)\nminmax_scaler = MinMaxScaler()\nX_normalized = minmax_scaler.fit_transform(X)\nprint(X_normalized)"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Feature Engineering & Feature Selection",
            "explanation": "Excellent! We have cleaned our data, handled missing values, and converted categorical values into numbers. Before training a Machine Learning model, there is one more important step that can significantly improve the model's performance—**Feature Engineering** and **Feature Selection**.\n\nAlthough they sound similar, they solve two different problems.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/ml/dp3.png]\n### What is Feature Engineering?\n\n**Feature Engineering** is the process of creating new, useful features from the existing data.\n\nSometimes the original data does not contain enough information for the model to learn useful patterns. In such cases, we create new columns that provide better insights.\n\nFor example, suppose a sales dataset contains only the purchase date:\n\n```text\nPurchase_Date\n2024-01-13\n2024-01-14\n2024-01-15\n```\n\nInstead of using only the date, we can create new features such as:\n\n• Day of Week\n\n• Month\n\n• Is_Weekend (Yes/No)\n\nThese new features often help the model discover hidden patterns. For example, the model may learn that sales are usually higher during weekends.\n\n### What is Feature Selection?\n\n**Feature Selection** is the process of choosing only the most useful features for training the model.\n\nNot every column in a dataset helps make predictions.\n\nFor example, consider a loan approval dataset:\n\n```text\nCustomer_ID\nFirst_Name\nIncome\nCredit_History\nLoan_Status\n```\n\nColumns such as **Customer_ID** and **First_Name** do not help determine whether a customer should receive a loan.\n\nKeeping unnecessary columns can:\n\n• Increase training time.\n\n• Make the model more complex.\n\n• Reduce prediction accuracy.\n\nTherefore, we remove such columns and keep only the important features.\n\n### Practical Example\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Sample Dataset\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    'Purchase_Date': ['2024-01-13', '2024-01-14', '2024-01-15'],\n    'Customer_ID': [101, 102, 103],\n    'Sales': [1200, 1500, 900]\n})\n\n# ---------- Feature Engineering ----------\n\n# Convert to datetime\n\ndf['Purchase_Date'] = pd.to_datetime(df['Purchase_Date'])\n\n# Create New Features\n\ndf['Day'] = df['Purchase_Date'].dt.day_name()\ndf['Is_Weekend'] = df['Day'].isin(['Saturday', 'Sunday']).astype(int)\n\n# ---------- Feature Selection ----------\n\n# Remove unnecessary column\n\ndf = df.drop(columns=['Customer_ID'])\n\nprint(df)\n```\n\n**Example Output**\n\n```text\n  Purchase_Date      Sales       Day    Is_Weekend\n0    2024-01-13      1200   Saturday       1\n1    2024-01-14      1500     Sunday       1\n2    2024-01-15       900     Monday       0\n```\n\n### When Should We Use Them?\n\n**Use Feature Engineering** when you want to create new information that can help the model learn better patterns.\n\nExamples:\n\n• Extract Year, Month, Day from Date\n\n• Calculate Age from Date of Birth\n\n• Calculate BMI from Height and Weight\n\n• Calculate Total Price = Quantity × Price\n\n**Use Feature Selection** when you want to remove columns that are unnecessary or have little impact on prediction.\n\nExamples:Customer_ID,First_Name,Phone Number\n\n• Email Address\n\n### Key Takeaway\n\n`Feature Engineering` creates **new useful features** from existing data, while `Feature Selection` removes **unnecessary features** that do not help the model learn. Both techniques improve model performance, reduce complexity, and are commonly used in real-world Machine Learning projects.\nExcellent! We've completed the Data Preprocessing module. In the next lesson, we'll begin learning Regression Algorithms, which help us predict continuous numerical values such as house prices, salaries, and sales."
          }
        },
        {
