    {
      "id": "M002-ml-lesson-9-limitations",
      "title": "Overfitting, Underfitting & ML Limitations",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#8b5cf6",
        "accent": "#7c3aed"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Challenge of Training a Good Model",
            "explanation": "Great job! We have learned how to train Machine Learning models and improve their performance. However, training a model is not enough. We also need to make sure the model learns the right patterns from the data.\n\nImagine two students preparing for an exam.\n\nThe first student studies very little and does not understand the topics properly. As a result, they perform poorly on both practice tests and the final exam.\nThe second student memorizes every answer from the practice tests. They score very well on those tests, but when the final exam contains new questions, they struggle because they did not truly understand the concepts.\n\nMachine Learning models can face the same problem.\n• **Underfitting:** The model learns too little and cannot understand the patterns in the data.\n• **Overfitting:** The model memorizes the training data instead of learning general patterns.\n• **Good Fit:** The model learns the important patterns and performs well on both training data and new unseen data.\n\n**Goal:** Build a model that learns the real patterns in the data instead of guessing or memorizing."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Underfitting (The Lazy Student)",
            "explanation": "Excellent! After training a Machine Learning model, one of the first things we need to check is whether the model has learned enough from the data. One common problem is called **Underfitting**.\n\nImagine a student named **Joe** who never attends classes and studies only the night before the exam. Since he hasn't learned the concepts properly, he performs poorly in the practice tests. On the final exam, he also performs poorly because he never understood the subject.\n\nA Machine Learning model can behave in exactly the same way.\n\n**Underfitting** happens when the model is **too simple** to learn the important patterns present in the training data. Instead of understanding the relationship between the input and output, it learns very little and makes poor predictions.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to predict the price of a house. If our model uses only the **house color** to predict the price while ignoring important features such as area, location, number of bedrooms, and age of the house, the predictions will be inaccurate. The model simply doesn't have enough information to learn the correct pattern.\n\n### How Can We Identify Underfitting?\n\nA model that is underfitting performs poorly on both the training data and the testing data.\n\n• Training Accuracy (or Score) → Low\n\n• Testing Accuracy (or Score) → Low\n\nThis tells us that the model has not learned the underlying pattern from the data.\n\n### How Can We Reduce Underfitting?\n\nThere are several ways to improve an underfitting model:\n\n• Add more useful features so the model has more information to learn from.\n\n• Choose a more powerful or complex model that can capture deeper relationships.\n\n• Train the model for more epochs or iterations so it has enough time to learn.\n\n• Improve the quality of the training data by removing noise and using better features.\n\n### Key Takeaway\n\nUnderfitting occurs when a model has **not learned enough** from the training data. As a result, it performs poorly on both the training and testing datasets because it cannot capture the important patterns required to make accurate predictions."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Overfitting (The Memorizer)",
            "explanation": "Now let's look at the second problem a Machine Learning model can face: **Overfitting**.\n Imagine Grace memorizes every answer from previous practice papers. She scores 100% on practice tests, but when the final exam contains slightly different questions, she struggles badly.\nThis is **Overfitting**. The model memorizes the training data instead of learning general rules.\n**Signs:**\n• Training Score = Very High\n• Test Score = Much Lower\n**Example:** A fraud detection model memorizes old transactions but fails to detect new fraud patterns.\n**Quick Fixes:**\n• Get more training data → Reduces memorization.\n• Reduce model complexity → Prevents learning unnecessary details.\n• Apply Regularization → Penalizes overly complex models.\n• Prune Decision Trees → Removes unnecessary branches.\n\n**Key Idea:** The model learned too much detail and cannot generalize.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"background: #ffffff; border-radius: 16px; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; box-shadow: 0 10px 25px rgba(0,0,0,0.05); font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; overflow: hidden; max-width: 900px; margin: 0 auto;\"><div style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, #334155, #0f172a); padding: 16px; color: white; text-align: center; font-weight: bold; font-size: 16px; letter-spacing: 0.5px; text-transform: uppercase;\">The Model Fitting Spectrum 📊</div><div style=\"padding: 20px; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 15px; align-items: stretch;\"><div style=\"flex: 1; min-width: 240px; background: #fffbeb; border: 1px solid #fde68a; border-radius: 12px; padding: 15px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center;\"><div style=\"font-size: 13px; font-weight: 800; color: #d97706; text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: 5px;\">Underfitting 😴</div><div style=\"font-size: 11px; font-weight: bold; color: #b45309; margin-bottom: 15px;\">\"The Lazy Student\"</div><svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 120\" style=\"width: 100%; height: auto; background: white; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #fcd34d; margin-bottom: 15px;\"><circle cx=\"30\" cy=\"90\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#94a3b8\"/><circle cx=\"70\" cy=\"50\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#94a3b8\"/><circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"30\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#94a3b8\"/><circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"45\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#94a3b8\"/><circle cx=\"170\" cy=\"80\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#94a3b8\"/><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/></svg><div style=\"width: 100%; display: flex; justify-content: space-between; font-size: 12px; margin-bottom: 8px; background: white; padding: 6px 10px; border-radius: 6px;\"><span style=\"color: #475569;\">Practice (Train):</span><span style=\"color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold;\">Fail ❌</span></div><div style=\"width: 100%; display: flex; justify-content: space-between; font-size: 12px; margin-bottom: 10px; background: white; padding: 6px 10px; border-radius: 6px;\"><span style=\"color: #475569;\">Exam (Test):</span><span style=\"color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold;\">Fail ❌</span></div><div style=\"font-size: 11px; color: #78350f; text-align: center; margin-top: auto; line-height: 1.4;\">Model is <strong>too simple</strong>. It didn't study enough to learn the underlying patterns.</div></div><div style=\"flex: 1; min-width: 240px; background: #f0fdf4; border: 1px solid #bbf7d0; border-radius: 12px; padding: 15px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; box-shadow: 0 4px 10px rgba(16,185,129,0.15); transform: scale(1.02); z-index: 10;\"><div style=\"font-size: 13px; font-weight: 800; color: #059669; text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: 5px;\">Optimal Fit 🧠</div><div style=\"font-size: 11px; font-weight: bold; color: #15803d; margin-bottom: 15px;\">\"The Smart Student\"</div><svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 120\" style=\"width: 100%; height: auto; background: white; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #86efac; margin-bottom: 15px;\"><circle cx=\"30\" cy=\"90\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#94a3b8\"/><circle cx=\"70\" cy=\"50\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#94a3b8\"/><circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"30\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#94a3b8\"/><circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"45\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#94a3b8\"/><circle cx=\"170\" cy=\"80\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#94a3b8\"/><path d=\"M 20 110 Q 100 -20 180 100\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/></svg><div style=\"width: 100%; display: flex; justify-content: space-between; font-size: 12px; margin-bottom: 8px; background: white; padding: 6px 10px; border-radius: 6px;\"><span style=\"color: #475569;\">Practice (Train):</span><span style=\"color: #16a34a; font-weight: bold;\">Pass ✅</span></div><div style=\"width: 100%; display: flex; justify-content: space-between; font-size: 12px; margin-bottom: 10px; background: white; padding: 6px 10px; border-radius: 6px;\"><span style=\"color: #475569;\">Exam (Test):</span><span style=\"color: #16a34a; font-weight: bold;\">Pass ✅</span></div><div style=\"font-size: 11px; color: #14532d; text-align: center; margin-top: auto; line-height: 1.4;\">Model is <strong>just right</strong>. It learned the core concepts and ignores the random noise.</div></div><div style=\"flex: 1; min-width: 240px; background: #fff1f2; border: 1px solid #fecdd3; border-radius: 12px; padding: 15px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center;\"><div style=\"font-size: 13px; font-weight: 800; color: #e11d48; text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: 5px;\">Overfitting 🤖</div><div style=\"font-size: 11px; font-weight: bold; color: #be185d; margin-bottom: 15px;\">\"The Memorizer\"</div><svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 120\" style=\"width: 100%; height: auto; background: white; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #fca5a5; margin-bottom: 15px;\"><circle cx=\"30\" cy=\"90\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#94a3b8\"/><circle cx=\"70\" cy=\"50\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#94a3b8\"/><circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"30\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#94a3b8\"/><circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"45\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#94a3b8\"/><circle cx=\"170\" cy=\"80\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#94a3b8\"/><path d=\"M 20 120 L 30 90 Q 50 120 70 50 Q 80 -10 100 30 Q 120 80 140 45 Q 160 -10 170 80 L 180 120\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/></svg><div style=\"width: 100%; display: flex; justify-content: space-between; font-size: 12px; margin-bottom: 8px; background: white; padding: 6px 10px; border-radius: 6px;\"><span style=\"color: #475569;\">Practice (Train):</span><span style=\"color: #16a34a; font-weight: bold;\">Perfect 💯</span></div><div style=\"width: 100%; display: flex; justify-content: space-between; font-size: 12px; margin-bottom: 10px; background: white; padding: 6px 10px; border-radius: 6px;\"><span style=\"color: #475569;\">Exam (Test):</span><span style=\"color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold;\">Fail ❌</span></div><div style=\"font-size: 11px; color: #881337; text-align: center; margin-top: auto; line-height: 1.4;\">Model is <strong>too complex</strong>. It perfectly memorized the practice test, but fails in the real world.</div></div></div></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Good Fit (The Smart Student)",
            "explanation": "Now let's look at the ideal situation every Machine Learning engineer aims for: **Good Fit**.\nImagine Max studies concepts instead of memorizing answers. He performs well in practice tests and continues performing well when new questions appear in the final exam.\n\nThis is a **Good Fit**. The model learns the underlying patterns and adapts well to unseen data.\n\n**Signs:**\n• Training Score = High\n• Test Score = High\n• Small Gap Between Scores\n\n**Example:** A movie recommendation system correctly suggests movies for both existing and new users.\n\n**Key Idea:** The model has learned the right amount and generalizes well."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Generalization: The Smart Student",
            "explanation": "Now that we understand Underfitting, Overfitting, and Good Fit, let's learn an important concept called **Generalization**.\nImagine a student who understands the concepts instead of memorizing answers. Because they truly understand the subject, they perform well not only in practice tests but also in the final exam, even when new questions appear.\nMachine Learning models should learn in the same way.\nThis ability to perform well on new unseen data is called **Generalization**.\n\nA generalized model learns useful patterns from the training data and successfully applies them to data it has never seen before.\n\n**Symptoms:**\n• Training Accuracy → High\n• Test Accuracy → High\n• Small Performance Gap\n\n**Example:** A recommendation system that accurately suggests products to new users it has never seen before.\n\n**Key Idea:** A good Machine Learning model does not memorize data. It learns patterns that can be used to make accurate predictions on new unseen data."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Model Performance Check",
            "question": "Identify the correct model behavior.",
