        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Naive Bayes (The Detective)",
            "explanation": "Imagine a detective investigating whether an email is Spam or Not Spam. The detective looks for clues such as words like 'Free', 'Winner', or 'Discount'. Based on past cases, some clues are more common in spam emails than normal emails.\n**Naive Bayes** uses probability to make predictions. It calculates how likely each class is based on the clues it sees.\n**How it works:**\n• Learn how often each feature appears in each class.\n• When new data arrives, calculate the probability of each possible class.\n• Combine the probabilities of all observed features.\n• Compare the final probabilities.\n• Choose the class with the highest probability.\n\n**Key Idea:** Naive Bayes assumes every feature is independent of the others, which makes it extremely fast and efficient for text classification.\n**Use Cases:** Spam Detection, Sentiment Analysis, News Categorization, and Document Classification.",
            "example": "from sklearn.naive_bayes import MultinomialNB\n\nmodel = MultinomialNB()\nmodel.fit(X_train, y_train)\n\nprediction = model.predict(X_test)\n\n`Use Case:` Spam Detection (Spam / Not Spam)"
