      "id": "M002-ml-lesson-7-clustering",
      "title": "Clustering & Unsupervised Learning",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#10b981",
        "accent": "#059669"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is Clustering?",
            "explanation": "Great job! So far, we have learned algorithms that make predictions using known answers. Now let's learn about **Clustering**, one of the most popular Unsupervised Learning techniques.\n\nImagine a news website automatically grouping articles into categories such as Sports, Politics, Business, and Technology. Nobody manually labels the articles—the system finds similar articles and groups them together.\n\n**Clustering** works in a similar way. It automatically groups similar data points together without using predefined labels or categories.\n\n**Use Cases:** Customer Segmentation, Grouping Similar News Articles, and Discovering Shopping Behavior Patterns."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Important Clustering Terminologies",
            "explanation": "Before learning clustering algorithms such as K-Means, it is important to understand a few common terms.\n **Cluster:**  cluster is a group of similar data points. The goal of clustering is to place similar items into the same group.\n **Centroid:** The center point of a cluster. In K-Means, the centroid represents the average position of all data points in that cluster.\n **Distance Metric:** A mathematical method used to measure how similar or different two data points are.\n`Common distance metrics:`\n• Euclidean Distance – Straight-line distance between points.\n• Manhattan Distance – Distance measured by moving horizontally and vertically.\n• Cosine Similarity – Measures similarity based on direction rather than actual distance.\n **Inertia (Within-Cluster Sum of Squares):** A measure of how closely data points are grouped around their centroid. Lower inertia generally indicates tighter clusters. resul\n **Similarity:** A measure of how closely related two data points are. Higher similarity usually means the points should belong to the same cluster.\nUnderstanding these terms makes it easier to learn clustering algorithms and interpret their results."
          },
          "example": "Imagine a customer segmentation project:\n\n• Data Points → Individual customers\n• Features → Age, Income, Spending Score\n• Distance Metric → Measures customer similarity\n• Centroid → Center of each customer group\n• Cluster → Customers with similar behavior\n• Scaling → Ensures income does not dominate age when creating clusters",
          "tip": "Remember: Clustering groups similar data points together using distance measurements, centroids, and feature values."
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "K-Means Clustering",
            "explanation": "Excellent! In the previous lesson, we learned that `Clustering` is used to group similar data points together without using labels. Now let's learn the most popular clustering algorithm called **K-Means Clustering**.\n\nImagine a shopping mall wants to understand its customers better. It has thousands of customers but doesn't know which customers have similar shopping habits. Instead of manually creating groups, the mall uses `K-Means Clustering` to automatically divide customers into different groups based on their behavior.\n\nFor example, after clustering, the mall may discover:\n\n• Customers who shop frequently and spend a lot.\n\n• Customers who visit occasionally.\n\n• Customers who spend very little.\n\nThese groups help the business create personalized offers and improve customer service.\nNow, you might be wondering `how K-Means automatically creates these groups.`\nThe answer is simple. **K-Means Clustering** is one of the most popular `Unsupervised Machine Learning algorithms`. It automatically groups similar data points into different clusters without using labeled data. The algorithm places similar data points into the same cluster while separating those that are different. Its goal is to create clusters where the data points within the same cluster are as similar as possible.\n\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/ml/clu1.png]\n\n### How K-Means Clustering Works\n\nLet's understand the complete process step by step.\n\n`Step 1: Choose the Number of Clusters (K)`\n\nFirst, we tell the algorithm how many groups we want to create. This number is called `K`.\n\nFor example, if we want to divide customers into `3 groups`, then `K=3`.\n\n**Step 2: Place Random Centroids**\n\nThe algorithm randomly places `3 center points` inside the dataset. These center points are called `Centroids`.\n\nAt this stage, these centroids are just random guesses.\n\n**Step 3: Assign Every Data Point to the Nearest Centroid**\n\nNow each data point measures its distance to every centroid.\n\nIt joins the cluster whose centroid is closest.\n\nThink of it like students choosing the nearest classroom.\n\n**Step 4: Move the Centroids**\n\nAfter all data points have been assigned, the algorithm calculates the average position of each cluster.\n\nThe centroid then moves to this new center.\n\nBecause the centroid has moved, some data points may now be closer to another centroid.\n\n**Step 5: Repeat Until the Centroids Stop Moving**\n\nThe algorithm again assigns points to the nearest centroid, recalculates the center, and moves the centroids.\n\nThis process continues until the centroids no longer move (or move only by a very small amount).\n\nAt this point, the clusters are considered stable and the algorithm stops.\n\n### Simple Python Example\n\n```python\nfrom sklearn.cluster import KMeans\n\n# Age and Spending Score\nX = [[22, 90],[25, 85],[45, 40],[48, 35]]\n\nmodel = KMeans(n_clusters=2, random_state=42)\nmodel.fit(X)\n\nprint(model.labels_)\nprint(model.cluster_centers_)\n```\n\n**Example Output**\n\n```text\nCluster Labels:\n[1 1 0 0]\n\nCluster Centers:\n[[46.5 37.5]\n [23.5 87.5]]\n```\n\nThe output shows that customers with similar shopping behavior are placed into the same cluster, and the algorithm also calculates the center of each cluster.\n\n### Limitation of K-Means\n\nOne important limitation of K-Means is that we must decide the value of `K` before training.\n\nIf we choose the wrong value, the clustering may not represent the natural groups in the data.\n\nFor example, if the data actually contains `5 groups` but we choose `K = 2`, many different customers may be forced into the same cluster, resulting in poor clustering.\n\n### Key Takeaway\n\nK-Means is one of the simplest and most widely used clustering algorithms. It works by repeatedly assigning data points to the nearest centroid and updating the centroid positions until stable clusters are formed. It is commonly used for `customer segmentation, recommendation systems, market analysis, image compression, and grouping similar data points`."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "K-Means Clustering",
            "explanation": "Now that you understand Clustering, let's learn the most popular clustering algorithm: **K-Means Clustering**.\nSuppose a pizza company wants to open 3 delivery centers in a city. They start by choosing locations randomly. Then, they look at where their customers live and move each center closer to the middle of the customers it serves.\nLets Discuss another real use case example imagine a shopping mall wants to group its customers based on their shopping behavior. Instead of manually creating groups, the mall uses K-Means to automatically find customers with similar behavior and place them into the same group.\n**K-Means** is the most popular clustering algorithm.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/ml/clu1.png]\n**How K-Means Clustering Works:**\nLet's understand the working of K-Means Clustering step by step with a simple example.\n• 1. You tell the algorithm how many groups you want (This is the 'K'). Let's say K=3.\n• 2. The AI drops 3 random 'Centroids' (center points) onto the data.\n• 3. Every data point looks around and assigns itself to the closest Centroid.\n• 4. The Centroid then moves to the exact geographical middle of its new group.\n• 5. It repeats this until the Centroids stop moving.\n**Drawback:** You have to guess the number 'K' before you start. If you choose K=2 but the data actually has 5 natural groups, the algorithm will perform poorly.",
            "example": "from sklearn.cluster import KMeans\n# Age, Spending Score\nX = [[22, 90],[25, 85]]\nmodel = KMeans(n_clusters=2, random_state=42)\nmodel.fit(X)\n\nprint(model.labels_)\nprint(model.cluster_centers_)"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
