            },
            "explanation": "Accuracy measures overall correctness, Precision measures prediction quality, Recall measures how many actual positives were found, and RMSE is a common regression evaluation metric.",
            "hint": "Precision uses FP, Recall uses FN, and RMSE is used for regression."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Cross-Validation (The Practice Exams)",
            "explanation": "Now that we know how to measure a model's performance, let's learn how to make sure those results are reliable.\nA model might achieve a high score on one test dataset, but that does not always mean it will perform well on new data.To get a more trustworthy estimate of model performance, we use a technique called **Cross-Validation**.\nImagine a student who only studies Chapter 1. They take a practice test on Chapter 1 and score 100%. However, when the final exam includes Chapter 2 and Chapter 3, they perform poorly.\n\nThe same problem can happen in Machine Learning. If a model is evaluated on only one specific test set, it may achieve a high score simply because it got lucky or memorized patterns from that particular data.\n**Cross-Validation** helps verify that a model performs consistently across different subsets of data.\n**K-Fold Cross-Validation:**\n1. Split the dataset into `K` equal parts called folds.\n2. Train the model on `K-1` folds.\n3. Test the model on the remaining fold.\n4. Repeat until every fold has been used as the test set once.\n5. Average all scores to obtain a more reliable performance estimate."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Types of Cross-Validation",
            "explanation": "Excellent! We now know why Cross-Validation is important. Depending on the type of dataset and problem, different Cross-Validation techniques are used. Let's understand the most common ones.\n\n**Let's discuss some common types of Cross-Validation:**\n\n• **K-Fold Cross-Validation** → The dataset is divided into `K equal parts (folds)`. The model is trained `K times`, each time using one fold for testing and the remaining folds for training. The final performance is calculated by averaging all the results.\n\n• **Stratified K-Fold Cross-Validation** → Similar to K-Fold, but it ensures that each fold contains the `same proportion of classes` as the original dataset. This is especially useful for imbalanced classification problems.\n\n• **Leave-One-Out Cross-Validation (LOOCV)** → Every iteration uses `one sample as the test set` and all the remaining samples for training. This continues until every sample has been used as the test set once. It provides a thorough evaluation but can be computationally expensive.\n\n• **Repeated K-Fold Cross-Validation** → Instead of performing K-Fold only once, the process is repeated multiple times with different random splits. This provides a more stable and reliable estimate of the model's performance.\n\n• **Group K-Fold Cross-Validation** → Used when the dataset contains `groups of related samples`. All samples belonging to the same group are kept together in either the training or testing set to prevent data leakage.\n\n• **Time Series Cross-Validation** → Used for time-dependent data such as stock prices or weather forecasting. It preserves the chronological order of the data, ensuring that future data is never used to predict past data.\n\n**Why It Matters:**\n\nInstead of relying on a single train-test split, Cross-Validation evaluates the model multiple times using different portions of the dataset. This gives a more reliable estimate of how the model will perform on unseen data, helps detect overfitting, and allows us to compare different models more confidently."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Hyperparameter Tuning",
            "explanation": "Great job! We have learned how to build and evaluate Machine Learning models. But even a good model may not perform at its best with the default settings.\nThink about buying a new smartphone. The phone works immediately, but you can improve your experience by adjusting settings such as brightness, volume, and display preferences.\nMachine Learning models also have settings that can be adjusted to improve performance. These settings are called **Hyperparameters**.\nA **Hyperparameter** is a value that we set before training the model.\nExamples: Number of Neighbors (K) in KNN., Number of Clusters (K) in K-Means.,Number of Trees in Random Forest.\nChoosing the right hyperparameter values can significantly improve model performance.\n\n**How do we find the best Hyperparameters?**\n• `Grid Search:` Tests every possible combination of hyperparameter values and selects the combination that produces the best result.\n• `Random Search:` Randomly tries different combinations of hyperparameter values and selects the best-performing combination.\n\n**Key Idea:** Hyperparameter Tuning helps us find the best model settings so that the model can make more accurate predictions.",
            "example": "from sklearn.datasets import load_iris\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import GridSearchCV\nfrom sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier\n\nX, y = load_iris(return_X_y=True)\n\nparams = {'n_neighbors': [1, 3, 5, 7, 9]}\n\ngrid = GridSearchCV(KNeighborsClassifier(), params, cv=5)\ngrid.fit(X, y)\n\nprint('Best K:', grid.best_params_['n_neighbors'])\nprint('Best Score:', grid.best_score_)"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Find the best max_depth for a Decision Tree using Grid Search.",
            "starterCode": "from sklearn.datasets import load_iris\nfrom sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier\n\nX, y = load_iris(return_X_y=True)\n\n# Complete Grid Search",
            "solution": "from sklearn.datasets import load_iris\nfrom sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import GridSearchCV\n\nX, y = load_iris(return_X_y=True)\n\nparams = {'max_depth':[2,3,4,5,6]}\n\nsearch = GridSearchCV(DecisionTreeClassifier(), params, cv=5)\nsearch.fit(X,y)\n\nprint(search.best_params_)\nprint(search.best_score_)",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use GridSearchCV with a parameter grid.",
            "explanation": "Grid Search systematically tests parameter combinations and selects the best one."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "teacher_vis",
          "content": {
            "title": "Tuning a Random Forest in Python",
            "sections": [
              {
                "label": "1. Import",
                "code": "from sklearn.model_selection import GridSearchCV\nfrom sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier"
              },
              {
                "label": "2. Define the 'Knobs'",
                "text": "We create a dictionary of the settings we want to test. We want to test 50 trees vs 100 trees, and a max_depth of 5 vs 10.",
                "code": "param_grid = {\n    'n_estimators': [50, 100],\n    'max_depth': [5, 10]\n}"
              },
              {
                "label": "3. Grid Search & Cross Validation",
