        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Automatic Differentiation and Model Classes",
            "explanation": "When training a Deep Learning model, the computer needs to know **how each weight should change** to reduce the prediction error. Imagine trying to calculate the derivative of millions of parameters by hand—it would be impossible. Fortunately, both **PyTorch** and **TensorFlow** automatically calculate these gradients for us. This feature is called **Automatic Differentiation**.\n\n### Automatic Differentiation\n\nAutomatic Differentiation computes gradients automatically during training. These gradients are later used by the optimizer to update the model's weights.\n\n• **PyTorch** uses **Autograd**.\n• **TensorFlow** uses **GradientTape**.\n\nAs a developer, you only define the calculations. The framework automatically keeps track of every operation and computes the required gradients.\n\n#### PyTorch (Autograd)\n\n```python\nimport torch\n\nx = torch.tensor(2.0, requires_grad=True)\n\ny = x ** 2\n\ny.backward()\n\nprint(x.grad)   # tensor(4.)\n```\n\nIn this example:\n\n• x = 2\n• y = x² = 4\n• dy/dx = 2x = 4\n\nAutograd automatically computes the derivative **4**.\n\n#### TensorFlow (GradientTape)\n\n```python\nimport tensorflow as tf\n\nx = tf.Variable(2.0)\n\nwith tf.GradientTape() as tape:\n    y = x ** 2\n\ngrad = tape.gradient(y, x)\n\nprint(grad)   # tf.Tensor(4.0, shape=(), dtype=float32)\n```\n\nGradientTape performs the same task as Autograd. It records every mathematical operation and automatically computes the gradients.\n\n---\n\n### nn.Module vs keras.Model\n\nAfter understanding gradients, the next important concept is **how Deep Learning models are created**.\n\nEvery model you build in PyTorch or TensorFlow starts by inheriting a base model class.\n\n• In **PyTorch**, every custom model inherits from **nn.Module**.\n• In **TensorFlow**, every custom model inherits from **tf.keras.Model**.\n\nThese classes provide everything needed to build a neural network, including storing layers, managing parameters, performing forward passes, and saving/loading models.\n\nYou won't manually calculate weights or gradients—these classes handle the complete training workflow.\n\n#### PyTorch Example\n\n```python\nimport torch.nn as nn\n\nclass MyModel(nn.Module):\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n\n        self.fc = nn.Linear(10,5)\n\n    def forward(self,x):\n        return self.fc(x)\n```\n\nHere,\n\n• **nn.Module** is the parent class.\n• **nn.Linear()** creates a fully connected layer.\n• **forward()** defines how the input moves through the network.\n\n#### TensorFlow Example\n\n```python\nimport tensorflow as tf\n\nclass MyModel(tf.keras.Model):\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n\n        self.fc = tf.keras.layers.Dense(5)\n\n    def call(self,x):\n        return self.fc(x)\n```\n\nHere,\n\n• **tf.keras.Model** is the parent class.\n• **Dense()** creates a fully connected layer.\n• **call()** defines the forward computation.\n\n### Why Is This Important?\n\nEvery Deep Learning architecture—including **Artificial Neural Networks (ANNs), CNNs, RNNs, LSTMs, Autoencoders, GANs, Vision Transformers, and Large Language Models (LLMs)**—is built by extending **nn.Module** or **tf.keras.Model**. Once you understand these base classes, learning advanced architectures becomes much easier because they all follow the same design pattern."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Common Layers Used in Tensorflow",
            "explanation": "Now that TensorFlow and Keras are installed, let's learn about the most commonly used layers for building an Artificial Neural Network (ANN).\n\nIn Keras, we don't manually connect neurons one by one. Instead, we build a model by stacking different layers. Each layer has a specific responsibility, such as receiving the input, learning patterns from the data, preventing overfitting, or producing the final prediction.\n\nThink of building a Deep Learning model like building a house. Every part of the house has a different purpose, but together they create a complete structure. In the same way, different layers work together to build a Neural Network.\n\nLet's look at the layers you'll use most often while building ANN models.\n\n### **1. Sequential Model**\n\nThe **Sequential** model is the simplest way to build a Neural Network. It allows us to add layers one after another in a linear sequence.\n\nIt is the most commonly used model for beginners and works well for many regression and classification problems.\n\n**Example**\n\n```python\nfrom tensorflow.keras.models import Sequential\n\nmodel = Sequential()\n```\n\n`Common Uses:` Regression, Binary Classification, Multi-Class Classification.\n\n### **2. Input Layer**\n\nThe **Input Layer** is the first layer of every Neural Network. It defines the number of input features that will be given to the model.\n\nFor example, if your dataset has 10 input columns, the Input Layer receives those 10 values.\n\n**Example**\n\n```python\nfrom tensorflow.keras.layers import Input\n\nmodel.add(Input(shape=(10,)))\n```\n\n`Common Uses:` Required in every Deep Learning model.\n\n### **3. Dense Layer**\n\nThe **Dense Layer** is the most frequently used layer in an Artificial Neural Network. Every neuron in this layer is connected to every neuron in the previous layer, allowing the model to learn patterns from the data.\n\nDepending on where it is placed, a Dense layer can act as a hidden layer or the final output layer.\n\n**Example**\n\n```python\nfrom tensorflow.keras.layers import Dense\n\nmodel.add(Dense(64, activation='relu'))\n```\n\n`Common Uses:` Hidden Layers, Output Layer, Classification, Regression.\n\n### **4. Dropout Layer**\n\nSometimes a model memorizes the training data instead of learning useful patterns. This is called **Overfitting**.\n\nThe **Dropout Layer** randomly disables some neurons during training. This encourages the model to learn more general patterns and improves its performance on unseen data. \n\n**Example**\n\n```python\nfrom tensorflow.keras.layers import Dropout\n\nmodel.add(Dropout(0.3))\n```\n\n`Common Uses:` Preventing Overfitting in Deep Learning models.\n\n### **5. Activation Layers**\n\nAfter a Dense layer performs its mathematical calculations, an **Activation Function** decides what information should be passed to the next layer.\n\nDifferent activation functions are used for different types of problems.\nNote - Don't worry if the Dropout layer and Activation Functions seem new right now. We'll learn both concepts in detail in the next lesson. For now, simply become familiar with the layers that are commonly added to a Neural Network.\n\n**Example**\n\n```python\nmodel.add(Dense(64, activation='relu'))\nmodel.add(Dense(1, activation='sigmoid'))\n```\n\n`Common Activation Functions:` ReLU, Sigmoid, Softmax, Tanh.\n\n**Note:** As you continue learning Deep Learning, you'll discover more specialized layers such as **Embedding**, **SimpleRNN**, **LSTM**, **GRU**, and **Convolutional Layers (CNN)**. We'll study these layers in their respective modules, where they're used to solve specific problems like Natural Language Processing and Computer Vision."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": " Layers, Dataset, DataLoader, and Model Saving",
            "explanation": "Now that you know how a Deep Learning model is created, let's look at the building blocks that every model uses. Whether you're building a **CNN, RNN, Transformer, GAN, or Large Language Model (LLM)**, these components remain almost the same.\n\nYou have already learned how these neural network layers work individually. The following table is a handy reference that summarizes the role of each layer when designing Deep Learning models.|\n\n#### PyTorch Example\n\n```python\nimport torch.nn as nn\n\nmodel = nn.Sequential(\n    nn.Linear(10,64),\n    nn.ReLU(),\n    nn.Dropout(0.2),\n    nn.Linear(64,3)\n)\n```\n\n#### TensorFlow Example\n\n```python\nimport tensorflow as tf\n\nmodel = tf.keras.Sequential([\n    tf.keras.layers.Dense(64),\n    tf.keras.layers.ReLU(),\n    tf.keras.layers.Dropout(0.2),\n    tf.keras.layers.Dense(3)\n])\n```\n\nNotice that both frameworks provide similar layers. Only the syntax changes.\n\n### Dataset and DataLoader\n\nBefore training a model, the data must be loaded efficiently.\n\nInstead of loading the entire dataset into memory at once, Deep Learning frameworks divide the data into **small batches**. This makes training faster and uses less memory.\n\n• **Dataset** stores the training data.\n• **DataLoader** reads the Dataset in batches.\n• It can also shuffle the data before every training epoch.\n\n#### PyTorch Example\n\n```python\nfrom torch.utils.data import TensorDataset, DataLoader\nimport torch\n\nX = torch.rand(100,10)\ny = torch.randint(0,2,(100,))\n\ndataset = TensorDataset(X,y)\nloader = DataLoader(dataset,batch_size=16,shuffle=True)\n```\n\n#### TensorFlow Example\n\n```python\nimport tensorflow as tf\n\nX = tf.random.normal((100,10))\ny = tf.random.uniform((100,),maxval=2,dtype=tf.int32)\n\ndataset = tf.data.Dataset.from_tensor_slices((X,y))\ndataset = dataset.shuffle(100).batch(16)\n```\n\nBoth frameworks now provide batches of data that are ready to be passed into the neural network.\n\n### Saving and Loading Models\n\nTraining Deep Learning models can take minutes, hours, or even days. After training, you should save the model so you don't have to train it again.\n\n#### PyTorch Example\n\n```python\nimport torch\n\n# Save Model\ntorch.save(model.state_dict(),'model.pth')\n\n# Load Model\nmodel.load_state_dict(torch.load('model.pth'))\n```\n\n#### TensorFlow Example\n\n```python\n# Save Model\nmodel.save('model.keras')\n\n# Load Model\nloaded_model = tf.keras.models.load_model('model.keras')\n```\n\nSaving a model stores the learned weights. Later, you can load the model and use it directly for predictions without retraining.\n**Key Takeaway**Before building advanced architectures you should understand this concept Layers, datasets, data loaders, and model saving/loading are the core building blocks used in almost every Deep Learning project."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Create a simple PyTorch model using `nn.Module`. Inside the model, add one `Linear` layer that takes 4 input features and produces 2 output features. Then create a random input tensor with shape `(1,4)`, pass it through the model, and print the output.",
            "starterCode": "import torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\n\n# Create your model\n\n\n# Create a random input tensor\n\n\n# Pass the input through the model\n\n\n# Print the output\n",
            "solution": "import torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\n\nclass MyModel(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.fc = nn.Linear(4, 2)\n\n    def forward(self, x):\n        return self.fc(x)\n\nmodel = MyModel()\n\nx = torch.rand((1, 4))\n\noutput = model(x)\n\nprint(output)",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Inherit from `nn.Module`, define the layer inside `__init__()`, implement the `forward()` method, then create an object of the model."
          }
