        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Binary Cross-Entropy (Log Loss)",
            "explanation": "**The Coin Flip**\nNow that we have learned loss functions for Regression problems, let's learn about loss functions used in Classification problems.\nLets Discuss Binary Cross Entropy.\n**Binary Cross-Entropy** is used when there are exactly TWO possible outcomes (e.g., Spam vs Not Spam, Sick vs Healthy). It uses Logarithms to measure *confidence*.\n\n• **Formula:** `BCE = -((y₁log(p₁)+(1-y₁)log(1-p₁)) + ... + (yₙlog(pₙ)+(1-yₙ)log(1-pₙ))) / N` \n• **Where:** y = Actual Label (0 or 1),p = Predicted Probability,N = Total Samples\n• **Example:** If the AI predicts \"100% chance of Spam\" but the email is actually Important, the logarithmic penalty goes to infinity. It destroys models that are overly confident but wrong.\n\n• **When to use:** Use when the feature has only two categories, such as Yes/No, Male/Female, or Pass/Fail.\n\n**How to Use** Here's a simple Python example",
            "example": "model.compile(loss='binary_crossentropy', optimizer='adam')\n`Use Case:` Binary Classification.\n`Example:` Spam Detection, Fraud Detection, Disease Prediction."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Binary Cross-Entropy Review",
            "question": "Fill in the blanks using the correct concepts.",
            "template": [
