            "hint": "Think about Spam vs Not Spam."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Categorical Cross-Entropy",
            "explanation": "Binary Cross-Entropy works well when there are only two classes. But what if we have multiple classes such as Cat, Dog, Bird, and Horse?\nIn such cases, we use **Categorical Cross-Entropy (CCE)**.\n**Categorical Cross-Entropy (CCE)** is used when you have 3 or more distinct categories (e.g., Dog, Cat, Bird, Horse).\nTo use this loss function, your true labels must be **One-Hot Encoded**. This means if you have 4 animals, a 'Cat' isn't labeled as the number 2. It is labeled as an array: `[0, 1, 0, 0]`.\n\n• **Formula:** `CCE = -(y₁log(p₁) + y₂log(p₂) + ... + yₘlog(pₘ))`\n• **Example:** The network outputs probabilities `[10%, 80%, 5%, 5%]`. The CCE formula calculates the logarithmic difference between the network's prediction and the true `[0, 1, 0, 0]` array.\n\n• **When to use:** Use when the problem has more than two classes and the labels are One-Hot Encoded.Example: Cat = [1,0,0], Dog = [0,1,0], Bird = [0,0,1] \n\n**How to Use :** Here's a simple Python example",
            "example": "model.compile(loss='categorical_crossentropy', optimizer='adam')\n`Use Case:` Multi-Class Classification with One-Hot Encoded Labels.\n`Example:` Cat/Dog/Bird Classification."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Sparse Categorical Cross-Entropy",
            "explanation": "Now that we understand Categorical Cross-Entropy, let's learn about a more memory-efficient version called **Sparse Categorical Cross-Entropy (SCCE)**.\nWhat if you are building an AI to classify 10,000 different species of plants? If you use One-Hot Encoding, every single image label requires an array of 9,999 zeros and one 1. This will completely crash your computer's RAM!\n\n**Sparse Categorical Cross-Entropy** is mathematically identical to CCE, but it allows you to leave the true labels as normal integers (e.g., Cat = 2, Oak Tree = 8452).\n\n• **Example:** If you have hundreds or thousands of categories, you *must* use Sparse CCE to save memory.\n\n• **When to use:** Use when the problem has more than two classes and the labels are stored as integer values instead of One-Hot Encoded vectors.\n\n**How to Use:** Here's a simple Python example",
            "example": "model.compile(loss='sparse_categorical_crossentropy', optimizer='adam')\n`Use Case:` Multi-Class Classification with Integer Labels.\n`Example:` Digit Recognition (0-9), Product Category Prediction."
          }
