        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Feature Maps",
            "explanation": "After the Kernel scans the image, what happens next?\nEach time the Kernel finds a useful pattern, it records that information in a new image called a **Feature Map**.\nYou can think of a Feature Map as a summary showing where specific patterns were found.One Feature Map might highlight edges.Another might highlight curves.Another might highlight color differences.\nCNNs create many Feature Maps simultaneously to learn different visual features.\n**The Feature Map**\nBy the time the kernel has finished sliding over the entire original image, it has created a completely new grid called a **Feature Map**.\n**Example:** Imagine building a Cat vs Dog classifier.The kernel scans the image to detect features such as ears, eyes, and fur. The feature map records where these features are found, allowing the CNN to combine them in deeper layers and correctly classify the image as a cat or a dog.\n\nA Deep Learning network will use dozens of different kernels simultaneously, creating dozens of different Feature Maps (one for horizontal edges, one for curves, one for color contrasts, etc.).",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"padding: 1rem; background-color: #1e293b; color: white; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; font-family: sans-serif;\"><div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; gap: 20px;\"><div style=\"width: 80px; height: 80px; background-color: #3b82f6; border: 2px solid #93c5fd; border-radius: 8px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; font-size: 12px; font-weight: bold;\">Input Image</div><div style=\"font-size: 24px; font-weight: bold;\">→</div><div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px;\"><div style=\"width: 100px; height: 30px; background-color: #10b981; border: 1px solid #6ee7b7; border-radius: 4px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; font-size: 12px; font-weight: bold;\">Edges Map</div><div style=\"width: 100px; height: 30px; background-color: #a855f7; border: 1px solid #d8b4fe; border-radius: 4px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; font-size: 12px; font-weight: bold;\">Curves Map</div><div style=\"width: 100px; height: 30px; background-color: #f59e0b; border: 1px solid #fcd34d; border-radius: 4px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; font-size: 12px; font-weight: bold;\">Color Map</div></div></div><p style=\"margin-top: 15px; font-size: 14px; color: #cbd5e1;\">A single kernel creates one feature map. Multiple kernels create a stack of different feature maps.</p></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Image Augmentation",
            "explanation": "Great! In the previous lesson, we learned how to preprocess images before training a Deep Learning model. But here's an important question.\n\nIs preprocessing alone enough to build a model that performs well on new, unseen images?\n\nThe answer is **No**.\n\nImagine teaching a child to recognize a cat using only **one photo**. The child may learn that specific image very well, but what happens if the same cat appears from a different angle, in dim lighting, or at a different distance?\n\nThe child may struggle to recognize it.\n\nA Neural Network faces the same challenge.\n\nIf we train the model using only the original images, it may simply memorize those images instead of learning the important features. As a result, the model may perform poorly when it encounters new images.\n\nTo solve this problem, we use **Image Augmentation**.\n\n**What is Image Augmentation?**\n\n`Image Augmentation` is a technique that creates different variations of the original training images without changing their actual meaning.\n\nInstead of collecting thousands of new images, we automatically generate new versions of the existing images during training.\n\n**How Image Augmentation Works**\n\nThe training images are randomly transformed before they are fed into the Neural Network.\n\nSome common transformations include:\n\n• Rotate the image.\n\n• Flip the image horizontally.\n\n• Zoom in or out.\n\n• Change the brightness.\n\n• Shift the image slightly.\n\nAlthough the image looks different after these transformations, it still represents the same object.\n\nThis forces the Neural Network to focus on the important features of the object instead of memorizing the exact pixel positions.\n\nFor example, the model learns that a **cat is still a cat** whether it is rotated, flipped, zoomed in, or captured under different lighting conditions.\n\n**Key Takeaway:**\n\nImage Augmentation helps increase the diversity of the training data, reduces overfitting, and enables the Neural Network to generalize better to new, unseen images.\n```python\n#Code Implementation:\nfrom tensorflow.keras.preprocessing.image import ImageDataGenerator\ndatagen = ImageDataGenerator(\n    rotation_range=20,\n    horizontal_flip=True,\n    brightness_range=[0.8, 1.2]\n)\n`Result:` The network learns that a cat is a cat, even if it's looking left, tilted, or in shadows.\n```\nGreat! Now that we know how images are prepared, in the next lesson we'll learn the fundamentals of Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) and understand why they perform much better than traditional Neural Networks for image-related tasks. it to generate an accurate and context-aware response."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
