    {
      "id": "M003-dl-lesson-6-cnn-core",
      "title": "CNN Foundations & Core Concepts",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#059669",
        "accent": "#047857"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Why Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) Were Invented",
            "explanation": "Great! In the previous lessons, we learned that images are represented as pixels and stored as tensors. But here's an important question.\n\nIf Artificial Neural Networks (ANNs) already exist, why do we need another type of Neural Network called a `Convolutional Neural Network (CNN)`?\n\nLet's understand the reason.\n\nTraditional Neural Networks work very well for **tabular data** such as student records, sales data, or house prices because this type of data is already organized into features.\n\nHowever, images are very different from tabular data.\n\nBefore an ANN can process an image, it first converts the entire image into one long list of numbers. This process is called **Flattening**.\n\nUnfortunately, flattening creates a major problem.\n\n• The original 2D structure of the image is lost.\n\n• Neighboring pixels become separated in the long list.\n\n• The network no longer knows which pixels are close to each other.\n\n• As a result, it becomes difficult for the model to recognize shapes, edges, and objects.\n\nTo solve this problem, `Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs)` were invented.\n\nInstead of flattening the image at the beginning, a CNN processes the image in its original **2D format**.\n\nThis allows the network to preserve the spatial relationship between pixels and gradually learn important visual features.\n\nAs the image passes through the CNN, it learns:\n\n• Edges\n\n• Lines\n\n• Corners\n\n• Textures\n\n• Shapes\n\n• Complete objects\n\nBecause CNNs preserve the image structure, they perform much better than traditional Neural Networks on image-related tasks.\n\n**Example**\n\nImagine you have a picture of a human face.\n\nIf an ANN flattens the image into a long list of numbers, the pixels representing the left eye and right eye may end up far apart. The model loses the understanding that both eyes belong to the same face.\n\nA CNN keeps the image in its original 2D form, allowing it to understand that the eyes, nose, and mouth are connected and together form a face.\n\n**Key Takeaway:**\n\n`CNNs` were invented because traditional Neural Networks lose the spatial structure of images after flattening. By preserving the original 2D layout, CNNs can learn visual patterns such as edges, textures, shapes, and objects much more effectively."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Convolution Operation & Feature Extraction",
            "explanation": "Excellent! In the previous lesson, we learned why Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) were invented. Now let's learn the most important operation inside a CNN.That is **Convolution**.\n\nImagine you have a large picture, but instead of looking at the entire image at once, you use a **small magnifying glass** to examine one small area at a time.\n\nA CNN works in exactly the same way.\n\nThe small magnifying glass is called a **Kernel** or **Filter**. It is usually a small matrix such as **3 × 3** that slides across the image from left to right and top to bottom.\n\nLet's understand how this process works step by step.\n\n**Step 1: Place the Kernel**\n\nThe Kernel is placed on a small part of the image. At this point, it can only see a few pixels instead of the entire image.\n\n**Step 2: Scan the Image**\n\nThe Kernel slowly moves across the image one small region at a time.\n\nAt every position, it performs simple mathematical calculations between the Kernel values and the image pixels.\n\nThese calculations help the CNN determine whether a particular pattern exists in that region.\n\n**Step 3: Detect Important Features**\n\nIf the Kernel finds a pattern that it is designed to detect, such as an edge or a line, it produces a **high value**.\n\nIf the pattern is not present, it produces a **low value**.\n\n**Step 4: Create a Feature Map**\n\nAfter scanning the entire image, the CNN creates a new image called a **Feature Map**.\n\nThis Feature Map highlights the locations where important patterns were found.\n\nIn practice, a CNN doesn't use just one Kernel. It uses many different Kernels.\n\nEach Kernel learns to detect different features, such as:\n\n• Horizontal edges\n\n• Vertical edges\n\n• Corners\n\n• Curves\n\n• Textures\n\nBy combining all these Feature Maps, the CNN gradually learns to recognize complete objects like faces, animals, cars, and many other real-world objects.\n\n**Key Takeaway:**\n\nA **Convolution** operation scans an image using small Kernels (Filters) to detect important patterns. The detected patterns are stored in **Feature Maps**, which help the CNN understand what is present in the image.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"font-family:sans-serif; text-align:center; max-width:500px; margin:auto; padding:20px; background:#f0fdf4; border-radius:12px; border:1px solid #bbf7d0;\"><h3 style=\"color:#166534; margin-bottom:10px;\">🔍 Convolution Math Visualized</h3><p style=\"font-size:14px; color:#15803d; margin-bottom:15px;\">How a 3x3 Kernel multiplies with a 3x3 piece of an image.</p><div style=\"display:flex; justify-content:center; align-items:center; gap:15px; font-weight:bold; color:#166534;\"><div style=\"display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(3, 35px); gap:2px; border:2px solid #22c55e; padding:2px;\"><div style=\"background:#dcfce7; padding:8px;\">1</div><div style=\"background:#dcfce7; padding:8px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#dcfce7; padding:8px;\">1</div><div style=\"background:#dcfce7; padding:8px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#dcfce7; padding:8px;\">1</div><div style=\"background:#dcfce7; padding:8px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#dcfce7; padding:8px;\">1</div><div style=\"background:#dcfce7; padding:8px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#dcfce7; padding:8px;\">1</div></div><div>X</div><div style=\"display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(3, 35px); gap:2px; border:2px solid #ef4444; padding:2px;\"><div style=\"background:#fee2e2; padding:8px;\">1</div><div style=\"background:#fee2e2; padding:8px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#fee2e2; padding:8px;\">-1</div><div style=\"background:#fee2e2; padding:8px;\">1</div><div style=\"background:#fee2e2; padding:8px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#fee2e2; padding:8px;\">-1</div><div style=\"background:#fee2e2; padding:8px;\">1</div><div style=\"background:#fee2e2; padding:8px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#fee2e2; padding:8px;\">-1</div></div><div>=</div><div style=\"font-size:24px; color:#15803d; background:#bbf7d0; padding:15px; border-radius:8px;\">0</div></div><p style=\"margin-top:15px; font-size:12px; color:#166534;\">(1x1) + (0x0) + (1x-1) ... etc. Sum them all up to get the final score!</p></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Convolution Review",
            "question": "Drag and drop the correct term into each blank.",
            "template": [
              "A CNN uses a ____ to scan the image.",
              "Convolution helps perform Feature ____. ",
              "Kernels can detect edges, corners, and ____."
            ],
            "options": [
              "Kernel",
              "Extraction",
              "shapes",
              "epochs",
              "labels"
            ],
            "answers": {
              "blank_1": "Kernel",
              "blank_2": "Extraction",
              "blank_3": "shapes"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Stride",
            "explanation": "Excellent! We know how the Kernel scans an image, but how far should it move each time?\nThe number of pixels a Kernel moves at each step is called the **Stride**.\n**Stride = 1 (The Careful Scan)**\nBy default, the kernel moves 1 pixel at a time. This is highly detailed, but it produces a massive Feature Map and takes a lot of time.\n**Stride = 2 (The Skipping Scan)**\nIf we set the stride to 2, the kernel jumps over 2 pixels at a time. \n**The Effect of Stride:**\nIncreasing the stride shrinks the physical dimensions (Height and Width) of your resulting Feature Map. A stride of 2 roughly cuts the image size in half. This is useful for reducing the computational workload as we go deeper into the network.",
            "example": "Code Implementation:\nConv2D(filters=32, kernel_size=(3,3), strides=(2,2))\n\nIf the input image is 100x100, a stride of 2 will output a feature map that is roughly 50x50."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Padding (Same vs Valid)",
            "explanation": "Great Progress! In the previous lesson, we learned that a **Kernel** slides across an image to detect important patterns. But here's an important question.\n\nWhat happens when the Kernel reaches the edge of the image?\n\nLet's understand this with a simple example.\n\nImagine you are cleaning a table using a small sponge. When you're cleaning the center of the table, the sponge fits perfectly. But when you reach the edge, part of the sponge hangs outside the table.\n\nThe same thing happens in a CNN.\n\nWhen the Kernel reaches the edge of an image, part of the Kernel would fall outside the image. Since there are no pixels outside the image, the Kernel cannot continue scanning.\n\nThis creates two problems.\n\n• The pixels near the edges are analyzed fewer times than the pixels in the center.\n\n• After every Convolution operation, the output image becomes slightly smaller.\n\nTo solve these problems, we use **Padding**.\n\nPadding simply means adding an extra border around the image before performing Convolution.\n\nNow let's look at the two common types of Padding.\n\n**1. Valid Padding**\n\n`Valid Padding` means **no extra border is added** around the image.\n\nThe Kernel only scans where it completely fits inside the image.\n\nAs a result:\n\n• The output image becomes smaller.\n\n• Edge pixels are used fewer times during scanning.\n\nFor example:\n\nInput Image → **5 × 5**\n\nKernel → **3 × 3**\n\nOutput Feature Map → **3 × 3**\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/dl/cnn.png]\n**2. Same Padding**\n\n`Same Padding` adds a border of zeros around the image before the Kernel starts scanning.\n\nBecause of this extra border, the Kernel can also scan the edge pixels properly.\n\nAs a result:\n\n• Every part of the image, including the edges, is analyzed.\n\n• The output Feature Map remains the **same size** as the input image.\n\nFor example:\n\nInput Image → **5 × 5**\n\nKernel → **3 × 3**\n\nOutput Feature Map → **5 × 5**\n\n**Key Takeaway:**\n\n`Valid Padding` does not add any border, so the output image becomes smaller.\n\n`Same Padding` adds a border of zeros around the image, allowing the Kernel to scan every pixel while keeping the output size the same.",
            "html_code": ""
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Stride and Padding Review",
            "question": "Drag and drop the correct term into each blank.",
            "template": [
              "Padding helps preserve image ____.",
