              "Valid Padding causes the image to ____.",
              "Same Padding adds extra ____ around the image."
            ],
            "options": [
              "edges",
              "shrink",
              "step",
              "zeros",
              "kernels",
              "weights"
            ],
            "answers": {
              "blank_1": "edges",
              "blank_2": "step",
              "blank_3": "shrink",
              "blank_4": "zeros"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Pooling (Max & Average)",
            "explanation": "Excellent! In the previous lesson, we learned that Convolution creates multiple **Feature Maps** by detecting important patterns such as edges, corners, and textures.\n\nBut here's an important question.\n\nAfter creating all these Feature Maps, do we really need to keep every single value?\n\nThe answer is **No**.\n\nImagine you are reading a long chapter before an exam. Instead of memorizing every sentence, you write down only the most important points. This makes studying easier while still keeping the important information.\n\nA **Pooling Layer** works in exactly the same way. It reduces the size of the Feature Maps while preserving the most useful information. This makes the CNN faster, uses less memory, and helps reduce overfitting.\n\nThere are two common types of Pooling:\n\n• **Max Pooling**\n\n• **Average Pooling**\n\nLet's understand each one.\n\n**1. Max Pooling**\n\n`Max Pooling` is the most commonly used pooling method in modern CNNs.\n\nIt uses a small window (usually **2 × 2**) that slides across the Feature Map.\n\nFor each window, it simply selects the **largest value** and discards the remaining values.\n\nThe largest value usually represents the strongest or most important feature detected in that region.\n\nBecause only the maximum value is kept, the Feature Map becomes smaller while still preserving the most important information.\n\n**2. Average Pooling**\n\n`Average Pooling` also uses a small window (usually **2 × 2**) that moves across the Feature Map.\n\nHowever, instead of selecting the largest value, it calculates the **average** of all the values inside the window.\n\nThis creates a smoother Feature Map by preserving the overall information instead of only the strongest feature.\n\nAlthough Average Pooling can be useful in some applications, it is used less frequently because averaging may weaken important features such as sharp edges and textures.\n\nFor this reason, **Max Pooling** is the preferred choice in most modern CNN architectures.\n\n**Key Takeaway:**\n\nBoth Pooling methods reduce the size of Feature Maps.\n\n• `Max Pooling` keeps the strongest feature by selecting the maximum value.\n\n• `Average Pooling` keeps the overall information by calculating the average value.\n\nIn practice, **Max Pooling** is the most widely used pooling technique in Convolutional Neural Networks.\nLook at the visualization below. Click the Step Algorithm button to move the 2×2 Max Pooling window across the feature map. At each step, observe how it selects the largest value from the current region and places it in the corresponding cell of the smaller output feature map.",
            "html_code": "<iframe style=\"width:100%; min-height:600px; border:none; background:transparent;\" srcdoc=\"<style>body{margin:0;padding:0;display:flex;justify-content:center;align-items:center;min-height:100vh;background:transparent;}</style><div style=&quot;font-family:sans-serif; text-align:center; max-width:550px; margin:auto; padding:20px; background:#fffbeb; border-radius:12px; border:1px solid #fde68a; box-shadow:0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);&quot;><h3 style=&quot;color:#b45309; margin-bottom:5px;&quot;>🗜️ Max Pooling (2x2) Simulator</h3><p style=&quot;font-size:13px; color:#92400e; margin-bottom:20px;&quot;>Click 'Step' to slide the 2x2 pooling window. It takes the MAX value and shrinks the grid.</p><div style=&quot;display:flex; justify-content:center; align-items:center; gap:30px;&quot;><div id=&quot;gridIn&quot; style=&quot;display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(4, 40px); gap:2px; position:relative;&quot;><div style=&quot;background:#fef3c7; padding:10px; font-weight:bold;&quot;>1</div><div style=&quot;background:#fef3c7; padding:10px; font-weight:bold;&quot;>8</div><div style=&quot;background:#fef3c7; padding:10px; font-weight:bold;&quot;>4</div><div style=&quot;background:#fef3c7; padding:10px; font-weight:bold;&quot;>2</div><div style=&quot;background:#fef3c7; padding:10px; font-weight:bold;&quot;>5</div><div style=&quot;background:#fef3c7; padding:10px; font-weight:bold;&quot;>9</div><div style=&quot;background:#fef3c7; padding:10px; font-weight:bold;&quot;>1</div><div style=&quot;background:#fef3c7; padding:10px; font-weight:bold;&quot;>3</div><div style=&quot;background:#fef3c7; padding:10px; font-weight:bold;&quot;>2</div><div style=&quot;background:#fef3c7; padding:10px; font-weight:bold;&quot;>4</div><div style=&quot;background:#fef3c7; padding:10px; font-weight:bold;&quot;>8</div><div style=&quot;background:#fef3c7; padding:10px; font-weight:bold;&quot;>6</div><div style=&quot;background:#fef3c7; padding:10px; font-weight:bold;&quot;>1</div><div style=&quot;background:#fef3c7; padding:10px; font-weight:bold;&quot;>3</div><div style=&quot;background:#fef3c7; padding:10px; font-weight:bold;&quot;>2</div><div style=&quot;background:#fef3c7; padding:10px; font-weight:bold;&quot;>5</div><div id=&quot;poolBox&quot; style=&quot;position:absolute; top:-2px; left:-2px; width:84px; height:84px; border:3px solid #ef4444; background:rgba(239,68,68,0.2); transition:all 0.3s ease; box-sizing:border-box;&quot;></div></div><div>➡</div><div id=&quot;gridOut&quot; style=&quot;display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(2, 40px); gap:2px;&quot;><div id=&quot;out0&quot; style=&quot;background:#fcd34d; padding:10px; font-weight:bold; color:transparent;&quot;>9</div><div id=&quot;out1&quot; style=&quot;background:#fcd34d; padding:10px; font-weight:bold; color:transparent;&quot;>4</div><div id=&quot;out2&quot; style=&quot;background:#fcd34d; padding:10px; font-weight:bold; color:transparent;&quot;>4</div><div id=&quot;out3&quot; style=&quot;background:#fcd34d; padding:10px; font-weight:bold; color:transparent;&quot;>8</div></div></div><button id=&quot;poolBtn&quot; style=&quot;margin-top:25px; padding:10px 20px; background:#d97706; color:white; border:none; border-radius:8px; font-weight:bold; cursor:pointer;&quot;>Step Algorithm</button><script>let step=0; const pBox=document.getElementById('poolBox'); const coords=[{x:0,y:0},{x:84,y:0},{x:0,y:84},{x:84,y:84}]; document.getElementById('poolBtn').addEventListener('click',()=>{ if(step>3){ step=0; pBox.style.left='-2px'; pBox.style.top='-2px'; for(let i=0;i<4;i++) document.getElementById('out'+i).style.color='transparent'; return; } pBox.style.left = (coords[step].x - 2) + 'px'; pBox.style.top = (coords[step].y - 2) + 'px'; document.getElementById('out'+step).style.color='#000'; step++; });</script></div>\"></iframe>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Flatten Layer & Fully Connected Layer",
            "explanation": "Excellent! In the previous lessons, we learned how a CNN uses **Convolution** to detect important features and **Pooling** to reduce the size of the Feature Maps while preserving the most useful information.\n\nAt this stage, the CNN has successfully extracted features such as edges, textures, shapes, and object parts.\n\nBut here's an important question.\n\nCan these Feature Maps directly tell us whether the image is a **Cat**, **Dog**, or **Bird**?\n\nThe answer is **No**.\n\nThe Feature Maps are still arranged as a **2D grid**, but the final classification part of the Neural Network expects the input as a **1D vector**.\n\nTo solve this problem, CNN uses two final layers: the **Flatten Layer** and the **Fully Connected (Dense) Layer**.\n\nLet's understand both.\n\n**1. Flatten Layer**\n\nThe `Flatten Layer` acts as a bridge between the Convolution part and the Dense part of the CNN.\n\nImagine you have several pages of notes stacked together. Before sending them through a scanner, you place all the pages one after another in a single line.\n\nThe Flatten Layer works in a similar way.\n\nIt takes all the 2D Feature Maps produced by the CNN and converts them into one long **1D vector**.\n\nIt does **not** learn anything or perform any calculations. Its only job is to reshape the data into a format that the Dense Layer can understand.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/dl/cnn2.png]\n**2. Fully Connected (Dense) Layer**\n\nOnce the data becomes a 1D vector, it is passed to the **Fully Connected (Dense) Layer**.\n\nThis layer combines all the extracted features and makes the final prediction.\n\nFor example, the previous Convolution Layers may have detected:\n\n• Pointed ears\n\n• Whiskers\n\n• Round eyes\n\n• Fur texture\n\nThe Dense Layer looks at all these features together and decides which object the image most likely represents.\n\nFinally, the Output Layer calculates the probability for each class and predicts the class with the highest probability.\n\nFor example:\n\n• Cat → 0.92\n\n• Dog → 0.06\n\n• Bird → 0.02\n\nSince **Cat** has the highest probability, the CNN predicts that the image is a **Cat**.\n\n**Key Takeaway:**\n\n• The `Flatten Layer` converts the extracted Feature Maps into a single 1D vector.\n\n• The `Fully Connected (Dense) Layer` uses those extracted features to make the final classification.\n\nExcellent! Now that you understand every individual layer of a CNN, in the next lesson we'll bring everything together and explore the complete **CNN Architecture**, following how an image travels through each layer before the model makes its final prediction."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Your Conv2D layer is shrinking the image too quickly, and the network is taking too long to process everything. Add 'same' padding to stop the shrinking, and increase the stride to (2,2) to reduce the workload.",
            "starterCode": "import tensorflow as tf\nfrom tensorflow.keras.layers import Conv2D\n\nmodel = tf.keras.Sequential([\n    # Fix the padding and strides\n    Conv2D(filters=64, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='valid', strides=(1, 1), input_shape=(128, 128, 3))\n])",
            "solution": "import tensorflow as tf\nfrom tensorflow.keras.layers import Conv2D\n\nmodel = tf.keras.Sequential([\n    # Fix the padding and strides\n    Conv2D(filters=64, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='same', strides=(2, 2), input_shape=(128, 128, 3))\n])",
            "language": "python",
