    {
      "id": "M003-dl-lesson-7-cnn-architectures",
      "title": "CNN Architectures & Evolution",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#ea580c",
        "accent": "#c2410c"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is CNN Architecture?",
            "explanation": "Now that we have learned individual CNN components like Convolution, Pooling, and Flatten, let's see how they work together as a complete CNN architecture.\n**CNN Architecture** is the overall structure of a Convolutional Neural Network. It is made up of different layers that work together to analyze images and identify objects.\n\nA CNN learns in stages:\n• Early layers detect simple patterns like edges and corners.\n• Middle layers detect shapes and textures.\n• Deeper layers recognize complex objects and important features.\n• Final layers use these learned features to make predictions.\n\nKey Idea: CNN architecture organizes different layers so the network can gradually learn from simple patterns to complex objects."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "CNN Architecture: How a CNN Understands an Image",
            "explanation": "Great! In the previous lesson, we learned what a **Convolutional Neural Network (CNN)** is and why it is widely used for image-related tasks. Now let's understand **how a CNN actually processes an image** and makes a prediction.\n\nLook at the diagram below and follow it from **left to right**. This shows the complete journey an image takes inside a CNN before the model identifies what object is present.\n\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/dl/cnna1.png]\n\n### **Step 1: Input Image**\n\nEvery CNN starts with an input image. In this example, the input is an image of a small bird.\n\nAt this point, the computer has no idea that it's looking at a bird. It only sees thousands of pixel values representing different colors and brightness levels.\n\nThe CNN's job is to gradually understand these pixels and identify the object.\n\nNow that the image has entered the network, the next step is to begin finding useful patterns inside it.\n\n### **Step 2: Convolution Layer**\n\nThe image now enters the **Convolution Layer**.\n\nHere, a small matrix called a **Kernel** (or Filter) slides across the image one small region at a time.\n\nThink of it like using a small magnifying glass to carefully examine every part of a picture. Instead of looking at the whole image at once, the CNN studies one small section after another.\n\nAs the kernel moves, it begins detecting simple patterns such as:\n\n• Edges\n\n• Lines\n\n• Corners\n\n• Simple textures\n\nThe output generated by this process is called a **Feature Map**, because it highlights where these important patterns were found.\n\nThe CNN has now extracted some useful features, but the Feature Maps are still quite large. The next step is to reduce their size while keeping the important information.\n\n### **Step 3: Pooling Layer**\n\nThe Feature Maps are now passed to the **Pooling Layer**.\n\nIts job is to reduce the size of the Feature Maps while preserving the most useful information.\n\nThink of it like summarizing a long chapter into a few important points. We remove unnecessary details but keep the key information.\n\nReducing the size of the Feature Maps makes the model faster and reduces the amount of computation required.\n\nAt this stage, the CNN has learned only simple patterns such as edges and textures. To recognize an actual object, it needs to learn more complex features.\n\n### **Step 4: Convolution + Pooling Repeat**\n\nInstead of stopping after one Convolution and Pooling operation, the CNN repeats these two steps several times.\n\nAs the network goes deeper, each new Convolution Layer learns more meaningful features.\n\nFor example:\n\n• The first Convolution Layer learns edges and lines.\n\n• The next layers learn eyes, wings, beaks, and feathers.\n\n• The deeper layers combine all these features and recognize the complete bird.\n\nThis gradual learning process is what makes CNNs so powerful for image recognition.\n\nOnce the CNN has extracted all the important features, it needs to prepare them for the final prediction.\n\n### **Step 5: Flatten Layer**\n\nAt this stage, the CNN has multiple Feature Maps containing everything it has learned about the image.\n\nHowever, the next part of the Neural Network cannot directly understand these two-dimensional Feature Maps.\n\nThe **Flatten Layer** converts all the Feature Maps into a single one-dimensional vector.\n\nThis vector now contains all the important information in a format that the Dense layers can process.\n\nNow that the data is ready, the Neural Network can begin making its final decision.\n\n### **Step 6: Fully Connected (Dense) Layer**\n\nThe flattened vector is passed to the **Fully Connected Layer**.\n\nThis layer combines all the extracted features and analyzes them together.\n\nYou can think of this layer as a person collecting all the clues before making the final answer.\n\nAfter analyzing every feature, the model is ready to calculate the probability for each possible class.\n\n### **Step 7: Output Layer (Softmax)**\n\nFinally, the output layer calculates the probability for every possible class.\n\nIn this example, the model predicts:\n\n• Donald → 0.2\n\n• Goofy → 0.1\n\n• Tweety → 0.7\n\nThe class with the highest probability is selected as the final prediction.\n\nSince **Tweety** has the highest probability (**0.7**), the CNN predicts that the image belongs to the **Tweety** class.\n\n### **Key Takeaway**\n\nA CNN doesn't recognize an object by looking at the entire image at once. It first learns **simple patterns** such as edges and lines, then gradually combines them into **object parts**, and finally recognizes the complete object before making its prediction.\n\nIn this lesson, we focused on understanding the complete workflow of a CNN. In the **next lesson**, we'll implement this architecture practically by building our first CNN model and training it on a real image dataset using TensorFlow and Keras."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Implementation of CNN (MNIST Digit Classifier)",
            "explanation": "Now that you understand how Convolutional Neural Networks work,Let's build our first Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) to classify handwritten digits using the **MNIST** dataset.\n\nBefore writing any code, create a new project folder and create a file named **main.py**.\n\n### Step 1: Install Required Libraries\nOpen the terminal and install TensorFlow:\n`pip install tensorflow`\n\n### Step 2: Import Required Libraries\n\n```python\nimport tensorflow as tf\nfrom tensorflow.keras import layers, models\nfrom tensorflow.keras.datasets import mnist\n```\n\nThese libraries allow us to load the MNIST dataset and build a CNN model.\n\n### Step 3: Load the Dataset\n\n```python\n(train_images, train_labels), (test_images, test_labels) = mnist.load_data()\n```\n\nThe MNIST dataset is automatically downloaded the first time you run this code.\n\nIt contains:\n\n• 60,000 training images\n• 10,000 testing images\n• Each image is 28 × 28 pixels\n• There are 10 classes (digits 0–9)\n\nLet's verify the dataset.\n\n```python\nprint(train_images.shape)\nprint(train_labels.shape)\n\nprint(test_images.shape)\nprint(test_labels.shape)\n```\n\nExpected Output:\n\n```\n(60000, 28, 28)\n(60000,)\n(10000, 28, 28)\n(10000,)\n```\n\n### Step 4: Preprocess the Images\n\n```python\ntrain_images = train_images.reshape((60000, 28, 28, 1)).astype('float32') / 255\n\ntest_images = test_images.reshape((10000, 28, 28, 1)).astype('float32') / 255\n```\n\nWe reshape the images to include the color channel and normalize pixel values from **0–255** to **0–1**, which helps the CNN learn faster.\n\n### Step 5: Build the CNN Model\n\n```python\nmodel = models.Sequential([\n    layers.Conv2D(32, (3,3), activation='relu', input_shape=(28,28,1)),\n    layers.MaxPooling2D((2,2)),\n\n    layers.Conv2D(64, (3,3), activation='relu'),\n    layers.MaxPooling2D((2,2)),\n\n    layers.Conv2D(64, (3,3), activation='relu'),\n\n    layers.Flatten(),\n\n    layers.Dense(64, activation='relu'),\n\n    layers.Dense(10, activation='softmax')\n])\n```\n\n### Step 6: Compile the Model\n\n```python\nmodel.compile(\n    optimizer='adam',\n    loss='sparse_categorical_crossentropy',\n    metrics=['accuracy']\n)\n```\n\nNow our CNN architecture is ready. In the next lesson, we'll train the model, evaluate its performance, and use it to predict handwritten digits."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Training, Evaluating & Predicting with CNN",
            "explanation": "Now that our CNN architecture is ready, let's train it using the MNIST training dataset.\n\n### Step 1: Train the Model\n\n```python\nhistory = model.fit(\n    train_images,\n    train_labels,\n    epochs=5,\n    batch_size=64,\n    validation_split=0.2\n)\n```\n\nDuring training:\n\n• The model learns from 80% of the training images.\n• The remaining 20% is used for validation.\n• Training continues for 5 epochs.\n\n### Step 2: Evaluate the Model\n\n```python\ntest_loss, test_acc = model.evaluate(\n    test_images,\n    test_labels\n)\n\nprint('Test Accuracy:', test_acc)\n```\n\nYou should obtain an accuracy close to **99%**, showing that the CNN has learned to recognize handwritten digits very effectively.\n\n### Step 3: Compare Training & Validation Accuracy\n\n```python\nprint(history.history['accuracy'])\nprint(history.history['val_accuracy'])\n```\n\nIf both values are high and close to each other, the model is generalizing well.\n\n### Step 4: Predict a New Digit\n\n```python\nprediction = model.predict(test_images[0:1])\n\npredicted_digit = tf.argmax(prediction[0]).numpy()\n\nprint('Predicted Digit:', predicted_digit)\nprint('Actual Digit:', test_labels[0])\n```\n\nThe model predicts the probabilities for all ten digits. We use **argmax()** to select the digit with the highest probability.\n\n### Step 5: View the Prediction\n\n```python\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nplt.imshow(test_images[0].reshape(28,28), cmap='gray')\nplt.title(f'Predicted: {predicted_digit}')\nplt.axis('off')\nplt.show()\n```\n\nThis displays the handwritten digit along with the model's prediction.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"text-align:center;padding:20px;\"><img src=\"/images/predicted_output7.png\" alt=\"predicted_output7\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px;border:1px solid #ddd;box-shadow:0 4px 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.15);\"/><p style=\"margin-top:12px;font-size:14px;color:#555;\"><b>Figure:</b> Predicted Digit Output.</p></div>"
          }
