        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Feature Hierarchies",
            "explanation": "Have you ever wondered how a CNN recognizes a dog?\nThe CNN does not directly learn what a dog looks like. Instead, it learns layer by layer. This is called a **Feature Hierarchy**.\n**Layer 1: Low-Level Features (Edges)**\nThe first convolutional layers are extremely simple. Their kernels look for raw edges, straight lines, color gradients, and corners. They don't know what an object is yet.\n**Layer 2: Mid-Level Features (Patterns & Parts)**\nThe next set of layers combines the edges from Layer 1. If Layer 1 found a circle of edges, Layer 2 realizes it's looking at an 'Eye' or a 'Wheel'. It detects textures, fur, and specific geometric shapes.\n**Layer 3: High-Level Features (Objects)**\nThe deepest convolutional layers combine the parts from Layer 2. If it sees two eyes, a nose, and furry texture, it activates the feature map for 'Dog Face'.",
            "html_code": "<iframe style=\"width:100%; min-height:600px; border:none; background:transparent;\" srcdoc=\"<style>body{margin:0;padding:0;display:flex;justify-content:center;align-items:center;min-height:100vh;background:transparent;}</style><div style=&quot;font-family:sans-serif; text-align:center; max-width:550px; margin:auto; padding:20px; background:#fff7ed; border-radius:12px; border:1px solid #fdba74; box-shadow:0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.05);&quot;><h3 style=&quot;color:#c2410c; margin-bottom:5px;&quot;>👁️ Feature Hierarchy Explorer</h3><p style=&quot;font-size:13px; color:#9a3412; margin-bottom:15px;&quot;>Click through the depth of the network to see what the kernels are extracting.</p><div id=&quot;visContainer&quot; style=&quot;height:150px; display:flex; justify-content:center; align-items:center; background:white; border:2px dashed #f97316; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:15px; font-size:40px;&quot;>🚗 Raw Pixels</div><div style=&quot;display:flex; justify-content:space-between; gap:10px;&quot;><button id=&quot;btn0&quot; style=&quot;flex:1; padding:10px; background:#fb923c; color:white; border:none; border-radius:6px; cursor:pointer; font-weight:bold;&quot;>Input</button><button id=&quot;btn1&quot; style=&quot;flex:1; padding:10px; background:#cbd5e1; color:#334155; border:none; border-radius:6px; cursor:pointer; font-weight:bold;&quot;>Layer 1</button><button id=&quot;btn2&quot; style=&quot;flex:1; padding:10px; background:#cbd5e1; color:#334155; border:none; border-radius:6px; cursor:pointer; font-weight:bold;&quot;>Layer 2</button><button id=&quot;btn3&quot; style=&quot;flex:1; padding:10px; background:#cbd5e1; color:#334155; border:none; border-radius:6px; cursor:pointer; font-weight:bold;&quot;>Layer 3</button></div><p id=&quot;layerDesc&quot; style=&quot;margin-top:15px; font-size:14px; color:#9a3412; font-weight:bold; height:40px;&quot;>Raw Image Tensor (RGB Pixels)</p><script>const vis=document.getElementById('visContainer'); const desc=document.getElementById('layerDesc'); const btns=[document.getElementById('btn0'), document.getElementById('btn1'), document.getElementById('btn2'), document.getElementById('btn3')]; const states=[{v:'🚗', d:'Raw Image Tensor (RGB Pixels)'}, {v:'| / - \\ < >', d:'Low-Level: Detecting raw edges and color gradients.'}, {v:'⭕ ⬛ 🔲 🔘', d:'Mid-Level: Combining edges into wheels, windows, and shapes.'}, {v:'🚘', d:'High-Level: Combining wheels and shapes into a Car pattern!'}]; btns.forEach((btn, idx) => { btn.addEventListener('click', () => { vis.innerText = states[idx].v; desc.innerText = states[idx].d; btns.forEach(b => { b.style.background='#cbd5e1'; b.style.color='#334155'; }); btn.style.background='#fb923c'; btn.style.color='white'; }); });</script></div>\"></iframe>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The CNN Training Process",
            "explanation": "Now you may be thinking, who teaches the CNN to find edges, eyes, and shapes?\nThe answer is simple: the CNN learns by itself.\nAt the beginning, all Kernel values are random.\n\n**Learning Process:**\n• The CNN makes a prediction.\n• The Loss Function calculates the error.\n• Backpropagation finds which weights caused the mistake.\n• Gradient Descent updates the weights.\n• The CNN improves its prediction.\nThis process repeats thousands of times until the CNN learns useful patterns.\n**Key Idea:** CNNs learn automatically by continuously correcting their mistakes."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "The CNN below is missing a Pooling layer. Add MaxPooling2D after the Convolution layer.",
