        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Dense vs Sparse Representations",
            "explanation": "Excellent! After converting text into numbers, we need to understand how those numbers are stored. In NLP, there are two main types of representations: **Sparse Representation** and **Dense Representation**.\n\nFor example, suppose our vocabulary contains four words: **Cat, Dog, Apple,** and **Car**.\n\n**Sparse Representation:**\n• Cat → [1, 0, 0, 0]\n• Dog → [0, 1, 0, 0]\n• Apple → [0, 0, 1, 0]\n• Car → [0, 0, 0, 1]\n\nNotice that most values are **0**, so these vectors contain a lot of unused space and do not capture relationships between words.\n\nImagine organizing items in a warehouse.\n\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/nlp/tr1.png]\n\nIn a **Sparse Representation**, every item gets its own large storage area, but most of the space remains empty. This wastes space and makes it difficult to understand relationships between similar words.\n\nIn a **Dense Representation**, similar items are organized close together. Instead of long vectors with mostly zeros, words are represented using compact numerical vectors that capture their meaning.\n\nFor example:\n• Cat → [0.82, 0.31, -0.15]\n• Dog → [0.79, 0.28, -0.12]\n• Apple → [-0.45, 0.91, 0.33]\n• Car → [0.12, -0.68, 0.87]\n\nNotice that **Cat** and **Dog** have similar values because they have similar meanings, while **Apple** and **Car** have very different values.\n\n**Key Takeaway:**\n• **Sparse Representation** → Large vectors with many zeros and little relationship information.\n• **Dense Representation** → Compact vectors that capture similarities and meaningful patterns between words."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Paradigm Shift: What are Word Embeddings?",
            "explanation": "Now let's learn one of the biggest breakthroughs in NLP: **Word Embeddings**.\n\nBoW and TF-IDF create very large sparse vectors. Most values are zeros. If your vocabulary has 50,000 words, every sentence becomes an array of 50,000 numbers, and 99% of them are just zeros. They also cannot understand that words like **Developer** and **Engineer** have similar meanings.\n\nWord Embeddings solve this problem by representing words as **Dense Vectors**.\n\nInstead of representing a word as a single '1' in a huge vector, each word is represented by a fixed-size array of decimal numbers (typically 100–300 values).\n\n**Word:** \"Engineer\"\n**Embedding:** [0.94, -0.42, 0.11, 0.88, ...]\n\nThese numbers are learned during training, allowing words with similar meanings to have similar vector representations. This helps Machine Learning models understand relationships between words.\n\nThere are two main types of Word Embeddings:\n\n• **Static Word Embeddings** – A word always has the same vector regardless of the sentence. Examples: **Word2Vec, GloVe, FastText**.\n\n• **Contextual Word Embeddings** – A word's vector changes depending on the surrounding words in the sentence. Examples: **ELMo, BERT, RoBERTa, GPT Embeddings**.\n\nIn the next lessons, we'll explore each of these embedding techniques in detail."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Word2Vec: CBOW vs. Skip-Gram",
            "explanation": "Excellent! In the previous lesson, we learned that **Word Embeddings** represent words as numerical vectors while preserving their meanings.\n\nBut here's an important question.\n\nHow does a computer actually learn these word vectors?\n\nOne of the most popular algorithms for learning Word Embeddings is **Word2Vec**, introduced by Google in 2013.\n\nThe main idea behind Word2Vec is simple:\n\n**Words that appear in similar contexts usually have similar meanings.**\n\nFor example, the words **King** and **Queen** often appear in similar sentences, so Word2Vec learns similar vector representations for them.\n\nTo learn these vectors, Word2Vec trains a small Neural Network using sentences from a large text corpus.\n\nIt uses a small sliding window that moves through every sentence and learns relationships between neighboring words.\n\nWord2Vec can learn these relationships in two different ways:\n\n**1. CBOW (Continuous Bag of Words)**\n\nIn CBOW, the model looks at the surrounding words (called **context words**) and tries to predict the missing middle word.\n\nFor example, consider the sentence:\n\n`I love learning NLP every day`\n\nSuppose the target word is **learning**.\n\nThe model sees:\n\nInput:\n\n`I` `love` ____ `NLP` `every`\n\nOutput:\n\n`learning`\n\nThe model repeats this process millions of times using different sentences. Over time, it learns meaningful word embeddings.\n\n**How CBOW Works:**\n\n• Step 1: Select a target word.\n\n• Step 2: Take the surrounding context words using a sliding window.\n\n• Step 3: Feed the context words into the Neural Network.\n\n• Step 4: Predict the missing target word.\n\n• Step 5: Update the word vectors based on the prediction error.\n\nCBOW is generally **faster to train** and works well for **common words** because it learns from multiple surrounding words at once.\n\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/nlp/tr2.png]\n\n**2. Skip-Gram**\n\nSkip-Gram works in the opposite direction.\n\nInstead of predicting the middle word, it starts with the target word and predicts the surrounding context words.\n\nUsing the same sentence:\n\nTarget Word:\n\n`learning`\n\nPredicted Outputs:\n\n`I` `love` `NLP` `every`\n\n**How Skip-Gram Works:**\n\n• Step 1: Select the target word.\n\n• Step 2: Feed the target word into the Neural Network.\n\n• Step 3: Predict each surrounding context word.\n\n• Step 4: Compare the predictions with the actual context words.\n\n• Step 5: Update the word vectors.\n\nSkip-Gram learns better representations for **rare and technical words**, but because it predicts multiple context words for every target word, it usually takes **more time to train** than CBOW.\n\n**CBOW vs Skip-Gram**\n\n• **CBOW:** Context Words → Predict Target Word\n\n• **Skip-Gram:** Target Word → Predict Context Words\n\n• **CBOW:** Faster training, better for frequent words.\n\n• **Skip-Gram:** Slower training, better for rare words.\n\n**Key Takeaway:**\n\nWord2Vec learns word embeddings by analyzing the relationship between neighboring words in a sentence. It offers two training approaches:\n\n• `CBOW` learns by predicting the missing word from its surrounding context.\n\n• `Skip-Gram` learns by predicting the surrounding context words from a single target word.\n\nAlthough both methods generate word embeddings, they differ only in the direction of prediction.\net's walk through the following Python example to understand how Word2Vec works. First, install the gensim library, then import the Word2Vec class.",
            "example": "#First Need to install gensim library\n#pip install gensim\nfrom gensim.models import Word2Vec\n\nsentences = [\n    ['i', 'love', 'nlp'],\n    ['nlp', 'with', 'word2vec'],\n    ['i', 'love', 'word2vec']\n]\n\n# CBOW Model (sg=0)\ncbow_model = Word2Vec(sentences, vector_size=50, window=2, min_count=1, sg=0)\nprint('CBOW Vector for NLP:')\nprint(cbow_model.wv['nlp'])\n\n# Skip-Gram Model (sg=1)\nskipgram_model = Word2Vec(sentences, vector_size=50, window=2, min_count=1, sg=1)\nprint('Skip-Gram Vector for NLP:')\nprint(skipgram_model.wv['nlp'])"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "GloVe: Global Vectors for Word Representation",
            "explanation": "Anther popular embedding technique is **GloVe (Global Vectors)** developed by Stanford, **GloVe** takes a different mathematical approach.Unlike Word2Vec, GloVe looks at the entire collection of documents instead of only nearby words.\n**Step-by-Step Process:**\n**Step 1: The Co-occurrence Matrix.** GloVe builds a massive grid tracking how many times every word appears in the same paragraph as every other word. If 'React' and 'Component' appear together 5,000 times, that cell gets a 5,000.\n**Step 2: Probability Ratios.** It calculates the statistical probability that two words are related. (e.g., 'Ice' co-occurs highly with 'Solid', but 'Steam' co-occurs highly with 'Gas').\n**Step 3: Dimensionality Reduction.** A matrix of all words in the English language is too huge (Sparse). GloVe uses mathematical factorization to compress this massive grid down to dense 300-dimension vectors, preserving the statistical relationships.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/nlp/tr3.png]\n",
            "example": "# First install the gensim library\n# pip install gensim\n\nimport gensim.downloader as api\n\n# Load the pre-trained GloVe model (50-dimensional)\nglove = api.load('glove-wiki-gigaword-50')\n\n# Get the embedding vector for the word 'king'\nprint('Embedding Vector for King:')\nprint(glove['king'])\n\n# Find words with similar meaning\nprint('\\nWords Similar to King:')\nprint(glove.most_similar('king', topn=5)) #[('prince', 0.8236179351806641), ('queen', 0.7839043140411377), ('emperor', 0.7736247777938843)]"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
