    {
      "id": "M004-nlp-lesson-3-similarity",
      "title": "Similarity Metrics & Topic Modeling",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#0d9488",
        "accent": "#0f766e"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Why Similarity Matters",
            "explanation": "Excellent! In the previous lessons, we learned how to convert text into numerical vectors using Word Embeddings.\n\nBut here's an important question.\n\nImagine you search on Google for **\"Best restaurants near me\"**. How does Google know which webpages are most relevant to your search?\n\nOr suppose you ask ChatGPT, **\"How do I learn Python?\"**. How does it understand that your question is similar to **\"Best way to study Python programming\"**, even though the exact words are different?\n\nTo answer questions like these, computers need a way to measure **how similar two pieces of text are**.\n\nThis is where **Similarity Metrics** come in.\n\n**What are Similarity Metrics?**\n\n`Similarity Metrics` are mathematical techniques used to measure how closely two words, sentences, or documents are related.\n\nA higher similarity score means the texts are more closely related, while a lower score means they are less related.\n\nFor example:\n\nSentence 1: `I love learning Artificial Intelligence.`\n\nSentence 2: `I enjoy studying AI.`\n\nAlthough the words are different, both sentences express a very similar meaning. A good similarity metric gives these sentences a **high similarity score**.\n\nNow consider another sentence: `Today is raining heavily.`\n\nThis sentence is about weather, so it has very little relationship with the first two sentences. Therefore, it receives a **low similarity score**.\n\nSimilarity Metrics are used in many real-world applications.\n\n**1. Search Engines** ➔ Find webpages that are most similar to the user's search query.\n\n**2. Recommendation Systems** ➔ Recommend similar movies, articles, songs, or products.\n\n**3. Chatbots and AI Assistants** ➔ Find responses that best match the user's question.\n\n**4. Plagiarism Detection** ➔ Compare two documents to determine how similar they are.\n\n**5. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)** ➔ Retrieve the most relevant documents from a Vector Database before sending them to a Large Language Model.\n\n**Key Takeaway:**\n\nSimilarity Metrics help computers measure how closely two words, sentences, or documents are related. They are one of the most important building blocks behind search engines, recommendation systems, chatbots, plagiarism detection, and modern AI applications such as RAG."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Tale of Two Distances: Euclidean & Manhattan",
            "explanation": "Fantastic! Before learning the most popular similarity metric, let's understand two basic ways of measuring distance.Imagine you are an AI detective trying to figure out if two documents are talking about the exact same subject. \nDocument A is a tiny 5-word tweet: *\"Apples are delicious red fruits.\"*\nDocument B is a massive 5,000-word Wikipedia article about *Apples*.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/nlp/sm1.png]\nIf we turn these texts into vectors and plot them on a graph, the traditional methods of measuring distance will completely fail us. Let's look at the two oldest ways to measure distance:\n`1. Euclidean Distance (The Crow's Flight)`\nThis is the straight-line distance between two points in space. It is the classic Pythagorean theorem you learned in geometry.\n\nDistance = √((x₂ - x₁)² + (y₂ - y₁)²)\n\n`2. Manhattan Distance (The Taxi Cab Grid)`\nImagine you are driving a taxi in Manhattan. You cannot drive a straight line through buildings; you must stick to the rigid grid of horizontal and vertical streets. It calculates the absolute sum of differences across all dimensions.\n\nDistance = |x₂ - x₁| + |y₂ - y₁|\nLet's walk through the following Python example to understand how distance metrics work. First, install the scipy library, then import the euclidean and cityblock functions to calculate the distance between vectors.",
            "example": "#First install scipy library\n#pip install scipy\nfrom scipy.spatial.distance import euclidean, cityblock\n\npoint_a = [1, 2]\npoint_b = [4, 6]\n\nprint('Euclidean Distance:', euclidean(point_a, point_b))   #Euclidean Distance: 5.0\nprint('Manhattan Distance:', cityblock(point_a, point_b))   #Manhattan Distance: 7"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Gold Standard: Cosine Similarity",
            "explanation": "In NLP, we care more about meaning than physical distance.That's why we use **Cosine Similarity**.\nInstead of measuring the distance between vectors, Cosine Similarity measures the angle between them. focusing on direction rather than length.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/nlp/sm2.png]\nCosine Similarity Formula:\n**Cosine Similarity (cos(θ))** = `(A · B) / (||A|| × ||B||)`\nThis formula compares two vectors by measuring the angle between them.\nWhere: A · B = Dot Product of vectors, ||A|| = Length of Vector A, ||B|| = Length of Vector B.\nThe value of Cosine Similarity depends on the angle between the two vectors. Smaller angles indicate higher similarity, while larger angles indicate lower similarity.\n`0° Angle` → Cosine Similarity = 1.0 (Perfectly Similar)\n`90° Angle` → Cosine Similarity = 0.0 (Unrelated)\n`180° Angle` → Cosine Similarity = -1.0 (Exact Opposites)\n`Example:`\nKing = [1,2], Queen = [2,3] → A·B = 8,\n ||A|| = 2.24, ||B|| = 3.61 \ncos(θ) = 8 / (2.24 × 3.61) ≈ 0.99 (very similar)\nUnlike Euclidean or Manhattan Distance, Cosine Similarity ignores vector size and focuses on semantic direction. This means a short tweet and a 500-page book about the same topic can still have a similarity score close to 1.0 if they point in the same semantic direction.\n The below The diagram demonstrates that words with similar meanings, such as 'King' and 'Qween', have vectors pointing in nearly the same direction, resulting in a high Cosine Similarity score.\nLet's walk through the following Python example to understand how Cosine Similarity works. First, import the cosine_similarity.",
            "example": "from sklearn.metrics.pairwise import cosine_similarity\n\nvector_a = [[1, 1, 1]]\nvector_b = [[1, 1, 0]]\n\nsimilarity = cosine_similarity(vector_a, vector_b)\nprint('Cosine Similarity:', similarity[0][0])"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Using sklearn's cosine_similarity function, calculate the cosine similarity between the following vectors and print the result rounded to 2 decimal places.\n\nvec1 = [[1, 1, 1, 1, 0]]\nvec2 = [[1, 1, 1, 0, 1]]",
            "starterCode": "from sklearn.metrics.pairwise import cosine_similarity\n\nvec1 = [[1, 1, 1, 1, 0]]\nvec2 = [[1, 1, 1, 0, 1]]\n\n# Write your code here\n",
            "solution": "from sklearn.metrics.pairwise import cosine_similarity\n\nsimilarity = cosine_similarity(vec1, vec2)\nprint(round(similarity[0][0], 2))",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use sklearn.metrics.pairwise.cosine_similarity and access the result using similarity[0][0]."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Jaccard Similarity: The Set Theory Approach",
            "explanation": "Excellent! In the previous lesson, we learned that computers use **Similarity Metrics** to measure how closely two pieces of text are related.\n\nBut here's an important question.\n\nWhat if we don't have Word Embeddings? What if we only have a list of words, tags, or keywords?\n\nIn these situations, we can use **Jaccard Similarity**.\n\n**What is Jaccard Similarity?**\n\n`Jaccard Similarity` is a simple similarity metric that compares two sets by measuring how many elements they have in common.\n\nIt is based on a very simple idea:\n\n**Number of Common Elements ÷ Total Unique Elements**\n\nThis is also called:\n\n`Jaccard Similarity = Intersection / Union`\n\nLet's understand how it works with an example.\n\nSuppose we have two sets:\n\n**Set A** `{React, NextJS, Tailwind}`\n\n**Set B** `{React, NodeJS}`\n\nNow let's calculate the similarity step by step.\n\n**Step 1: Find the Intersection**\n\nThe **Intersection** contains only the elements that appear in both sets.\n\nIntersection:\n\n`{React}`\n\nNumber of common elements = **1**\n\n**Step 2: Find the Union**\n\nThe **Union** contains all unique elements from both sets.\n\nUnion:\n\n`{React, NextJS, Tailwind, NodeJS}`\n\nTotal unique elements = **4**\n\n**Step 3: Apply the Formula**\n\n`Jaccard Similarity = Intersection ÷ Union`\n\n`= 1 ÷ 4`\n\n`= 0.25`\n\nThis means the two sets are **25% similar**.\n\nThe more common elements two sets share, the higher their Jaccard Similarity score.\n\n**Where is Jaccard Similarity Used?**\n\n• Comparing keyword lists.\n\n• Finding duplicate or near-duplicate documents.\n\n• Comparing hashtags.\n\n• Comparing user interests.\n\n• Recommendation systems based on shared items.\n\n**Key Takeaway:**\n\nJaccard Similarity measures the similarity between two sets by dividing the number of **shared elements (Intersection)** by the **total unique elements (Union)**. The score ranges from **0 to 1**, where:\n\n• `0` means the sets have nothing in common.\n\n• `1` means the sets are exactly the same.",
            "example": "set_a = {'React', 'NextJS', 'Tailwind'}\nset_b = {'React', 'NodeJS'}\n\nintersection = len(set_a.intersection(set_b))\nunion = len(set_a.union(set_b))\n\njaccard_similarity = intersection / union\nprint('Jaccard Similarity:', jaccard_similarity)"
          }
        },
        {
