        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Calculate the Cosine Similarity between two text vectors using `scikit-learn`.",
            "starterCode": "from sklearn.metrics.pairwise import cosine_similarity\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Document Vectors (e.g., extracted from a Word2Vec model)\ndoc_A = np.array([[1.5, 0.2, 0.8]])\ndoc_B = np.array([[1.4, 0.1, 0.9]])\ndoc_C = np.array([[-0.5, 2.1, -1.2]]) # Unrelated document\n\n# TODO: Calculate the similarity between doc_A and doc_B\nsim_AB = ____(____, ____)\n\n# TODO: Calculate the similarity between doc_A and doc_C\nsim_AC = ____(____, ____)\n\nprint(f\"Similarity A to B: {sim_AB[0][0]:.3f}\")\nprint(f\"Similarity A to C: {sim_AC[0][0]:.3f}\")",
            "solution": "from sklearn.metrics.pairwise import cosine_similarity\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Document Vectors (e.g., extracted from a Word2Vec model)\ndoc_A = np.array([[1.5, 0.2, 0.8]])\ndoc_B = np.array([[1.4, 0.1, 0.9]])\ndoc_C = np.array([[-0.5, 2.1, -1.2]]) # Unrelated document\n\n# TODO: Calculate the similarity between doc_A and doc_B\nsim_AB = cosine_similarity(doc_A, doc_B)\n\n# TODO: Calculate the similarity between doc_A and doc_C\nsim_AC = cosine_similarity(doc_A, doc_C)\n\nprint(f\"Similarity A to B: {sim_AB[0][0]:.3f}\")\nprint(f\"Similarity A to C: {sim_AC[0][0]:.3f}\")",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Pass the two numpy arrays directly into the `cosine_similarity(array1, array2)` function."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Introduction to Topic Modeling",
            "explanation": "Excellent! In the previous lessons, we learned how to measure the similarity between words, sentences, and documents.\n\nBut here's an important question.\n\nWhat if you have **thousands or even millions of documents**? Is it possible to read every document one by one and manually organize them into categories?\n\nThe answer is **No**.\n\nThis is where **Topic Modeling** becomes useful.\n\nImagine a company receives **100,000 customer support tickets** every month. Reading every ticket manually would take an enormous amount of time. Instead, the company wants an AI system that can automatically group similar tickets into topics such as:\n\n• Password Reset\n\n• Billing Issues\n\n• Login Problems\n\n• Delivery Delays\n\n• Website Bugs\n\nThis process is called **Topic Modeling**.\n\n**What is Topic Modeling?**\n\n`Topic Modeling` is an **Unsupervised Machine Learning** technique that automatically discovers hidden topics or themes from a collection of documents.\n\nUnlike classification, we do **not** provide predefined labels. The algorithm analyzes the words that frequently appear together across many documents and automatically identifies meaningful topics.\n\nFor example, suppose many documents frequently contain words like:\n\n`goal`, `football`, `player`, `match`, `stadium`\n\nThe algorithm may automatically identify the topic as **Sports**.\n\nSimilarly, if another group of documents contains words like:\n\n`doctor`, `hospital`, `medicine`, `patient`, `treatment`\n\nThe algorithm may identify the topic as **Healthcare**.\n\nIn this way, Topic Modeling helps us understand large collections of text without reading every document manually.\n\n**Real-World Applications**\n\n• Analyze customer support tickets to identify the most common customer issues.\n\n• Group news articles into topics such as Politics, Sports, Technology, and Business.\n\n• Discover trending discussions on social media platforms.\n\n• Organize research papers into different subject areas.\n\n• Analyze customer reviews to identify common complaints and popular product features.\n\n• Automatically categorize large collections of emails or documents.\n\n**Key Takeaway:**\n\n`Topic Modeling` is an Unsupervised Learning technique that automatically discovers hidden topics from a large collection of text documents by analyzing patterns of word occurrences. It helps organize and understand massive amounts of text without requiring manually labeled data."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "LSA (Latent Semantic Analysis)",
            "explanation": "Great! In the previous lesson, we learned that **Topic Modeling** helps us automatically discover hidden topics from a large collection of documents.\n\nBut here's an important question.\n\nHow can a computer find these hidden topics without reading and understanding the documents like humans?\n\nOne of the earliest techniques developed to solve this problem is **Latent Semantic Analysis (LSA)**.\n\n**What is LSA?**\n\n`Latent Semantic Analysis (LSA)` is an **Unsupervised Learning** technique that discovers hidden relationships between words and documents.\n\nThe word **Latent** means **hidden**, and **Semantic** refers to **meaning**. Therefore, LSA tries to uncover the hidden meanings and topics present in a collection of documents.\n\nImagine you have thousands of news articles. Some articles use the word **Car**, while others use **Automobile**. Although these are different words, they often appear in similar contexts.\n\nLSA identifies these hidden relationships and understands that both words are closely related.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/nlp/sm3.png]\n**How Does LSA Work?**\n\n**Step 1:** Create a **Term-Document Matrix**.\n\nRows represent words, columns represent documents, and each cell stores how many times a word appears in a document.\n\n**Step 2:** Apply **Singular Value Decomposition (SVD)**.\n\nSVD compresses the large matrix into a smaller representation while preserving the most important information.\n\nYou can think of SVD as removing unnecessary noise and keeping only the important patterns in the data.\n\n**Step 3:** Discover Hidden Topics.\n\nAfter compression, words that frequently appear together become closer to each other, allowing LSA to identify the underlying topics shared across documents.\n\nFor example:\n\nWords like:`football`, `goal`, `player`, `stadium`\n\nmay form the topic: `Sports`\n\nSimilarly,\n\n`doctor`, `hospital`, `medicine`, `patient`\n\nmay form the topic: `Healthcare`\n\nNotice that we never told the algorithm what the topics were. It discovers them automatically by analyzing patterns in the documents.\n\n**Real-World Applications**\n\n• Discover hidden topics in news articles.\n\n• Organize research papers into subject areas.\n\n• Group customer feedback into common themes.\n\n• Improve document search and information retrieval.\n\n• Automatically organize large collections of documents.\nLet's walk through the following Python example. First, import the CountVectorizer and TruncatedSVD classes to perform Latent Semantic Analysis (LSA).",
            "example": "from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import CountVectorizer\nfrom sklearn.decomposition import TruncatedSVD\n\n# Sample Documents\ndocuments = [\n    'Football players scored many goals in the match',\n    'The team won the football tournament',\n    'Doctors treated patients in the hospital'\n]\n\n# Step 1: Create Document-Term Matrix\nvectorizer = CountVectorizer(stop_words='english')\nX = vectorizer.fit_transform(documents)\n\n# Step 2: Apply LSA using SVD\nlsa = TruncatedSVD(n_components=2, random_state=42)\nlsa.fit(X)\n\n# Step 3: Display the discovered topics\nterms = vectorizer.get_feature_names_out()\n\nfor i, topic in enumerate(lsa.components_):\n    print(f'Topic {i+1}:')\n    top_words = [terms[index] for index in topic.argsort()[-5:][::-1]]\n    print(top_words)\n\n# Expected Output:\n# Topic 1:\n# ['football', 'team', 'match', 'goals', 'tournament']\n#\n# Topic 2:\n# ['doctors', 'hospital', 'patients', 'treated']"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "LDA (Latent Dirichlet Allocation)",
            "explanation": "Excellent! In the previous lesson, we learned how **LSA** discovers hidden topics by reducing the dimensions of a Term-Document Matrix using **SVD**.\n\nBut here's an important question.\n\nCan we discover topics by understanding the probability of words appearing together instead of simply compressing the data?\n\nThe answer is **Yes**, and this is where **Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA)** comes in.\n\n**What is LDA?**\n\n`Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA)` is one of the most popular **Unsupervised Topic Modeling** algorithms.\n\nUnlike LSA, LDA takes a **probabilistic approach**. Instead of reducing dimensions, it assumes that every document is made up of multiple topics, and every topic is made up of multiple words.\n\nLDA is based on two important assumptions.\n\n**Assumption 1: Every Document is a Mixture of Topics**\n\nA single document can discuss more than one topic.\n\nFor example, a news article might contain:\n\n• 70% Politics\n\n• 30% Economics\n\nSimilarly, another article may contain:\n\n• 60% Sports\n\n• 40% Health\n\n**Assumption 2: Every Topic is a Mixture of Words**\n\nEach topic has a group of words that frequently appear together.\n\nFor example:\n\n**Politics** `election`, `vote`, `government`, `minister`\n\n**Sports** `football`, `player`, `goal`, `coach`\n\n**Healthcare** `doctor`, `hospital`, `patient`, `medicine`\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/nlp/sm4.png]\n**How Does LDA Work?**\n\n**Step 1:** Read all the documents in the corpus.\n\n**Step 2:** Randomly assign words to different topics.\n\n**Step 3:** Repeatedly update these assignments based on the probability of words appearing together.\n\n**Step 4:** After many iterations, similar words naturally group into meaningful topics.\n\nFinally, LDA tells us:\n\n• Which topics exist in the corpus.\n\n• Which words belong to each topic.\n\n• How much each topic contributes to every document.\n\nFor example, if you ask LDA to find **3 topics**, it may automatically discover:\n\n**Topic 1:** Football, Goal, Player, Match\n\n**Topic 2:** Doctor, Hospital, Patient, Medicine\n\n**Topic 3:** Election, Government, Vote, Minister\n\nNotice that we never manually labeled these topics. LDA discovered them automatically by analyzing word probabilities.\n\n**Real-World Applications**\n\n• Discover topics in customer reviews.\n\n• Group news articles automatically.\n\n• Analyze research papers.\n\n• Organize support tickets.\n\n• Discover trending discussions on social media.\nLet's walk through the following Python example.To understand the practical implementation of LSA.",
            "example": "# First install the library\n# pip install scikit-learn\n\nfrom sklearn.feature_extraction.text import CountVectorizer\nfrom sklearn.decomposition import LatentDirichletAllocation\n\n# Sample Documents\ndocuments = [\n    'Football players scored many goals in the match',\n    'The team won the football tournament',\n    'Doctors treated patients in the hospital',\n    'Medicine helps patients recover quickly'\n]\n\n# Step 1: Create Document-Term Matrix\nvectorizer = CountVectorizer(stop_words='english')\nX = vectorizer.fit_transform(documents)\n\n# Step 2: Train the LDA Model\nlda = LatentDirichletAllocation(n_components=2, random_state=42)\nlda.fit(X)\n\n# Step 3: Display the discovered topics\nterms = vectorizer.get_feature_names_out()\n\nfor i, topic in enumerate(lda.components_):\n    print(f'Topic {i+1}:')\n    top_words = [terms[index] for index in topic.argsort()[-5:][::-1]]\n    print(top_words)\n\n# Expected Output:\n# Topic 1:\n# ['football', 'team', 'goals', 'match', 'tournament']\n#\n# Topic 2:\n# ['doctors', 'hospital', 'patients', 'medicine', 'treated']"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "content": {
            "questions": [
              {
                "question": "What is the primary goal of Topic Modeling?",
                "options": [
                  "To automatically discover hidden topics from a collection of documents",
