    {
      "id": "M004-nlp-lesson-5-transformers",
      "title": "Attention Mechanism & Transformers",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#8b5cf6",
        "accent": "#6d28d9"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Bottleneck of LSTMs",
            "explanation": "LSTMs solved many of the problems faced by traditional RNNs. However, they still have some limitations. In particular, they suffer from two major drawbacks.Let's discuss below.\n**1. The Information Bottleneck:**\nImagine reading a 1,000-word article. An LSTM processes it word-by-word, constantly trying to compress everything it reads into a single, fixed-size 'Hidden State' vector. By the time it reaches the end, the nuances of the first paragraph are almost entirely crushed and lost.\n\n**2. The Sequential Speed Limit:**\nBecause step `t` requires the output of step `t-1`, LSTMs are strictly sequential. You cannot process word 100 until you finish word 99. This means they **cannot be parallelized**. Thousands of GPU cores sit completely idle, waiting for the loop to finish. Training large models took months."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Birth of Attention",
            "explanation": "In 2014, a groundbreaking idea changed the way researchers thought about processing language. Until then, models like RNNs and LSTMs tried to compress an entire sentence or document into a small internal representation. This worked for short sentences, but as the text became longer, important information could be lost.\n\nResearchers asked a simple question: 'Why force the model to remember everything in a single memory?' Instead of compressing the entire input into one vector, they introduced the Attention Mechanism, which allows the model to keep access to all the words in the input sequence.\n\nThink of **Attention** like a student answering a question from a textbook. Instead of trying to memorize the entire book, the student quickly looks back at the relevant pages and focuses only on the important information. Similarly, when making a prediction, the model looks at all the words in the input, assigns an importance score (weight) to each word, and focuses more on the words that matter most. This ability to selectively focus on relevant information makes Attention highly effective for understanding long and complex text."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Self-Attention: Context is Everything",
            "explanation": "Fantastic! Now let's learn the most important idea behind modern AI models: Self-Attention.\n\n**Self-Attention** is the core idea behind Transformers. It allows every word in a sentence to look at all other words and decide which ones are important for understanding its meaning.\n\nSelf-Attention is a mechanism that enables each word in a sequence to focus on the most relevant words in the same sequence, helping the model understand the correct context and meaning.\n\nWhy is this needed? Many words can have multiple meanings, and their actual meaning depends on the surrounding context.\n\nExample:\nSentence 1: 'I sat near the river bank.'\nSentence 2: 'I deposited money in the bank.'\n\nBoth sentences contain the word 'bank', but the meaning is different. In the first sentence, the word 'bank' pays more attention to 'river', so it understands that bank means the side of a river. In the second sentence, the word 'bank' pays more attention to 'money' and 'deposited', so it understands that bank means a financial institution.\n\n`How it works:`\n\n• `Step 1:` Every word creates Query, Key, and Value vectors.\n\n• `Step 2:` The current word compares its Query with the Keys of all other words.\n\n• `Step 3:` Important words receive higher attention scores.\n\n• `Step 4:` These scores are used to combine the Values of important words.\n\n• `Step 5:` The word updates its meaning using this contextual information.\n\nWithout Self-Attention:\n'bank' → Same meaning everywhere.\n\nWith Self-Attention:\n'river' + 'bank' → River Side.\n'money' + 'bank' → Financial Institution.\n\n`Key Idea:` Self-Attention allows words to dynamically change their meaning based on the words around them, helping the model understand context much more accurately."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What are Query (Q), Key (K), and Value (V)?",
            "explanation": "Great! We learned that Self-Attention helps a word focus on other important words in the same sentence. But here's an important question.\n\nHow does the model decide which words are important?\n\nTo answer this, every word creates three special vectors called **Query (Q)**, **Key (K)**, and **Value (V)**.\n\n**Query (Q):** Represents what the current word is looking for. It asks, *'Which other words can help me understand my meaning?'*\n\n**Key (K):** Represents what each word in the sentence contains. It is compared with the Query to determine how relevant that word is.\n\n**Value (V):** Represents the actual information carried by the word. Once the important words are found, their Values are used to build the final meaning.\n\nLet's understand this using the sentence shown in the image.\n\nSuppose the model is currently processing the word **'it'**. The word **'it'** does not have a clear meaning by itself, so it needs to find which word it refers to.\n\n`Step 1:` The current word is **'it'**.\n\n`Step 2:` The model creates a **Query vector** for **'it'**.\n\n`Step 3:` Every word in the sentence creates its own **Key** and **Value** vectors.\n\n`Step 4:` The Query of **'it'** is compared with the Key of every word. This produces a similarity score for each word.\n\n`Step 5:` Words with higher similarity receive higher Attention scores. In the image, the word **'generative'** receives the highest score because it is the most relevant to **'it'**.\n\n`Step 6:` The model retrieves the **Value** of **'generative'** and uses that information to understand what **'it'** refers to.\n\nIn simple words:\n\n• **Query** = What am I looking for?\n\n• **Key** = What information do I have?\n\n• **Value** = What information should I provide?\n\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/nlp/am1.png]\n\n**Key Takeaway:** Every word creates a Query, Key, and Value vector. The Query searches for the most relevant Keys, and the corresponding Values are combined to produce the final contextual meaning of the word."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "How Attention Works?",
            "explanation": "Excellent! Now that we understand `Query (Q)`, `Key (K)`, and `Value (V)`, let's see how the complete Attention Mechanism works.\n\nLook at the diagram below and follow each step carefully. The Python code also implements the same workflow.\n\n**Step 1: Input Sentence**\n\nThe model receives the input sentence:\n\n`I Love AI`\n\nFirst, each word is converted into an `Embedding Vector`, which is a numerical representation that the model can understand.\n\n**Step 2: Create Query, Key, and Value**\n\nFor every word, the model creates three vectors:\n\n• `Query (Q)` → What information am I looking for?\n\n• `Key (K)` → What information do I contain?\n\n• `Value (V)` → What information should I provide?\n\nFor example, if the model is processing the word `'Love'`, it creates `Q(Love)`, while every word creates its own `Key` and `Value` vectors.\n\n**Step 3: Calculate Attention Scores**\n\nNext, the model compares the `Query` of the current word with the `Keys` of every word using a dot product.\n\nFor the word `'Love'`, the comparison might produce scores like:\n\n`Love → I = 20`\n\n`Love → Love = 30`\n\n`Love → AI = 50`\n\nA larger score means the words are more closely related.\n\n**Step 4: Apply Softmax**\n\nThese scores are converted into probabilities using the `Softmax` function.\n\nFor example:\n\n`[20, 30, 50] → [0.2, 0.3, 0.5]`\n\nNow the attention weights sum to `1`, making them easy to interpret.\n\n**Step 5: Create the Context Vector**\n\nThe attention weights are multiplied by the corresponding `Value (V)` vectors.\n\nThe weighted Values are then added together to create the `Context Vector`.\n\nSince `AI` has the highest attention weight (`0.5`), it contributes the most to the final representation of the word `'Love'`.\n\n**Step 6: Generate the Output**\n\nThe Context Vector becomes the new representation of the word.\n\nThis new representation contains information not only about `'Love'`, but also about `'I'` and `'AI'`.\n\nThe same process is repeated for every word in the sentence, allowing each word to understand the context provided by all the other words.\n\nLook at the diagram below to see how each step flows from the input sentence to the final contextual representation. The Python code implements this exact process step by step.\n\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/nlp/am.png]\n\n\nThe following example demonstrates how Attention works for the sentence `'I Love AI'`.\n\n```python\n# Practical Attention Example\nsentence = [\"I\", \"Love\", \"AI\"]\n\n# Step 1: Generate Embeddings\nI = Embedding(\"I\")\nLove = Embedding(\"Love\")\nAI = Embedding(\"AI\")\n\n# Step 2: Create Query, Key and Value\nQ_love = Query(Love)\n\nK_i = Key(I)\nK_love = Key(Love)\nK_ai = Key(AI)\n\nV_i = Value(I)\nV_love = Value(Love)\nV_ai = Value(AI)\n\n# Step 3: Calculate Attention Scores\nscore_i = Q_love @ K_i          # 20\nscore_love = Q_love @ K_love    # 30\nscore_ai = Q_love @ K_ai        # 50\n\nprint([score_i, score_love, score_ai])\n# [20, 30, 50]\n\n# Step 4: Apply Softmax\nweights = Softmax([20, 30, 50])\n\nprint(weights)\n# [0.2, 0.3, 0.5]\n\n# Step 5: Create Context Vector\ncontext = (\n    0.2 * V_i +\n    0.3 * V_love +\n    0.5 * V_ai\n)\n\nprint(context)\n# Context Vector\n\n# Output\nprint(\"Love pays highest attention to AI\")\n# Love pays highest attention to AI\n\nprint(\"Context-Aware Representation Generated\")\n# Context-Aware Representation Generated```\n\nIn this example, the word `'Love'` compares itself with every word in the sentence. Since `'AI'` receives the highest attention score, it contributes the most to the final `Context Vector`. This is how Transformers understand relationships between words, even when they are far apart in a sentence."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Multi-Head Attention",
            "explanation": "Fantastic! One attention mechanism works well. But researchers wondered: 'What if multiple attention mechanisms worked together?'\n\nImagine five students reading the same sentence. One student focuses on grammar, another focuses on meaning, another looks for relationships between words, another tracks important entities, and another captures the overall context. Each student learns something different from the same sentence.\n\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/nlp/am2.png]\n\n`Multi-Head Attention` works in a similar way. Instead of using a single attention mechanism, a Transformer uses multiple attention heads. Each head learns to focus on a different pattern or relationship in the text.\n\nFor example, one head may connect a pronoun to the correct noun, another may identify subject-verb relationships, while another may capture sentiment or topic information.\n\n`How it works:`\n\n• `Step 1:` The input sentence is converted into embeddings.\n\n• `Step 2:` Each attention head creates its own `Query (Q)`, `Key (K)`, and `Value (V)` vectors.\n\n• `Step 3:` Every head independently calculates its own Attention Scores.\n\n• `Step 4:` Each head generates its own Context Vector by focusing on different relationships in the sentence.\n\n• `Step 5:` The outputs from all attention heads are concatenated (combined) together.\n\n• `Step 6:` A final linear layer processes the combined output to produce the final contextual representation.\n\nAll heads process the sentence simultaneously and their outputs are combined together. This gives the model a richer and deeper understanding of the sentence than a single attention mechanism could provide."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Self Attention Review",
            "Question": "Drag and drop the correct term into each blank.",
            "template": [
              "Q,K,V stands for ____.",
