            ],
            "answers": {
              "blank_1": "Query,Key,Value",
              "blank_2": "Parallel",
              "blank_3": "Context"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Parallel Speed Hack: Positional Encoding",
            "explanation": "Excellent! Multi-Head Attention solved many problems.But now a new challenge appeared.\nUnlike RNNs and LSTMs, Transformers process all words at the same time.\nThis makes them incredibly fast.However, if all words are processed simultaneously, how does the model know their order?\nFor example:\n• Dog bites man.\n• Man bites dog.\nSame words.Different meaning.To solve this, Transformers use **Positional Encoding**.\n**The Fix: Positional Encoding**\nBefore feeding the words into the network, the Transformer injects mathematical sine and cosine waves into the word embeddings. This acts like a 'timestamp' or 'GPS coordinate' for the word, allowing the model to instantly know where the word sits in the sentence without needing to process it sequentially.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/nlp/am3.png]\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "explanation": "After the Attention Mechanism proved successful, researchers took the idea one step further and introduced the Transformer architecture.\n A **Transformer** is a deep learning model designed to understand relationships between words in a sentence using Attention.\n**The Transformer has two main blocks:** \n**1.Encoder** Encoder reads and understands the input text by learning relationships and context between words (e.g., in 'The cat sat on the mat', it understands that 'cat' performs the action 'sat')\n **2.Decoder** Decoder uses that understanding to generate the output one token at a time (e.g., 'Translate Hello to French' → 'Bonjour'); BERT mainly uses the Encoder, whereas GPT models mainly use the Decoder.\n\n**Key Characteristics:**\n•`Parallel Processing:` Analyzes all words in a sentence simultaneously\n• `Self-Attention Mechanism:` Words 'attend' to each other to understand context\n• `Positional Encoding:` Adds position information to word embeddings\n\n**Real-world Impact:**\n• Powers models like GPT-4 and BERT\n• Enables faster training than RNNs\n\n**Common Mistake:**\nForgetting that Transformers require positional encoding to understand word order since they process all words at once",
            "title": "The Transformer Architecture: Encoder & Decoder"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "How Transformers Work: Encoder → Decoder Pipeline",
            "explanation": "Great! We now know that Transformers use Attention to understand text. But how does a Transformer convert an input sentence into a meaningful output? To answer this, let's explore the Transformer architecture step by step.\n\nThe process involves two key components: the **Encoder**, which reads and understands the input, and the **Decoder**, which generates the output based on that understanding.\n\nLet's discuss how it works step by step.\n\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/nlp/am4.png]\n\n**How It Works:**\n\n• `Step 1:` Input text is converted into tokens.\n\n• `Step 2:` Tokens are transformed into embeddings and positional encodings are added so the model understands word order.\n\n• `Step 3:` The Encoder receives these embeddings and applies Multi-Head Self-Attention to learn relationships between all words.\n\n• `Step 4:` A Feed Forward Neural Network further refines the representations.\n\n• `Step 5:` The Encoder outputs contextual representations containing the meaning of the entire sentence.\n\n• `Step 6:` The Decoder receives the previously generated token and applies Masked Self-Attention so it can only see past tokens.\n\n• `Step 7:` Cross-Attention allows the Decoder to focus on the Encoder outputs and access the input sentence meaning.\n\n• `Step 8:` A Feed Forward Network processes this information.\n\n• `Step 9:` A Softmax layer predicts the next token.\n\n• `Step 10:` The process repeats until the complete output sentence is generated.\n\n**Real-World Applications:**\n\n• Machine Translation (Google Translate)\n\n• Chatbots and Virtual Assistants (ChatGPT, Gemini, Claude)\n\n• Text Summarization\n\n• Question Answering Systems\n\n• Code Generation (GitHub Copilot)\n\n• Speech Recognition\n\n• Content Generation\n\n• Document Understanding and Information Extraction"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Transformer Review",
            "Question": "Drag and drop the correct term into each blank.",
            "template": [
              "The Transformer contains an ____ and Decoder.",
