    {
      "id": "M004-nlp-lesson-8-capstone",
      "title": "Capstone Project: End-to-End Sentiment Analysis",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#f59e0b",
        "accent": "#d97706"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Mission: The PR Crisis",
            "explanation": "Welcome to your final project. You have just been hired as the Lead AI Engineer for 'CineStream', a massive movie streaming platform.\n\nOver the weekend, CineStream released a highly anticipated, multi-million dollar original movie. Overnight, 50,000 user reviews flooded the platform. The CEO bursts into your office: *\"Are people loving it or hating it? I need a report in 10 minutes!\"*\n\nYou cannot physically read 50,000 reviews in 10 minutes. You need to build a Machine Learning pipeline that can read the raw text of a review and automatically classify it as Positive (1) or Negative (0). This is called Sentiment Analysis."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Step 1: The NLP Pipeline Recap",
            "explanation": "To solve this, we cannot just throw raw English text at a neural network. We have to build an end-to-end pipeline using everything we have learned in this course.\n\nOur pipeline will look like this:\n**1. Data Cleaning:** Strip out HTML tags, punctuation, and lowercase everything.\n**2. Tokenization:** Break the paragraphs into lists of individual words.\n**3. Word Embeddings:** Convert those words into dense mathematical vectors so the computer understands their meaning.\n**4. Sequence Modeling:** Feed those vectors into an LSTM or Transformer so the AI understands the *order* and *context* of the words.\n**5. Prediction:** Output a final score between 0 and 1.",
            "example": "Raw Text: 'I LOVED this movie!!! <br>' -> Cleaned: 'i loved this movie' -> Tokens: ['i', 'loved', 'this', 'movie'] -> Model -> 98% Positive."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Step 2: Data Preprocessing. You have loaded the 50,000 reviews into a Pandas DataFrame. Use Keras to Tokenize the text and pad the sequences so every review is exactly 100 words long (neural networks require uniform input sizes!).",
            "starterCode": "from tensorflow.keras.preprocessing.text import Tokenizer\nfrom tensorflow.keras.preprocessing.sequence import pad_sequences\n\nreviews = [\"This movie was amazing\", \"I absolutely hated the plot\", \"It was okay\"]\n\n# 1. Initialize the Tokenizer to keep the top 10,000 most common words\ntokenizer = Tokenizer(num_words=____)\n\n# 2. Fit the tokenizer on our text\ntokenizer.____(reviews)\n\n# 3. Convert the English text into sequences of numbers\nsequences = tokenizer.texts_to_sequences(reviews)\n\n# 4. Pad the sequences so they are all exactly 100 numbers long\npadded_data = ____(sequences, maxlen=100)\n\nprint(padded_data[0])",
            "solution": "from tensorflow.keras.preprocessing.text import Tokenizer\nfrom tensorflow.keras.preprocessing.sequence import pad_sequences\n\nreviews = [\"This movie was amazing\", \"I absolutely hated the plot\", \"It was okay\"]\n\n# 1. Initialize the Tokenizer to keep the top 10,000 most common words\ntokenizer = Tokenizer(num_words=10000)\n\n# 2. Fit the tokenizer on our text\ntokenizer.fit_on_texts(reviews)\n\n# 3. Convert the English text into sequences of numbers\nsequences = tokenizer.texts_to_sequences(reviews)\n\n# 4. Pad the sequences so they are all exactly 100 numbers long\npadded_data = pad_sequences(sequences, maxlen=100)\n\nprint(padded_data[0])",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use `Tokenizer(num_words=10000)`, `.fit_on_texts()`, and finally `pad_sequences()` to ensure uniform length."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Step 3: Choosing the Brain (LSTM)",
            "explanation": "Now that our text is converted into numbers, we need to build the Neural Network.\n\nWhy not use a standard, basic Dense Neural Network or Bag of Words? Because sequence matters in reviews! Consider the sentence: *\"The movie was not good, it was terrible.\"* If we ignore the sequence, the AI just sees the word 'good' and might guess Positive. \n\nWe will use an **LSTM (Long Short-Term Memory)** network. As the LSTM reads the review word-by-word, its Cell State will remember the word 'not' and apply that negative context to the word 'good'.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"font-family:sans-serif; text-align:center; max-width:600px; margin:auto; padding:20px; background:#f0fdfa; border-radius:12px; border:1px solid #5eead4;\"><h4 style=\"color:#0f766e; margin-top:0; margin-bottom:15px;\">The Keras Architecture</h4><div style=\"display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:10px; align-items:center;\"><div style=\"background:#ffffff; padding:15px; border-radius:8px; border:2px solid #94a3b8; width:80%; font-weight:bold; color:#334155;\">1. Input Layer (100 Tokens)</div><div style=\"font-size:20px; color:#0f766e;\">↓</div><div style=\"background:#dbeafe; padding:15px; border-radius:8px; border:2px solid #3b82f6; width:80%;\"><strong style=\"color:#1e40af;\">2. Embedding Layer</strong><br><span style=\"font-size:12px; color:#1e3a8a;\">Converts the 100 raw numbers into rich, dense Semantic Vectors (Word2Vec)</span></div><div style=\"font-size:20px; color:#0f766e;\">↓</div><div style=\"background:#fef08a; padding:15px; border-radius:8px; border:2px solid #eab308; width:80%;\"><strong style=\"color:#854d0e;\">3. LSTM Layer</strong><br><span style=\"font-size:12px; color:#713f12;\">Reads the vectors in order, maintaining context and tracking negation</span></div><div style=\"font-size:20px; color:#0f766e;\">↓</div><div style=\"background:#fecaca; padding:15px; border-radius:8px; border:2px solid #ef4444; width:80%;\"><strong style=\"color:#991b1b;\">4. Dense Output (Sigmoid)</strong><br><span style=\"font-size:12px; color:#7f1d1d;\">Squeezes the final LSTM thought into a probability between 0.0 (Hate) and 1.0 (Love)</span></div></div></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Step 4: Build the Model. Translate the architecture diagram above into actual Keras code. Build a Sequential model with an Embedding layer, an LSTM layer, and a final Dense output layer.",
            "starterCode": "from tensorflow.keras.models import Sequential\nfrom tensorflow.keras.layers import Embedding, LSTM, Dense\n\n# Vocabulary size = 10,000. Sequence length = 100.\nmodel = Sequential()\n\n# 1. Add an Embedding layer (input_dim=10000, output_dim=64, input_length=100)\nmodel.add(____)\n\n# 2. Add an LSTM layer with 128 hidden units\nmodel.add(____)\n\n# 3. Add a Dense output layer with 1 neuron and a 'sigmoid' activation function\nmodel.add(____)\n\n# Compile the model for binary classification\nmodel.compile(optimizer='adam', loss='binary_crossentropy', metrics=['accuracy'])\nmodel.summary()",
            "solution": "from tensorflow.keras.models import Sequential\nfrom tensorflow.keras.layers import Embedding, LSTM, Dense\n\n# Vocabulary size = 10,000. Sequence length = 100.\nmodel = Sequential()\n\n# 1. Add an Embedding layer (input_dim=10000, output_dim=64, input_length=100)\nmodel.add(Embedding(input_dim=10000, output_dim=64, input_length=100))\n\n# 2. Add an LSTM layer with 128 hidden units\nmodel.add(LSTM(128))\n\n# 3. Add a Dense output layer with 1 neuron and a 'sigmoid' activation function\nmodel.add(Dense(1, activation='sigmoid'))\n\n# Compile the model for binary classification\nmodel.compile(optimizer='adam', loss='binary_crossentropy', metrics=['accuracy'])\nmodel.summary()",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use `Embedding(...)`, `LSTM(128)`, and `Dense(1, activation='sigmoid')` since we only need to output a single probability for Positive/Negative."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "title": "The Sarcasm Dilemma",
          "description": "Your LSTM model finishes training. You test it by typing a custom review into the console: *\"Oh, brilliant. Another superhero movie where the sky shoots a blue laser. Just what I always wanted. A true cinematic masterpiece.\"*\n\nThe model outputs a score of 0.98 (98% Positive). The CEO sees this and is furious. What went wrong?",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "The model suffered from the Vanishing Gradient problem.",
              "outcome": "Incorrect. LSTMs are specifically designed to prevent the Vanishing Gradient. The problem here is semantic, not mathematical degradation.",
              "score": 0
            },
            {
              "text": "The model failed to understand Sarcasm. It saw strong positive word embeddings ('brilliant', 'masterpiece') and lacked the deep world-context to realize they were being used ironically.",
              "outcome": "Correct! Sarcasm is the final boss of Sentiment Analysis. Standard LSTMs struggle with it because the literal words are positive. Fixing this usually requires upgrading to massive Transformer models (like BERT) that have read the whole internet and understand subtle sarcastic context.",
              "score": 100
            },
            {
              "text": "The learning rate was too high during backpropagation.",
              "outcome": "Incorrect. While a high learning rate can cause instability, it doesn't cause a model to specifically fail at sarcasm. This is a data context issue.",
              "score": 10
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Step 5: The Final Deployment",
            "explanation": "Despite the sarcasm bug, your model achieves an F1 Score of 91% on the testing vault! It is ready for the real world.\n\nYou save the model as an `.h5` file and hand it to the Backend Engineering team. They wrap your AI in a Python Flask API. \n\nNow, every time a user clicks 'Submit Review' on the website, the text is instantly sent to your API. Your AI cleans the text, tokenizes it, runs it through the LSTM, and returns a score in milliseconds. You run all 50,000 weekend reviews through the model. You generate a beautiful pie chart showing the CEO that 78% of the reviews are overwhelmingly positive. You just saved the company thousands of hours of manual reading.",
            "example": "This exact pipeline is how Amazon sorts product reviews, how Twitter flags toxic hate speech, and how banks automatically route angry customer service emails to priority managers."
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "M005_llm_01",
      "title": "Getting Started with LLMs",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "From Models to Language Models",
            "explanation": "Excellent! Before we start learning about Large Language Models (LLMs), let's first understand a few important terms that you'll hear throughout this course.\n\nImagine someone says, 'I'm using a model.' The first question you should ask is, **What kind of model?**\n\nIn Artificial Intelligence, a **Model** is a computer program that learns patterns from data so it can make predictions or generate outputs.\n\nFor example:\n\n• A model can identify whether an email is spam.\n\n• A model can recognize objects in an image.\n\n• A model can predict tomorrow's weather.\n\n• A model can understand and generate human language.\n\nNotice that every model is designed for a particular type of task.\n\nNow let's focus on models that work with human language.\n\nA **Language Model (LM)** is an AI model trained to understand, predict, and generate human language. It learns by reading large amounts of text and predicts the most likely next word or token.\n\nToday, Language Models are commonly divided into two categories based on their size:\n\n• **Small Language Models (SLMs)**\n\n• **Large Language Models (LLMs)**\n\nLet's first understand **Small Language Models (SLMs).**\n\nSmall Language Models are compact language models that require fewer computing resources. Because they are smaller, they consume less memory, generate responses quickly, and can often run directly on laptops, mobile phones, and edge devices.\n\nSLMs are commonly used when speed, low cost, privacy, or offline execution is more important than maximum intelligence.\n\nSome popular Small Language Models include:\n\n• Microsoft Phi-3 Mini\n\n• Google Gemma 2B\n\n• TinyLlama\n\n• SmolLM\n\n• Qwen 2.5 (Small Variants)\n\nCommon use cases of SLMs include:\n\n• Mobile AI applications\n\n• Offline chatbots\n\n• AI assistants running on laptops\n\n• Edge devices and IoT\n\n• Fast document summarization\n\nWe've now learned about one category of Language Models: **Small Language Models (SLMs).** The second category is **Large Language Models (LLMs)**. In the next lesson, we'll explore what LLMs are, how they work, and why they have become the foundation of modern Generative AI.",
            "tip": ""
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is a Large Language Model (LLM)?",
            "explanation": "Excellent! In the previous lessons, we learned about Transformers and Attention Mechanisms. Now let's see what happens when we train a Transformer on an enormous amount of text.This creates a Large Language Model, commonly called an LLM.\n\nA **Large Language Model (LLM)** is a type of AI model trained to understand and generate human language. It learns by reading a massive amount of text such as books, websites, articles, and code.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/llm/in1.png]\nThink of it like a smart autocomplete system. When you type a sentence, the model looks at the words you have already written and predicts what word (or token) should come next.\nFor example:\nInput: 'The sky is'\nPossible predictions:\n• blue (80%)\n• cloudy (15%)\n• green (5%)\n\nThe model selects one of these predictions and then repeats the process again for the next word. By predicting one token at a time, it can generate complete sentences, paragraphs, stories, code, and answers.\nLLMs become powerful because they are trained on huge amounts of text and contain billions of parameters that help them learn patterns, grammar, facts, and relationships between words.\nSome popular Large Language Models include: GPT-4o, Claude 4, Gemini 2.5, Llama 3.1 70B, DeepSeek-R1, Qwen 3.\n**Key Ideas:**\n• LLMs learn from large amounts of text.\n• They generate text one token at a time.\n• They predict the most likely next token based on previous tokens.\n• They can write, summarize, translate, answer questions, and generate code.",
            "html_code": "<iframe style=\"width:100%; min-height:600px; border:none; background:transparent;\" srcdoc=\"<style>body{margin:0;padding:0;display:flex;justify-content:center;align-items:center;min-height:100vh;background:transparent;}</style><div style=&quot;font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif; text-align: center; max-width: 500px; margin: auto; padding: 25px; background: linear-gradient(135deg, #1e1b4b, #311042); border-radius: 16px; border: 1px solid rgba(236, 72, 153, 0.3); box-shadow: 0 8px 32px rgba(236, 72, 153, 0.15); color: #f8fafc;&quot;>\n    <h3 style=&quot;color: #f472b6; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 15px; font-weight: 600;&quot;>Token Probability Simulator</h3>\n    <p style=&quot;font-size: 14px; color: #cbd5e1; margin-bottom: 20px;&quot;>Enter a seed phrase and simulate next-token selection:</p>\n    \n    <div style=&quot;display: flex; gap: 8px; justify-content: center; margin-bottom: 15px;&quot;>\n        <select id=&quot;l1-seed&quot; style=&quot;padding: 10px; background: #0f172a; border: 1px solid #f472b6; color: white; border-radius: 8px;&quot;>\n            <option value=&quot;Artificial intelligence is&quot;>Artificial intelligence is</option>\n            <option value=&quot;Deep learning works by&quot;>Deep learning works by</option>\n            <option value=&quot;Large language models can&quot;>Large language models can</option>\n        </select>\n        <button id=&quot;l1-predict-btn&quot; style=&quot;padding: 10px 15px; background: #ec4899; border: none; color: white; font-weight: bold; border-radius: 8px; cursor: pointer; transition: 0.2s;&quot; onmouseover=&quot;this.style.transform='scale(1.05)'&quot; onmouseout=&quot;this.style.transform='scale(1)'&quot;>Predict</button>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div id=&quot;l1-sentence&quot; style=&quot;font-size: 16px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); padding: 12px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 15px; min-height: 44px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; border: 1px dashed rgba(244, 114, 182, 0.4);&quot;>\n        Artificial intelligence is...\n    </div>\n    \n    <div id=&quot;l1-distribution&quot; style=&quot;text-align: left; display: none;&quot;>\n        <h4 style=&quot;font-size: 13px; color: #f472b6; margin-bottom: 8px;&quot;>Next Token Probabilities:</h4>\n        <div id=&quot;l1-bars&quot; style=&quot;display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px;&quot;></div>\n    </div>\n\n    <script>\n    (function() {\n        const predictions = {\n            &quot;Artificial intelligence is&quot;: [\n                { token: &quot;transforming&quot;, prob: 45 },\n                { token: &quot;evolving&quot;, prob: 25 },\n                { token: &quot;learning&quot;, prob: 18 },\n                { token: &quot;hyped&quot;, prob: 12 }\n            ],\n            &quot;Deep learning works by&quot;: [\n                { token: &quot;adjusting&quot;, prob: 40 },\n                { token: &quot;mimicking&quot;, prob: 30 },\n                { token: &quot;processing&quot;, prob: 20 },\n                { token: &quot;optimizing&quot;, prob: 10 }\n            ],\n            &quot;Large language models can&quot;: [\n                { token: &quot;generate&quot;, prob: 50 },\n                { token: &quot;reason&quot;, prob: 20 },\n                { token: &quot;write&quot;, prob: 18 },\n                { token: &quot;hallucinate&quot;, prob: 12 }\n            ]\n        };\n\n        const btn = document.getElementById('l1-predict-btn');\n        const seedSelect = document.getElementById('l1-seed');\n        const sentenceDiv = document.getElementById('l1-sentence');\n        const distDiv = document.getElementById('l1-distribution');\n        const barsDiv = document.getElementById('l1-bars');\n\n        btn.onclick = function() {\n            const seed = seedSelect.value;\n            const data = predictions[seed];\n            sentenceDiv.innerHTML = '<strong>' + seed + '</strong> <span style=&quot;color: #ec4899; animation: pulse 1s infinite;&quot;>[generating...]</span>';\n            distDiv.style.display = 'block';\n            barsDiv.innerHTML = '';\n            \n            setTimeout(() => {\n                const winner = data[0].token;\n                sentenceDiv.innerHTML = '<strong>' + seed + '</strong> <span style=&quot;background: rgba(236,72,153,0.3); padding: 2px 6px; border-radius: 4px; color: #f472b6;&quot;>' + winner + '</span>';\n                \n                data.forEach((item, idx) => {\n                    const row = document.createElement('div');\n                    row.style.margin = '4px 0';\n                    row.innerHTML = `\n                        <div style=&quot;display: flex; justify-content: space-between; font-size: 12px; margin-bottom: 2px;&quot;>\n                            <span>${item.token}</span>\n                            <span>${item.prob}%</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div style=&quot;background: #0f172a; height: 8px; border-radius: 4px; overflow: hidden; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);&quot;>\n                            <div style=&quot;background: ${idx === 0 ? '#ec4899' : '#a21caf'}; width: ${item.prob}%; height: 100%; transition: width 0.8s;&quot;></div>\n                        </div>\n                    `;\n                    barsDiv.appendChild(row);\n                });\n            }, 600);\n        };\n    })();\n    </script>\n</div>\"></iframe>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Traditional NLP Models vs Modern LLMs",
            "explanation": "Before Large Language Models, most Natural Language Processing systems were designed for one specific task. Modern LLMs, however, are foundation models that can perform many language tasks using the same model through prompting.",
            "blocks": [
              {
                "type": "vsComparison",
                "content": {
                  "left": {
                    "title": "Traditional NLP Models",
                    "points": [
                      "Designed for one specific task (Spam Detection, Sentiment Analysis, Translation).",
                      "Requires a separate model for every new NLP task.",
                      "Relies heavily on manually labeled training datasets.",
                      "Limited context understanding and short memory.",
                      "Often uses rule-based systems, RNNs, LSTMs, or classical ML models.",
                      "Changing tasks usually requires retraining or rebuilding the model."
                    ]
                  },
                  "right": {
                    "title": "Modern Large Language Models (LLMs)",
                    "points": [
                      "A single foundation model performs many NLP tasks.",
                      "Uses prompts instead of training a new model for every task.",
                      "Pretrained on massive amounts of unlabeled internet-scale text.",
                      "Understands long-range context using the Transformer architecture.",
                      "Supports Zero-shot, One-shot, and Few-shot learning.",
                      "Can be adapted using Prompt Engineering, Fine-tuning, or LoRA."
                    ]
                  }
                }
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Foundation Models & Popular Providers",
            "explanation": "Wonderful! If one model can perform many different tasks, what do we call such a model?We call it a Foundation Model.\nA **Foundation Model** is trained on massive amounts of data and can later be adapted for many different applications.Today, many companies build and provide these models lets discuss below:\n\n**Closed API Providers:**\n• **OpenAI:** Pioneers of GPT-3, GPT-4, and GPT-4o. Famous for high intelligence, reasoning, and developers APIs.\n• **Anthropic:** Creators of the Claude series (Claude 3.5 Sonnet). Noted for safety, long context, and superb coding skills.\n• **Google:** Developers of Gemini models. Integrates native multimodality (image/video/audio) and extremely large context windows (up to 2M tokens).\n• **Groq:** A hardware-focused provider using **LPUs (Language Processing Units)**. Instead of designing models, they host open-source models at hyper-fast speeds (hundreds of tokens/second).\n\n**Open Source / Open Weights:**\n• Developed by Meta (Llama), Mistral AI, Qwen, DeepSeek, and Google (Gemma). Users download these model weights to run them privately on their own servers.",
            "html_code": "<iframe style=\"width:100%; min-height:600px; border:none; background:transparent;\" srcdoc=\"<style>body{margin:0;padding:0;display:flex;justify-content:center;align-items:center;min-height:100vh;background:transparent;}</style><div style=&quot;font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif; max-width: 600px; margin: auto; padding: 25px; background: #18181b; border-radius: 16px; border: 1px solid #ec4899; color: #f4f4f5; text-align: left;&quot;>\n    <h3 style=&quot;color: #ec4899; text-align: center; margin-top:0;&quot;>Model Selection Matrix</h3>\n    <p style=&quot;font-size:13px; color:#a1a1aa; text-align:center; margin-bottom: 20px;&quot;>Click on a provider to analyze its engineering trade-offs:</p>\n    \n    <div style=&quot;display: flex; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px; flex-wrap: wrap; justify-content: center;&quot;>\n        <button onclick=&quot;selectProvider('openai', this)&quot; style=&quot;padding:8px 12px; background:#ec4899; border:none; color:white; border-radius:8px; cursor:pointer; font-weight:bold;&quot;>OpenAI</button>\n        <button onclick=&quot;selectProvider('anthropic', this)&quot; style=&quot;padding:8px 12px; background:#27272a; border:none; color:white; border-radius:8px; cursor:pointer; font-weight:bold;&quot;>Anthropic</button>\n        <button onclick=&quot;selectProvider('google', this)&quot; style=&quot;padding:8px 12px; background:#27272a; border:none; color:white; border-radius:8px; cursor:pointer; font-weight:bold;&quot;>Google</button>\n        <button onclick=&quot;selectProvider('groq', this)&quot; style=&quot;padding:8px 12px; background:#27272a; border:none; color:white; border-radius:8px; cursor:pointer; font-weight:bold;&quot;>Groq</button>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div id=&quot;provider-details&quot; style=&quot;background: #242427; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; border: 1px solid #3f3f46;&quot;>\n        <h4 id=&quot;provider-name&quot; style=&quot;margin-top:0; color:#f472b6; font-size:18px;&quot;>OpenAI</h4>\n        <p id=&quot;provider-desc&quot; style=&quot;font-size:14px; color:#d4d4d8; line-height: 1.5;&quot;>Leading general intelligence and agentic workflows. Powers GPT-4o. Great for structured output, tooling, and general coding.</p>\n        <div style=&quot;display:grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap:10px; font-size:12px; margin-top:15px; border-top:1px solid #3f3f46; padding-top:10px;&quot;>\n            <div><strong>Latency:</strong> <span id=&quot;provider-lat&quot; style=&quot;color:#f472b6;&quot;>Medium-Low</span></div>\n            <div><strong>Context Size:</strong> <span id=&quot;provider-ctx&quot; style=&quot;color:#f472b6;&quot;>128k tokens</span></div>\n            <div><strong>Best Use:</strong> <span id=&quot;provider-use&quot; style=&quot;color:#f472b6;&quot;>Complex reasoning, structured JSON</span></div>\n            <div><strong>State:</strong> <span id=&quot;provider-type&quot; style=&quot;color:#f472b6;&quot;>Proprietary APIs</span></div>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <script>\n    const data = {\n        openai: {\n            name: &quot;OpenAI (GPT-4o / GPT-4o-mini)&quot;,\n            desc: &quot;The gold standard for general API capabilities. Excels at reasoning tasks, tool calls (function calling), and maintaining structured outputs. Good balance of cost, performance, and developer tooling.&quot;,\n            lat: &quot;Medium-Low (30-50 tokens/s)&quot;,\n            ctx: &quot;128,000 tokens&quot;,\n            use: &quot;Structured outputs, multi-agent systems&quot;,\n            type: &quot;Closed API&quot;\n        },\n        anthropic: {\n            name: &quot;Anthropic (Claude 3.5 Sonnet)&quot;,\n            desc: &quot;Highly praised by developers for coding, advanced logic, and tone. Claude 3.5 Sonnet displays excellent software engineering capabilities and provides a large context window with precise retrieval.&quot;,\n            lat: &quot;Medium (40-60 tokens/s)&quot;,\n            ctx: &quot;200,000 tokens&quot;,\n            use: &quot;Advanced programming, document analysis, academic research&quot;,\n            type: &quot;Closed API&quot;\n        },\n        google: {\n            name: &quot;Google (Gemini 1.5 Pro / Flash)&quot;,\n            desc: &quot;Built natively multimodal from the ground up, Gemini can ingest and reason across images, audio, and videos directly. Offers a massive context window that easily handles long books or massive code repositories.&quot;,\n            lat: &quot;Low-Medium (50-70 tokens/s)&quot;,\n            ctx: &quot;2,000,000 tokens&quot;,\n            use: &quot;Long repository digestion, video analysis&quot;,\n            type: &quot;Closed API&quot;\n        },\n        groq: {\n            name: &quot;Groq (LPU Inference Engine)&quot;,\n            desc: &quot;Custom silicon LPU technology. Instead of running its own models, it hosts open weight models like Llama 3 and Mixtral. Perfect for building ultra-low latency real-time voice and chat assistants.&quot;,\n            lat: &quot;Very Low (300+ tokens/s)&quot;,\n            ctx: &quot;8k - 32k tokens&quot;,\n            use: &quot;Real-time chat, instant agents, fast pipelines&quot;,\n            type: &quot;Hosting Provider (Open Weights)&quot;\n        }\n    };\n\n    window.selectProvider = function(key, btnEl) {\n        document.getElementById('provider-name').innerText = data[key].name;\n        document.getElementById('provider-desc').innerText = data[key].desc;\n        document.getElementById('provider-lat').innerText = data[key].lat;\n        document.getElementById('provider-ctx').innerText = data[key].ctx;\n        document.getElementById('provider-use').innerText = data[key].use;\n        document.getElementById('provider-type').innerText = data[key].type;\n\n        const buttons = btnEl.parentNode.querySelectorAll('button');\n        buttons.forEach(b => {\n            b.style.background = '#27272a';\n        });\n        btnEl.style.background = '#ec4899';\n    }\n    </script>\n</div>\"></iframe>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Capabilities of Modern LLMs",
            "explanation": "Now let's see why LLMs became so popular.Because Foundation Models learn the fundamental structure of logic and language, they possess a massive array of capabilities out-of-the-box:\n\n• **Content Generation:** Drafting emails, writing essays, or generating creative storytelling.\n• **Summarization & Extraction:** Reading a 50-page PDF and extracting a structured JSON array of key data points.\n• **Code Generation:** LLMs can write complete, production-ready frontend components using modern frameworks like React, Next.js, and Tailwind CSS.\n• **Semantic Reasoning:** Solving logic puzzles, explaining complex topics simply, or identifying the tonal sentiment of a customer review.\n• **Translation:** Seamlessly translating between dozens of human languages, or even translating between programming languages (e.g., Python to Rust)."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Limitations & Risks of LLMs",
            "explanation": "So far, we've seen how LLMs can understand and generate language. But no model is perfect. To use LLMs effectively, we must also understand their limitations, risks, and common failure cases.Let's discuss below.\n• **Hallucinations:** Because they predict the next token probabilistically, they will confidently invent fake facts, fake citations, or fake code libraries if they don't know the answer.\n• **Context Windows:** Models have a strict limit on how much text they can 'hold in their head' at once (ranging from 8k tokens to 2 Million tokens). If you exceed it, they crash or forget early information.\n• **Knowledge Cutoffs:** Their internal knowledge freezes on the day their training data was collected. They do not natively know what happened yesterday.\n• **Stochastic Parrots:** They do not possess true human reasoning or a 'world model'; they are executing highly advanced pattern matching."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Real-World LLM Applications",
            "explanation": "Outstanding! Now let's see how companies actually use LLMs in the real world.\nDevelopers use LLMs to build applications like.:\n\n• **Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG):** Customer support bots that search a company's internal knowledge base and use the LLM to format the answers, bypassing the 'Knowledge Cutoff' limitation.\n• **Autonomous Agents:** AI that doesn't just chat, but takes action. For instance, an Agentic AI Resume Editor can be built to automatically score a user's resume, compare it to a job description, and iteratively rewrite the bullet points to optimize for ATS systems.\n• **Code Assistants:** Tools like GitHub Copilot that live in the IDE, predicting the next block of code and explaining legacy infrastructure.\n• **Data Pipelines:** Automatically cleaning messy, unstructured data logs into clean, queryable SQL databases."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "questions": [
            {
              "question": "What is the primary training objective of autoregressive Large Language Models?",
              "options": [
                "Translating input text into mathematical graphs",
                "Predicting the next token given preceding text context",
                "Comparing document embeddings using Cosine Similarity",
                "Searching indexed web links for matching phrases"
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explanation": "Modern LLMs (like GPT, Claude, Llama) are autoregressive models trained primarily to predict the next token in a sequence of text."
            },
            {
              "question": "Which provider is known for native multi-million token context windows and native audio/video processing?",
              "options": [
                "OpenAI",
                "Groq",
                "Google Gemini",
                "Anthropic"
              ],
              "answer": 2,
              "explanation": "Google Gemini stands out for supporting up to 2 million tokens of context window and processing video/audio natively."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Hands-On: Setting Up API Access",
            "explanation": "Excellent! Now that we understand how Large Language Models (LLMs) work, let's learn how to use them in our own applications.\n\nToday, many companies provide powerful AI models through APIs. These providers can be broadly divided into two categories:\n\n• **Closed-Source Providers:** OpenAI, Google Gemini, Anthropic, Mistral AI, Cohere, etc.\n\n• **Open-Source Model Providers:** Groq, Together AI, Fireworks AI, Ollama (for running models locally), Hugging Face Inference, and others.\n\nAlthough many providers are available, the process of using them is almost the same. You first create an account, generate an API key, and then use that key in your application.\n\nIn this course, we'll use two of the most popular providers:\n\n• **OpenAI** (Closed-Source)\n\n• **Groq** (Provides fast inference for many open-source models such as Llama, Gemma, DeepSeek, and Qwen.)\n\nLet's see how to create API keys for both providers.\n\n`Step 1: Create an OpenAI API Key`\n\n1. Sign up or log in at **[platform.openai.com](https://platform.openai.com)**.\n2. Navigate to the **API Keys** section.\n3. Click **Create new secret key**.\n4. Copy the key and store it safely because it will not be shown again.\n\n`Step 2: Create a Groq API Key`\n\n1. Sign up or log in at **[console.groq.com](https://console.groq.com)**.\n2. Open the **API Keys** page.\n3. Click **Create API Key**.\n4. Copy and securely save the generated key.\n\n`Security Best Practice:`\n\nNever hardcode your API keys inside your Python files.\n\nInstead, store them in a `.env` file:\n\n```python\nOPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...\nGROQ_API_KEY=gsk-...\n```\n\nUse the **python-dotenv** library to load these environment variables automatically into your application.",
            "tip": "Never commit your `.env` file to GitHub. Add `.env` to your `.gitignore` file to keep your API keys secure and prevent unauthorized usage."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "title": "Choosing the API Setup",
          "description": "You are building a voice assistant that needs to respond to the user in less than 200 milliseconds. Which API hosting choice should you prioritize for text generation?",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "Call OpenAI GPT-4o API",
              "outcome": "GPT-4o is highly capable but typically has a latency of 1 to 2 seconds, which is too slow for real-time fluid voice chat.",
