    {
      "id": "M005-llm-lesson-3-huggingfae",
      "title": "Hugging Face Transformers",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Introduction to Hugging Face",
            "explanation": "In this module, we'll focus on the fundamentals of using pre-trained LLMs with Hugging Face. Later in the course, we'll have a dedicated Hugging Face module where we'll explore its complete ecosystem, libraries, and advanced features in detail.\nAlright ! So far, we have learned how NLP models and Large Language Models (LLMs) work internally. But here's an important question—do developers build every AI model from scratch?\n\nThe answer is **No**.\n\nImagine you want to build a chatbot, a text summarizer, or a language translator. Training an AI model yourself would require millions of training examples, powerful GPUs, and a lot of time. For most projects, this is not practical.\n\nThis is where **Hugging Face** comes in.\n\n**Hugging Face** is one of the world's most popular open-source AI platforms. It provides thousands of pre-trained models that developers can use directly in their applications without training them from scratch.\n\n**Why Hugging Face is Important:**\n\n• Provides thousands of ready-to-use AI models.\n\n• Saves time by eliminating the need to train models from scratch.\n\n• Supports popular frameworks like **PyTorch**, **TensorFlow**, and **JAX**.\n\n• Offers easy-to-use libraries for building AI applications.\n\n• Widely used by developers, researchers, startups, and large companies.\n\n**What Can You Build Using Hugging Face?**\n\n• Chatbots\n\n• Text Summarization\n\n• Language Translation\n\n• Question Answering Systems\n\n• Sentiment Analysis\n\n• Text Generation\n\n• Image Generation\n\n• Speech Recognition\n\nToday, Hugging Face is often called the **'GitHub of AI'** because developers can easily discover, share, download, and use AI models created by the community.\n**Example:**\nInstead of spending weeks training a chatbot model, you can download a pre-trained model like BERT or Llama from Hugging Face and start building your application in just a few lines of code.\nIn the upcoming lessons, we'll explore the Hugging Face ecosystem and learn how to use these pre-trained models in our own Python applications.",
            "tip": "Think of Hugging Face as the GitHub or App Store for AI models—developers can easily discover, share, and reuse powerful AI models."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Building Your First Hugging Face Pipeline",
            "explanation": "Excellent! Now that we know what Hugging Face is, it's time to use our first AI model.\n\nOne of the easiest ways to work with Hugging Face models is by using a **Pipeline**. A Pipeline is a high-level API that lets you use pre-trained AI models with just a few lines of code. It automatically loads the model, tokenizer, processes the input, runs the prediction, and formats the output.\n\nBut why do we use Pipelines?\n\nWithout a Pipeline, you would have to manually load the tokenizer, load the model, prepare the input, run inference, and process the output yourself. This requires more code and a deeper understanding of the Hugging Face library. A Pipeline performs all these steps automatically, making it much easier to build AI applications.\n\nLet's discuss why Pipelines are so useful.\n\n**Why Use Pipelines?**\n\n• Beginner-friendly and easy to learn.\n\n• Requires only a few lines of code.\n\n• Automatically loads the correct tokenizer and model.\n\n• Supports many NLP tasks using the same interface.\n\n• Great for quickly building and testing AI applications.\n\nNow that we understand why Pipelines are useful, let's install the required libraries.\n\n**Installation:**\n\n`pip install transformers torch`\n\nAfter installing the libraries, we need to import the Pipeline class into our Python program.\n\n**Import Pipeline:**\n\n`from transformers import pipeline`\n\nNow let's explore some of the common tasks that Pipelines support.\n\n**Common Pipeline Tasks:**\n\n• `text-generation`\n\n• `sentiment-analysis`\n\n• `question-answering`\n\n• `summarization`\n\n• `translation`\n\n• `text-classification`\n\nBefore writing our first program, let's understand the overall workflow of a Pipeline.\n\n**Pipeline Workflow:**\n\nImport Pipeline → Choose Task → Load Model → Pass Input → Get Output\n\nPipelines also provide several parameters that help control how the model generates its output. Let's discuss the most commonly used ones.\n\n**Common Parameters:**\n\n• `model` → Specifies which pre-trained model to use.\n\n• `max_length` → Sets the maximum length of the generated output.\n\n• `temperature` → Controls how creative the generated text should be.\n\n• `top_p` → Controls how many likely words the model can choose from.\n\n• `do_sample` → Enables random sampling instead of always selecting the most likely word.\n\n• `num_return_sequences` → Specifies how many different responses to generate.\n\nNow that we understand the Pipeline and its components, let's build our first AI application using a Hugging Face Pipeline.",
            "example": "from transformers import pipeline\n\ngenerator = pipeline(\n    task='text-generation',\n    model='gpt2'\n)\n\nresult = generator(\n    'Artificial Intelligence is',\n    max_length=50,\n    temperature=0.7,\n    top_p=0.9,\n    do_sample=True\n)\n\nprint(result[0]['generated_text'])\n\nSample Output:\nArtificial Intelligence is a field of computer science focused on building systems that can learn and make decisions.",
            "tip": "Start with Pipelines because they are the simplest way to use Hugging Face models. Once you're comfortable, you can move to `AutoTokenizer` and `AutoModel` for greater flexibility and control."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Exploring Different Pipeline Tasks",
            "explanation": "Excellent! We now know how to create a Hugging Face Pipeline. But can the same Pipeline perform every AI task?\n\nThe answer is **No**.\n\nDifferent AI problems require different types of pipelines. For example, generating a story is very different from translating text or answering questions. Hugging Face provides a separate pipeline for each task, making it easy to use the right model for your application.\n\nLet's look at some of the most commonly used pipeline tasks.\n\n**Popular Pipeline Tasks:**\n\n• **Text Generation** – Generates new text from a given prompt.\n\n• **Summarization** – Produces a shorter version of a long document while preserving the important information.\n\n• **Translation** – Converts text from one language to another.\n\n• **Question Answering** – Answers questions based on the given context.\n\n• **Sentiment Analysis** – Identifies whether the text expresses a positive, negative, or neutral opinion.\n\n• **Text Classification** – Classifies text into predefined categories such as spam detection or topic classification.\n\nEach pipeline is designed for a specific AI task, but they all follow the same simple interface. You only need to change the pipeline task and model to build different AI applications.\n**Example**\nIf you want to generate a blog, use the `text-generation` pipeline. If you want to translate English into French, simply switch to the `translation` pipeline. The coding style remains almost the same; only the pipeline task changes.\nIn the next topic,we will explore how to load pretrained models.",
            "tip": "Think of a Pipeline as a tool, and each Pipeline task as a different attachment. You choose the attachment based on the job you want to perform."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Use a Hugging Face pipeline to perform sentiment analysis.",
            "starterCode": "from transformers import pipeline\n\n# Create sentiment pipeline\n\n# Analyze text\n",
            "solution": "from transformers import pipeline\nclassifier = pipeline('sentiment-analysis')\nresult = classifier('I love learning Hugging Face!')\nprint(result)",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use pipeline('sentiment-analysis').",
            "explanation": "The pipeline automatically loads a pretrained sentiment analysis model."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Loading Pretrained Models",
            "explanation": "Excellent! In the previous lesson, we used a Pipeline without specifying which model to use. Hugging Face automatically selected a suitable model for us. But what if we want to choose a specific model for our application?\n\nTraining a Large Language Model from scratch requires massive datasets, powerful GPUs, and weeks or even months of training. For most developers, this is expensive and unnecessary.\n\nInstead, developers use **pretrained models**. A pretrained model is a model that has already been trained on a large amount of data. Rather than training everything again, we simply download the model and use it in our own application.\n\nHugging Face provides thousands of pretrained models through the **Hugging Face Hub**, making it easy to find and use models for different AI tasks.\n\nLet's discuss why pretrained models are so useful.\n\n**Benefits of Pretrained Models:**\n\n• Save weeks or months of training time.\n\n• Reduce the need for expensive GPUs and computing resources.\n\n• Provide high accuracy because they are already trained on large datasets.\n\n• Can be fine-tuned later for your own custom tasks.\n\nNow let's look at some of the most popular pretrained models available on Hugging Face.\n\n**Popular Pretrained Models:**\n\n• `gpt2` → Text Generation.\n\n• `bert-base-uncased` → Text Understanding and Classification.\n\n• `t5-small` → Translation, Summarization, and Question Answering.\n\n• `distilbert-base-uncased` → A smaller and faster version of BERT.\n\nIn the next example, we'll learn how to load a pretrained model and its tokenizer into Python using the Hugging Face Transformers library.",
            "example": "from transformers import AutoTokenizer, AutoModelForCausalLM\n\nmodel_name = 'gpt2'\n\ntokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(model_name)\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(model_name)\n\nprint('Model Loaded Successfully!')",
            "tip": "Whenever possible, use `AutoTokenizer` and the appropriate `AutoModel` class. Hugging Face automatically downloads the correct tokenizer and model architecture based on the model name you provide."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Understanding Tokenizers",
            "explanation": "Excellent! In the previous lesson, we learned how to load a pretrained model. But before a model can understand our input, there is one important step.\n\nCan we directly pass normal English text like 'Hello World' to a language model?\n\nThe answer is **No**.\n\nLanguage models work only with numbers, not raw text. Before sending text to the model, it must first be converted into a format that the model understands. This is the job of a **Tokenizer**.\n\nA **Tokenizer** converts text into smaller pieces called **tokens** and then converts those tokens into unique numerical IDs. These token IDs are finally passed to the language model for prediction.\n\nLet's discuss the main responsibilities of a Tokenizer.\n\n**Responsibilities of a Tokenizer:**\n\n• Split the input text into smaller tokens.\n\n• Convert each token into a unique token ID.\n\n• Add special tokens when required by the model.\n\n• Prepare the final input that can be understood by the language model.\n\nNow let's understand the overall flow.\n\n**Tokenizer Workflow:**\n\nRaw Text → Tokens → Token IDs → Model Input → Prediction\n\nIn the next example, we'll use Hugging Face's `AutoTokenizer` to convert text into tokens and token IDs.",
            "example": "from transformers import AutoTokenizer\n\ntokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained('gpt2')\n\ntext = 'Hello World'\n\ntokens = tokenizer.tokenize(text)\nids = tokenizer.encode(text)\n\nprint('Tokens:', tokens)\nprint('Token IDs:', ids)\n\nOutput:\nTokens: ['Hello', 'ĠWorld']\nToken IDs: [15496, 2159]",
            "tip": "Always use the tokenizer that matches your model. For example, GPT-2, BERT, and T5 each have their own tokenizer because they tokenize text differently."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "visual",
          "content": {
            "title": "Tokenization Process",
            "diagram": [
              "Raw Text",
              "Tokens",
              "Token IDs",
              "Model Input"
            ],
            "explanation": "Tokenizers act as translators between human language and machine-readable numbers."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Downloading and Running a Pretrained Model Locally",
            "explanation": "Excellent! So far, we've been using models directly from the Hugging Face Hub. But what if you want to download a model and run it on your own computer?\n\nRunning a model locally gives you more control over your application. Your data stays on your machine, you don't need to call an external API for every request, and once the model is downloaded, it can even work without an internet connection.\n\nMany developers use this approach when building AI applications for offline use or when working with private data.\n\nLet's discuss the steps to download and run a pretrained model locally.\n\n**Step 1: Install the Required Libraries**\n\n```bash\npip install transformers torch accelerate sentencepiece\n```\n\n**Step 2: Create a Hugging Face Account**\n\nGo to **https://huggingface.co** and create a free account if you don't already have one.\n\n**Step 3: Accept the Model License**\n\nSome models, such as Meta's Llama models, are gated. Visit the model page, read the license, and click **Accept** to gain access.\n\nExample:\n\nhttps://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct\n\n**Step 4: Generate a Hugging Face Access Token**\n\nGo to **Settings → Access Tokens** and create a token with **Read** permission.\n\n**Step 5: Login from Python**\n\n```python\nfrom huggingface_hub import login\n\nlogin(\"hf_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx\")\n```\n\n**Step 6: Download and Load the Model**\n\nThe first time you load the model, Hugging Face automatically downloads it and stores it in the local cache. Future runs load the model directly from your computer.\n\nOnce the model is downloaded, you can start generating text locally without downloading it again.",
            "example": "from transformers import AutoTokenizer, AutoModelForCausalLM\nimport torch\n\nmodel_name = 'meta-llama/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct'\n\n# Load Tokenizer\ntokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(model_name)\n\n# Load Model\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\n    model_name,\n    torch_dtype=torch.float16,\n    device_map='auto'\n)\n\nprompt = 'Explain Machine Learning in simple words.'\n\ninputs = tokenizer(prompt, return_tensors='pt').to(model.device)\n\noutputs = model.generate(\n    **inputs,\n    max_new_tokens=150,\n    temperature=0.7\n)\n\nresponse = tokenizer.decode(outputs[0], skip_special_tokens=True)\n\nprint(response)",
            "tip": "The first download may take several minutes because the model files are stored on your computer. After that, Hugging Face loads the model from the local cache, so you don't need to download it again."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Running Your First LLM",
            "explanation": "Excellent! We have now learned how to use Pipelines, load pretrained models, and work with tokenizers. It's time to put everything together and run our first Large Language Model (LLM).\n\nThere are two common ways to run an LLM:\n\n• **Using the Hugging Face Inference API** – The model runs on Hugging Face's servers, and you simply send a request and receive the generated response. This is the easiest option because you don't need powerful hardware.\n\n• **Running the Model Locally** – The model is downloaded to your computer and runs using your own CPU or GPU. This gives you more control and allows you to work offline after the model has been downloaded.\n\nLet's understand the common workflow followed in both approaches.\n\n**LLM Workflow:**\n\n• Load the model.\n\n• Load the tokenizer.\n\n• Provide a prompt.\n\n• Generate the output.\n\n• Display the generated response.\n\nAlthough the way we load the model may differ, this workflow remains almost the same for most LLM applications.\n\nIn the examples below, we'll generate text using both the Hugging Face Inference API and a locally downloaded model.\nIn this module, we'll learn just enough Hugging Face to load and use LLMs in our applications. Later, we'll dedicate an entire module to Hugging Face, where we'll explore its libraries, Model Hub, Datasets, Spaces, Inference APIs, fine-tuning, deployment, and many other features in depth.",
            "example": "### Example 1: Using Hugging Face Inference API\n\nfrom huggingface_hub import InferenceClient\n\nclient = InferenceClient(\n    provider='hf-inference',\n    api_key='hf_your_token'\n)\n\nresponse = client.text_generation(\n    model='meta-llama/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct',\n    prompt='Explain Artificial Intelligence in simple words.',\n    max_new_tokens=100\n)\n\nprint(response)\n\n\n### Example 2: Running the Model Locally\n\nfrom transformers import AutoTokenizer, AutoModelForCausalLM\n\nmodel_name = 'gpt2'\n\ntokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(model_name)\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(model_name)\n\nprompt = 'Explain Artificial Intelligence in simple words.'\n\ninputs = tokenizer(prompt, return_tensors='pt')\n\noutputs = model.generate(\n    **inputs,\n    max_new_tokens=80\n)\n\nresponse = tokenizer.decode(outputs[0], skip_special_tokens=True)\n\nprint(response)",
            "tip": "Use the Hugging Face Inference API when you want to get started quickly without downloading large models. Run models locally when you need offline access, greater control, or want to avoid sending data to external servers."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Use the GPT-2 model to generate text from a given prompt using the Hugging Face Transformers pipeline.",
            "starterCode": "# Import required library\nfrom transformers import pipeline\n\n# Load text generation pipeline\n\n# Define input prompt\n\n# Generate text\n\n# Display result",
            "solution": "# Import Required Library\nfrom transformers import pipeline\n\n# Load GPT-2 Text Generation Pipeline\ntext_generator = pipeline(\n    'text-generation',\n    model='gpt2'\n)\n\n# Input Prompt\nprompt = 'Artificial Intelligence is'\n\n# Generate Text\nresult = text_generator(\n    prompt,\n    max_length=50,\n    num_return_sequences=1,\n    do_sample=True\n)\n\n# Display Generated Text\nprint('Input Prompt:')\nprint(prompt)\n\nprint('\\nGenerated Text:')\nprint(result[0]['generated_text'])",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "GPT-2 is a text generation model. Use the 'text-generation' pipeline task.",
            "explanation": "The Hugging Face pipeline API provides a simple interface for using pretrained models. The 'text-generation' task loads GPT-2 and generates new text based on the provided prompt. Parameters such as max_length and do_sample control the length and randomness of the generated output."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "interactive_simulation",
          "title": "Run Your First LLM",
          "description": "Follow the journey of a prompt through a Hugging Face model.",
          "steps": [
