    {
      "id": "M005-llm-lesson-4-working-with-apis",
      "title": "Working with LLM APIs & Model Providers",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "API-Based Models vs Local Models",
            "explanation": "Excellent! Now that we understand how LLMs are trained, a practical question arises—how do we actually use these models in our own applications?\n\nThere are two common approaches. You can either use a model that is hosted by another company through an API, or you can download the model and run it on your own computer or server. Both approaches are widely used in the industry, and the best choice depends on your project requirements.\n\nLet's explore both approaches one by one.\n\n**1. API-Based Models**\n\nIn this approach, the model runs on the provider's servers. Instead of downloading the model, your application sends a request over the internet, the provider processes it, and returns the generated response.\n\nSome of the most popular API providers are:\n\n• OpenAI\n\n• Google Gemini\n\n• Anthropic Claude\n\n• Groq\n\n• Cohere\n\n• Mistral AI\n\n**Why Choose API-Based Models?**\n\n• No need to download or manage large models.\n\n• Easy to integrate with just a few lines of code.\n\n• Access to the latest and most powerful AI models.\n\n• No need to purchase expensive GPUs.\n\n• The provider handles infrastructure, updates, and scaling.\n\n**Limitations:**\n\n• Requires an internet connection.\n\n• API usage is usually charged based on tokens or requests.\n\n• Response time depends on network latency.\n\n• Your data is sent to the API provider, so you should consider privacy requirements.\n\nNow let's look at the second approach.\n\n**2. Local Models**\n\nInstead of sending requests to an external service, you download the model from platforms like Hugging Face and run it directly on your own computer or server.\n\nPopular local models include: Llama, Gemma, Mistral, DeepSeek, Phi, Qwen\n\n**Why Choose Local Models?**\n\n• Your data remains completely private.\n\n• Can work even without an internet connection.\n\n• No API or token usage charges after the model is downloaded.\n\n• Full control over the model, including fine-tuning and customization.\n\n**Limitations:**\n\n• Requires sufficient CPU or GPU resources.\n\n• Large models consume significant memory and storage.\n\n• Initial setup is more complex than using an API.\n\n• Performance depends on your hardware.\n\n**Which Approach Should You Choose?**\n\n• If you're building a prototype or learning LLMs, API-based models are usually the easiest choice.\n\n• If your application requires privacy, offline access, or handles a very large number of requests, running a local model may be a better option.\n\nIn the next lessons, we'll learn how to use both API-based models and locally hosted models in Python applications.",
            "tip": "Many companies start with API-based models to build products quickly. As their applications grow and API costs increase, they often switch some workloads to locally hosted open-source models.",
            "html_code": ""
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Comparing Popular Closed-Source Models",
            "explanation": "Excellent! In the previous lesson, we learned that many companies provide Large Language Models through APIs. But another important question arises—if multiple companies offer AI models, which one should you choose?\n\nToday, several companies have developed their own families of closed-source models. Each company also releases multiple versions of its models with different capabilities, speeds, and pricing. For example, OpenAI offers GPT models, Anthropic offers Claude models, and Google offers Gemini models. Every model family has smaller, faster versions as well as larger, more powerful versions.\n\nChoosing the right model depends on your application's requirements, such as reasoning ability, coding performance, context length, speed, and cost.\n\nLet's compare three of the most popular closed-source models used by developers today.\n\n**1. GPT (OpenAI)**\n\n• Excellent for general-purpose AI applications.\n\n• Strong at reasoning, coding, structured outputs, and function calling.\n\n• Widely used for chatbots, AI assistants, automation, and content generation.\n\n• Popular models include **GPT-4o**, **GPT-4.1**, and **GPT-4o mini**.\n\n**2. Claude (Anthropic)**\n\n• Known for high-quality coding and software development tasks.\n\n• Performs well on long documents and complex reasoning.\n\n• Produces detailed and natural responses.\n\n• Popular models include **Claude 4 Opus**, **Claude 4 Sonnet**, and **Claude 3.5 Haiku**.\n\n**3. Gemini (Google)**\n\n• Designed for multimodal applications, including text, images, audio, and video.\n\n• Supports very large context windows, making it suitable for analyzing long documents and codebases.\n\n• Integrates well with Google's ecosystem and cloud services.\n\n• Popular models include **Gemini 2.5 Pro**, **Gemini 2.5 Flash**, and **Gemini Flash Lite**.\n\nBefore selecting a model, let's understand two important terms.\n\n**Latency**\n\nLatency is the time taken for the model to begin generating a response after receiving your request. Lower latency provides a faster user experience.\n\n**Throughput**\n\nThroughput refers to the number of tokens a model can generate per second. Higher throughput allows responses to be generated more quickly.\n\nThere is no single 'best' model. The right choice depends on your application's needs. Some models focus on better reasoning, some on coding, some on multimodal capabilities, while others prioritize speed and lower cost.",
            "tip": ""
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "OpenAI API Basics",
            "explanation": "Excellent! In the previous lesson, we learned about different closed-source AI models. Now it's time to interact with one of them using Python.\n\nWhen building AI applications such as chatbots, AI assistants, content generators, or automation tools, we don't manually open ChatGPT and type prompts. Instead, our applications communicate directly with OpenAI's servers using the **OpenAI API**.\n\nThe OpenAI API allows your Python application to send a prompt to a GPT model and receive an AI-generated response. To make this process simple, OpenAI provides an official Python SDK that handles the communication for us.\n\nLet's discuss how to get started.\n\n**Step 1: Install the OpenAI Library**\n\nBefore using the OpenAI API, we need to install the official Python package.\n\n`pip install openai`\n\n**Step 2: Import the Library**\n\nAfter installation, import the `OpenAI` class into your Python program.\n\n`from openai import OpenAI`\n\n**Step 3: Connect to OpenAI**\n\nCreate an OpenAI client by providing your API key. This client is responsible for sending requests to the OpenAI servers.\n\n**Step 4: Send a Prompt**\n\nChoose a GPT model and send your prompt using the client.\n\n**Step 5: Read the Response**\n\nThe model processes your request and returns an AI-generated response, which you can display or use in your application.\n\nThis simple workflow is the foundation of almost every application built using the OpenAI API.",
            "example": "from openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI(api_key='YOUR_API_KEY')\n\nresponse = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model='gpt-4o-mini',\n    messages=[\n        {\n            'role': 'user',\n            'content': 'What is Artificial Intelligence?'\n        }\n    ]\n)\n\nprint(response.choices[0].message.content)",
            "tip": "Never hardcode your API key in your source code. Store it in a `.env` file and load it using the `python-dotenv` library to keep your credentials secure."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Sending Prompts",
            "explanation": "Excellent! We can now connect to an LLM using the OpenAI API. But simply connecting to the model isn't enough—we also need to tell it what we want it to do.\n\nThe instruction or input that we send to an AI model is called a **Prompt**. The quality of the prompt directly affects the quality of the response. A clear and detailed prompt usually produces a more accurate and useful answer, while a vague prompt may lead to incomplete or incorrect results.\n\nThink of a prompt as giving instructions to a person. The more clearly you explain your requirements, the better the outcome will be.\n\nLet's understand the basic structure of a good prompt.\n\n**Prompt Formula:**\n\n`Role + Task + Context + Output Format`\n\n• **Role** → Tell the model who it should act as.\n\n• **Task** → Clearly describe what you want the model to do.\n\n• **Context** → Provide any background information that helps the model understand your request.\n\n• **Output Format** → Specify how you want the response to be presented.\n\n**Example Prompt:**\n\n\"You are a Machine Learning Teacher. Explain Neural Networks using simple language and present the answer in bullet points.\"\n\nNow let's look at some common types of prompts.\n\n**Types of Prompts:**\n\n• **Question Prompt** → Ask the model a question.\n\n• **Instruction Prompt** → Give the model a specific task to perform.\n\n• **Role Prompt** → Assign a role, such as teacher, interviewer, or software engineer.\n\n• **Few-Shot Prompt** → Provide one or more examples before asking the model to generate a new response.\n\nAs you continue working with LLMs, you'll discover that writing effective prompts is one of the most important skills for building reliable AI applications.",
            "example": "response = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model='gpt-4o-mini',\n    messages=[\n        {\n            'role': 'user',\n            'content': 'Explain Machine Learning in simple terms.'\n        }\n    ]\n)\n\nprint(response.choices[0].message.content)",
            "tip": "A good prompt is clear, specific, and provides enough context. In general, the better your prompt, the better the model's response."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Understanding Responses",
            "explanation": "Excellent! We can now send prompts to an OpenAI model and receive AI-generated answers. But after sending a request, what exactly does the API return?\n\nThe OpenAI API doesn't return just plain text. Instead, it returns a **JSON response** containing the generated answer along with useful information about the request, the model used, and token usage.\n\nLet's understand the most important fields returned by the API.\n\n**Important Response Fields:**\n\n• **id** → A unique identifier for the API request.\n\n• **model** → The name of the model that generated the response.\n\n• **choices** → A list containing one or more generated responses.\n\n• **message** → Contains the AI-generated response.\n\n• **usage** → Shows how many input and output tokens were used.\n\nAlthough the response contains several fields, in most applications developers only extract the generated text from the response.\n\nThe generated response is usually available inside:\n\n`response.choices[0].message.content`\n\nIn the following example, we'll extract and display the model's generated response.",
            "example": "response = client.chat.completions.create(...)\n\nprint(response.choices[0].message.content)\n\nOutput:\nArtificial Intelligence is a branch of computer science.",
            "tip": "Most of the time you'll use `response.choices[0].message.content` to access the generated answer, while the `usage` field is useful for tracking token consumption and API costs."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Temperature & Max Tokens",
            "explanation": "Excellent! We can now send prompts and receive responses from the model. But can we control how the model generates its answers?\n\nThe answer is **Yes**.\n\nOpenAI provides several parameters that allow us to control the model's behavior. Two of the most commonly used parameters are **Temperature** and **Max Tokens**.\n\nLet's understand each parameter one by one.\n\n**Temperature**\n\nTemperature controls how creative or random the model's responses are.\n\n• **0.1** → Produces more factual, focused, and consistent responses.\n\n• **0.5** → Produces a balanced mix of accuracy and creativity.\n\n• **1.0** → Produces more creative and diverse responses, but the answers may become less predictable.\n\nNow let's look at another important parameter.\n\n**Max Tokens**\n\nMax Tokens controls the maximum number of tokens the model is allowed to generate in its response.\n\nA smaller value produces shorter responses, while a larger value allows the model to generate longer and more detailed answers.\n\nBy combining these parameters, you can control both the creativity and the length of the generated response based on your application's requirements.",
            "example": "response = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model='gpt-4o-mini',\n    messages=[\n        {\n            'role': 'user',\n            'content': 'Write a short poem about Artificial Intelligence.'\n        }\n    ],\n    temperature=0.9,\n    max_tokens=100\n)\n\nprint(response.choices[0].message.content)",
            "tip": "Use a low Temperature (0.1–0.3) for factual tasks such as coding, customer support, and business applications. Use a higher Temperature (0.7–1.0) for creative tasks like storytelling, poetry, and brainstorming."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Complete the Python code to initialize the OpenAI client and generate a basic text completion.",
            "starterCode": "import os\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\n# Instantiate the OpenAI client. It automatically reads the environment variable OPENAI_API_KEY.\nclient = OpenAI()\n\n# TODO: Call chat.completions.create with a system instruction and user prompt using model 'gpt-4o-mini'\nresponse = client.____.____.create(\n    model=\"gpt-4o-mini\",\n    messages=[\n        {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are a helpful software tutor.\"},\n        {\"role\": \"____\", \"content\": \"What is an API key?\"}\n    ]\n)\n\n# Print the response\nprint(response.choices[0].message.content)",
            "solution": "import os\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\n# Instantiate the OpenAI client. It automatically reads the environment variable OPENAI_API_KEY.\nclient = OpenAI()\n\n# TODO: Call chat.completions.create with a system instruction and user prompt using model 'gpt-4o-mini'\nresponse = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=\"gpt-4o-mini\",\n    messages=[\n        {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are a helpful software tutor.\"},\n        {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"What is an API key?\"}\n    ]\n)\n\n# Print the response\nprint(response.choices[0].message.content)",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Access 'chat.completions' and set the second role to 'user'.",
            "explanation": "The OpenAI SDK uses `client.chat.completions.create` to call the completion API, passing the model and a list of messages."
          }
