    {
      "id": "M006-prompt-eng-02",
      "title": "Core Prompting Techniques",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#3b82f6",
        "accent": "#1d4ed8"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Different Prompting Techniques",
            "explanation": "Excellent! So far, we've learned what a prompt is, why writing a good prompt is important, and how to structure prompts using the C-I-I-O Framework.\n\nHowever, writing a good prompt is only the beginning.\n\nDepending on the task you're trying to solve, there are different prompting techniques. Each technique helps the AI in a different way and is useful for different scenarios.\n\nIn this chapter, we'll learn the following prompting techniques:\n\n1. **Zero-Shot Prompting** – Ask the AI to perform a task without providing any examples.\n\n2. **One-Shot Prompting** – Give the AI one example before asking it to perform a similar task.\n\n3. **Few-Shot Prompting** – Provide multiple examples so the AI can learn the desired pattern.\n\n4. **Role Prompting** – Ask the AI to respond as a specific professional or expert, such as a doctor, software engineer, or teacher.\n\n5. **Persona Prompting** – Control the AI's personality, tone, and communication style, such as being friendly, professional, humorous, or motivational.\n\n6. **Format-Constrained Prompting** – Instruct the AI to generate responses in a specific format like JSON, XML, Markdown, CSV, or SQL.\n\nEach technique solves a different problem, and there is no single technique that is best for every situation. As an AI Engineer, choosing the right prompting technique is just as important as writing the prompt itself.\n\nTake a look at the overview below. In the next lessons, we'll explore each technique one by one with explanations, practical examples, and hands-on exercises."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Zero-Shot Prompting: Solving a Task Without Examples",
            "explanation": "Excellent! In the previous lesson, we learned how to write clear and effective prompts. Now let's explore one of the most common prompting techniques used when working with Large Language Models.\n\nImagine your teacher asks you, `'Write a short paragraph about the importance of exercise.'`The teacher doesn't show you any sample paragraph or example. Instead, you use your own knowledge to complete the task.\n\nThis is exactly how **Zero-Shot Prompting** works.\n\nIn Zero-Shot Prompting, you simply tell the AI what task you want it to perform without providing any examples of the expected answer. The model relies entirely on the knowledge it learned during training to generate a response.\n\nIt is called **Zero-Shot** because the AI receives **zero examples** before solving the task.\n\nFor example, if you ask:\n\n `'Summarize the following paragraph in one sentence.'`\n\nThe AI immediately understands the task and generates a summary, even though you never showed it an example of a good summary.\n\nZero-Shot Prompting is simple, fast, and works well for many everyday tasks such as summarization, translation, question answering, brainstorming ideas, or writing emails.\n\nHowever, because the AI receives no examples, it may sometimes choose a different writing style, format, or level of detail than you expected. For more complex tasks, providing examples usually leads to more consistent results.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; background: #0f172a; color: white; padding: 20px; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid #3b82f6;\"><h4 style=\"color: #60a5fa; margin-top:0; text-align:center;\">Zero-Shot Prompting</h4><div style=\"background: rgba(255,255,255,0.05); padding: 12px; border-radius: 8px;\"><strong>Prompt:</strong><br><span style=\"font-style: italic;\">Summarize the following paragraph in one sentence.</span></div><div style=\"text-align:center; font-size:28px; margin:15px 0; color:#60a5fa;\">↓</div><div style=\"background: rgba(96,165,250,0.12); padding: 12px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #60a5fa;\"><strong>LLM:</strong><br>Reads the prompt, uses its existing knowledge, and generates the response.</div><div style=\"margin-top:15px; background:#1e293b; padding:10px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px;\"><strong>Key Point:</strong> No examples are provided. The model solves the task using only what it learned during training.</div></div>",
            "example": "# Zero-Shot Prompt\nprompt = '''\nSummarize the following paragraph in one sentence.\n\nArtificial Intelligence is transforming healthcare by helping doctors diagnose diseases faster and more accurately.\n'''\n\nresponse = llm(prompt)\nprint(response)"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "One-Shot: The Map Maker",
            "explanation": "Excellent! In the previous lesson, we learned about **Zero-Shot Prompting**, where we ask the AI to perform a task without giving it any examples. This works well for many simple tasks, but sometimes the AI may not understand the exact style, format, or type of response we expect.\n\nIn these situations, we can use **One-Shot Prompting**.\n\nImagine you've hired a new employee to write professional emails. Instead of simply saying, `'Write a professional email,'` you first show them `one well-written email` and then ask them to write another email in the same style.\n\nBy looking at just one example, the employee quickly understands the writing style, structure, tone, and formatting you expect.\n\nLarge Language Models work in a very similar way.\n\nIn **One-Shot Prompting**, you provide `one example` of the task along with its correct answer before asking the AI to solve a new problem. The model studies that example, identifies the pattern, and then follows the same pattern when generating the next response.\n\nIt is called `One-Shot` because the AI learns the expected format from `one example`.\n\nCompared to Zero-Shot Prompting, One-Shot Prompting often produces more consistent, better-formatted, and more accurate responses, especially when the output needs to follow a specific style or structure.\nLet's walk through a practical example of One-Shot Prompt Engineering.\n```python\n# One-Shot Prompt\nprompt = '''\nExample:\nQuestion: What is the capital of France?\nAnswer: Paris\n\nNow answer this:\nQuestion: What is the capital of Japan?\nAnswer:\n'''\n\nresponse = llm(prompt)\nprint(response)\n# Expected Output:\n# Tokyo```",
            "example": "",
            "html_code": "<iframe style=\"width:100%; min-height:240px; border:none; background:transparent;\" srcdoc=\"<style>body{margin:0;font-family:'Segoe UI',sans-serif;display:flex;justify-content:center;align-items:center;background:transparent;color:#f8fafc;} .container{background:linear-gradient(145deg, #1e1b4b, #2e1065);border:1px solid #6366f1;border-radius:12px;padding:20px;width:100%;max-width:550px;box-shadow:0 8px 32px rgba(99,102,241,0.2);} .header{text-align:center;color:#a5b4fc;font-weight:bold;margin-bottom:15px;font-size:14px;letter-spacing:0.5px;} .layout{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:12px;} .box{background:rgba(15,23,42,0.8);border:1px solid #4f46e5;border-radius:8px;padding:15px;flex:1;} .prompt-title{color:#f472b6;font-size:12px;font-weight:bold;margin-bottom:10px;text-transform:uppercase;} .code-line{font-family:monospace;font-size:12px;color:#cbd5e1;margin:6px 0;padding:6px;background:rgba(0,0,0,0.3);border-radius:4px;} .highlight{color:#4ade80;border-left:3px solid #4ade80;background:rgba(74, 222, 128, 0.05);} .target{color:#fcd34d;border-left:3px solid #fcd34d;} .arrow{color:#c084fc;font-size:24px;animation:pulse 1.5s infinite;} .result-box{background:rgba(16, 185, 129, 0.1);border:1px solid #10b981;text-align:center;display:flex;flex-direction:column;justify-content:center;height:104px;} .result-title{color:#34d399;font-size:12px;font-weight:bold;margin-bottom:8px;text-transform:uppercase;} .result-value{font-size:20px;font-weight:bold;color:#fff;} .badge{margin-top:10px;background:#10b981;color:#022c22;font-size:10px;font-weight:bold;padding:4px 10px;border-radius:12px;display:inline-block;} @keyframes pulse{0%{transform:translateX(0);opacity:0.5;}50%{transform:translateX(6px);opacity:1;}100%{transform:translateX(0);opacity:0.5;}}</style><div class='container'><div class='header'>🎯 One-Shot Pattern Recognition</div><div class='layout'><div class='box'><div class='prompt-title'>📝 The Prompt</div><div class='code-line'>Task: Convert to color</div><div class='code-line highlight'>Ex: Apple ➔ Red</div><div class='code-line target'>Q: Grape ➔</div></div><div class='arrow'>→</div><div class='box result-box'><div class='result-title'>✨ AI Output</div><div class='result-value'>Purple</div><div><span class='badge'>Pattern Locked ✔️</span></div></div></div></div>\"></iframe>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Few-Shot: The Pattern Architect",
            "explanation": "Great! But what if one example is still not enough?\nImagine you want to train a new employee to handle customer support emails. If you show only one example, they may understand the general pattern but still make mistakes in unusual situations.\nInstead, you show them several examples covering different customer requests and the correct responses for each one. After reviewing multiple examples, they begin to understand the rules, tone, structure, and decision-making process much more clearly.\n\n **Few-Shot Prompting** works in the same way. Instead of providing only one example, you provide a few examples (typically 3 to 5) of inputs and their expected outputs. The AI studies all of these examples, identifies common patterns, and learns how to handle similar tasks more accurately.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/prompt/in1.png]\n`Real Use Cases:` Classifying customer reviews as Positive, Negative, or Neutral, and teaching the AI a specific email format, brand voice, JSON structure, or customer support response style using multiple examples.",
            "example": "# Few-Shot Prompt\nprompt = '''\nExample:\nCustomer Message: \"My internet has been disconnecting every 10 minutes.\"\nCategory: Technical Support\n\nExample:\nCustomer Message: \"I was charged twice for my subscription.\"\nCategory: Billing\n\nExample:\nCustomer Message: \"I want to cancel my premium plan.\"\nCategory: Cancellation\n\nNow classify:\nCustomer Message: \"I forgot my account password and can't log in.\"\nCategory:\n'''\n\nresponse = llm(prompt)\nprint(response)\n# Expected Output:\n# Account Support"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Role Prompting: The Expert Mask",
            "explanation": "Excellent! So far, we've learned how to improve AI responses by giving clear instructions and, when needed, providing examples. But sometimes that's still not enough.\n\nImagine you ask the same question to three different professionals:\n\n**'How can I improve my website?'**\n\nA **Software Engineer** might suggest improving performance, fixing bugs, and optimizing the code.\n\nA **UI/UX Designer** might focus on making the website easier to use and improving the user experience.\n\nA **Marketing Manager** might recommend better content, stronger branding, and improving conversion rates.\n\nNotice something interesting. The question is exactly the same, but the answers are completely different because each person thinks from their own area of expertise.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/prompt/cp1.png]\nLarge Language Models work in a similar way.\n\nWith **Role Prompting**, you tell the AI to behave like a specific professional, expert, or character before giving it a task. This helps the model understand the perspective, knowledge, writing style, and vocabulary it should use while generating the response.\n\nInstead of behaving like a general AI assistant, it begins responding as if it were a software engineer, teacher, doctor, lawyer, financial advisor, marketing expert, or any other role you assign.\n\nRole Prompting is especially useful when you want more professional, realistic, and domain-specific answers. It helps the AI produce responses that better match the expectations of the role you have assigned.",
            "example": "# Role Prompting\nprompt = '''\nYou are an experienced doctor.\n\nExplain the symptoms of dehydration to a patient using simple language and provide basic self-care tips.\n'''\n\nresponse = llm(prompt)\nprint(response)"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Challenge: Write a Zero-Shot Prompt",
            "difficulty": "Easy",
            "question": "Write a prompt that asks the AI to summarize a news article in 3 bullet points without providing any examples.",
            "starterCode": "Write a prompt: ''",
            "language": "python",
            "expected_output": "• Main event\n• Important details\n• Final outcome",
            "hint": "Give only instructions. Do not provide any examples.",
            "solution": "Summarize the following news article in exactly 3 bullet points highlighting the most important information."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Persona Prompting: The Voice Actor",
            "explanation": "Excellent! In the previous lesson, we learned that `Role Prompting` changes the AI's expertise by asking it to think like a specific professional, such as a doctor, software engineer, or marketing manager.\n\nBut what if we don't want to change the AI's expertise? What if we only want to change `how it speaks`?\n\nImagine two people explaining the same topic. One is a `friendly teache`, while the other is a `professional consultant`. Both have the same knowledge, but their way of communicating is completely different. One uses simple and encouraging language, while the other uses a more formal and business-like tone.\n\nThis is exactly what **Persona Prompting** does.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/prompt/cp2.png]\n\nInstead of changing the AI's knowledge or expertise, Persona Prompting changes its **personality, tone, and communication style**. You can ask the AI to respond like a friendly teacher, a professional consultant, a funny friend, a motivational coach, an empathetic listener, or many other personalities.\n\nTake a look at the illustration below. Notice that the **topic remains the same**, but each persona communicates it in a unique way. This helps you choose the communication style that best fits your audience or application.\n\nPersona Prompting is especially useful when building chatbots, virtual assistants, educational applications, customer support systems, or marketing content where the way the AI communicates is just as important as the information it provides.\n\nNow, let's look at the practical code example below to see how Persona Prompting works in practice.",
            "example": "# Persona Prompting\nprompt = '''\nYou are a motivational fitness coach.\n\nGive me advice on exercising every day.\n'''\n\nresponse = llm(prompt)\nprint(response)\n\n# Output:\n# You've got this! Start with just 15 minutes today.\n# Small daily steps lead to big results over time!"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Format-Constrained Prompting",
            "explanation": "Excellent! So far, we've learned how to control the AI's knowledge using examples, its expertise using roles, and its communication style using personas.\n\nBut when building real-world AI applications, developers face another challenge.\n\nImagine you ask an AI to extract information from a customer's message. Instead of returning only the required data, the AI replies with a long explanation like:\n\n'Certainly! Based on the customer's request, here is the information...'\n\nWhile this response is easy for a human to read, it creates a problem for software. Applications, APIs, and AI agents cannot reliably understand long conversational responses. They need data in a fixed and predictable format.\n\nThis is where **Format-Constrained Prompting** becomes useful.\n\nIn Format-Constrained Prompting, you explicitly tell the AI **how the final response should be formatted**. Instead of allowing the model to generate free-form text, you instruct it to return the response in a specific structure such as **JSON, XML, Markdown, CSV, SQL, or a table**.\n\nBecause every response follows the same structure, other applications can easily read, validate, and process the output automatically without manual editing.\n\nFor example, an AI Agent may generate a JSON object, an automation workflow may expect CSV data, or a web application may require Markdown for displaying formatted content.\n\nTake a look at the example below. Notice that the AI returns `only the required JSON data` without any greetings, explanations, or extra text. This makes the response predictable, machine-readable, and ready to be used directly by software.\n\nNow, let's look at the practical example below to see how Format-Constrained Prompting works in real applications.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"font-family: monospace; background: #000; color: #4ade80; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px;\"><span style=\"color: #f472b6;\">{</span><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;\"action\": <span style=\"color: #fbbf24;\">\"send_email\"</span>,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;\"recipient\": <span style=\"color: #fbbf24;\">\"sarah@company.com\"</span>,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;\"urgent\": <span style=\"color: #60a5fa;\">true</span><br><span style=\"color: #f472b6;\">}</span><div style=\"color: #ef4444; font-size: 11px; margin-top: 10px; border-top: 1px solid #333; padding-top: 5px;\">// No conversational text allowed. Only raw JSON data.</div></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Challenge: Write a Few-Shot Prompt",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Write a prompt that classifies customer reviews as Positive or Negative using multiple examples.",
            "starterCode": "Write a prompt: ''",
            "language": "python",
            "expected_output": "Negative",
            "hint": "Provide 3 examples before asking the AI to classify the final review.",
            "solution": "Review: 'Amazing product and great quality.' Sentiment: Positive. Review: 'Terrible experience and poor support.' Sentiment: Negative. Review: 'Fast delivery and excellent packaging.' Sentiment: Positive. Now classify: 'The product stopped working after one day.'"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Project: Agentic Resume Optimizer. Use a Role and Format-Constrained prompt to extract skills from a raw resume bio into a clean list.",
            "starterCode": "from openai import OpenAI\nclient = OpenAI()\n\ndef optimize_resume(bio_text):\n    # TODO: Create a prompt that uses Role Prompting and Format Constraints\n    prompt = f\"\"\"\n    Role: You are a Senior Technical Recruiter.\n    Task: Extract a list of professional skills from the bio below.\n    Format: Output only a valid JSON array of strings.\n    \n    Bio: {bio_text}\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    response = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=\"gpt-4o-mini\",\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}],\n        temperature=0.0\n    )\n    return response.choices[0].message.content\n\nbio = \"Joe has 5 years of experience in Python, React, and AWS Cloud architectures.\"\nprint(optimize_resume(bio))",
            "solution": "from openai import OpenAI\nclient = OpenAI()\n\ndef optimize_resume(bio_text):\n    prompt = f\"\"\"\n    Role: You are a Senior Technical Recruiter.\n    Task: Extract a list of professional skills from the bio below.\n    Format: Output only a valid JSON array of strings.\n    \n    Bio: {bio_text}\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    response = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=\"gpt-4o-mini\",\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}],\n        temperature=0.0\n    )\n    return response.choices[0].message.content\n\nbio = \"Joe has 5 years of experience in Python, React, and AWS Cloud architectures.\"\nprint(optimize_resume(bio))",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Set the role to 'Recruiter' and specify 'Output only a valid JSON array'.",
            "explanation": "By combining Role (expertise) and Format (JSON), you ensure the AI behaves like a backend service rather than a conversational assistant."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "title": "The Professional Email Writer",
          "description": "You need to build a tool for a CEO who is very busy. The CEO wants to send emails that are 'Direct, professional, and never more than 3 sentences.' However, the AI keeps writing long, flowery introductions like 'I hope this email finds you well...'. How do you fix this using the techniques learned?",
          "choices": [
