          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Hands-On: The Math Solver",
            "difficulty": "Easy",
            "question": "Upgrade this standard prompt to use Zero-Shot Step-by-Step Reasoning so the AI reliably calculates the correct final cost of the server infrastructure.",
            "starterCode": "#Write a prompt below in prompt variable",
            "language": "python",
            "expected_output": "The model will break down 3*15, 1*50, 200*0.10, sum them, and apply the 10% discount.",
            "hint": "Add the magic phrase to the very end of the prompt.",
            "solution": "prompt = \"\"\"\nCalculate the total monthly cost for my cloud setup:\n- 3 Web Servers at $15/month each.\n- 1 Database at $50/month.\n- 200GB of storage at $0.10 per GB.\n- A 10% discount on the total.\nWhat is the final cost?\n\nLet's think step by step.\n\"\"\""
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Hands-On: Business Case Analyzer",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Write a Reflection Prompt. First, you have a draft analysis of a business merger. Now, write the prompt that instructs the AI to verify and critique that draft.",
            "starterCode": "#Write a prompt below in prompt variable",
            "language": "python",
            "expected_output": "A balanced review pointing out the hyperbole and asking for risk factors.",
            "hint": "Tell the AI to adopt the role of a critical financial auditor, review the draft for unrealistic claims, and suggest corrections.",
            "solution": "reflection_prompt = \"\"\"\nRole: You are a strict, skeptical Financial Auditor and Risk Analyst.\nTask: Review the following draft business analysis. Identify any overly optimistic claims, logical fallacies, or missing risk factors. Provide a detailed critique and write a corrected, balanced version.\n\nDraft: {draft_analysis}\n\"\"\""
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "title": "The Hasty Analyst",
          "description": "You are building an AI tool to evaluate whether a user's startup idea is viable. Currently, the prompt simply says: 'Evaluate this startup idea and say YES if viable, or NO if not: [Idea]'. Users are complaining that the AI is rejecting great ideas without explanation, and its judgments seem random. How do you fix this architecture using reasoning techniques?",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "Change the prompt to format the output as a JSON object with a 'viable' boolean flag.",
              "outcome": "Incorrect. While JSON helps with parsing, it does not fix the underlying logic issue. The model is still making a massive cognitive leap to a YES/NO answer.",
              "score": 0
            },
            {
              "text": "Inject a Chain of Thought framework. Instruct the model to first list pros, list cons, evaluate market size, and only THEN output a final YES or NO.",
              "outcome": "Correct! By forcing the model to generate intermediate reasoning steps (Pros, Cons, Market) before the final conclusion, you eliminate random guessing and improve accuracy.",
              "score": 100
            },
            {
              "text": "Provide 100 Zero-Shot examples in the system prompt.",
              "outcome": "Incorrect. 'Zero-Shot examples' is an oxymoron. Even if you meant Few-Shot, without reasoning steps, the model will still struggle with novel ideas.",
              "score": 20
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "questions": [
            {
              "question": "Which prompting framework teaches the AI to pause its reasoning, trigger an external tool (like a web search), observe the result, and then continue thinking?",
              "options": [
                "Tree of Thoughts (ToT)",
                "Self-Consistency",
                "ReAct (Reason + Act)",
                "Step-by-Step Generation"
              ],
              "answer": 2,
              "explanation": "The ReAct framework explicitly structures the prompt to loop between Thought, Action (tool use), and Observation."
            },
            {
              "question": "You are using an LLM to solve a complex math equation. To use the 'Self-Consistency' technique, what must you do?",
              "options": [
                "Ask the AI to check its own math in a second prompt.",
                "Run the exact same prompt multiple times and select the most frequent answer (majority vote).",
                "Add 'Let's think step by step' to the end of the prompt.",
                "Ask the AI to generate three different ways to solve the problem and pick the best one itself."
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explanation": "Self-Consistency relies on generating a diverse set of reasoning paths via multiple executions and aggregating the results (majority vote) to find the most robust answer."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "M006-prompt-eng-04",
      "title": "Structured Output Generation",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#f59e0b",
        "accent": "#d97706"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Babel Problem: Why Standard Text Fails",
            "explanation": "Excellent! So far, we've learned how to write prompts that help Large Language Models generate accurate and useful responses. These responses are great for humans to read, but real-world software applications have different requirements.\n\nImagine you're building an AI-powered application that extracts customer information and stores it in a database. You ask the AI to return a customer's age.\n\nInstead of replying with only the value **28**, the AI responds:\n\n\"Sure! The customer is 28 years old. Let me know if you need anything else!\"\n\nWhile this answer is perfectly understandable to a person, it creates a problem for software. Applications, databases, APIs, and automation workflows expect data in a fixed structure. Extra conversational text makes the response difficult to process and may even cause errors.\n\nThis is why **Structured Output Generation** is so important.\n\nInstead of allowing the AI to respond freely, we instruct it to return data in a predefined structure such as **JSON, XML, Markdown, CSV, or other machine-readable formats**. Because every response follows the same structure, software can reliably parse the data without complicated text processing.\n\nTake a look at the illustration below. On the left, the AI returns conversational text along with the data, making it difficult for software to use. On the right, the AI returns only the structured JSON object. This clean, predictable format allows applications to directly consume the response without any extra processing.\n\nStructured Outputs are widely used in AI Agents, APIs, chatbots, workflow automation, frontend applications, backend services, and database systems because they make AI responses reliable, predictable, and easy to integrate.\n\n Now, let's explore different techniques for generating structured outputs and see how they are used in real-world AI applications.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; background: #fffbeb; border: 1px solid #fcd34d; padding: 20px; border-radius: 12px;\"><div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 20px;\"><div style=\"background: #fef2f2; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #ef4444;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 14px; color: #991b1b;\">Conversational (Breaks Code)</strong><p style=\"font-size: 12px; margin-top: 8px; color: #7f1d1d; font-family: monospace;\">\"Here is the data you requested:\\n{\\n 'name': 'Alice',\\n 'role': 'Dev'\\n}\\nHope this helps!\"</p></div><div style=\"background: #ecfdf5; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #10b981;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 14px; color: #065f46;\">Structured (Code Safe)</strong><p style=\"font-size: 12px; margin-top: 8px; color: #064e3b; font-family: monospace;\">{\n  \"name\": \"Alice\",\n  \"role\": \"Dev\"\n}</p></div></div><p style=\"font-size: 12px; margin-top: 15px; color: #b45309; font-weight: bold; text-align: center;\">The goal is zero conversational wrappers.</p></div>",
            "example": "# Structured Output Example\nfrom langchain_core.prompts import PromptTemplate\n\nprompt = PromptTemplate(\n    input_variables=[\"text\"],\n    template=\"\"\"Extract the person's information from the following text.\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON.\n\nText:\n{text}\"\"\"\n)\n\nchain = prompt | llm\n\nresponse = chain.invoke({\n    \"text\": \"Alice is a 28-year-old Software Engineer living in Pune.\"\n}).content\n\nprint(response)\n\n# Expected Output:\n# {\n#   \"name\": \"Alice\",\n#   \"age\": 28,\n#   \"profession\": \"Software Engineer\",\n#   \"city\": \"Pune\"\n# }"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "JSON Output & Schema-Based Prompting",
            "explanation": "Excellent! In the previous lesson, we learned why structured outputs are important for AI applications. Returning data in a predictable format makes it much easier for software systems to process AI responses.\n\nHowever, simply telling the model **'Return the output as JSON'** is often not enough. The model might still include extra explanations, greetings, or unexpected fields, making the response difficult for applications to parse.\n\nTo solve this problem, developers use **Schema-Based Prompting**.\n\nA **schema** is a blueprint that defines exactly what the output should look like. It specifies the field names, their data types (such as string, integer, boolean, or array), and the overall structure of the response. Instead of allowing the model to decide the format, the schema clearly tells it what information to return.\n\nFor example, if you're extracting information from a resume, the schema might define fields like **candidate_name**, **ats_score**, and **is_qualified**. The AI then generates data that matches this predefined structure.\n\nMany modern LLM APIs also provide features such as **JSON Mode** or **Structured Output**, which work together with your schema to encourage the model to produce valid JSON responses.\n\nTake a look at the example below. Notice how the schema acts as a template that guides the AI to generate only the required fields in the expected format.\n\nSchema-Based Prompting is widely used in AI Agents, resume screening, document extraction, customer support automation, workflow automation, APIs, and backend systems where applications need predictable and machine-readable data.\n\n**Now, let's look at the practical code example below to see how Schema-Based Prompting works in practice.**",
            "tip": "Always inject a blank JSON template directly into your prompt. Don't make the AI guess what keys your database is expecting.",
            "example": "```python\n# Schema Blueprinting Example\n\nsystem_prompt = \"\"\"\nExtract the data using strictly this schema:\n{\n  \"candidate_name\": \"string\",\n  \"ats_score\": 0,\n  \"is_qualified\": true\n}\n\nRule: Return ONLY valid JSON.\n\"\"\"\n\nuser_prompt = \"Resume: John Smith has 5 years of Python experience and matches most job requirements.\"\n\nresponse = llm.invoke([\n    {\n        \"role\": \"system\",\n        \"content\": system_prompt\n    },\n    {\n        \"role\": \"user\",\n        \"content\": user_prompt\n    }\n])\n\nprint(response)\n```"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Markdown and XML: Choosing the Right Output Format",
            "explanation": "Excellent! In the previous lesson, we learned how to generate structured JSON outputs using schemas. JSON is one of the most popular formats because it is easy for applications and APIs to process.\n\nHowever, JSON is not the only structured output format. Different applications require different formats depending on how the AI's response will be used.\n\nTwo other commonly used formats are **Markdown** and **XML**.\n\n**Markdown** is designed for displaying rich, human-readable content. It allows you to create headings, bullet lists, tables, links, images, code blocks, and formatted text. If you're building a chatbot, documentation website, blog, React application, or Next.js application, Markdown is often the best choice because it can be rendered directly into a well-formatted user interface.\n\n**XML (Extensible Markup Language)** is another structured format that represents information using opening and closing tags. Although many modern applications prefer JSON, XML is still widely used in enterprise software, banking systems, healthcare applications, SOAP web services, and other legacy systems where XML remains the standard.\n\nTake a look at the examples below. Notice that the same information can be represented using different formats depending on the requirements of the application.\n\nAs an AI Engineer, choosing the correct output format is just as important as generating the correct information. The best format depends on the system that will consume the AI's response.\n\n**Now, let's look at a practical example of generating Markdown and XML outputs using prompt engineering.**",
            "tip": "Choose the output format based on your application's needs. Use JSON for APIs and structured data, Markdown for user interfaces and documentation, and XML when integrating with enterprise or legacy systems.",
            "example": "# Markdown Output\nprompt = '''\nSummarize the following meeting notes.\n\nReturn the response in Markdown using:\n- A heading\n- A short summary\n- A bullet list of action items\n'''\n\nresponse = llm.invoke(prompt)\nprint(response)\n\n# Expected Output:\n# ## Meeting Summary\n# The team discussed the upcoming product launch.\n#\n# ### Action Items\n# - Finalize the UI design.\n# - Complete API testing.\n# - Schedule the client demo.\n\n\n# XML Output\nprompt = '''\nSummarize the meeting.\n\nReturn ONLY XML using this structure:\n\n<meeting>\n    <summary></summary>\n    <actions>\n        <item></item>\n    </actions>\n</meeting>\n'''\n\nresponse = llm.invoke(prompt)\nprint(response)\n\n# Expected Output:\n# <meeting>\n#   <summary>The team discussed the upcoming product launch.</summary>\n#   <actions>\n#     <item>Finalize the UI design.</item>\n#     <item>Complete API testing.</item>\n#     <item>Schedule the client demo.</item>\n#   </actions>\n# </meeting>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Function Calling: Letting the AI Use Your Functions",
            "explanation": "Excellent! In the previous lessons, we learned how to generate structured outputs such as JSON, Markdown, and XML. Structured outputs help the AI return information in a predictable format.\n\nBut what if the user doesn't just want information? What if they want the AI to perform an action?\n\nImagine a user asks:\n\n**'What's the weather in Delhi?'**\n\nA Large Language Model doesn't know the current weather because it cannot automatically access live data. Instead of guessing or hallucinating an answer, the model should use a weather service.\n\nThis is where **Function Calling** becomes useful.\n\nFunction Calling allows you to give the LLM a list of functions that your application can execute. Each function has a name, a description, and the parameters it expects.\n\nWhen the user asks a question, the model first decides whether one of the available functions can solve the request. If a function is needed, the model **does not execute it**. Instead, it generates a structured function call containing the function name and the required arguments.\n\nYour application receives this function call, executes the actual code, collects the result, and sends that result back to the model. Using the returned information, the LLM generates the final response for the user.\n\nTake a look at the illustration below. Notice that the LLM acts like a decision-maker. It decides which function should be used, your application performs the actual work, and then the result is returned to the model before the final answer is generated.\n\nFunction Calling is widely used in AI Agents to interact with weather APIs, databases, payment systems, calendars, email services, inventory systems, search engines, and many other external tools.\n\n**Now, let's look at a practical code example below to see how Function Calling works in practice.**",
            "html_code": "<iframe style=\"width:100%; min-height:260px; border:none; background:transparent;\" srcdoc=\"<style>body{margin:0;font-family:'Segoe UI',sans-serif;background:transparent;display:flex;justify-content:center;align-items:center;color:#f8fafc;}.box{background:linear-gradient(145deg, #1e1b4b, #2e1065);border:1px solid #6366f1;border-radius:12px;padding:20px;width:100%;max-width:600px;box-shadow:0 8px 32px rgba(99,102,241,0.2);text-align:center;}.title{margin:0 0 20px 0;color:#a5b4fc;font-size:16px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:0.5px;}.flow{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:12px;}.step{background:rgba(15,23,42,0.8);border:1px solid #4f46e5;border-radius:8px;padding:16px;width:30%;position:relative;transition:transform 0.3s;}.step:hover{transform:translateY(-5px);}.step h4{margin:0 0 8px 0;color:#c084fc;font-size:14px;}.step p{margin:0;font-size:12px;color:#94a3b8;line-height:1.4;}.arrow{color:#ec4899;font-weight:bold;font-size:24px;animation:pulse 1.5s infinite;}@keyframes pulse{0%{transform:translateX(0);opacity:0.4;}50%{transform:translateX(6px);opacity:1;}100%{transform:translateX(0);opacity:0.4;}}.llm{border-color:#ec4899;background:rgba(236,72,153,0.1);box-shadow:inset 0 0 15px rgba(236,72,153,0.1);}.llm h4{color:#f472b6;}.code{background:#020617;padding:6px;border-radius:4px;color:#4ade80;font-family:monospace;font-size:10px;display:block;margin-top:10px;border:1px solid #334155;}</style><div class='box'><div class='title'>⚡ Function Calling Execution Flow</div><div class='flow'><div class='step'><h4>🧑‍💻 1. User Prompt</h4><p>&quot;What's the weather like in Delhi?&quot;</p></div><div class='arrow'>→</div><div class='step llm'><h4>🧠 2. LLM Decision</h4><p>Suspends generation &amp; requests a tool:</p><span class='code'>get_weather({&quot;loc&quot;: &quot;Delhi&quot;})</span></div><div class='arrow'>→</div><div class='step'><h4>⚙️ 3. Application</h4><p>Executes local code, gets live data, and returns it to LLM.</p></div></div></div>\"></iframe>",
            "example": "# Function Calling Example\nfrom langchain_core.tools import tool\n\n@tool\ndef get_weather(location: str):\n    \"\"\"Returns the current weather for a location.\"\"\"\n    return f\"The current weather in {location} is 31°C and Sunny.\"\n\n# User Question\nquestion = \"What's the weather in Delhi?\"\n\n# LLM decides to call:\n# get_weather(location=\"Delhi\")\n\nresult = get_weather.invoke({\"location\": \"Delhi\"})\n\nprint(result)\n\n# Expected Output:\n# The current weather in Delhi is 31°C and Sunny."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
