    {
      "id": "M007-vector-db-01",
      "title": "From Keywords to Semantic Search",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#0ea5e9",
        "accent": "#0369a1"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Why Traditional Keyword Search Fails",
            "explanation": "Before we learn Semantic Search, let's think about how we normally search for information.Imagine you visit an online store and search for:\"car insurance\"Now suppose the website contains an article that talks about:\"automobile coverage\"As humans, we can immediately understand that both phrases are talking about the same thing.\nBut a traditional search system does not think like a human.It simply checks whether the exact words typed by the user are present in the document.\nSince the words 'car' and 'automobile' are different, the search system may fail to show the correct result.\nThis creates our first problem.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/vectordb/in1.png]\n### Problem 1: Different Words, Same Meaning\nPeople often use different words to express the same idea.\nFor example:\n• Car = Automobile\n• Baby = Infant\n• Clothes = Apparel\nEven though the meaning is the same, a keyword-based search system treats them as completely different words.As a result, useful information can be missed.\nNow let's look at another problem.\n### Problem 2: Same Word, Different Meaning\nThink about the word:\n'Apple'\nWhat comes to your mind?\nSome people may think about the fruit.\nOthers may think about the technology company.\nNow imagine a user searches for:\n'Apple bank account'\nA traditional keyword search system only sees the word 'Apple'. It does not understand the context behind the search.Because of this, it may return irrelevant results.\n### So What Is The Main Problem?\nTraditional search only focuses on:'What words were typed?'\nIt does not understand:'What does the user actually mean?'\n\nAnd this limitation is exactly why modern systems moved toward Semantic Search.\nIn the next section, we'll learn how Semantic Search understands meaning instead of just matching words.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; background: #0f172a; color: white; padding: 20px; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid #0ea5e9;\"><h4 style=\"color: #38bdf8; margin-top:0;\">Keyword Search Deficit Visualizer</h4><div style=\"background: rgba(239, 68, 68, 0.1); padding: 12px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #ef4444; margin-bottom: 10px;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 12px;\">Query:</strong> <span style=\"font-family: monospace;\">\"infant apparel\"</span></div><div style=\"background: rgba(255,255,255,0.05); padding: 12px; border-radius: 8px;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #94a3b8;\">Database Document:</strong><p style=\"margin: 4px 0 0 0; font-size: 13px;\">\"Premium 100% organic cotton outfits for babies and newborns.\"</p></div><div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 12px 0; color: #ef4444; font-weight: bold; font-size: 13px;\">❌ 0% Keyword Overlap -> Zero Results Surfaced</div><div style=\"font-size: 11px; color: #94a3b8;\">Lexical analysis fails because 'infant/baby' and 'apparel/outfit' have entirely separate character tokens.</div></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is Semantic Search?",
            "explanation": "In the previous section, we discovered an important problem.Traditional search can only match words.But humans search using meaning.\nSo the question becomes:How can a computer understand meaning instead of simply matching words?\nThe answer is Semantic Search.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/vectordb/in.png]\n**Semantic Search** focuses on understanding meaning rather than matching exact words. It converts both queries and documents into vector embeddings and compares their similarity. Because it understands concepts, a search for 'car' can also find documents about 'automobiles' or 'vehicles' even when the exact keyword is not present.\n\n The similarity between vectors is commonly measured using Cosine Similarity:\n\n **Cosine Similarity (cos(θ))** = `(A · B) / (||A|| × ||B||)`\n\n Higher similarity scores indicate that two pieces of text have similar meanings, making semantic search the foundation of modern search engines, recommendation systems, and RAG applications.",
            "example": "from sklearn.metrics.pairwise import cosine_similarity\n\nvector_a = [[1, 1, 1]]\nvector_b = [[1, 1, 0]]\n\nsimilarity = cosine_similarity(vector_a, vector_b)\nprint('Cosine Similarity:', similarity[0][0])"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Keyword Search vs Semantic Search",
            "explanation": "Before the rise of AI-powered search systems, most applications relied on **Keyword Search**, which looks for exact words in a document. Modern AI systems use **Semantic Search**, which understands the meaning and context of a query instead of matching exact keywords. This allows users to find relevant information even when different words or phrases are used.",
            "blocks": [
              {
                "type": "vsComparison",
                "content": {
                  "left": {
                    "title": "Keyword Search",
                    "points": [
                      "Matches exact words or phrases in the query.",
                      "Does not understand the meaning or context of the sentence.",
                      "Requires the search term to exist in the document.",
                      "Fast and simple to implement using SQL or search engines.",
                      "Works well for product IDs, names, codes, and exact text matching.",
                      "May miss relevant documents that use different words with the same meaning."
                    ]
                  },
                  "right": {
                    "title": "Semantic Search",
                    "points": [
                      "Understands the meaning and intent of the user's query.",
                      "Uses embeddings to compare semantic similarity.",
                      "Can retrieve relevant documents even when keywords are different.",
                      "Provides more accurate results for natural language questions.",
                      "Widely used in RAG systems, AI chatbots, and intelligent search engines.",
                      "Requires embedding models and a Vector Database for retrieval."
                    ]
                  }
                }
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Real-World Industrial Architectures",
            "explanation": "At this point you might be wondering:'Is Semantic Search really used in real applications?'The answer is yes.In fact, many popular products that we use every day depend on this idea.\nLet's look at a few examples.\n\n1. **Google:** Uses models like BERT and MUM to interpret search intentions, ensuring query modifications or typos still fetch highly contextual web pages.\n2. **ChatGPT:** Relies on semantic overlap to query internal knowledge graphs and provide relevant response fragments.\n3. **Netflix & Amazon:** Track user behaviors and item descriptions. If a user watches gritty sci-fi thrillers, the system vectors that taste and surfaces items with highly aligned content vectors, even if the keywords differ entirely from previous items.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; background: #111827; color: white; padding: 20px; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid #1f2937;\"><h3 style=\"color: #f97316; margin-top:0; font-size: 16px;\">🎬 Netflix Production Paradigm</h3><div style=\"display: flex; gap: 10px; margin-top: 10px;\"><div style=\"background: #1e293b; padding: 10px; border-radius: 6px; flex: 1; border: 1px solid #334155;\"><b style=\"color: #38bdf8; font-size: 12px;\">User History Profile</b><p style=\"margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 11px; color: #cbd5e1;\">Watched dark space psychological shows.</p></div><div style=\"background: #1e293b; padding: 10px; border-radius: 6px; flex: 1; border: 1px solid #334155;\"><b style=\"color: #a855f7; font-size: 12px;\">Recommendation Vector Engine</b><p style=\"margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 11px; color: #cbd5e1;\">Fetches deep-space mind benders without matching words.</p></div></div></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Introduction to Embeddings",
            "explanation": "So far we have learned that Semantic Search understands meaning.But an important question still remains.How can a computer understand meaning?After all, computers do not understand language like humans.\nFor a computer, text is simply a collection of characters\nTo work with meaning, the computer first needs to convert text into numbers.This is where Embeddings come into the picture.\n**Embeddings:**\nAn embedding is an array of numbers (a vector) generated by a deep learning model. Each position in that array represents a hidden dimensions of meaning. Typical industrial embedding models (like OpenAI's `text-embedding-3-small` or open-source HuggingFace models) generate arrays with 384, 768, or 1536 decimal values.\n\nWhen you pass text through an embedding model, the model extracts semantic attributes, positioning the text in a high-dimensional mathematical matrix. This vector array is then saved inside specialized index engines known as **Vector Databases**.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"background: #090d16; padding: 20px; border-radius: 12px; font-family: monospace; color: #4ade80; border: 1px solid #1e293b;\"><div style=\"color: #94a3b8; font-size: 12px;\"># Pipeline Architecture: Generating a Vector Embedding</div><div>text_input = <span style=\"color: #fbbf24;\">\"Machine Learning for Vector Databases\"</span></div><div style=\"color: #38bdf8; margin: 8px 0;\">=== PASSING THROUGH EMBEDDING MODEL ===</div><div>embedding_vector = <span style=\"color: #f472b6;\">[0.0142, -0.0891, 0.3121, ..., 0.0054]</span></div><div style=\"color: #64748b; font-size: 11px; margin-top: 10px; border-top: 1px solid #1e293b; padding-top: 5px;\">// Vector length: 1536 floating-point values capturing raw meaning.</div></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
