      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Why Vector Comparison Matters",
            "explanation": "In the previous lesson, we learned that text is converted into embeddings.We also learned that similar meanings produce similar vectors.\nNow let's think about an important question.\nSuppose a user searches for example 'React Developer'The search query is converted into a vector.Your database may already contain thousands or even millions of vectors created from resumes, documents, or products.\nNow the system must answer a simple question:\n`'Which vectors are most similar to the user's query?'` To answer that question, we need a way to compare vectors.\nJust like we compare exam scores to find the top student, AI systems compare vectors to find the closest match.The process of measuring how close two vectors are is called Similarity Search.\nTo perform Similarity Search, we use mathematical techniques called Distance Metrics.\nThese metrics help us determine whether two vectors represent similar meanings or completely different meanings.\n\nIn this lesson, we will learn the most common distance metrics used in modern Vector Databases.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; background: #09090b; color: white; padding: 24px; border-radius: 16px; border: 1px solid #27272a; box-shadow: 0 10px 30px rgba(0,0,0,0.5);\"><h4 style=\"color: #f59e0b; margin-top:0; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 1.2em;\">🎯</span> The Matching Engine</h4><div style=\"background: #18181b; border: 1px solid #3f3f46; padding: 16px; border-radius: 12px; margin-top: 15px;\"><div style=\"color: #a78bfa; font-family: monospace; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 12px;\">QUERY_VECTOR = \"React Developer\"</div><div style=\"display: grid; gap: 10px;\"><div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; background: rgba(16, 185, 129, 0.1); border: 1px solid rgba(16, 185, 129, 0.3); padding: 10px 15px; border-radius: 8px;\"><span style=\"color: #d4d4d8; font-size: 13px;\">Resume_442 (UI Engineer)</span><span style=\"color: #34d399; font-weight: bold; font-family: monospace;\">Sim: 0.92 ✅</span></div><div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; background: rgba(239, 68, 68, 0.1); border: 1px solid rgba(239, 68, 68, 0.3); padding: 10px 15px; border-radius: 8px;\"><span style=\"color: #d4d4d8; font-size: 13px;\">Resume_891 (Chef)</span><span style=\"color: #f87171; font-weight: bold; font-family: monospace;\">Sim: 0.05 ❌</span></div></div></div></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Euclidean Distance and Manhattan Distance",
            "explanation": "Excellent! In the previous lesson, we learned that embeddings are stored inside a Vector Space. But how does AI determine whether two vectors are close to each other?\n\nTo answer this, we use **distance metrics**. A distance metric is simply a mathematical way of measuring how far apart two vectors are.\n\nTwo of the most common distance metrics are **Euclidean Distance** and **Manhattan Distance**.\n\n### 1. Euclidean Distance (Straight-Line Distance)\n\nImagine you and your friend are standing at two different locations on a map. If you use a ruler to measure the shortest straight-line distance between both locations, you are measuring the **Euclidean Distance**.\n\nThe formula is:\n\nDistance = `√((q₁ − p₁)² + (q₂ − p₂)² + ... + (qₙ − pₙ)²)`\n\nThis metric measures the shortest possible path between two points.\n\n### 2. Manhattan Distance (City Block Distance)\n\nNow imagine you're driving through a city where roads form a grid, like Manhattan in New York. You cannot drive diagonally through buildings. Instead, you must travel along the streets by moving horizontally and vertically.\n\nThe total distance you travel is called the **Manhattan Distance**.\n\nThe formula is:\n\nDistance = `|q₁ − p₁| + |q₂ − p₂| + ... + |qₙ − pₙ|`\n\nUnlike Euclidean Distance, Manhattan Distance measures the total path along the grid instead of the straight-line distance.\n\nTake a look at the example below. Both metrics use the same two points, but because they measure distance differently, they produce different results.\n\nAlthough both metrics are useful in mathematics, robotics, logistics, and clustering problems, they are generally **not the preferred choice for comparing text embeddings**. This is because both are affected by the length (magnitude) of vectors. In the next lesson, we'll learn **Cosine Similarity**, which compares the direction of vectors instead of their length and is the most widely used similarity metric for semantic search and RAG systems.\n\n**Simple Rule:**\n\n• Euclidean Distance → Measures the shortest straight-line distance.\n• Manhattan Distance → Measures the distance traveled along horizontal and vertical paths.\n• Smaller Distance → More Similar\n• Larger Distance → Less Similar",
            "example": "Point A = (0, 3)\nPoint B = (4, 0)\n\nEuclidean Distance\nFormula:\nDistance = √((4 - 0)² + (0 - 3)²)\n\nCalculation:\n= √(16 + 9)\n= √25\n= 5\n\nAnswer:\nEuclidean Distance = 5\n\n-----------------------------\n\nManhattan Distance\nFormula:\nDistance = |4 - 0| + |0 - 3|\n\nCalculation:\n= 4 + 3\n= 7\n\nAnswer:\nManhattan Distance = 7",
            "tip": "Euclidean Distance measures the shortest straight-line path, while Manhattan Distance measures movement along horizontal and vertical paths. For text embeddings, both are usually less effective than Cosine Similarity because they are influenced by vector magnitude."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Gold Standard: Cosine Similarity",
            "explanation": "Since Euclidean Distance has problems with vector size, the NLP industry uses a different approach called **Cosine Similarity**..\nInstead of measuring the distance between vectors, Cosine Similarity measures the angle between them. focusing on direction rather than length.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/nlp/sm2.png]\nCosine Similarity Formula:\n**Cosine Similarity (cos(θ))** = `(A · B) / (||A|| × ||B||)`\nThis formula compares two vectors by measuring the angle between them.\nWhere: A · B = Dot Product of vectors, ||A|| = Length of Vector A, ||B|| = Length of Vector B.\nThe value of Cosine Similarity depends on the angle between the two vectors. Smaller angles indicate higher similarity, while larger angles indicate lower similarity.\n`0° Angle` → Cosine Similarity = 1.0 (Perfectly Similar)\n`90° Angle` → Cosine Similarity = 0.0 (Unrelated)\n`180° Angle` → Cosine Similarity = -1.0 (Exact Opposites)\n`Example:`\nKing = [1,2], Queen = [2,3] → A·B = 8,\n ||A|| = 2.24, ||B|| = 3.61 \ncos(θ) = 8 / (2.24 × 3.61) ≈ 0.99 (very similar)\nUnlike Euclidean or Manhattan Distance, Cosine Similarity ignores vector size and focuses on semantic direction. This means a short tweet and a 500-page book about the same topic can still have a similarity score close to 1.0 if they point in the same semantic direction.\n The below The diagram demonstrates that words with similar meanings, such as 'King' and 'Qween', have vectors pointing in nearly the same direction, resulting in a high Cosine Similarity score.\nLet's walk through the following Python example to understand how Cosine Similarity works. First, import the cosine_similarity.",
            "example": "from sklearn.metrics.pairwise import cosine_similarity\n\nvector_a = [[1, 1, 1]]\nvector_b = [[1, 1, 0]]\n\nsimilarity = cosine_similarity(vector_a, vector_b)\nprint('Cosine Similarity:', similarity[0][0])"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Distance Metrics Review",
            "introduction": "Great work! We have learned why vectors need to be compared and explored the two most common similarity metrics. Let's quickly review the key ideas before moving forward.",
