    {
      "id": "M007-vector-db-05-rag-pipeline",
      "title": "Building a Semantic Search Engine",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#ec4899",
        "accent": "#be185d"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The End-to-End Architecture",
            "explanation": "So far, we have learned several important concepts.We have learned about embeddings, distance metrics, and vector databases. Now, we put them together into a complete system: A Semantic Search Engine, which is the foundational retrieval mechanism for **RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation)**.\n\nThe pipeline is split into two distinct phases:\n1. **Data Ingestion (Offline):** Preparing and storing your knowledge base.\n2. **Query Retrieval (Runtime):** Finding the right information when the user asks a question.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"background: #0f172a; padding: 20px; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid #ec4899; color: white; font-family: sans-serif; text-align: center;\"><h4 style=\"color: #f472b6; margin-top: 0;\">⚙️ The Semantic Pipeline</h4><div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start; margin-top: 15px; font-size: 12px;\"><div style=\"width: 45%; background: #1e293b; padding: 10px; border-radius: 8px; border-top: 3px solid #3b82f6;\"><b style=\"color: #60a5fa;\">Phase 1: Ingestion</b><div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #cbd5e1; text-align: left; line-height: 1.5;\">1. Get Raw Document<br>2. Chunk Document<br>3. Generate Embeddings<br>4. Store in Vector DB</div></div><div style=\"width: 45%; background: #1e293b; padding: 10px; border-radius: 8px; border-top: 3px solid #10b981;\"><b style=\"color: #34d399;\">Phase 2: Retrieval</b><div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #cbd5e1; text-align: left; line-height: 1.5;\">1. User types Query<br>2. Embed Query<br>3. Cosine Similarity Search<br>4. Return Top-K Chunks</div></div></div></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Step 1: Document Chunking",
            "explanation": "Let's start with the first step.You cannot pass a 50-page PDF or a dense 3-page resume into an embedding model all at once. \n\nEmbedding models have strict token limits (often 512 or 8192 tokens). Furthermore, embedding a massive document dilutes its meaning. If an entire resume is one vector, a specific skill like 'Next.js' gets lost in the noise of the candidate's hobbies and education history.\n\n**Chunking** is the process of splitting a large document into smaller, semantically meaningful pieces (e.g., 500 characters per chunk, or splitting by paragraphs). Each chunk gets its own vector embedding."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Document Chunking Review",
            "introduction": "Great! We have completed the first stage of the Semantic Search Pipeline. Before moving forward, let's quickly review why chunking is important.",
            "question": "Drag and drop the correct term into each blank.",
            "template": [
              "Large documents are split into smaller ____.",
              "Chunking helps preserve important ____.",
              "Embedding models have ____ limits.",
              "Smaller chunks often improve search ____."
            ],
            "options": [
              "chunks",
              "information",
              "token",
              "accuracy",
              "database",
              "vectors",
              "metadata"
            ],
            "answers": {
              "blank_1": "chunks",
              "blank_2": "information",
              "blank_3": "token",
              "blank_4": "accuracy"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Code Challenge: Basic Text Chunking",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Write a Python function to split a string into chunks of a specific character length. (Note: In production, you would use tools like LangChain's RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter).",
            "starterCode": "def chunk_text(text, chunk_size):\n    # TODO: Return a list of text chunks\n    pass\n\nresume = \"Alex is an AI Engineer. He works with React and Python. He lives in Delhi.\"\nprint(chunk_text(resume, 25))",
