      "id": "M008-rag-01-intro",
      "title": "Introduction to Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#8b5cf6",
        "accent": "#6d28d9"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "LLM Limitations and Hallucinations",
            "explanation": "Let's start with a simple question.Suppose you ask ChatGPT:'What was our company's revenue last month?'\n`Can the model answer that question?`\nThe answer is usually `no`.\nWhy?Because the model has never seen your company's private data.\nNow imagine you ask:`'Who is the current CEO of Google?'`\nIf the CEO changed recently, the model may not know that information either.\nThis happens because Large Language Models learn from data that was available during training.After training is completed, the model does not automatically learn new information.\nThis limitation is known as the Knowledge Cutoff.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/rag/in1.png]\n**As a result, the model may:**\n\n• Give outdated information\n• Say it does not know\n• Generate a confident but incorrect answer\nThat incorrect answer is called a Hallucination.\nBefore we solve this problem, we first need to understand the different types of knowledge that AI systems work with."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Static vs. Dynamic Knowledge",
            "explanation": "Excellent! Now that we understand how LLMs generate responses, an important question comes to mind: **Where does the information in those responses come from?**\n\nThe answer is that not all knowledge is the same. Some information remains unchanged for many years, while other information changes every minute. Based on how often information changes, we can divide knowledge into two categories: **Static Knowledge** and **Dynamic Knowledge**.\n\n### 1. Static Knowledge\n\nStatic Knowledge refers to information that changes very rarely or may never change. During training, an LLM learns and stores this type of knowledge inside its parameters, allowing it to answer questions without needing to access an external source.\n\nExamples include:\n• How does a combustion engine work?\n• What is the capital of France?\n• How do you write a Python for loop?\n• What is Newton's First Law?\n\nSince this information remains stable, LLMs are very good at answering these kinds of questions.\n\n### 2. Dynamic Knowledge\n\nDynamic Knowledge refers to information that changes frequently, such as every day, every hour, or even every second. Because this information is constantly changing, it cannot be permanently stored inside an LLM during training.\n\nExamples include:\n• Today's stock market prices\n• Current weather\n• Your bank account balance\n• Yesterday's meeting notes\n• Product inventory in an online store\n• Today's news headlines\n\nSince an LLM's knowledge is fixed after training, it cannot automatically know these latest updates. To answer questions about dynamic information, the LLM needs access to an external source such as a database, documents, APIs, or a Vector Database.\n\nThis is exactly why technologies like **RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation)** are used. They allow the LLM to retrieve the latest information before generating a response.\n\n**Simple Rule:**\n\n• **Static Knowledge** → Rarely changes → Stored inside the LLM.\n• **Dynamic Knowledge** → Changes frequently → Retrieved from external sources when needed.\n\nIn the next topic, we'll learn Why RAG we use instead of Fine-Tuning.",
            "tip": ""
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
