              "text": "LLMs cannot be fine-tuned once they are deployed.",
              "outcome": "Incorrect. Models can be continuously fine-tuned, but it is slow and expensive.",
              "score": 10
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Introduction to Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)",
            "explanation": "Great! In the previous lessons, we learned how Large Language Models (LLMs) generate responses based on the knowledge they learned during training.\n\nBut here's an important question.\n\nWhat happens if we ask an LLM about information that is `new`, `private`, or `not included in its training data`?\n\n**For example:**\n\n• 'What is my company's latest leave policy?'\n\n• 'Summarize this confidential PDF.'\n\n• 'What products are currently available in our inventory?'\n\nAn LLM cannot answer these questions accurately because it doesn't automatically know your company's private data or the latest information.\n\nOne possible solution would be to retrain the LLM every time new data becomes available. But retraining a Large Language Model is extremely expensive, takes a long time, and is not practical whenever the data changes.\n\nSo, how do we solve this problem?\n\nInstead of teaching the LLM every new piece of information, we simply `give it the relevant information whenever a question is asked`.\n\nThis idea is called **Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)**.\n\nThink of it like an `open-book exam`.\n\nDuring an open-book exam, you don't memorize every answer. Instead, when a question is asked, you first look up the relevant information in your book and then write the answer.\n\nA RAG system works in exactly the same way.\n\nWhen a user asks a question:\n\n• First, it searches a knowledge base or document collection to find the most relevant information.\n\n• Then, it sends both the user's question and the retrieved information to the LLM.\n\n• Finally, the LLM reads that information and generates a natural, accurate response.\n\nNotice that the LLM is `not memorizing your documents`. It simply uses the retrieved information as additional context while generating the answer.\n\n**Real-World Examples**\n\n**Customer Support Chatbot**\n\nA customer asks, 'What is your refund policy?'\n\nInstead of guessing the answer, the chatbot first searches the company's internal documentation, retrieves the latest refund policy, and then explains it to the customer.\n\n**Legal Assistant**\n\nA lawyer asks about similar court cases.\n\nThe system first retrieves relevant legal documents and previous judgments before the LLM prepares a summary or draft.\n\n**Healthcare Assistant**\n\nA doctor asks about a patient's medical history.\n\nThe system retrieves the patient's records from the hospital database and provides them to the LLM, which then generates an easy-to-understand summary.\n\n**ChatGPT Web Search**\n\nWhen ChatGPT searches the web before answering a question, it is also following the same idea. It first retrieves relevant information and then generates the response.\n\n**Key Takeaway:**\n\nA RAG system combines the strengths of both components.\n\n• The `Knowledge Base` stores the latest and private information.\n\n• The `Retriever` finds the most relevant documents.\n\n• The `LLM` reads those documents and generates a clear, natural-language response.\n\nThis allows the LLM to answer questions using `up-to-date`, `private`, and `domain-specific` information without retraining the model."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The End-to-End RAG Workflow",
            "explanation": "Great! In the previous lesson, we learned what Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) is and why it is used. Now let's understand `how a RAG system works from start to finish`.\n\nA RAG system can be divided into `two phases`.\n\n• **Indexing Phase** → Preparing and storing the documents.\n\n• **Retrieval Phase** → Finding the right information and generating an answer.\n\nLet's walk through the complete workflow step by step.\n\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/rag/in2.png]\n\n **Phase 1: Indexing (Preparing the Knowledge Base)**\n\nBefore users can ask questions, we first need to prepare our documents so the RAG system can search them efficiently.\n\n **Step 1: Load the Data Source**\n\nThe first step is to collect the documents that contain the information we want the chatbot to answer.\n\nThese documents can come from different sources such as:\n\n• PDF files\n\n• Word documents\n\n• Websites\n\n• Company knowledge bases\n\n• Databases\n\n• Text files\n\nAt this stage, we simply load all the required documents into our application.\n\n **Step 2: Extract the Text**\n\nOnce the documents are loaded, we extract the actual text from them.\n\nFor example, if the document is a PDF, the system reads every page and extracts the text so it can be processed further.\n\n **Step 3: Split the Text into Chunks**\n\nThe extracted documents are usually very large.\n\nInstead of storing one huge document, we divide it into many smaller pieces called `Chunks`.\n\nThink of a large book. Instead of searching the entire book every time, it's much faster to search individual pages or paragraphs.\n\nChunking makes retrieval faster and helps the LLM receive only the most relevant information.\n\n **Step 4: Generate Embeddings**\n\nNow each chunk is converted into a numerical representation called an `Embedding`.\n\nAn Embedding captures the meaning of the text, allowing the computer to compare documents based on meaning instead of exact words.\n\n **Step 5: Store in the Vector Database**\n\nFinally, all the embeddings are stored inside a `Vector Database`.\n\nThe Vector Database acts as the knowledge base for the RAG system. It stores both the text chunks and their corresponding embeddings so they can be searched later.\n\nAt this point, the RAG system is fully prepared to answer user questions.\n\n **Phase 2: Retrieval & Answer Generation**\n\nNow let's see what happens when a user asks a question.\n\n **Step 6: User Asks a Question**\n\nSuppose a user asks:\n\n`\"What was our company's Q3 Revenue?\"`\n\nThe RAG pipeline now begins searching for the answer.\n\n **Step 7: Convert the Query into an Embedding**\n\nJust like the documents, the user's question is also converted into an embedding.\n\nThis allows the system to compare the question with the stored document embeddings.\n\n **Step 8: Similarity Search in the Vector Database**\n\nThe query embedding is sent to the Vector Database.\n\nThe database compares it with all the stored embeddings and searches for the chunks that have the most similar meaning.\n\n **Step 9: Retrieve the Top-K Relevant Chunks**\n\nInstead of returning every document, the Vector Database returns only the `Top-K` most relevant chunks.\n\nThese are the pieces of text that are most likely to contain the correct answer.\n\n **Step 10: Create the Prompt (Context Injection)**\n\nNow the retrieved chunks are combined with the user's question.\n\nTogether, they form a new prompt that is sent to the Large Language Model.\n\nThis process is called `Context Injection` because we are giving the LLM additional information before it generates an answer.\n\n **Step 11: LLM Generates the Answer**\n\nThe LLM carefully reads both:\n\n• The user's question.\n\n• The retrieved context.\n\nUsing this information, it generates a clear and accurate response.\n\n **Step 12: Return the Final Response**\n\nFinally, the generated answer is returned to the user.\n\nBecause the answer is based on the retrieved documents instead of only the model's memory, it is much more accurate, up-to-date, and relevant.\n\n**Key Takeaway:**\n\nA RAG system first prepares documents by loading, extracting, chunking, embedding, and storing them in a Vector Database. Later, whenever a user asks a question, the system retrieves the most relevant information and provides it to the LLM, allowing it to generate an accurate and context-aware response."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Building a Simple End-to-End RAG Chatbot",
            "explanation": "This example shows how to build a complete RAG pipeline using ChromaDB, Sentence Transformers, and OpenAI.\n\nThe workflow follows: Documents → Embeddings → Vector Database → Retrieval → Context Injection → LLM → Answer.\n```python\n# 1. Install Dependencies\npip install openai chromadb sentence-transformers\n\n# 2. Import Libraries\nfrom openai import OpenAI\nimport chromadb\nfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\n\n# 3. Initialize Models\nclient = OpenAI(api_key='YOUR_API_KEY')\nembedding_model = SentenceTransformer('all-MiniLM-L6-v2')\n\n# 4. Create Vector Database\ndb = chromadb.Client()\ncollection = db.create_collection('company_docs')\n\n# 5. Load Documents\ndocuments = [\n    'Office timing is 9 AM to 6 PM.',\n    'Guest WiFi password is coffee123.',\n    'Products can be returned within 45 days.'\n]\n\n# 6. Generate Document Embeddings\nembeddings = embedding_model.encode(documents).tolist()\n\n# 7. Store Embeddings in ChromaDB\ncollection.add(\n    ids=['1', '2', '3'],\n    documents=documents,\n    embeddings=embeddings\n)\n\n# 8. User Query\nquestion = 'What is the WiFi password?'\n\n# 9. Generate Query Embedding\nquery_embedding = embedding_model.encode(question).tolist()\n\n# 10. Retrieve Similar Document\nresults = collection.query(\n    query_embeddings=[query_embedding],\n    n_results=1\n)\n\ncontext = results['documents'][0][0]\n\n# 11. Build the RAG Prompt\nprompt = f'''\nAnswer only using the provided context.\n\nContext:\n{context}\n\nQuestion:\n{question}\n'''\n\n# 12. Generate Final Answer\nresponse = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model='gpt-4o-mini',\n    messages=[\n        {\n            'role': 'user',\n            'content': prompt\n        }\n    ]\n)\n\nprint(response.choices[0].message.content)\n# Guest WiFi password is coffee123.\n``` \n**Output**\n```python # Output\nGuest WiFi password is coffee123.\n\nProcess finished with exit code 0```",
            "tip": "If you don't have an OpenAI API key, you can use an open-source model from Hugging Face or a free Groq Cloud API as an alternative. Simply replace the OpenAI client with your preferred provider while keeping the rest of the RAG pipeline the same."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Build an Employee Handbook RAG Assistant",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Build a simple Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) assistant using the provided knowledge base and user question.\n\nInstructions:\n1. Generate embeddings for the provided documents.\n2. Generate an embedding for the given user question.\n3. Retrieve the most relevant document.\n4. Create a RAG prompt using the retrieved context.\n5. Send the prompt to GPT-4o-mini.\n6. Return the final answer based only on the retrieved context.",
            "starterCode": "from sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\nfrom sklearn.metrics.pairwise import cosine_similarity\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()\n\n# TODO: Load the embedding model\nembedding_model = \n\nknowledge_base = [\n    \"Employees may work from home up to three days per week.\",\n    \"Expense reimbursement requests must be submitted within 30 days.\",\n    \"The annual performance review takes place every December.\"\n]\n\nquestion = \"When should employees submit reimbursement requests?\"\n\n# TODO:\n# 1. Generate embeddings for all handbook documents.\n# 2. Generate an embedding for the user's question.\n# 3. Retrieve the most relevant policy.\n# 4. Build a RAG prompt.\n# 5. Send the prompt to GPT-4o-mini.\n",
            "language": "python",
            "expected_output": "Expense reimbursement requests must be submitted within 30 days.",
            "hint": "Use the 'all-MiniLM-L6-v2' embedding model, cosine similarity for retrieval, and pass the retrieved policy as context to GPT-4o-mini.",
            "solution": "from sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\nfrom sklearn.metrics.pairwise import cosine_similarity\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()\n\n# Load embedding model\nembedding_model = SentenceTransformer('all-MiniLM-L6-v2')\n\n# Knowledge Base\nknowledge_base = [\n    \"Employees may work from home up to three days per week.\",\n    \"Expense reimbursement requests must be submitted within 30 days.\",\n    \"The annual performance review takes place every December.\"\n]\n\n# User Question\nquestion = \"When should employees submit reimbursement requests?\"\n\n# Step 1: Generate embeddings for the documents\ndocument_embeddings = embedding_model.encode(knowledge_base)\n\n# Step 2: Generate embedding for the user's question\nquery_embedding = embedding_model.encode([question])\n\n# Step 3: Retrieve the most relevant document\nsimilarities = cosine_similarity(query_embedding, document_embeddings)[0]\nbest_index = similarities.argmax()\nretrieved_context = knowledge_base[best_index]\n\n# Step 4: Build the RAG prompt\nprompt = \"\"\"\nAnswer the question using ONLY the provided context.\n\nContext:\n{retrieved_context}\n\nQuestion:\n{question}\n\"\"\"\n\n# Step 5: Generate the answer\nresponse = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=\"gpt-4o-mini\",\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}]\n)\n\nprint(response.choices[0].message.content)"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "drag_drop",
          "content": {
            "question": "Arrange the RAG Workflow steps in the correct order.",
            "buckets": [
              {
                "name": "Step 1",
                "accepts": [
                  "User Submits a Query"
                ]
              },
              {
