    {
      "id": "M008-rag-02-pipeline",
      "title": "The RAG Data Pipeline",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#14b8a6",
        "accent": "#0f766e"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Loading Documents: Gathering the Knowledge",
            "explanation": "Before an LLM can answer questions about your private data, you have to extract that data. An AI cannot natively 'read' a PDF file or a SQL database. It only understands raw text.\n\nThe first step of any RAG pipeline is the **Document Loader**. Frameworks like LangChain and LlamaIndex provide hundreds of pre-built loaders to extract raw text from:\n• **Unstructured Data:** PDFs, Word Documents (.docx), PowerPoints.\n• **Semi-Structured Data:** Websites (HTML scraping), Notion pages, Confluence.\n• **Structured Data:** SQL Databases, CSV files, JSON APIs.",
            "example": "# Install Required Libraries\n# pip install langchain langchain-community pypdf\n\nfrom langchain_community.document_loaders import (PyPDFLoader,CSVLoader,TextLoader,WebBaseLoader,JSONLoader)\n\n#  1. Load a PDF Document\npdf_loader = PyPDFLoader(\"report.pdf\")\npdf_docs = pdf_loader.load()\n\nprint(f\"PDF Pages Loaded: {len(pdf_docs)}\")\n\n# Expected Output:\n# PDF Pages Loaded: 10\n\n\n#  2. Load a CSV File\ncsv_loader = CSVLoader(\"sales_data.csv\")\ncsv_docs = csv_loader.load()\n\nprint(f\"CSV Rows Loaded: {len(csv_docs)}\")\n\n# Expected Output:\n# CSV Rows Loaded: 500\n\n\n#  3. Load a Text File\ntext_loader = TextLoader(\"notes.txt\")\ntext_docs = text_loader.load()\n\nprint(f\"Text Documents Loaded: {len(text_docs)}\")\n\n# Expected Output:\n# Text Documents Loaded: 1\n\n\n#  4. Load a Website\nweb_loader = WebBaseLoader(\"https://python.org\")\nweb_docs = web_loader.load()\n\nprint(f\"Web Pages Loaded: {len(web_docs)}\")\n\n# Expected Output:\n# Web Pages Loaded: 1\n\n\n#  5. Load a JSON File\njson_loader = JSONLoader(\n    file_path=\"employees.json\",\n    jq_schema=\".\",\n    text_content=False\n)\njson_docs = json_loader.load()\n\nprint(f\"JSON Records Loaded: {len(json_docs)}\")\n\n# Expected Output:\n# JSON Records Loaded: 100",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"background: #0f172a; padding: 20px; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid #14b8a6; color: white; font-family: monospace;\"><h4 style=\"color: #5eead4; margin-top:0;\">// Data Ingestion Connectors</h4><div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 10px; font-size: 12px;\"><div style=\"background: #1e293b; padding: 10px; border-radius: 6px; border-left: 3px solid #ef4444;\">📄 PyPDFLoader(\"report.pdf\")</div><div style=\"background: #1e293b; padding: 10px; border-radius: 6px; border-left: 3px solid #3b82f6;\">🌐 WebBaseLoader(\"https://wiki\")</div><div style=\"background: #1e293b; padding: 10px; border-radius: 6px; border-left: 3px solid #10b981;\">📊 CSVLoader(\"sales_data.csv\")</div><div style=\"background: #1e293b; padding: 10px; border-radius: 6px; border-left: 3px solid #f59e0b;\">🗄️ SQLDatabaseLoader(db_conn)</div></div><div style=\"color: #94a3b8; font-size: 11px; margin-top: 10px;\">Goal: Convert every format into one universal format -> <span style=\"color: #2dd4bf; font-weight: bold;\">Plain Text Strings</span></div></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Text Cleaning: Preparing Documents for RAG",
            "explanation": "Great! In the previous lesson, we learned that the first step in a RAG pipeline is loading documents from different data sources. However, before we convert those documents into embeddings, we need to make sure the extracted text is clean and useful.\n\nThink about reading a PDF that contains page numbers, headers, footers, advertisements, or broken sentences. If we directly create embeddings from this noisy text, the Vector Database may retrieve incorrect or irrelevant information.\n\nThat's why `Text Cleaning` is an important step in every RAG pipeline. The goal is to remove unnecessary content while preserving the actual information that will help the LLM answer user questions.\n\nLet's understand the complete text cleaning workflow.\n\n**Step 1: Extract Raw Text**\n\nFirst, we extract the text from different document sources such as PDFs, Word files, websites, databases, or text files.\n\nAt this stage, the extracted text usually contains a lot of unwanted content.\n\n**Step 2: Remove HTML Tags**\n\nIf the data comes from a website, it may contain HTML tags such as `div`, `nav`, `header`, or `footer`.\n\nThese tags are useful for displaying a webpage but not for understanding its content, so we remove them.\n\n**Step 3: Remove Headers and Footers**\n\nMany documents contain repeated information such as company names, page numbers, copyright notices, or document titles.\n\nSince this information appears on every page, it doesn't add value to retrieval and should be removed.\n\n**Step 4: Fix Broken Sentences**\n\nSometimes sentences are split across multiple lines because of PDF formatting.\n\nWe merge these broken lines so that every sentence becomes complete and easier for the embedding model to understand.\n\n**Step 5: Remove Extra Spaces**\n\nNext, we remove unnecessary spaces, tabs, and blank lines to create clean and consistent text.\n\nThis improves readability and avoids unnecessary noise.\n\n**Step 6: Remove Unwanted Characters**\n\nDocuments may contain unnecessary symbols, encoding errors, or special characters that don't contribute to the meaning of the text.\n\nThese characters are removed to make the text cleaner.\n\n**Step 7: Normalize the Text**\n\nNow we standardize the document by applying consistent formatting.\n\nDepending on the project, this may include converting text to lowercase, fixing punctuation, or standardizing dates and numbers.\n\n**Step 8: Remove Irrelevant Content**\n\nIf the document contains advertisements, navigation menus, social media links, cookie banners, or other unrelated information, we remove it.\n\nOnly the meaningful content that helps answer user questions should remain.\n\n**Step 9: Validate the Content**\n\nBefore moving to the next stage, we quickly verify that the cleaned text still contains complete and useful information.\n\nThis ensures that important content wasn't accidentally removed during cleaning.\n\n**Step 10: Send the Clean Text for Chunking**\n\nOnce the document has been cleaned, it is finally ready for the next stage of the RAG pipeline.\n\nIn the next step, we'll split this clean text into smaller chunks before converting those chunks into embeddings.\n\n**Key Takeaway**\n\nThe quality of a RAG system depends heavily on the quality of its documents. Clean documents produce better embeddings, better retrieval results, and ultimately more accurate answers from the LLM.",
            "example": "# Text Cleaning Function\n\nimport re\n\ndef clean_text(text):\n    # Remove HTML tags\n    text = re.sub(r'<.*?>', ' ', text)\n\n    # Remove URLs\n    text = re.sub(r'http\\S+|www\\S+', ' ', text)\n\n    # Remove page numbers\n    text = re.sub(r'Page\\s+\\d+', ' ', text, flags=re.IGNORECASE)\n\n    # Remove special characters\n    text = re.sub(r'[^A-Za-z0-9\\s.,]', ' ', text)\n\n    # Replace multiple line breaks with a space\n    text = re.sub(r'\\n+', ' ', text)\n\n    # Remove extra spaces\n    text = re.sub(r'\\s+', ' ', text).strip()\n\n    return text\n\n# Example\nraw_text = '''\n<div>Welcome to AI Academy</div>\n\nPage 1\n\nVisit https://example.com\n\nMachine Learning is amazing!!!\n'''\n\ncleaned_text = clean_text(raw_text)\n\nprint(cleaned_text)\n\n#Output:Welcome to AI Academy Machine Learning is amazing"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Chunking Strategies: Size & Overlap",
            "explanation": "Excellent! In the previous lesson, we learned how to load documents. But can we send a 200-page PDF directly to an embedding model?\n\nThe answer is `No`. Large documents are difficult to process and often contain multiple topics. Instead, we split them into smaller pieces called `Chunks`. This process is known as `Chunking`.\n\nEach chunk is converted into its own embedding, making it easier for the Vector Database to search and retrieve relevant information.\n\nWhile creating chunks, we configure two important settings:\n\n**Chunk Size**\n\n`Chunk Size` determines how much text is stored in each chunk. A common size is `500–1000 tokens` (about one or two paragraphs).\n\n• If the chunk is `too large`, it may contain multiple topics.\n• If the chunk is `too small`, it may not contain enough context.\n\n**Chunk Overlap**\n\n`Chunk Overlap` copies a small portion of text from the end of one chunk to the beginning of the next chunk. This prevents important information from being lost when a sentence is split between two chunks.\n\nTake a look at the illustration below to see how overlap preserves context between neighboring chunks.\n\n**Simple Rule:**\n\n• `Chunk Size` → Amount of text in each chunk.\n• `Chunk Overlap` → Shared text between consecutive chunks to preserve context.",
            "html_code": "<div style='font-family:Arial,sans-serif;padding:15px;background:#ffffff;border:1px solid #d1d5db;border-radius:10px;'><h4 style='margin-top:0;color:#111827;'>Chunk Overlap</h4><div style='display:flex;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap;'><div style='flex:1;background:#fef2f2;padding:10px;border-radius:8px;border-left:4px solid #ef4444;color:#111827;'><b>❌ No Overlap</b><br>Chunk 1: The new iPhone. It<br>Chunk 2: features a titanium body<br><span style='color:#b91c1c;'>Context Lost</span></div><div style='flex:1;background:#f0fdf4;padding:10px;border-radius:8px;border-left:4px solid #22c55e;color:#111827;'><b>✅ With Overlap</b><br>Chunk 1: The new iPhone. It<br>Chunk 2: <b>new iPhone. It</b> features...<br><span style='color:#166534;'>Context Preserved</span></div></div><div style='margin-top:10px;background:#eff6ff;padding:8px;border-radius:6px;text-align:center;color:#1e3a8a;font-size:13px;'><b>Overlap = Shared text between chunks.</b></div></div>",
            "example": "Practical Example:\nfrom langchain.text_splitter import RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter\ntext = '''\nChunking is the process of breaking a large piece of text\ninto smaller, manageable pieces to make it easier to process and analyze.\n'''\n\nsplitter = RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter(\n    chunk_size=40,\n    chunk_overlap=10\n)\n\nchunks = splitter.split_text(text)\n\nfor i, chunk in enumerate(chunks, 1):\n    print(f'Chunk {i}:', chunk)\n\nOutput:\nChunk 1: Chunking is the process of breaking\nChunk 2: of breaking a large piece of text\nChunk 3: piece of text into smaller,\nChunk 4: into smaller, manageable pieces."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Types of Chunking Strategies",
            "explanation": "Chunking is the process of breaking large documents into smaller pieces before creating embeddings.\n`Types of Chunking statergies:`\n **Fixed-Size Chunking** splits text into equal-sized chunks,\n **Overlap Chunking** shares content between neighboring chunks to preserve context,\n **Sentence Chunking** splits text at sentence boundaries,\n **Paragraph Chunking** keeps complete paragraphs together, \n**Recursive Chunking** intelligently splits using paragraphs, sentences, and words,\n **Semantic Chunking** groups text based on meaning rather than size,\n **Document Structure Chunking** uses headings and sections as boundaries, \n**Token-Based Chunking** splits based on token count, and \n**Agentic Chunking** uses an LLM to decide optimal chunk boundaries. For most RAG applications, Recursive Chunking with Overlap is the recommended starting point.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/rag/th1.png]\n",
            "example": "# Install LangChain\n# pip install langchain-text-splitters\n\nfrom langchain_text_splitters import CharacterTextSplitter\n\ntext = \"Machine Learning enables computers to learn from data without being explicitly programmed.\"\n\n# Fixed-Size Chunking\nfixed_splitter = CharacterTextSplitter(\n    chunk_size=30,\n    chunk_overlap=0\n)\n\nfixed_chunks = fixed_splitter.split_text(text)\n\nprint('Fixed-Size Chunking:')\nprint(fixed_chunks)\n\n# Overlap Chunking\noverlap_splitter = CharacterTextSplitter(\n    chunk_size=30,\n    chunk_overlap=10\n)\n\noverlap_chunks = overlap_splitter.split_text(text)\n\nprint('\\nOverlap Chunking:')\nprint(overlap_chunks)\n\n# Output\n# Fixed-Size Chunking\n# ['Machine Learning enables comp',\n#  'uters to learn from data with',\n#  'out being explicitly programm',\n#  'ed.']\n#\n# Overlap Chunking\n# ['Machine Learning enables comp',\n#  'ables computers to learn from',\n#  'learn from data without being',\n#  'out being explicitly programm',\n#  'programmed.']"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Metadata Creation: Preparing Data for Retrieval",
            "explanation": "A chunk of text floating in a database is useless without context. When you create a chunk, you must attach **Metadata** (a JSON dictionary of facts about that chunk).\n\nIf the chunk says: *'Revenue grew by 20% this quarter.'*\nYour Metadata should say: \n```python\n{\"source\": \"Q3_Financials.pdf\",\n\"date\": \"2023-10-01\",\n\"department\": \"Finance\", \n\"page\": 4}\n```\n\nMetadata is the secret weapon of enterprise RAG. It allows you to filter the database *before* running the semantic search, guaranteeing the LLM only reads the correct documents.",
            "example": "Without metadata, if a user asks 'What is our revenue?',\nthe system might retrieve a report from 2018 instead of 2024!"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Code: Chunking with LangChain",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Use LangChain's RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter to split a document with a chunk size of 100 characters and an overlap of 20 characters.",
            "starterCode": "from langchain.text_splitter import RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter\n\ntext = \"Artificial Intelligence is transforming the world. RAG makes LLMs smarter by giving them memory.\"\n\n# TODO: Initialize the text splitter with chunk_size=100 and chunk_overlap=20\nsplitter = \n\nchunks = splitter.split_text(text)\nprint(f\"Created {len(chunks)} chunks.\")\nprint(f\"Chunk 1: {chunks[0]}\")",
            "language": "python",
            "expected_output": "Created 2 chunks.\nChunk 1: Artificial Intelligence is transforming the world. RAG makes LLMs smarter by giving them memory.",
            "hint": "RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter(chunk_size=100, chunk_overlap=20)",
            "solution": "from langchain.text_splitter import RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter\n\ntext = \"Artificial Intelligence is transforming the world. RAG makes LLMs smarter by giving them memory.\"\n\n# Initialize the splitter\nsplitter = RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter(\n    chunk_size=100, \n    chunk_overlap=20\n)\n\nchunks = splitter.split_text(text)\nprint(f\"Created {len(chunks)} chunks.\")\nprint(f\"Chunk 1: {chunks[0]}\")"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
