    {
      "id": "M008-rag-03-indexing",
      "title": "Advanced Vector Search & Indexing Strategies",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#f97316",
        "accent": "#ea580c"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Dense Search, Sparse Search, and Hybrid Search",
            "explanation": "Great! Before we learn about Vector Databases, let's first understand **how a RAG system searches for information**. Different search techniques are used to find relevant documents, and each one has its own strengths and limitations.\n\nThe three most common search techniques are `Dense Search`, `Sparse Search`, and `Hybrid Search`.\n\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/rag/ad1.png]\n\n**1. Dense Search**\n\nDense Search uses an **Embedding Model** to understand the meaning of both the user's query and the stored documents. Instead of matching exact words, it searches based on semantic similarity.\n\n`How it Works:`\n\n• Convert the query into an embedding.\n\n• Compare it with the document embeddings stored in the Vector Database.\n\n• Retrieve documents whose meaning is most similar to the query.\n\n`Best For:` Semantic search, question answering, and finding similar content.\n\n**2. Sparse Search**\n\nSparse Search focuses on `exact keyword matching`. It uses algorithms such as `BM25` to calculate how important each keyword is within a document.\n`BM25` is a keyword-based search algorithm that ranks documents according to how relevant they are to the user's search query.\n\n`How it Works:`\n\n• Break the query into individual keywords.\n\n• Search documents containing those exact keywords.\n\n• Rank the documents based on keyword frequency and importance.\n\n`Best For:` Error codes, product IDs, names, and exact keyword searches.\n\n**3. Hybrid Search**\n\nHybrid Search combines both **Dense Search** and **Sparse Search** to get the advantages of each approach.\n\nIt understands the meaning of the query while also considering exact keyword matches.\n\n`How it Works:`\n\n• Perform Dense Search using embeddings.\n\n• Perform Sparse Search using keywords.\n\n• Combine both search results and rank the best matching documents.\n\n`Best For:` Enterprise RAG systems where both semantic understanding and exact keyword matching are important.\n\n**Key Takeaway:**\n\n• `Dense Search` understands meaning.\n\n• `Sparse Search` matches exact keywords.\n\n• `Hybrid Search` combines both approaches to produce more accurate retrieval results."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Exact Search (k-NN) vs Approximate Search (ANN)",
            "explanation": "Great! In the previous lesson, we learned how a Vector Database stores embeddings and retrieves the most relevant documents. But here's an important question.\n\nImagine your Vector Database contains **1 million document embeddings**. When a user asks a question, how does the database quickly find the most similar vectors?\n\nThere are two common approaches to solve this problem: `Exact Search (k-NN)` and `Approximate Search (ANN)`.\n\nLet's understand both.\n\n**1. Exact Search (k-Nearest Neighbors)**\n\nIn `Exact Search`, the database compares the query vector with **every single vector** stored in the database.\n\nFor example, if there are **1 million vectors**, the system calculates the similarity score for all **1 million vectors** before deciding which ones are the closest.\n\n`How it Works:`\n\n• Compare the query vector with every stored vector.\n\n• Calculate the similarity score for each comparison.\n\n• Rank all vectors based on similarity.\n\n• Return the Top-K most similar vectors.\n\nBecause every vector is checked, the result is **100% accurate**. However, as the database grows larger, this process becomes much slower.\n\n`Best For:` Small datasets where accuracy is more important than speed.\n\n**2. Approximate Search (ANN)**\n\nNow imagine searching through **1 million vectors** without comparing every single one.\n\nInstead, the database uses a special indexing algorithm to quickly identify the regions that are most likely to contain similar vectors.\n\nFor example, instead of checking all **1 million vectors**, it may only compare **10,000 vectors** and still find almost the same results.\n\n`How it Works:`\n\n• Build a search index over all vector embeddings.\n\n• Quickly identify the most promising search regions.\n\n• Compare only a small subset of vectors.\n\n• Return the nearest matching vectors.\n\nBecause fewer vectors are compared, the search becomes much faster. Although the result may not always be the exact best match, it is usually **very close** while saving a huge amount of time.\n\n`Best For:` Large-scale Vector Databases and real-world RAG applications.\n\n**Key Takeaway:**\n\n• `Exact Search (k-NN)` checks every vector, giving the highest accuracy but slower search.\n\n• `Approximate Search (ANN)` checks only the most promising vectors, making retrieval much faster with only a very small loss in accuracy.\n\nThis is why almost all modern Vector Databases use `ANN` for production systems—it provides the perfect balance between speed and accuracy."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Search Methods Review",
            "introduction": "Great work! We have learned the three major search approaches used in modern retrieval systems. Let's quickly review them before moving forward.",
            "question": "Drag and drop the correct term into each blank.",
            "template": [
