    {
      "id": "M008-rag-04-advanced-retrieval",
      "title": "Advanced Retrieval Techniques",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#3b82f6",
        "accent": "#1e40af"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Hybrid Retrieval & Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF)",
            "explanation": "Great! In the previous lesson, we learned about Dense Search and Sparse Search. Each approach has its own strengths, but what if we want to take advantage of both?\n\nThis is where **Hybrid Retrieval** comes in.\n\nImagine you're searching for a document. Sometimes understanding the meaning of the query is important, while other times finding an exact keyword is more useful. Instead of choosing only one approach, Hybrid Retrieval combines both.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/rag/ar.png]\n**1. Dense Retrieval**\n\nDense Retrieval searches based on the **meaning** of the query using embeddings.\n\nFor example, if a user searches for `space ships`, it may also retrieve documents containing words like `galactic cruisers` because they have similar meanings.\n\n**2. Sparse Retrieval (BM25)**\n\nSparse Retrieval searches using **exact keywords**.\n\nFor example, if a user searches for `Model X-99`, BM25 looks for documents containing that exact keyword and ranks them based on keyword importance.\n\n**3. Hybrid Retrieval**\n\nHybrid Retrieval performs both Dense Search and Sparse Search at the same time.\n\nThis allows the system to understand the meaning of the query while also considering exact keyword matches, producing more accurate retrieval results.\n\nBut now another question arises.\n\nIf Dense Search gives one ranking and BM25 gives another ranking, **how do we combine both results?**\n\nThis is where **Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF)** comes into the picture.\n**How RRF Works**\n\nInstead of comparing the different scores produced by Dense Search and BM25, RRF simply looks at the **rank** of each document.\n\n• First, Dense Search returns its ranked list of documents.\n\n• Next, BM25 returns its own ranked list.\n\n• RRF gives every document a score based on its position in each ranking.\n\n• Documents that appear near the top in both rankings receive a higher combined score.\n\n• Finally, the document with the highest combined score is returned as the best result.\n\nThis approach makes Hybrid Retrieval more reliable because it benefits from both semantic understanding and exact keyword matching.\n\n**Key Takeaway:**\n\n• `Dense Retrieval` understands meaning.\n\n• `Sparse Retrieval (BM25)` matches exact keywords.\n\n• `Hybrid Retrieval` combines both approaches.\n\n• `RRF` merges the rankings from both searches to return the most relevant documents.",
            "example": "# Install Dependencies\n# pip install rank-bm25 sentence-transformers chromadb\n\nfrom rank_bm25 import BM25Okapi\nfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\nimport chromadb\n\n# Sample Documents\ndocuments = [\n    'Office timing is 9 AM to 6 PM.',\n    'Guest WiFi password is coffee123.',\n    'Products can be returned within 45 days.',\n    'Employees receive medical insurance.'\n]\n\n# ----------------------------\n# Dense Retrieval (Embeddings)\n# ----------------------------\nmodel = SentenceTransformer('all-MiniLM-L6-v2')\nembeddings = model.encode(documents).tolist()\n\nclient = chromadb.Client()\ncollection = client.create_collection('docs')\n\ncollection.add(\n    ids=['1','2','3','4'],\n    documents=documents,\n    embeddings=embeddings\n)\n\n# ----------------------------\n# Sparse Retrieval (BM25)\n# ----------------------------\ntokenized_docs = [doc.lower().split() for doc in documents]\nbm25 = BM25Okapi(tokenized_docs)\n\n# User Question\nquery = 'What is the office WiFi password?'\n\n# Dense Search\nquery_embedding = model.encode(query).tolist()\ndense_results = collection.query(\n    query_embeddings=[query_embedding],\n    n_results=3\n)\ndense_docs = dense_results['documents'][0]\n\n# Sparse Search\nbm25_results = bm25.get_top_n(\n    query.lower().split(),\n    documents,\n    n=3\n)\n\n# ----------------------------\n# Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF)\n# ----------------------------\ndef rrf(rank, k=60):\n    return 1 / (k + rank)\n\nscores = {}\n\nfor rank, doc in enumerate(dense_docs, start=1):\n    scores[doc] = scores.get(doc, 0) + rrf(rank)\n\nfor rank, doc in enumerate(bm25_results, start=1):\n    scores[doc] = scores.get(doc, 0) + rrf(rank)\n\n# Final Ranking\nfinal_results = sorted(\n    scores.items(),\n    key=lambda x: x[1],\n    reverse=True\n)\n\nprint('Hybrid Retrieval Results:')\nfor doc, score in final_results:\n    print(doc, score)\n\n# Output\n# Guest WiFi password is coffee123.\n# Office timing is 9 AM to 6 PM.\n# Products can be returned within 45 days."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is Query Transformation?",
            "explanation": "In the previous lessons, we learned that a Vector Database searches documents based on the user's question. But in real-world applications, users don't always ask clear or complete questions.\n\nFor example, a user might ask:\n\n`\"How do I fix it?\"`\n\nThe problem is that the question doesn't mention `what` needs to be fixed. Without enough information, the Vector Database may retrieve the wrong documents or no documents at all.\n\nTo solve this problem, we use `Query Transformation`.\n\nBefore searching the Vector Database, the user's question is first sent to an LLM. The LLM improves the query so that the search becomes more accurate.\n\nThere are three common Query Transformation techniques:\n\n** 1. Query Rewriting** \n\nThe LLM rewrites the user's question into a clearer and more detailed query.\n\n`User Question:`\"How do I fix it?\"\n\n`Rewritten Query:`\"How to troubleshoot Acme Router Model 4?\"\n\nNow the Vector Database has enough information to retrieve the correct document.\n\n**2. Query Expansion** \n\nSometimes different documents use different words for the same concept.\n\nThe LLM automatically adds related keywords and synonyms.\n\n`User Question:`\"Find HR policy\"\n\n`Expanded Query:`\"Find HR policy, Human Resources policy, Employee Handbook, PTO Rules\"\n\nThis increases the chances of finding relevant documents.\n\n**3. Multi-Query Search** \n\nInstead of searching with only one query, the LLM creates multiple versions of the same question.\n\nExample:\n\n• What is the refund policy?\n\n• How can I return a product?\n\n• Product return guidelines.\n\n• Return and refund rules.\n\nThe Vector Database searches using all these queries and combines the results.\n\nThis helps retrieve more complete and accurate information.\n\n**Why is Query Transformation Important?** \n\n• Improves search accuracy.\n\n• Handles vague or incomplete questions.\n\n• Retrieves more relevant documents.\n\n• Reduces the chances of missing useful information.\n\n**Key Idea:** Query Transformation improves the user's question **before** it reaches the Vector Database. A better query leads to better document retrieval, which ultimately produces more accurate RAG responses.",
            "tip": "Query Transformation usually adds a small amount of extra processing time, but it significantly improves retrieval accuracy in most RAG applications."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Retrieval Enhancement Review",
            "introduction": "Excellent! We have learned how Hybrid Retrieval combines multiple search methods and how Query Transformation improves user questions before retrieval begins.",
