        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Retrieval Evaluation: How do we know it works?",
            "explanation": "Excellent! We have learned how a RAG system retrieves the most relevant document chunks. But an important question still remains.\n\n`How do we know if our retrieval system is actually working well?`\n\nSuppose you improve your RAG pipeline by changing the embedding model, using a different chunking strategy, or adding a re-ranker. How can you tell whether these changes made the retrieval better?\n\nInstead of guessing, we use `Evaluation Metrics`. These metrics compare the documents retrieved by our RAG system with the documents that are actually correct.\n\nBefore understanding these metrics, let's first understand two important terms used in the code example.\n\n**Ground Truth (Qrels)**\n\n`Ground Truth` represents the correct answers. It is a manually created dataset that tells us which document should be retrieved for each query.\n\nFor example, in the code:\n\n• Query `q1` should retrieve `doc_A`.\n• Query `q2` should retrieve `doc_X`.\n\nThis serves as the reference against which the retrieval system is evaluated.\n\n**Run**\n\nA `Run` contains the documents retrieved by your RAG system along with their similarity scores.\n\nFor example, for query `q1`, the system retrieved:\n\n• `doc_C` (Score: 0.9)\n• `doc_A` (Score: 0.8)\n• `doc_B` (Score: 0.7)\n\nAlthough `doc_A` is the correct document, it appears at `Rank 2` instead of `Rank 1`.\n\nFor query `q2`, the system retrieved:\n\n• `doc_X` (Score: 0.9)\n• `doc_Y` (Score: 0.8)\n• `doc_Z` (Score: 0.7)\n\nHere, the correct document `doc_X` appears at `Rank 1`.\n\nNow we can evaluate how well the retrieval system performed.\n\n**Hit Rate**\n\n`Hit Rate` measures whether the correct document appears within the top `K` retrieved results.\n\nIn the example, both `doc_A` and `doc_X` appear within the Top 3 results.\n\nTherefore:\n\n`Hit Rate@3 = 1.0`\n\nThis means the retrieval system successfully found the correct document for every query. Notice that Hit Rate only checks whether the correct document was found. It does not consider its exact position.\n\n**MRR (Mean Reciprocal Rank)**\n\nSometimes retrieving the correct document is not enough. We also want it to appear as close to the top as possible.\n\n`MRR` measures the rank of the first correct document.\n\nIn our example:\n\n• For `q1`, the correct document is at `Rank 2`, so the score is `1/2 = 0.5`.\n• For `q2`, the correct document is at `Rank 1`, so the score is `1/1 = 1.0`.\n\nThe average of these scores gives:\n\n`MRR@3 = 0.75`\n\nA higher MRR means the correct document is consistently ranked near the top, allowing the LLM to generate more accurate responses.\n\n**Simple Rule:**\n\n• `Ground Truth (Qrels)` → The correct documents.\n• `Run` → The documents retrieved by the RAG system.\n• `Hit Rate` → Was the correct document retrieved?\n• `MRR` → How high was the correct document ranked?\n\nTake a look at the example below to see how the `ranx` library calculates these metrics automatically.",
            "example": "# Install Library\n# pip install ranx\n\nfrom ranx import Qrels, Run, evaluate\n\n# 1. Define Ground Truth Documents\nqrels_dict = {\n    \"q1\": {\"doc_A\": 1},\n    \"q2\": {\"doc_X\": 1}\n}\n\n# 2. Define Retrieved Documents\nrun_dict = {\n    \"q1\": {\n        \"doc_C\": 0.9,\n        \"doc_A\": 0.8,\n        \"doc_B\": 0.7\n    },\n    \"q2\": {\n        \"doc_X\": 0.9,\n        \"doc_Y\": 0.8,\n        \"doc_Z\": 0.7\n    }\n}\n\n# Create Qrels and Run objects\nqrels = Qrels(qrels_dict)\nrun = Run(run_dict)\n\n# 3. Calculate Hit Rate@3\nhit_rate = evaluate(qrels, run, \"hit_rate@3\")\n\nprint(f\"Hit Rate@3: {hit_rate}\")\n\n# Expected Output:\n# Hit Rate@3: 1.0\n\n\n# 4. Calculate Multiple Metrics\nresults = evaluate(qrels,run,[\"mrr@3\", \"hit_rate@1\"])\n\nprint(results)\n\n# Expected Output:\n# {\n#     'mrr@3': 0.75,\n#     'hit_rate@1': 0.5\n# }"
          }
        },
