        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Open Source Inference",
            "explanation": "Running models yourself — instead of paying per-token to OpenAI or Anthropic — offers three critical advantages: complete data privacy (nothing leaves your infrastructure), predictable costs at scale (GPU rental vs per-token pricing), and the ability to fine-tune and customize models. The tradeoff is operational complexity: you're now responsible for GPU management, model serving, scaling, and monitoring. This section covers the leading open-source inference engines and when to use each one.",
            "blocks": [
              {
                "type": "sevenPoint",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "number": 1,
                    "title": "vLLM",
                    "body": "The gold standard for high-throughput production serving. Uses PagedAttention — a memory management technique inspired by operating system virtual memory — to minimize GPU memory waste during batched inference. Supports continuous batching (new requests join the batch without waiting for current ones to finish), achieving 2-4x higher throughput than naive serving. Start with: `python -m vllm.entrypoints.openai.api_server --model meta-llama/Meta-Llama-3-8B`."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 2,
                    "title": "TGI (Text Generation Inference)",
                    "body": "Hugging Face's production inference server. Excellent integration with the HF Hub — specify any model ID and TGI downloads, quantizes, and serves it automatically. Supports tensor parallelism for multi-GPU serving, watermarking (embedding invisible signatures in generated text), and grammar-constrained generation (forcing output to match a JSON schema). Written in Rust for maximum performance."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 3,
                    "title": "Ollama",
                    "body": "The easiest way to run LLMs locally for development. Wraps llama.cpp in a Docker-like CLI: `ollama pull llama3:8b` then `ollama run llama3:8b`. Runs models in GGUF format with automatic quantization. Exposes an OpenAI-compatible API on localhost:11434. Not designed for production multi-user serving — use vLLM or TGI for that. Perfect for: local development, testing, and edge deployment."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 4,
                    "title": "SGLang",
                    "body": "A newer engine from UC Berkeley that focuses on program-level optimization. Instead of optimizing individual requests, SGLang optimizes entire programs (chains of LLM calls). Features RadixAttention for efficient prefix caching across related requests. Benchmark results show 2-5x speedup over vLLM for multi-turn conversations and agent workflows where many requests share common prefixes."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 5,
                    "title": "Quantization for Inference",
                    "body": "Running a 70B parameter model in full fp16 requires ~140GB of VRAM (two A100-80GB GPUs). Quantization reduces precision: GPTQ/AWQ (4-bit) reduces this to ~35GB (one A100). GGUF (4-bit) enables CPU inference with partial GPU offloading. Quality loss from 4-bit quantization is typically <1% on benchmarks. Always quantize for inference unless you have unlimited GPU budget."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 6,
                    "title": "Choosing the Right Engine",
                    "body": "Decision framework:\n\n1. Local development? → Ollama.\n2. Single-user prototype? → Ollama or vLLM.\n3. Production API with high throughput? → vLLM.\n4. Hugging Face ecosystem integration? → TGI.\n5. Complex agent workflows with shared prefixes? → SGLang.\n6. Edge deployment on consumer hardware? → Ollama + GGUF quantization."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 7,
                    "title": "Cost Comparison: Self-Hosted vs API",
                    "body": "At low volume (<1M tokens/day), APIs are cheaper — no GPU rental overhead. At high volume (>10M tokens/day), self-hosting becomes dramatically cheaper. Example: serving Llama-3-70B on 2x A100-80GB costs ~$6/hour ($4,300/month) for unlimited tokens. At OpenAI's GPT-4o pricing ($2.50/1M input tokens), 10M tokens/day = $750/month for input alone. The crossover point depends on your volume, latency requirements, and team's operational capability."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "visual",
          "content": {
            "title": "Open Source Inference Stack",
            "explanation": "Comparing vLLM, TGI, and Ollama architectures.",
            "image": "images/inference_stack.png"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Model Routing & Multi-Provider Strategies",
            "explanation": "Production AI applications rarely rely on a single model or provider. Model routing dynamically selects the best model for each request based on complexity, cost, latency, and availability — maximizing quality while minimizing cost.",
            "blocks": [
              {
                "type": "sevenPoint",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "number": 1,
                    "title": "The Model Router Pattern",
                    "body": "A classifier (either rule-based or LLM-based) analyzes each incoming request and routes it to the optimal model. Simple questions go to GPT-4o-mini ($0.15/1M tokens). Complex reasoning goes to Claude Opus ($15/1M tokens). The user always gets the best answer at the lowest cost."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 2,
                    "title": "Complexity Classification",
                    "body": "Train a small classifier (or use a fast LLM) to categorize requests into complexity tiers: Tier 1 (simple facts, greetings) → cheapest model. Tier 2 (summarization, translation) → mid-tier. Tier 3 (coding, math, multi-step reasoning) → premium model."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 3,
                    "title": "Fallback Chains",
                    "body": "If the primary model fails (rate limit, timeout, outage), automatically retry with a fallback provider. Chain: OpenAI GPT-4o → Anthropic Claude → Google Gemini → Local Llama. The user never sees an error — they get a seamless response."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 4,
                    "title": "Latency-Based Routing",
                    "body": "Monitor real-time latency across providers. Route to the provider with the lowest current latency for time-sensitive requests (chat, autocomplete). Use a weighted round-robin algorithm that favors faster providers."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 5,
                    "title": "Cost Tracking Per Provider",
                    "body": "Track token usage and costs across all providers in real-time. Build dashboards showing: cost per request, cost per user, daily spend by provider, and projected monthly costs. Set budget alerts at 50%, 80%, and 100% of monthly limits."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 6,
                    "title": "Prompt Compatibility",
                    "body": "Different models have different strengths and prompt formats. Maintain provider-specific prompt templates that optimize for each model's capabilities. A prompt optimized for GPT-4o may perform poorly on Claude without adaptation."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 7,
                    "title": "A/B Testing Across Models",
                    "body": "Route a percentage of production traffic to different models and compare quality metrics (user satisfaction, task completion rate). This data-driven approach reveals which models actually perform best for YOUR specific use cases, not just on general benchmarks."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "visual",
          "content": {
            "title": "Model Routing Strategy",
            "explanation": "A decision-tree flowchart showing how an intelligent router directs different types of user requests to appropriate LLMs based on cost and capability.",
            "image": "images/model_routing_strategy.png"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "content": {
            "title": "Model Selection Challenge",
            "description": "Your AI chatbot handles 50,000 requests/day. Analysis shows 70% are simple FAQs, 20% need summarization, and 10% require complex reasoning. Using GPT-4o for everything costs $2,000/day. How do you optimize?",
            "choices": [
              {
                "text": "Implement a model router: GPT-4o-mini for FAQs (70%), GPT-4o for summarization (20%), and Claude Opus for complex reasoning (10%). This cuts costs by ~80% while maintaining quality.",
                "score": 100,
                "outcome": "Correct! Model routing matches request complexity to model capability. The 70% of simple requests on a cheap model delivers massive savings with no quality loss."
              },
              {
                "text": "Switch everything to GPT-4o-mini to save money.",
                "score": 20,
                "outcome": "Too aggressive. GPT-4o-mini will fail on the 10% of complex reasoning tasks, degrading user experience for your hardest queries."
              },
              {
                "text": "Keep using GPT-4o for everything but negotiate a volume discount.",
                "score": 10,
                "outcome": "Volume discounts help but don't solve the fundamental waste of using a premium model for trivial tasks. Routing is a better approach."
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Model Routing Function",
            "language": "python",
            "question": "Write a Python function called `route_model` that takes a `user_prompt` string and returns the model name to use. If the prompt is under 500 characters, return 'gpt-4o-mini' (cheap, fast). If 500 or more characters, return 'gpt-4o' (powerful, expensive).",
            "starterCode": "def route_model(user_prompt: str) -> str:\n    # TODO: Route to 'gpt-4o-mini' if prompt length < 500 chars\n    # Route to 'gpt-4o' if prompt length >= 500 chars\n    pass",
            "solution": "def route_model(user_prompt: str) -> str:\n    if len(user_prompt) < 500:\n        return 'gpt-4o-mini'\n    return 'gpt-4o'",
            "hint": "Use `len(user_prompt)` to check the character count, then return the appropriate model name string based on a threshold of 500.",
            "explanation": "Model routing is one of the simplest and most effective cost optimization strategies. By routing simple, short queries to cheaper models, you can cut API costs by 50-80% with minimal quality loss on easy tasks.",
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "def\\s+route_model.*len\\(user_prompt\\).*500.*gpt-4o-mini.*gpt-4o"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Synthetic Data Generation with Frontier Models",
            "explanation": "High-quality training data is the bottleneck for fine-tuning. Synthetic data generation uses powerful frontier models (GPT-4o, Claude) to automatically create diverse, high-quality training datasets — dramatically reducing the cost and time of manual data annotation.",
            "blocks": [
              {
                "type": "sevenPoint",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "number": 1,
                    "title": "Why Synthetic Data?",
                    "body": "Manual annotation is expensive ($5-50 per example) and slow (weeks for 5,000 examples). Frontier models can generate 10,000 high-quality instruction-response pairs in hours for under $100. The quality of synthetic data from GPT-4o or Claude often matches or exceeds crowdsourced annotations."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 2,
                    "title": "The Self-Instruct Pipeline",
                    "body": "Start with 50-100 manually written 'seed' examples that represent your target task. Feed these to a frontier model with the instruction: 'Generate 20 new, diverse examples similar to these seeds.' Filter for quality, add to the seed pool, and repeat. This bootstraps thousands of examples from a small starting set."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 3,
                    "title": "Persona-Driven Generation",
                    "body": "To maximize diversity, assign the frontier model different personas: 'You are a confused beginner asking about X', 'You are an expert seeking edge-case clarification about X', 'You are a non-native English speaker asking about X.' Different personas produce naturally diverse question styles and difficulty levels."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 4,
                    "title": "Multi-Turn Conversation Synthesis",
                    "body": "For chat fine-tuning, generate complete multi-turn conversations. Prompt the frontier model with a scenario and two roles (user and assistant). It produces realistic back-and-forth dialogues that teach the fine-tuned model how to handle follow-up questions, clarifications, and topic shifts."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 5,
                    "title": "Quality Filtering Pipeline",
                    "body": "Not all synthetic data is good. Implement a filtering pipeline:\n\n1. LLM-as-Judge scores each example on relevance, accuracy, and formatting.\n2. Deduplication using embedding similarity (remove near-duplicates).\n3. Human spot-check of a random 5% sample. Discard examples scoring below your quality threshold."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 6,
                    "title": "Domain-Specific Augmentation",
                    "body": "Feed the frontier model your domain documents (product docs, legal contracts, medical guidelines) as context, then ask it to generate Q&A pairs grounded in those documents. This produces domain-accurate synthetic data that would be impossible to generate without the source material."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 7,
                    "title": "Ethical Considerations",
                    "body": "Check the terms of service of the frontier model provider — some prohibit using outputs to train competing models. Ensure synthetic data doesn't encode biases from the generator model. Always validate factual claims in synthetic data against ground truth sources."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
