        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Beyond LoRA — Other PEFT Methods You Should Know",
            "explanation": "Great, so you now deeply understand LoRA and QLoRA — the sticky note approach to fine-tuning. And honestly, for 90% of your projects, LoRA is all you'll ever need.\n\nBut as a professional AI engineer, you should know that LoRA isn't the ONLY Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning method out there. There's a whole family of PEFT techniques, each with its own philosophy. Think of it like knowing that besides sticky notes, you could also use highlighters, bookmarks, or margin annotations.\n\nLet me walk you through the most important ones so you can make an informed choice.\n\n---\n\n**🔵 Method 1: Prefix Tuning — Prepending Learnable Context**\n\n**Example Analogy:** Imagine every time you open a chapter of the textbook, there's a sticky header at the top that says 'Read this chapter as if you're a doctor.' The header doesn't change the text below — it just changes HOW you interpret it.\n\nPrefix Tuning adds a set of **learnable vectors** (soft prompts) to the beginning of each transformer layer's key and value matrices. These vectors act as a 'context layer' that guides the model's attention without touching the actual weights.\n\n```python\nfrom peft import PrefixTuningConfig\n\nconfig = PrefixTuningConfig(\n    task_type=\"CAUSAL_LM\",\n    num_virtual_tokens=20,   # How many 'prefix' tokens to add\n    prefix_projection=True    # Use a small MLP to generate prefixes\n)\n```\n\n✅ Very memory-efficient — even less than LoRA\n❌ Lower quality than LoRA for most tasks\n📌 Best for: Multi-task scenarios where you want different prefixes for different tasks\n\n---\n\n**🟢 Method 2: Prompt Tuning — The Simplest PEFT Method**\n\n**Example Analogy:** Instead of sticky notes inside the book, you write a magic sentence on a card and always hold it in front of the book while reading. The card changes how you interpret everything.\n\nPrompt Tuning adds learnable 'soft tokens' at the input level — before your actual text. These tokens are optimized during training but don't correspond to any real words. It's the simplest PEFT method.\n\n```python\nfrom peft import PromptTuningConfig, PromptTuningInit\n\nconfig = PromptTuningConfig(\n    task_type=\"CAUSAL_LM\",\n    num_virtual_tokens=8,\n    prompt_tuning_init=PromptTuningInit.TEXT,\n    prompt_tuning_init_text=\"Classify the sentiment of this text:\"\n)\n```\n\n✅ Extremely lightweight — only a few thousand parameters\n❌ Significantly lower quality than LoRA\n📌 Best for: Very simple classification tasks or when you have almost zero compute\n\n---\n\n**🟡 Method 3: IA³ (Infused Adapter by Inhibiting and Amplifying Inner Activations)**\n\n**Example Analogy:** Instead of adding sticky notes, you adjust the VOLUME of different parts of the textbook. Some paragraphs get louder (amplified), others get quieter (inhibited). The text stays the same, but the emphasis changes.\n\nIA³ multiplies the model's key, value, and feedforward activations by learnable scaling vectors. It's incredibly parameter-efficient — even more than LoRA.\n\n✅ Fewest trainable parameters of any PEFT method\n✅ Almost zero inference overhead\n❌ Less expressive than LoRA for complex tasks\n📌 Best for: When inference speed is critical and the task is simple\n\n---\n\n**🔴 Method 4: AdaLoRA — Adaptive Rank for LoRA**\n\n**Example Analogy:** Regular LoRA uses the same size sticky note everywhere. But some chapters need bigger notes than others! AdaLoRA automatically figures out which layers need big sticky notes and which need small ones.\n\nAdaLoRA dynamically allocates different ranks to different layers based on their importance. Important layers get more capacity (higher rank), unimportant layers get less.\n\n```python\nfrom peft import AdaLoraConfig\n\nconfig = AdaLoraConfig(\n    task_type=\"CAUSAL_LM\",\n    init_r=12,         # Starting rank\n    target_r=4,        # Minimum rank after pruning\n    lora_alpha=32,\n    target_modules=[\"q_proj\", \"v_proj\"]\n)\n```\n\n✅ Automatically optimizes rank allocation\n✅ Often better quality than standard LoRA with fewer total parameters\n❌ More complex to configure\n📌 Best for: When you want to squeeze maximum quality from minimum parameters\n\n---\n\n**📊 The PEFT Comparison Cheat Sheet:**\n\n| Method | Parameters | Quality | Speed | Best For |\n|--------|-----------|---------|-------|----------|\n| **LoRA** | ~0.1% | ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ | Fast | 90% of all tasks (DEFAULT) |\n| **QLoRA** | ~0.1% | ⭐⭐⭐⭐ | Fast | Budget-constrained training |\n| **Prefix Tuning** | ~0.01% | ⭐⭐⭐ | Very fast | Multi-task with shared base |\n| **Prompt Tuning** | ~0.001% | ⭐⭐ | Fastest | Simple classification only |\n| **IA³** | ~0.001% | ⭐⭐⭐ | Fastest | Speed-critical inference |\n| **AdaLoRA** | ~0.05% | ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ | Fast | Optimal rank allocation |\n\n**🔑 The Golden Rule:** Start with LoRA (or QLoRA). Only explore other methods if you have a very specific constraint — like needing the absolute minimum inference latency (IA³) or running dozens of task-specific adapters (Prefix Tuning).\n\nNow that you understand the full PEFT landscape, let's dive deeper into the SFT training process itself — the foundation that all fine-tuning builds on."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
