        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Alignment — Teaching the Model Right from Wrong",
            "explanation": "So far, we've talked about **Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT)** — showing the model examples of 'here's a question, here's how to answer it.' That teaches the model HOW to talk.\n\nBut there's a problem. A model trained only with SFT might:\n• Generate technically correct but unhelpful responses\n• Produce offensive or harmful content when cleverly prompted\n• Be overly verbose (talks too much) or unnaturally brief\n• Sound robotic or generic\n\nSFT teaches the model WHAT to say, but it doesn't teach it what is GOOD versus what is BAD. For that, we need **Alignment**.\n\n**Example Analogy:** Imagine teaching a child to draw. SFT is like showing them 100 example drawings — they learn to draw. But Alignment is like sitting next to them and saying 'This drawing is great! This one is not so good. Can you see why?' The child learns TASTE — what makes a drawing good or bad.\n\nLet me walk you through the three generations of alignment techniques. This is how the field evolved:\n\n<img src=\"/images/m10 fine tunning/m10_alignment_process.png\" alt=\"m10 alignment process\" />\n\n---\n\n**Generation 1: RLHF (Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback) — 2022**\n\nThis is what OpenAI used to create ChatGPT. It's powerful but incredibly complex.\n\nHow it works:\n1. First, train a **Reward Model** — a separate AI that learns to predict whether humans would like or dislike a response. You show it thousands of examples where humans rated responses as 'good' or 'bad.'\n2. Then use this Reward Model to SCORE your main model's outputs in real-time.\n3. Use **PPO** (a reinforcement learning algorithm) to nudge the main model toward higher-scoring responses.\n\nThe problem? You need THREE separate models running at the same time (the main model, the reward model, and a reference model). It's expensive, unstable, and hard to get right.\n\n---\n\n**Generation 2: DPO (Direct Preference Optimization) — 2023**\n\nDPO was a breakthrough. It asked: 'What if we skip the reward model entirely?'\n\nInstead of training a whole separate AI to judge quality, DPO just needs pairs of answers:\n• **Chosen** — the GOOD response (what we want)\n• **Rejected** — the BAD response (what we don't want)\n\nThe model mathematically adjusts its weights to prefer the good one. No reward model, no reinforcement learning — just clean, simple optimization.\n\n```python\n# DPO training data format — so simple!\n{\n    'prompt': 'How do I hack into a website?',\n    'chosen': 'I cannot help with that. Unauthorized access to systems is illegal.',\n    'rejected': 'Sure! First, find vulnerabilities using...'\n}\n```\n\n**Example Analogy:** Instead of hiring a separate art critic (reward model), you just show the child two drawings and ask 'Which one is better?' The child learns taste directly from comparing good vs bad.\n\n---\n\n**Generation 3: ORPO (Odds Ratio Preference Optimization) — 2024**\n\nORPO is the cutting edge. It combines SFT and alignment into a SINGLE training step. Instead of first doing SFT and then doing DPO (two separate training runs), ORPO does both simultaneously.\n\nThis saves massive compute time and often produces better results because the model learns 'how to respond' and 'what's good vs bad' at the same time."
          }
        },
