          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Scaling Up — Multi-GPU Training with DeepSpeed & FSDP",
            "explanation": "So far, we've been fine-tuning on a SINGLE GPU — which works great for models up to ~13B parameters with QLoRA. But what happens when you need to go bigger? What if you're fine-tuning a 70B model, or you need faster training on large datasets?\n\nThat's where **multi-GPU training** comes in, and there are two major frameworks you should know about.\n\n**🧒 The Kitchen Analogy:**\n\nImagine you're cooking for a wedding — 500 guests. One chef in one kitchen can't handle it. You need MULTIPLE chefs working in PARALLEL. But how do you coordinate them?\n\n• **DeepSpeed** is like having a head chef who splits the recipe into parts — each sous-chef handles one section, and the head chef combines everything.\n• **FSDP** is like having each chef carry a portion of the ingredients — nobody has to carry everything, and they pass ingredients to each other as needed.\n\n---\n\n**DeepSpeed ZeRO — The Industry Standard:**\n\nDeepSpeed, created by Microsoft, has three 'stages' of memory optimization:\n\n**ZeRO Stage 1:** Splits optimizer states across GPUs\n→ Saves ~4x optimizer memory\n→ Each GPU still has the full model\n\n**ZeRO Stage 2:** Splits optimizer states + gradients across GPUs\n→ Saves ~8x memory\n→ Each GPU still has the full model weights\n\n**ZeRO Stage 3:** Splits EVERYTHING — optimizer + gradients + model weights\n→ Maximum memory savings\n→ Can fine-tune 70B+ models on consumer GPUs!\n\n```python\n# DeepSpeed config file (ds_config.json)\n{\n    \"zero_optimization\": {\n        \"stage\": 2,\n        \"offload_optimizer\": {\n            \"device\": \"cpu\"\n        },\n        \"allgather_partitions\": true,\n        \"reduce_scatter\": true\n    },\n    \"fp16\": {\n        \"enabled\": true\n    },\n    \"train_batch_size\": \"auto\",\n    \"train_micro_batch_size_per_gpu\": \"auto\"\n}\n```\n\n**Using DeepSpeed with HuggingFace:**\n\n```python\nfrom transformers import TrainingArguments\n\ntraining_args = TrainingArguments(\n    output_dir=\"./results\",\n    deepspeed=\"ds_config.json\",  # Just point to the config!\n    per_device_train_batch_size=4,\n    gradient_accumulation_steps=8,\n    # ... rest of your args\n)\n```\n\nThat's it! Add one line (`deepspeed=\"ds_config.json\"`) and your training automatically distributes across all available GPUs.\n\n**Launch with multiple GPUs:**\n```bash\n# Using accelerate (recommended)\naccelerate launch --num_processes 4 train.py\n\n# Or using deepspeed directly\ndeepspeed --num_gpus 4 train.py\n```\n\n---\n\n**FSDP (Fully Sharded Data Parallel) — PyTorch Native:**\n\nFSDP is PyTorch's built-in alternative to DeepSpeed. It's simpler to configure:\n\n```python\nfrom transformers import TrainingArguments\n\ntraining_args = TrainingArguments(\n    output_dir=\"./results\",\n    fsdp=\"full_shard auto_wrap\",\n    fsdp_config={\n        \"fsdp_transformer_layer_cls_to_wrap\": \"LlamaDecoderLayer\"\n    },\n    # ... rest of your args\n)\n```\n\n**DeepSpeed vs FSDP — Which to Choose?**\n\n| Feature | DeepSpeed ZeRO | FSDP |\n|---------|---------------|------|\n| Provider | Microsoft | PyTorch (Meta) |\n| Ease of use | Config file + 1 line | 2 lines of code |\n| CPU offloading | Excellent | Good |\n| Community adoption | Higher (more tutorials) | Growing fast |\n| Best for | Maximum memory efficiency | Simplicity + PyTorch native |\n\n**🔑 The Practical Advice:**\n- **Single GPU (≤24GB):** Use QLoRA — no need for multi-GPU\n- **2-4 GPUs:** Use DeepSpeed ZeRO Stage 2 or FSDP\n- **8+ GPUs:** Use DeepSpeed ZeRO Stage 3 for maximum efficiency\n- **CPU offloading:** When GPU memory is still tight, offload optimizer states to CPU RAM\n\nMost fine-tuning projects never need multi-GPU. But knowing HOW to scale is what makes you a professional AI engineer — the one the team calls when the model is too big for a single machine."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
