          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Project Overview",
            "explanation": "**🎓 Capstone Project — Module 10: Fine-Tuning**\n\nCongratulations on completing the Fine-Tuning module! You have learned LoRA, QLoRA, data engineering, alignment, training pipelines, and evaluation. Now it is time to prove your skills by building a real project **on your own**.\n\n---\n\n**🎯 Problem Statement:**\n\nYou are hired as an AI Engineer at **TechMart**, a fictional online electronics store. TechMart's customer support team is overwhelmed — they handle 5,000+ tickets per day covering order tracking, returns, product questions, and complaints.\n\nYour mission: **Fine-tune an open-source LLM to act as TechMart's first-line customer support bot.** The bot must respond in TechMart's brand voice — friendly, professional, and solution-oriented — and handle the most common customer queries accurately.\n\n**The base GPT/LLM already knows general English, but it does NOT know:**\n- TechMart's 30-day return policy\n- TechMart's friendly-but-professional brand tone\n- How to format responses with order numbers and next steps\n- TechMart-specific product categories (laptops, phones, accessories)\n\n**Your job is to teach it all of this through fine-tuning.**\n\n---\n\n**📋 Deliverables (What You Must Submit):**\n\n| # | Deliverable | Description |\n|---|-----------|-------------|\n| 1 | Training Dataset | At least 100 conversation pairs in JSONL format |\n| 2 | Training Script | Complete Python script that loads model, applies LoRA, and trains |\n| 3 | Evaluation Report | Side-by-side comparison of base model vs fine-tuned model on 10 test queries |\n| 4 | Saved LoRA Adapter | The exported adapter weights (should be ~10-50 MB) |\n| 5 | Inference Script | A script that loads the adapter and generates responses |\n\n**⏱️ Estimated Time:** 4-6 hours\n\n**💡 Important:** The following slides provide guidance and hints, but **you must write all the code yourself.** This is YOUR project. Struggle with it, debug it, and learn from the errors — that is where the real learning happens."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Resources & Tools You Will Need",
            "explanation": "**🔗 Resources — Everything You Need Is Here**\n\nBelow are the exact resources you should use for this capstone. Bookmark all of them before you start.\n\n---\n\n**Base Model (Choose ONE):**\n| Model | Size | VRAM Needed | Link |\n|-------|------|------------|------|\n| meta-llama/Llama-3.2-1B | 1B params | ~6 GB (4-bit) | [Hugging Face](https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-3.2-1B) |\n| meta-llama/Llama-3.2-3B | 3B params | ~10 GB (4-bit) | [Hugging Face](https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-3.2-3B) |\n| microsoft/phi-2 | 2.7B params | ~8 GB (4-bit) | [Hugging Face](https://huggingface.co/microsoft/phi-2) |\n\n*Recommendation: Start with Llama-3.2-1B. You can always upgrade later.*\n\n**Libraries You Will Need:**\n```\npip install torch transformers peft trl datasets bitsandbytes accelerate\n```\n\n**Documentation Links:**\n- **PEFT (LoRA/QLoRA):** [https://huggingface.co/docs/peft](https://huggingface.co/docs/peft)\n- **TRL (SFTTrainer):** [https://huggingface.co/docs/trl](https://huggingface.co/docs/trl)\n- **Transformers:** [https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers](https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers)\n- **BitsAndBytes (Quantization):** [https://huggingface.co/docs/bitsandbytes](https://huggingface.co/docs/bitsandbytes)\n\n**Compute Platform (Choose ONE):**\n| Platform | GPU | Cost | Link |\n|---------|-----|------|------|\n| Google Colab | T4 (16 GB) | Free | [colab.research.google.com](https://colab.research.google.com) |\n| Kaggle Notebooks | P100 (16 GB) | Free | [kaggle.com/code](https://www.kaggle.com/code) |\n| Your Local Machine | Any NVIDIA GPU ≥ 8 GB | Free | — |\n\n**Reference Papers (Optional but recommended reading):**\n- LoRA Paper: [https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.09685](https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.09685)\n- QLoRA Paper: [https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14314](https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14314)\n\n**Hugging Face Access Token:**\nYou will need a Hugging Face token to download gated models like LLaMA. Generate one at: [https://huggingface.co/settings/tokens](https://huggingface.co/settings/tokens)"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Step 1: Create Your Training Dataset",
            "explanation": "**📝 Step 1: Build the TechMart Customer Support Dataset**\n\nThis is the most important step. Your fine-tuned model is only as good as your training data.\n\n---\n\n**Your Task:**\nCreate **at least 100 customer support conversation pairs** covering the following categories:\n\n| Category | Minimum Examples | Example Topics |\n|---------|-----------------|----------------|\n| Order Tracking | 20 | 'Where is my order?', shipping delays, tracking numbers |\n| Returns & Refunds | 20 | Return policy, how to return, refund timelines |\n| Product Questions | 20 | Laptop specs, phone comparisons, accessory compatibility |\n| Account Issues | 15 | Password reset, update email, payment methods |\n| Complaints | 15 | Damaged items, wrong product delivered, escalation |\n| General | 10 | Store hours, warranty info, promotions |\n\n**TechMart Brand Voice Guidelines:**\n- Always greet the customer warmly\n- Use the customer's name if provided\n- Reference order numbers in format `#TM-XXXX`\n- TechMart's return window is **30 days**\n- Always end with 'Is there anything else I can help you with?'\n- Tone: Friendly, professional, solution-oriented — never robotic\n\n**Format Requirements:**\nSave your data as a `.jsonl` file where each line is a JSON object. You must decide the right format yourself.\n\n**💡 Hints (don't skip these):**\n- Think about which chat template format your chosen base model expects. Revisit the **Chat Templates** lesson if unsure.\n- Include **varied phrasings** of the same question. A real customer might say 'Where's my stuff?' or 'Can you track order #TM-1234?' or 'My package hasn't arrived' — all mean the same thing.\n- Include some **edge cases**: angry customers, multi-part questions, typos.\n- Make sure your data is **internally consistent** — don't say the return window is 30 days in one example and 14 days in another.\n\n**🚫 Common Mistakes to Avoid:**\n- Do NOT use generic/templated responses — each answer should feel unique\n- Do NOT make responses too long (aim for 2-4 sentences per response)\n- Do NOT forget the system prompt — it defines the persona"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Step 2: Configure LoRA & Load the Model",
            "explanation": "**⚙️ Step 2: Set Up Your Model and LoRA Adapter**\n\nNow that your dataset is ready, you need to load your base model and attach a LoRA adapter.\n\n---\n\n**Your Task:**\nWrite a Python script that does the following (in order):\n\n1. **Load the base model with 4-bit quantization** — You will need `BitsAndBytesConfig`. Think about which quantization type and compute dtype to use.\n\n2. **Load the tokenizer** — Remember to handle the padding token. Most LLaMA models do not have a dedicated pad token — what should you set it to?\n\n3. **Prepare the model for QLoRA training** — There is a specific PEFT function for this. Check the PEFT docs.\n\n4. **Configure LoRA** — You must decide:\n   - What `rank (r)` to use? (Hint: 8-32 is typical for small datasets)\n   - What `lora_alpha` to set? (Hint: Revisit the hyperparameters lesson — what is the convention?)\n   - Which `target_modules` to apply LoRA to? (Hint: Attention layers are a must. Should you also include MLP layers?)\n   - What `lora_dropout` rate? (Hint: For small datasets, some regularization helps)\n\n5. **Apply the adapter and print trainable parameters** — You should see less than 1% of parameters are trainable.\n\n**💡 Hints:**\n- Revisit the **LoRA Engine** lessons if you are unsure about any hyperparameter\n- The `prepare_model_for_kbit_training()` function is critical — if you skip it, gradient checkpointing will not work and training will fail or use too much VRAM\n- Use `device_map='auto'` when loading the model to let the library handle GPU placement\n\n**🧪 Self-Check:**\nAfter this step, run `model.print_trainable_parameters()`. You should see something like:\n```\ntrainable params: X,XXX,XXX || all params: X,XXX,XXX,XXX || trainable%: 0.XX%\n```\nIf the trainable percentage is above 1%, something is wrong with your LoRA config.\nIf you get a CUDA out-of-memory error, reduce the rank or switch to a smaller model."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Step 3: Train Your Model",
            "explanation": "**🚀 Step 3: Set Up Training Arguments and Train**\n\nTime to train! This is where your dataset and LoRA adapter come together.\n\n---\n\n**Your Task:**\n\n1. **Load and format your dataset** — Load the JSONL file you created in Step 1. Split it into training (90%) and validation (10%) sets. Apply the correct chat template formatting.\n\n2. **Configure TrainingArguments** — You need to decide:\n   - How many **epochs** to train for? (Hint: With ~100 examples, 3-5 epochs is typical. More epochs = risk of overfitting)\n   - What **batch size** fits in your GPU memory?\n   - What **learning rate** to use? (Hint: The standard LoRA learning rate is in the range of 1e-4 to 3e-4)\n   - Should you use a **learning rate scheduler**? Which one?\n   - What **optimizer** saves the most memory? (Hint: Check what `paged_adamw_8bit` does)\n   - How often to **log** and **evaluate**?\n\n3. **Create the SFTTrainer** — Use the `SFTTrainer` from the `trl` library. You need to pass your model, training arguments, datasets, tokenizer, and set a reasonable `max_seq_length`.\n\n4. **Run training** — Call `trainer.train()` and monitor the output.\n\n**💡 Hints:**\n- If training loss is not going down after the first 20 steps, your learning rate is probably too low\n- If training loss drops to near-zero very quickly but validation loss goes up, you are overfitting — reduce epochs or increase dropout\n- Use `gradient_accumulation_steps` to simulate a larger batch size without using more VRAM\n- Enable `fp16=True` for mixed precision — it speeds up training and reduces memory\n\n**🧪 Self-Check:**\n- Training loss should steadily decrease across steps\n- Validation loss should decrease across epochs (or at least not increase)\n- Training should take approximately 10-30 minutes on a free T4/P100 GPU\n- After training completes, **save your LoRA adapter** — not the full model. The adapter should be ~10-50 MB."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Step 4: Evaluate & Submit",
            "explanation": "**📊 Step 4: Evaluate Your Fine-Tuned Model and Prepare Submission**\n\nTraining is done — but how do you know if your model actually learned anything useful? Time to evaluate.\n\n---\n\n**Your Task:**\n\n1. **Write an inference function** — Create a function that takes a customer question, formats it with the correct chat template, generates a response using your fine-tuned model, and returns the clean text.\n\n2. **Create 10 test queries** that your model has NEVER seen during training. Cover different categories:\n   - 2 order tracking questions\n   - 2 return/refund questions\n   - 2 product questions\n   - 2 complaint scenarios\n   - 2 general questions\n\n3. **Run each test query through BOTH the base model (without adapter) and your fine-tuned model.** Record the responses side by side.\n\n4. **Write an evaluation report** comparing the two. For each test query, rate the response on:\n\n| Criteria | Score (1-5) | What to Look For |\n|---------|------------|-----------------|\n| Tone Match | 1-5 | Does it sound like TechMart's brand voice? |\n| Accuracy | 1-5 | Are policies and facts correct? |\n| Helpfulness | 1-5 | Does it actually solve the problem? |\n| Format | 1-5 | Does it use order numbers, greetings, sign-offs? |\n| Conciseness | 1-5 | Is it the right length — not too short, not rambling? |\n\n---\n\n**📦 Final Submission Checklist:**\n\n- [ ] `techmart_dataset.jsonl` — Your training dataset (100+ examples)\n- [ ] `train.py` — Complete training script\n- [ ] `inference.py` — Script that loads adapter and generates responses\n- [ ] `evaluation_report.md` — Side-by-side comparison with scores\n- [ ] `adapter_weights/` — Saved LoRA adapter directory\n\n**🏆 Stretch Goals (Bonus Points):**\n- Add QLoRA 4-bit quantization to reduce VRAM usage\n- Build a simple Gradio or Streamlit chat UI for the bot\n- Push your adapter to Hugging Face Hub and share the link\n- Implement BERTScore or ROUGE evaluation metrics programmatically\n- Run A/B testing: show responses from both models to 3 friends and record which they prefer\n\n**🎉 Congratulations!**\nOnce you have completed all deliverables, you have successfully demonstrated end-to-end fine-tuning skills — from data engineering to training to evaluation. This is exactly the workflow used by AI engineers in production. Well done!"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "questions": [
            {
              "question": "Before launching a 2-hour fine-tuning run on your mini-project dataset, what is the single most important validation step? (Select the correct option)",
              "options": [
                "Decode the first 3 tokenized samples back to string to verify special tokens (<|eot_id|>) are formatted correctly",
                "Train for 100 epochs on 1 sample",
                "Convert the JSONL dataset to CSV",
                "Upgrade Python to 3.12"
              ],
              "answer": 0,
              "explanation": "Decoding tokenized samples back to string ensures special header and end-of-turn tokens match the model's exact chat template. A missing end-of-turn token will cause the model to generate infinitely without stopping."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Hands-On Sample Project: Fine-Tune LLaMA 3.2 on a Kaggle Mental Health FAQ Dataset",
            "explanation": "**Welcome to the Hands-On Fine-Tuning Project!**\n\nEverything you have learned so far — LoRA, QLoRA, data preparation, training pipelines, and evaluation — comes together here. In this project you will download a real open-source model from Hugging Face, grab a real dataset from Kaggle, fine-tune the model end-to-end, and test it with your own questions.\n\n**Example Analogy:** Remember all those Lego pieces we've been studying — the engine (LoRA), the fuel (data), the steering wheel (training arguments)? Now we're actually building the whole Lego car and driving it!\n\n**What We Are Building:**\nA Mental Health FAQ assistant that answers common mental health questions with empathetic, accurate responses. We will fine-tune Meta's LLaMA 3.2-1B model using LoRA so it learns the tone and knowledge of a curated FAQ dataset.\n\n**Why This Combination?**\n- **LLaMA 3.2-1B** — Small enough to fine-tune on a single free-tier GPU (Google Colab T4 / Kaggle P100), yet powerful enough to produce coherent answers.\n- **Kaggle Mental Health FAQ** — A clean, real-world dataset with question-answer pairs that is perfect for supervised fine-tuning.\n\n**The Complete Roadmap:**\n```\nStep 1 → Set up environment and install libraries\nStep 2 → Download the LLaMA 3.2-1B model from Hugging Face\nStep 3 → Download and prepare the Kaggle dataset\nStep 4 → Configure LoRA and training arguments\nStep 5 → Train the model\nStep 6 → Test the fine-tuned model with new questions\n```\n\nLet's begin step by step. Follow along in a Google Colab or Kaggle notebook."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Resources & Links",
            "explanation": "**Everything You Need — Bookmarked and Ready**\n\nBefore we start coding, here are all the resources and links you will use throughout this project. Bookmark them now so you can reference them as we go.\n\n**Model & Dataset:**\n- **Model:** [meta-llama/Llama-3.2-1B](https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-3.2-1B) — Our base model on Hugging Face\n- **Dataset:** [Mental Health FAQ for Chatbot](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/narendrageek/mental-health-faq-for-chatbot) — Our training data on Kaggle\n\n**Research Papers:**\n- **LoRA Paper:** [https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.09685](https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.09685) — The original Low-Rank Adaptation paper by Hu et al.\n- **QLoRA Paper:** [https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14314](https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14314) — Quantized LoRA paper by Dettmers et al.\n\n**Library Documentation:**\n- **PEFT Library Docs:** [https://huggingface.co/docs/peft](https://huggingface.co/docs/peft) — Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning library\n- **TRL Library Docs:** [https://huggingface.co/docs/trl](https://huggingface.co/docs/trl) — Transformer Reinforcement Learning library (contains SFTTrainer)\n\n**Platform Links:**\n- **Google Colab:** [https://colab.research.google.com](https://colab.research.google.com) — Free T4 GPU runtime\n- **Kaggle Notebooks:** [https://www.kaggle.com/code](https://www.kaggle.com/code) — Free P100 GPU runtime\n- **Hugging Face Tokens:** [https://huggingface.co/settings/tokens](https://huggingface.co/settings/tokens) — Generate your access token here\n\nKeep these links handy — you will use them in the steps ahead."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Next Steps After This Project",
            "explanation": "**Where to Go After You Complete This Project**\n\nOnce you have successfully fine-tuned LLaMA 3.2-1B on the Mental Health FAQ dataset, here are five powerful next steps to level up your skills:\n\n**1. Try a Larger Model**\nUpgrade from `meta-llama/Llama-3.2-1B` to `meta-llama/Llama-3.2-3B` for better quality responses. The 3B model produces more nuanced and detailed answers — and with QLoRA, it still fits on a free-tier GPU!\n\n**2. Use a Bigger Dataset**\nExplore Kaggle for larger mental health or conversational datasets. More training data = better generalization. Try datasets with 5,000+ examples instead of the ~100 we used here.\n\n**3. Experiment with LoRA Hyperparameters**\nChange the rank (`r=8, 32, 64`), adjust `lora_alpha`, or target different modules. Compare the results! This hands-on experimentation builds real intuition for what works and what doesn't.\n\n**4. Deploy with a Gradio UI**\nBuild a chat interface so anyone can use your model:\n```python\n!pip install gradio\nimport gradio as gr\n\ndef chat(message, history):\n    return generate_response(message, model, tokenizer)\n\ngr.ChatInterface(chat, title='Mental Health FAQ Assistant').launch()\n```\nThis turns your fine-tuned model into a web app in 5 lines of code!\n\n**5. Push Your Adapter to Hugging Face Hub**\nShare your fine-tuned adapter with the community:\n```python\nmodel.push_to_hub('your-username/llama-3.2-1b-mental-health-faq')\ntokenizer.push_to_hub('your-username/llama-3.2-1b-mental-health-faq')\n```\nOther developers can then load your adapter and use it instantly — contributing to the open-source AI ecosystem.\n\nNow let's start building! Head to the next slide to set up your environment."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Step 1: Environment Setup & Library Installation",
            "explanation": "**Setting Up Your Workspace**\n\nYou can run this project on **Google Colab (free T4 GPU)**, **Kaggle Notebooks (free P100 GPU)**, or your own machine with an NVIDIA GPU (≥ 8 GB VRAM).\n\n**Install Required Libraries:**\n\n```python\n# Run this cell first — installs everything you need\n!pip install -q \\\\\n    torch \\\\\n    transformers==4.44.0 \\\\\n    peft==0.12.0 \\\\\n    trl==0.9.6 \\\\\n    datasets==2.20.0 \\\\\n    bitsandbytes==0.43.1 \\\\\n    accelerate==0.33.0 \\\\\n    kagglehub\n```\n\n**What Each Library Does (Quick Reference):**\n| Library | Purpose |\n|---------|--------|\n| `transformers` | Load and work with LLaMA and other Hugging Face models |\n| `peft` | Provides LoRA and QLoRA adapters for parameter-efficient fine-tuning |\n| `trl` | Supervised Fine-Tuning Trainer (SFTTrainer) built on top of transformers |\n| `datasets` | Load and manipulate datasets in Hugging Face format |\n| `bitsandbytes` | 4-bit quantization so large models fit in small GPUs |\n| `accelerate` | Handles distributed training and mixed precision |\n| `kagglehub` | Download datasets directly from Kaggle |\n\n**Verify GPU Availability:**\n\n```python\nimport torch\nprint(f'PyTorch version: {torch.__version__}')\nprint(f'CUDA available: {torch.cuda.is_available()}')\nif torch.cuda.is_available():\n    print(f'GPU: {torch.cuda.get_device_name(0)}')\n    print(f'VRAM: {torch.cuda.get_device_properties(0).total_mem / 1e9:.1f} GB')\n```\n\nIf CUDA is not available, switch to a GPU runtime in Colab (Runtime → Change runtime type → T4 GPU) or enable GPU in Kaggle (Settings → Accelerator → GPU P100)."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Step 2: Download LLaMA 3.2-1B from Hugging Face",
            "explanation": "**Getting the Base Model**\n\nWe will use **Meta LLaMA 3.2-1B** — a small but capable open-source model released by Meta under a permissive license.\n\n**Important — Hugging Face Access Token:**\nLLaMA models require you to accept Meta's license agreement. Here is what to do:\n\n1. Go to https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-3.2-1B\n2. Click **'Request access'** and accept the license terms\n3. Go to https://huggingface.co/settings/tokens and create a new token\n4. Copy your token — you will use it below\n\n**Login to Hugging Face:**\n\n```python\nfrom huggingface_hub import login\n\n# Paste your Hugging Face token here\nlogin(token='hf_YOUR_TOKEN_HERE')\n```\n\n**Download and Load the Model in 4-bit (QLoRA):**\n\n```python\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer, BitsAndBytesConfig\nimport torch\n\n# Model identifier on Hugging Face\nMODEL_ID = 'meta-llama/Llama-3.2-1B'\n\n# 4-bit quantization config — allows the 1B model to fit in ~2 GB VRAM\nbnb_config = BitsAndBytesConfig(\n    load_in_4bit=True,\n    bnb_4bit_quant_type='nf4',               # NormalFloat4 — best for LLMs\n    bnb_4bit_compute_dtype=torch.float16,     # Compute in FP16 for speed\n    bnb_4bit_use_double_quant=True,           # Nested quantization saves more memory\n)\n\n# Download and load model\nprint('Downloading LLaMA 3.2-1B from Hugging Face...')\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\n    MODEL_ID,\n    quantization_config=bnb_config,\n    device_map='auto',\n    trust_remote_code=True,\n)\n\n# Download tokenizer\ntokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(MODEL_ID, trust_remote_code=True)\ntokenizer.pad_token = tokenizer.eos_token\ntokenizer.padding_side = 'right'\n\nprint(f'Model loaded! Parameters: {model.num_parameters():,}')\nprint(f'Vocabulary size: {tokenizer.vocab_size:,}')\n```\n\n**What Just Happened (let me break it down for you):**\n- The model weights were downloaded from Hugging Face (~2 GB download in 4-bit)\n- `BitsAndBytesConfig` quantized the model to 4-bit precision — this is what makes fine-tuning possible on a free GPU\n- The tokenizer converts text into token IDs that the model understands\n- We set `pad_token = eos_token` because LLaMA doesn't have a dedicated padding token\n\n**Test the Base Model Before Fine-Tuning:**\n\n```python\n# Let us see how the base model responds BEFORE fine-tuning\nprompt = 'What are some coping strategies for anxiety?'\ninputs = tokenizer(prompt, return_tensors='pt').to(model.device)\n\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    outputs = model.generate(**inputs, max_new_tokens=150, temperature=0.7)\n\nprint('=== BASE MODEL RESPONSE (before fine-tuning) ===')\nprint(tokenizer.decode(outputs[0], skip_special_tokens=True))\n```\n\nNotice how the base model gives a generic or rambling response. After fine-tuning it will give focused, empathetic answers. Keep this output saved — you'll want to compare it later!"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Step 3: Download & Prepare the Kaggle Mental Health FAQ Dataset",
            "explanation": "**Getting Real Training Data from Kaggle**\n\nWe will use the **Mental Health FAQ** dataset from Kaggle. It contains curated question-answer pairs about mental health topics like anxiety, depression, stress management, therapy, and self-care.\n\n**Kaggle Dataset Page:** https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/narendrageek/mental-health-faq-for-chatbot\n\n**Download Using kagglehub:**\n\n```python\nimport kagglehub\nimport pandas as pd\nimport os\n\n# Download the dataset\npath = kagglehub.dataset_download('narendrageek/mental-health-faq-for-chatbot')\nprint(f'Dataset downloaded to: {path}')\n\n# Find the CSV file\ncsv_file = None\nfor root, dirs, files in os.walk(path):\n    for f in files:\n        if f.endswith('.csv'):\n            csv_file = os.path.join(root, f)\n            break\n\nprint(f'CSV file: {csv_file}')\n\n# Load into pandas\ndf = pd.read_csv(csv_file)\nprint(f'\\nDataset shape: {df.shape}')\nprint(f'Columns: {list(df.columns)}')\ndf.head(3)\n```\n\n**Explore and Clean:**\n\n```python\nprint(f'Total FAQ pairs: {len(df)}')\nprint(f'\\nSample Question:\\n{df.iloc[0][\"Questions\"]}')\nprint(f'\\nSample Answer:\\n{df.iloc[0][\"Answers\"]}')\n\n# Drop rows with missing questions or answers\ndf = df.dropna(subset=['Questions', 'Answers'])\nprint(f'Clean rows: {len(df)}')\n```\n\n**Format for Fine-Tuning (The Critical Step!):**\n\nRemember chat templates from earlier? This is where they matter. We need to convert the raw CSV into the format LLaMA expects:\n\n```python\nfrom datasets import Dataset\n\ndef format_chat(row):\n    \"\"\"Convert a FAQ row into LLaMA chat format.\"\"\"\n    chat = f\"\"\"<|begin_of_text|><|start_header_id|>system<|end_header_id|>\nYou are a compassionate and knowledgeable mental health assistant. Provide supportive, accurate, and empathetic responses to mental health questions. Always encourage professional help when appropriate.<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n{row['Questions'].strip()}<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n{row['Answers'].strip()}<|eot_id|>\"\"\"\n    return {'text': chat}\n\n# Apply formatting\nformatted = df.apply(format_chat, axis=1, result_type='expand')\ndataset = Dataset.from_pandas(formatted)\n\n# Train/validation split (90/10)\ndataset = dataset.train_test_split(test_size=0.1, seed=42)\nprint(f'Training examples: {len(dataset[\"train\"])}')\nprint(f'Validation examples: {len(dataset[\"test\"])}')\n\n# Preview one formatted example\nprint('\\n=== Formatted Example ===')\nprint(dataset['train'][0]['text'][:500])\n```\n\n**Why This Format Matters:**\n- The special tokens (`<|begin_of_text|>`, `<|start_header_id|>`, `<|eot_id|>`) are LLaMA's chat template markers\n- The model learns to associate the system prompt with tone, the user header with questions, and the assistant header with ideal answers\n- If you use the wrong template the model will produce garbage — this is the #1 mistake in fine-tuning!"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Step 4: Configure LoRA & Training Arguments",
            "explanation": "**Setting Up the LoRA Adapter**\n\nNow we configure exactly which parts of the model to fine-tune and how aggressively to train. This is where all those hyperparameter lessons pay off!\n\n```python\nfrom peft import LoraConfig, get_peft_model, TaskType, prepare_model_for_kbit_training\n\n# Prepare model for QLoRA training\n# This freezes the base weights and enables gradient checkpointing\nmodel = prepare_model_for_kbit_training(model)\n\n# LoRA Configuration — using our recommended defaults\nlora_config = LoraConfig(\n    task_type=TaskType.CAUSAL_LM,\n    r=16,                    # Rank — higher = more capacity, more VRAM\n    lora_alpha=32,           # Scaling factor — 2x the rank (our convention!)\n    lora_dropout=0.05,       # Dropout for regularization\n    target_modules=[\n        'q_proj',            # Query projection in attention\n        'k_proj',            # Key projection in attention\n        'v_proj',            # Value projection in attention\n        'o_proj',            # Output projection in attention\n        'gate_proj',         # MLP gate projection\n        'up_proj',           # MLP up projection\n        'down_proj',         # MLP down projection\n    ],\n    bias='none',\n)\n\n# Apply LoRA adapter to the model\nmodel = get_peft_model(model, lora_config)\n\n# Check trainable parameters\nmodel.print_trainable_parameters()\n# Expected: trainable params: 3,407,872 || all params: 1,239,503,872 || trainable%: 0.27%\n```\n\n**See that output?** Only 0.27% of the model is being trained! We're saving 99.73% of the compute.\n\n**Configure Training Arguments:**\n\n```python\nfrom transformers import TrainingArguments\n\ntraining_args = TrainingArguments(\n    output_dir='./llama-mental-health-finetuned',\n    num_train_epochs=3,                    # 3 passes over the dataset\n    per_device_train_batch_size=4,         # Batch size per GPU\n    per_device_eval_batch_size=4,\n    gradient_accumulation_steps=4,         # Effective batch size = 4 × 4 = 16\n    learning_rate=2e-4,                    # Standard for LoRA fine-tuning\n    weight_decay=0.01,                     # Light regularization\n    warmup_ratio=0.03,                     # Warm up for first 3% of steps\n    lr_scheduler_type='cosine',            # Cosine decay\n    logging_steps=10,                      # Log every 10 steps\n    save_strategy='epoch',                 # Save checkpoint after each epoch\n    evaluation_strategy='epoch',           # Evaluate after each epoch\n    fp16=True,                             # Mixed precision for speed\n    report_to='none',                      # Disable wandb for simplicity\n    optim='paged_adamw_8bit',              # Memory-efficient optimizer\n    max_grad_norm=0.3,                     # Gradient clipping\n    group_by_length=True,                  # Group similar-length examples\n)\n```\n\n**Why these specific values?**\n| Hyperparameter | Value | Why |\n|---------------|-------|-----|\n| `learning_rate` | 2e-4 | Standard for LoRA — too high = instability, too low = slow |\n| `num_train_epochs` | 3 | Enough to learn without overfitting on a small dataset |\n| `gradient_accumulation_steps` | 4 | Simulates larger batch without more VRAM |\n| `lr_scheduler_type` | cosine | Smoothly reduces learning rate — prevents catastrophic forgetting |\n| `optim` | paged_adamw_8bit | Uses 8-bit optimizer states — saves ~50% optimizer memory |"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Step 5: Train the Model",
            "explanation": "**This is it — the moment of truth! Let's launch fine-tuning!**\n\n```python\nfrom trl import SFTTrainer\n\n# Create the trainer\ntrainer = SFTTrainer(\n    model=model,\n    args=training_args,\n    train_dataset=dataset['train'],\n    eval_dataset=dataset['test'],\n    tokenizer=tokenizer,\n    max_seq_length=512,           # Maximum sequence length\n    dataset_text_field='text',    # Column name containing formatted text\n    packing=False,                # Do not pack multiple examples into one sequence\n)\n\n# Start training!\nprint('Starting fine-tuning...')\nprint(f'Training examples: {len(dataset[\"train\"])}')\nprint(f'Validation examples: {len(dataset[\"test\"])}')\nprint(f'Epochs: {training_args.num_train_epochs}')\nprint(f'Batch size: {training_args.per_device_train_batch_size}')\nprint(f'Gradient accumulation: {training_args.gradient_accumulation_steps}')\nprint(f'Effective batch size: {training_args.per_device_train_batch_size * training_args.gradient_accumulation_steps}')\nprint('---')\n\ntrain_result = trainer.train()\n\n# Print training metrics\nprint('\\n=== Training Complete ===')\nprint(f'Training Loss: {train_result.training_loss:.4f}')\nprint(f'Training Time: {train_result.metrics[\"train_runtime\"]:.0f} seconds')\nprint(f'Samples/second: {train_result.metrics[\"train_samples_per_second\"]:.1f}')\n```\n\n**What to Watch During Training:**\n\n```\nStep  | Training Loss | Validation Loss\n------|--------------|-----------------\n10    | 2.4500       |       —\n20    | 1.8200       |       —\n30    | 1.3100       |       —\nEpoch 1 end |    — | 1.2500\nEpoch 2 end |    — | 1.0800\nEpoch 3 end |    — | 1.0200   ← Good! Decreasing steadily\n```\n\nRemember the loss curve lesson:\n- **Training loss decreasing** → The model is learning ✓\n- **Validation loss decreasing** → It is generalizing, not just memorizing ✓\n- **Validation loss increasing while training loss decreases** → OVERFITTING! Stop! ✗\n\n**Save the Fine-Tuned LoRA Adapter:**\n\n```python\n# Save only the LoRA adapter weights (small — ~13 MB)\ntrainer.save_model('./llama-mental-health-finetuned/final')\ntokenizer.save_pretrained('./llama-mental-health-finetuned/final')\n\nprint('LoRA adapter saved!')\nprint('Adapter size is only ~13 MB — compared to the full 2+ GB model!')\n```\n\n**Training Time Estimates:**\n| Platform | GPU | Approximate Time |\n|---------|-----|------------------|\n| Google Colab (free) | T4 (16 GB) | ~15-25 minutes |\n| Kaggle Notebooks | P100 (16 GB) | ~10-20 minutes |\n| Local (RTX 3060) | 12 GB VRAM | ~10-15 minutes |\n| Local (RTX 4090) | 24 GB VRAM | ~3-5 minutes |"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Step 6: Test Your Fine-Tuned Model",
            "explanation": "**The Moment of Truth — Compare Base vs Fine-Tuned!**\n\nLet us load the fine-tuned adapter and test it with mental health questions the model has never seen.\n\n```python\nfrom peft import PeftModel\n\ndef generate_response(prompt, model, tokenizer, max_tokens=256):\n    \"\"\"Generate a response from the model.\"\"\"\n    system_prompt = (\n        'You are a compassionate and knowledgeable mental health assistant. '\n        'Provide supportive, accurate, and empathetic responses to mental health questions. '\n        'Always encourage professional help when appropriate.'\n    )\n    \n    full_prompt = f\"\"\"<|begin_of_text|><|start_header_id|>system<|end_header_id|>\n{system_prompt}<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n{prompt}<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\"\"\"\n    \n    inputs = tokenizer(full_prompt, return_tensors='pt').to(model.device)\n    \n    with torch.no_grad():\n        outputs = model.generate(\n            **inputs,\n            max_new_tokens=max_tokens,\n            temperature=0.7,\n            top_p=0.9,\n            do_sample=True,\n            repetition_penalty=1.1,\n        )\n    \n    response = tokenizer.decode(outputs[0], skip_special_tokens=True)\n    if 'assistant' in response:\n        response = response.split('assistant')[-1].strip()\n    return response\n\n# === Test with unseen questions ===\ntest_questions = [\n    'How can I manage panic attacks at work?',\n    'What is the difference between sadness and depression?',\n    'My friend told me they are feeling suicidal. What should I do?',\n    'Are there natural remedies for anxiety?',\n    'How do I know if I need therapy?',\n]\n\nprint('=' * 60)\nprint('FINE-TUNED MODEL RESPONSES')\nprint('=' * 60)\n\nfor i, question in enumerate(test_questions, 1):\n    print(f'\\nQ{i}: {question}')\n    print('-' * 40)\n    response = generate_response(question, model, tokenizer)\n    print(f'A: {response}')\n    print()\n```\n\n**Expected Improvements After Fine-Tuning:**\n\n| Aspect | Base Model | Fine-Tuned Model |\n|--------|-----------|------------------|\n| Tone | Generic, sometimes clinical | Warm, empathetic, supportive |\n| Structure | Rambling paragraphs | Clear, organized responses |\n| Accuracy | Surface-level advice | Specific coping strategies |\n| Safety | May miss crisis signals | Encourages professional help |\n| Consistency | Varies wildly | Reliable format and quality |\n\n**Push to Hugging Face Hub (Optional):**\n\n```python\n# Share your fine-tuned adapter with the world!\nmodel.push_to_hub('your-username/llama-3.2-1b-mental-health-faq')\ntokenizer.push_to_hub('your-username/llama-3.2-1b-mental-health-faq')\nprint('Model uploaded to Hugging Face Hub!')\n```\n\n**Congratulations!** You have just completed a real, end-to-end fine-tuning project. You downloaded an open-source LLM from Hugging Face, loaded a real dataset from Kaggle, fine-tuned using QLoRA, and tested the results — exactly the same workflow used by AI engineers in production."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Complete Project Code — Copy-Paste Ready",
            "explanation": "**Full Script — Run This in One Go**\n\nHere is the entire project combined into a single script you can paste into Google Colab or a Kaggle notebook:\n\n```python\n# ============================================================\n# SAMPLE PROJECT: Fine-Tune LLaMA 3.2-1B on Mental Health FAQs\n# ============================================================\n# Model:   https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-3.2-1B\n# Dataset: https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/narendrageek/mental-health-faq-for-chatbot\n# Method:  QLoRA (4-bit quantization + LoRA adapters)\n# Runtime: ~15-25 min on Google Colab T4 GPU (free tier)\n# ============================================================\n\n# --- Step 1: Install libraries ---\n# !pip install -q torch transformers==4.44.0 peft==0.12.0 trl==0.9.6 datasets==2.20.0 bitsandbytes==0.43.1 accelerate==0.33.0 kagglehub\n\nimport torch\nimport pandas as pd\nimport os\nfrom datasets import Dataset\nfrom transformers import (\n    AutoModelForCausalLM,\n    AutoTokenizer,\n    BitsAndBytesConfig,\n    TrainingArguments,\n)\nfrom peft import LoraConfig, get_peft_model, TaskType, prepare_model_for_kbit_training\nfrom trl import SFTTrainer\nimport kagglehub\n\n# --- Step 2: Login to Hugging Face ---\nfrom huggingface_hub import login\nlogin(token='hf_YOUR_TOKEN_HERE')  # Replace with your token\n\n# --- Step 3: Load model in 4-bit ---\nMODEL_ID = 'meta-llama/Llama-3.2-1B'\n\nbnb_config = BitsAndBytesConfig(\n    load_in_4bit=True,\n    bnb_4bit_quant_type='nf4',\n    bnb_4bit_compute_dtype=torch.float16,\n    bnb_4bit_use_double_quant=True,\n)\n\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\n    MODEL_ID, quantization_config=bnb_config, device_map='auto', trust_remote_code=True\n)\ntokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(MODEL_ID, trust_remote_code=True)\ntokenizer.pad_token = tokenizer.eos_token\ntokenizer.padding_side = 'right'\n\n# --- Step 4: Download Kaggle dataset ---\npath = kagglehub.dataset_download('narendrageek/mental-health-faq-for-chatbot')\ncsv_file = None\nfor root, dirs, files in os.walk(path):\n    for f in files:\n        if f.endswith('.csv'):\n            csv_file = os.path.join(root, f)\n            break\n\ndf = pd.read_csv(csv_file).dropna(subset=['Questions', 'Answers'])\nprint(f'Loaded {len(df)} FAQ pairs')\n\n# --- Step 5: Format data ---\ndef format_chat(row):\n    chat = f\"\"\"<|begin_of_text|><|start_header_id|>system<|end_header_id|>\nYou are a compassionate mental health assistant. Provide supportive, accurate responses.<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n{row['Questions'].strip()}<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n{row['Answers'].strip()}<|eot_id|>\"\"\"\n    return {'text': chat}\n\nformatted = df.apply(format_chat, axis=1, result_type='expand')\ndataset = Dataset.from_pandas(formatted).train_test_split(test_size=0.1, seed=42)\n\n# --- Step 6: Configure LoRA ---\nmodel = prepare_model_for_kbit_training(model)\nlora_config = LoraConfig(\n    task_type=TaskType.CAUSAL_LM, r=16, lora_alpha=32, lora_dropout=0.05,\n    target_modules=['q_proj','k_proj','v_proj','o_proj','gate_proj','up_proj','down_proj'],\n    bias='none',\n)\nmodel = get_peft_model(model, lora_config)\nmodel.print_trainable_parameters()\n\n# --- Step 7: Train ---\ntraining_args = TrainingArguments(\n    output_dir='./llama-mental-health-finetuned',\n    num_train_epochs=3, per_device_train_batch_size=4,\n    gradient_accumulation_steps=4, learning_rate=2e-4,\n    warmup_ratio=0.03, lr_scheduler_type='cosine',\n    logging_steps=10, save_strategy='epoch', evaluation_strategy='epoch',\n    fp16=True, report_to='none', optim='paged_adamw_8bit',\n    max_grad_norm=0.3, group_by_length=True, weight_decay=0.01,\n)\n\ntrainer = SFTTrainer(\n    model=model, args=training_args,\n    train_dataset=dataset['train'], eval_dataset=dataset['test'],\n    tokenizer=tokenizer, max_seq_length=512,\n    dataset_text_field='text', packing=False,\n)\n\ntrain_result = trainer.train()\nprint(f'Training loss: {train_result.training_loss:.4f}')\n\n# --- Step 8: Save adapter ---\ntrainer.save_model('./llama-mental-health-finetuned/final')\ntokenizer.save_pretrained('./llama-mental-health-finetuned/final')\n\n# --- Step 9: Test ---\ndef ask(question):\n    prompt = f'<|begin_of_text|><|start_header_id|>system<|end_header_id|>\\nYou are a compassionate mental health assistant.<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\\n{question}<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\\n'\n    inputs = tokenizer(prompt, return_tensors='pt').to(model.device)\n    with torch.no_grad():\n        out = model.generate(**inputs, max_new_tokens=256, temperature=0.7, top_p=0.9, do_sample=True)\n    resp = tokenizer.decode(out[0], skip_special_tokens=True)\n    return resp.split('assistant')[-1].strip() if 'assistant' in resp else resp\n\nfor q in ['How do I cope with anxiety?', 'What are signs of depression?', 'Should I see a therapist?']:\n    print(f'\\nQ: {q}\\nA: {ask(q)}\\n' + '='*50)\n\nprint('\\nDone! Your fine-tuned mental health assistant is ready.')\n```\n\n**Resources & Links:**\n- **Model:** https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-3.2-1B\n- **Dataset:** https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/narendrageek/mental-health-faq-for-chatbot\n- **LoRA Paper:** https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.09685\n- **QLoRA Paper:** https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14314\n- **PEFT Library Docs:** https://huggingface.co/docs/peft\n- **TRL Library Docs:** https://huggingface.co/docs/trl\n\n**Next Steps After This Project:**\n1. Try a larger model — `meta-llama/Llama-3.2-3B` for better quality\n2. Use a bigger dataset from Kaggle for more training data\n3. Experiment with different LoRA ranks (r=8, 32, 64) and compare results\n4. Deploy your model with a Gradio UI: `!pip install gradio` and build a chat interface\n5. Push your adapter to Hugging Face Hub to share with the community"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "title": "Production LLaMA 3.2 Deployment Architecture",
          "scenario": "You have finished fine-tuning LLaMA 3.2-1B on the Kaggle Mental Health dataset. You need to deploy it to a low-cost CPU instance for fast real-time chat response. What is the optimal export flow?",
          "options": [
            "Merge LoRA adapters into base model weights, then convert & quantize to 4-bit GGUF for llama.cpp / Ollama",
            "Keep LoRA adapter separate and load it dynamically over HTTP on every request",
            "Save raw PyTorch checkpoint to S3 and run on GPU",
            "Export as ONNX without merging weights"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Merging LoRA adapters into the base model produces a unified model file. Quantizing it to 4-bit GGUF allows lightning-fast CPU inference using llama.cpp with under 2GB RAM usage."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "M011-langchain-langgraph",
      "title": "Module 11: LangChain & LangGraph",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is LangChain?",
            "explanation": "Alright, let's zoom out for a second. In the last module, you became a fine-tuning engineer — you learned how to take a foundation model and specialize it for YOUR specific tasks. That's incredibly powerful.\n\nBut here's the thing. Fine-tuning isn't always the answer. Sometimes you don't WANT to retrain a model. Sometimes you just want to BUILD things with AI — connect it to tools, databases, APIs, and make it DO things in the real world.\n\nThat's exactly where **LangChain** comes in.\n\n**🧒 The Universal Remote Analogy (Explain Like I'm 5):**\n\nImagine you have a living room with a TV, a Blu-ray player, a soundbar, and smart lights. Each one has its OWN remote control. You've got 4 remotes on your coffee table, and every time you want to watch a movie, you're juggling remotes like a circus performer.\n\nLangChain is like a **universal remote**. One remote that controls ALL your devices — TV, Blu-ray, soundbar, lights — with one consistent set of buttons.\n\nIn AI terms, the \"devices\" are different LLM providers — OpenAI (GPT-4), Anthropic (Claude), Google (Gemini), Meta (Llama). Each one has a DIFFERENT API, different authentication, different response formats. Without LangChain, you'd be writing separate code for each one.\n\nWith LangChain? Write your code ONCE, then swap between GPT-4, Claude, Gemini, or Llama by changing a single line. Your entire app logic stays exactly the same.\n\n<img src=\"/images/m11 langchain/m11_langchain_overview.png\" alt=\"What is LangChain?\" className=\"w-full h-auto rounded-lg my-6 shadow-xl border border-[#30363d]\" />\n\n**But wait — LangChain does WAY more than just calling models.**\n\nThink of LangChain as a box of **Lego blocks for AI applications**. Each block is a different capability:\n\n• **Prompt Templates** — Reusable prompt designs (no more messy f-strings!)\n• **Output Parsers** — Get clean JSON instead of messy text paragraphs\n• **Chains (LCEL)** — Connect multiple steps into a pipeline using the `|` pipe operator\n• **Memory** — Make your chatbot actually remember conversations\n• **Tools** — Let LLMs search the web, run calculations, query databases\n• **Retrievers** — Fetch relevant documents for RAG systems\n\nEach block snaps together with the others. A PromptTemplate connects to a ChatModel, which connects to an OutputParser, and you've built a complete AI pipeline in 3 lines of code.\n\n**✅ When to Use LangChain:**\n• You need multi-provider support (OpenAI + Claude + Gemini)\n• You're building complex pipelines (prompt → model → parser → database)\n• You want memory/chat history management\n• You're building RAG systems\n• You need tool-calling agents\n\n**❌ When NOT to Use LangChain:**\n• Simple one-off API calls (just use `openai` directly)\n• Quick prototypes where you only need one model\n• Projects where you want zero dependencies\n\n**🔑 The Golden Rule of LangChain:**\nLangChain adds structure — but structure has a cost. For simple scripts, raw API calls are faster to write. Use LangChain when you need to BUILD something real.\n\nAlright, now that you understand WHY LangChain exists, let's get our hands dirty and actually use it. First up — talking to an LLM."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "questions": [
            {
              "question": "What is the PRIMARY problem that LangChain solves? (Select the correct option)",
              "options": [
                "It makes LLMs faster at generating text",
                "It provides a unified interface to work with multiple LLM providers",
                "It trains custom LLMs from scratch",
                "It replaces the need for prompt engineering"
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explanation": "LangChain's main value is provider abstraction — you write your code once and can swap between GPT-4, Claude, Gemini, or any other model by changing a single import."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "ChatModels: Your Universal LLM Interface",
            "explanation": "Now that you understand WHY LangChain exists (universal remote for AI, remember?), let's get hands-on. The very first thing you need to learn is HOW to talk to an LLM through LangChain.\n\n**🧒 The Phone Call Analogy:**\n\nThink of a ChatModel as a phone. You pick it up, dial a number (the provider), and start talking. The cool thing? Every phone works the same way — you pick up, dial, talk, hang up. Whether it's a Samsung, iPhone, or rotary phone, the basic operation is identical.\n\nLangChain's ChatModels work exactly like this. Whether you're talking to GPT-4, Claude, or Gemini, you always use the same method: `.invoke()`. The phone is different, but the way you use it is the same.\n\n<img src=\"/images/m11 langchain/m11_chatmodels_phone.png\" alt=\"ChatModels Phone Analogy\" />\n\n**Creating Your First ChatModel:**\n```python\nfrom langchain_openai import ChatOpenAI\n\nmodel = ChatOpenAI(model='gpt-4o-mini')\n```\n\nThat's it. One import, one line. Now `model` can talk to GPT-4.\n\n**🎯 Switching Providers (The Magic Trick):**\n\nThis is where LangChain shines. Want to use Claude instead of GPT-4? Change TWO things — the import and the class name:\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_anthropic import ChatAnthropic\n\nmodel = ChatAnthropic(model='claude-sonnet-4-20250514')\n```\n\nYour ENTIRE app code stays the same because both use `.invoke()`. This is the \"universal remote\" in action.\n\n**The invoke() Method — Your One Button:**\nEvery ChatModel has `.invoke()`. You pass in messages, it returns a response:\n\n```python\nresponse = model.invoke('Hello!')\nprint(response.content)  # 'Hi there! How can I help?'\n```\n\n**⚙️ Configuration Knobs:**\nYou can tune any ChatModel with:\n• `temperature` — creativity level (0 = focused & deterministic, 1 = creative & random)\n• `max_tokens` — maximum response length\n• `model` — which specific model to use\n\n```python\nmodel = ChatOpenAI(\n    model='gpt-4o-mini',\n    temperature=0.7,\n    max_tokens=500\n)\n```\n\nChatModels also support `.ainvoke()` (async for web servers), `.stream()` (tokens one at a time for that ChatGPT typing effect), and `.batch()` (multiple prompts at once). These work identically across ALL providers.\n\nNow let's try switching providers with actual code."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Switching LLM Providers",
            "difficulty": "Easy",
            "question": "One of LangChain's superpowers is provider switching. Below is code using OpenAI. Change it to use Anthropic's Claude instead. Update the import AND the model creation line. Use the model name 'claude-sonnet-4-20250514'.",
            "starterCode": "from langchain_openai import ChatOpenAI\nfrom langchain_core.messages import HumanMessage\n\n# Change this to use Anthropic instead of OpenAI\nmodel = ChatOpenAI(model='gpt-4o-mini', temperature=0)\n\nresponse = model.invoke([HumanMessage(content='Hello!')])",
            "solution": "from langchain_anthropic import ChatAnthropic\nfrom langchain_core.messages import HumanMessage\n\nmodel = ChatAnthropic(model='claude-sonnet-4-20250514', temperature=0)\n\nresponse = model.invoke([HumanMessage(content='Hello!')])",
            "hint": "Import ChatAnthropic from langchain_anthropic and use it instead of ChatOpenAI.",
            "explanation": "Notice how only the import and model creation changed. The .invoke() call and the message format are IDENTICAL. This is the power of LangChain's provider abstraction.",
