          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "LCEL — The Pipe Operator for AI",
            "explanation": "So far, you've been creating Prompts, Models, and Parsers as separate pieces — calling each one manually with `.invoke()`. It works, but it's like washing dishes by hand when you have a dishwasher.\n\n**🧒 The Assembly Line Analogy:**\n\nImagine a car factory. Instead of one person building an entire car alone, there's an assembly line:\n• Station 1: Attach the chassis (**PromptTemplate**)\n• Station 2: Install the engine (**ChatModel**)\n• Station 3: Paint and polish (**OutputParser**)\n\nEach station does ONE job, and the car flows automatically from one station to the next. Nobody carries anything — the conveyor belt handles it.\n\nLCEL (LangChain Expression Language) IS that assembly line. It lets you connect components using the `|` pipe operator, just like Unix pipes. Data flows left to right through each component:\n\n<img src=\"/images/m11 langchain/m11_lcel_pipeline.png\" alt=\"LCEL — The Pipe Operator for AI\" className=\"w-full h-auto rounded-lg my-6 shadow-xl border border-[#30363d]\" />\n\n```python\nchain = prompt | model | parser\nresult = chain.invoke({'topic': 'Python'})\n```\n\n**That single line replaces three separate `.invoke()` calls.** The prompt formats the input, the model generates a response, and the parser extracts clean text.\n\n**🔍 Understanding the Type Flow:**\nThis is crucial — know what flows between each station:\n\n• `dict` → PromptTemplate → `ChatPromptValue`\n• `ChatPromptValue` → ChatModel → `AIMessage`\n• `AIMessage` → StrOutputParser → `str`\n\n**🔧 RunnablePassthrough (Forwarding Data):**\nSometimes you need the original input alongside the processed output. Think of it as a bypass pipe:\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_core.runnables import RunnablePassthrough\n\nchain = {\n    'context': retriever,\n    'question': RunnablePassthrough()\n} | prompt | model | parser\n```\n\nThis is the foundation of every RAG chain. The dictionary sends data to two places simultaneously.\n\n**🔧 RunnableLambda (Custom Functions):**\nInsert any Python function into a chain:\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_core.runnables import RunnableLambda\n\ndef uppercase(text):\n    return text.upper()\n\nchain = prompt | model | parser | RunnableLambda(uppercase)\n```\n\n**🔑 The Golden Rule of LCEL:**\nLCEL isn't just syntax sugar — it gives you streaming, async execution, batching, and fallbacks FOR FREE. Every component you connect with `|` automatically supports all these features. You write a chain once, and it works everywhere — in a script, a web server, or a streaming API.\n\nTime to build your first chain!"
          }
        },
        {
