        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Tools: Giving LLMs Superpowers",
            "explanation": "Our chatbot can now remember conversations — great! But it's still stuck inside its own head. Ask it \"What's the weather in Tokyo?\" and it'll make something up, because it has NO access to the real world. Tools are how we fix that.\n\n**🧒 The Swiss Army Knife Analogy:**\n\nImagine the LLM is a brilliant detective — incredibly smart, great at reasoning, excellent at connecting dots. But they're locked in an office with no phone, no computer, no internet. They can THINK, but they can't DO anything outside the office.\n\nTools are like giving that detective a phone, a laptop, and a police database. Now they can CALL people, SEARCH records, and VERIFY facts. They're the same brilliant detective, but now they can interact with the real world.\n\n<img src=\"/images/m11%20langchain/m11_tools_agents.png\" alt=\"Tools: Giving LLMs Superpowers\" />\n\n**What is a Tool in LangChain?**\nA Tool is a Python function that the LLM can CHOOSE to call. The LLM doesn't execute the function directly — it tells LangChain \"I want to call `search_web` with query='AI news'\", and YOUR code executes it. Think of it as the detective writing a request slip, and an assistant carrying it out.\n\n**Creating a Tool — It's Just a Decorator:**\n```python\nfrom langchain_core.tools import tool\n\n@tool\ndef search_web(query: str) -> str:\n    '''Search the internet for current information.'''\n    return f'Search results for: {query}'\n```\n\nThe `@tool` decorator is all you need. Three things matter:\n1. **The function name** — the LLM sees this\n2. **The docstring** — the LLM reads this to decide WHEN to use the tool\n3. **The type hints** — the LLM uses these to know WHAT to pass\n\n**Binding Tools to a Model:**\n```python\nmodel_with_tools = model.bind_tools([search_web, calculator])\n```\n\nNow the LLM knows these tools exist and can request to call them.\n\n**⚠️ THE CRITICAL SAFETY RULE:**\nThe LLM NEVER executes code directly. It DECIDES what to call and with what arguments. YOUR application code runs the actual function. This is both a safety feature and a design pattern. The LLM is the brain; your code is the hands.\n\nLet's create our first tool."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Retrievers: Fetching Relevant Documents",
            "explanation": "Tools let the LLM take actions on demand — like giving it hands. But what about giving it access to YOUR data — documents, manuals, knowledge bases? That's where Retrievers come in. If Tools are the LLM's hands, Retrievers are its reference library.\n\n**🧒 The Librarian Analogy:**\n\nImagine you're writing a research paper. You walk up to the librarian (the Retriever) and ask: \"I need information about climate change in the Arctic.\" The librarian doesn't hand you EVERY book in the library — they find the 3 most relevant books and hand THOSE to you.\n\nThat's exactly what a Retriever does. It searches through your documents and returns only the most relevant ones for the current question.\n\n**🔄 How RAG Works (The Full Pattern):**\nRemember RAG from Module 8? Here's the 4-step flow:\n1. User asks a question\n2. **Retriever** finds relevant documents from your knowledge base\n3. Documents are stuffed into the prompt as context\n4. LLM generates an answer grounded in YOUR data\n\n**Vector Store Retrievers:**\nThe most common retriever uses vector embeddings — converting text into numbers and finding the \"closest\" matches:\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_community.vectorstores import FAISS\n\nvectorstore = FAISS.from_texts(documents, embeddings)\nretriever = vectorstore.as_retriever(search_kwargs={'k': 3})\n\n# Find 3 most relevant documents\ndocs = retriever.invoke('What is our refund policy?')\n```\n\n**🔑 Tool vs Retriever — Know the Difference:**\n• **Tool** — Executes an ACTION (search web, run calculation, send email)\n• **Retriever** — FETCHES DOCUMENTS (finds relevant text from a knowledge base)\n\nBoth extend what the LLM can access, but in fundamentally different ways. A tool DOES something; a retriever READS something.\n\nNow, before we move on to building autonomous agents, let me show you how all these LangChain pieces fit together architecturally."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Creating a Custom Tool",
