        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Expert Concept: The LangChain Package Ecosystem",
            "explanation": "You've now used ChatModels, Messages, PromptTemplates, OutputParsers, LCEL chains, Memory, Tools, and Retrievers. That's a LOT of building blocks! Before we move to building autonomous agents with LangGraph, let's zoom out and see how all these pieces are organized.\n\n**🧒 The Mall Analogy:**\n\nThink of LangChain as a shopping mall. The mall has different stores (packages), each specializing in something:\n\n• **langchain-core** — This is the mall's foundation — the floor, the walls, the elevators. It contains the base classes, the LCEL pipe operator, and interfaces for Messages, Prompts, Parsers, and Runnables. Everything else is built on top of this.\n\n• **langchain** — This is the main department store. It contains Chains, Agents, and higher-level orchestration. This is what most tutorials mean when they say \"LangChain.\"\n\n• **langchain-openai, langchain-anthropic, langchain-google-genai** — These are the brand-specific stores. Each wraps ONE LLM provider's API. You only install the ones you actually use.\n\n• **langchain-community** — This is the food court — a massive collection of community-maintained integrations. Vector stores, document loaders, tools, and more.\n\n• **langgraph** — This is the NEW wing of the mall, still under active development. It's where we build stateful, multi-step agents using a graph-based architecture. We're going there NEXT!\n\n**Why This Matters in Practice:**\nWhen you see `from langchain_core.prompts import ChatPromptTemplate`, you now know exactly which package to install: `pip install langchain-core`.\n\nWhen you see `from langchain_openai import ChatOpenAI`, you install: `pip install langchain-openai`.\n\nThis modular design keeps your project lean — you don't need to install everything, just the pieces you use.\n\nAlright, you've mastered all the LangChain building blocks. Now it's time for the really exciting stuff — building autonomous AI agents with LangGraph! Let's go."
          }
        },
