        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Easy Way: create_react_agent",
            "explanation": "You just built a complete agent from scratch — defining State, adding nodes, wiring conditional edges, and creating the tool-calling loop by hand. That's powerful knowledge, and you should be proud.\n\nBut here's the good news: for the MOST common pattern (LLM + Tools), LangGraph provides a shortcut that does all of that in ONE line.\n\n**🧒 The Instant Noodles Analogy:**\n\nYou now know how to cook a full meal from scratch — chop the vegetables, prepare the broth, boil the noodles separately. But sometimes you just want instant noodles. Same result, 3 minutes instead of 30.\n\n`create_react_agent` is the instant noodles of LangGraph.\n\n**One Line Agent:**\n```python\nfrom langgraph.prebuilt import create_react_agent\n\nagent = create_react_agent(model, tools)\n```\n\nThat's it. No StateGraph, no nodes, no edges, no routing function. One line, and you have a fully functional tool-calling agent.\n\n**🔍 What It Creates Under the Hood:**\n`create_react_agent` builds a graph with:\n1. An 'agent' node (calls the LLM)\n2. A 'tools' node (executes tool calls)\n3. A conditional edge (loop between agent and tools until done)\n\nThis is the ReAct pattern: **Re**ason → **Act** → Observe → Repeat.\n\n**Adding a System Prompt:**\n```python\nagent = create_react_agent(\n    model, tools,\n    prompt='You are a helpful research assistant. Always cite your sources.'\n)\n```\n\n**✅ When to Use create_react_agent:**\n• LLM + Tools pattern (90% of use cases!)\n• Simple reasoning loops\n• Quick prototypes and demos\n\n**❌ When to Build a Custom StateGraph:**\n• Multi-agent systems\n• Complex routing logic with multiple branches\n• Human-in-the-loop approval gates\n• Custom state beyond just messages\n\n**🔑 The Golden Rule:**\nStart with `create_react_agent` for 90% of your projects. Only build a custom StateGraph when you hit a wall. Don't over-engineer.\n\nLet's build a ReAct agent with tools."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Building a ReAct Agent",
            "difficulty": "Easy",
            "question": "Use create_react_agent to build a tool-calling agent. The model is ChatOpenAI with 'gpt-4o-mini'. Create a tool called 'get_weather' that takes a city (str) and returns a weather string. Then create the agent with create_react_agent. Store it in a variable called 'agent'.",
            "starterCode": "from langchain_openai import ChatOpenAI\nfrom langchain_core.tools import tool\nfrom langgraph.prebuilt import create_react_agent\n\n@tool\ndef get_weather(city: str) -> str:\n    '''Get the current weather for a city.'''\n    return f'The weather in {city} is sunny, 25°C'\n\nmodel = ChatOpenAI(model='gpt-4o-mini')\n\n# Create the agent\nagent = ___",
            "solution": "from langchain_openai import ChatOpenAI\nfrom langchain_core.tools import tool\nfrom langgraph.prebuilt import create_react_agent\n\n@tool\ndef get_weather(city: str) -> str:\n    '''Get the current weather for a city.'''\n    return f'The weather in {city} is sunny, 25°C'\n\nmodel = ChatOpenAI(model='gpt-4o-mini')\n\nagent = create_react_agent(model, [get_weather])",
            "hint": "Use create_react_agent(model, [get_weather]) — pass the model and a list of tools.",
            "explanation": "create_react_agent takes a ChatModel and a list of tools, and builds a complete ReAct agent. The agent will automatically call get_weather when asked about weather, loop back to reason about the result, and return a final answer.",
            "concepts": [
              "create_react_agent",
              "ReAct Pattern",
              "Tool-Calling Agent"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "create_react_agent\\(model.*get_weather"
            },
            "hints": [
              "Use create_react_agent(model, [get_weather]) — pass the model and a list of tools.",
              "Focus on these key concepts: create_react_agent, ReAct Pattern, Tool-Calling Agent.",
              "Check the parameter names carefully — Python is case-sensitive and typos will cause failures."
            ]
          }
        },
