        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Multi-Agent Patterns: Teams of Specialized Agents",
            "explanation": "<img src=\"/images/m11 langchain/m11_multi_agent_hospital.png\" alt=\"Multi-Agent Patterns: Teams of Specialized Agents\" className=\"w-full h-auto rounded-lg my-6 shadow-xl border border-[#30363d]\" />\n\nYou've mastered building individual agents with tools, memory, streaming, and safety gates. But what happens when one agent isn't enough? Complex tasks often require a TEAM of specialized agents working together.\n\n**🧒 The Hospital Analogy:**\n\nWhen you go to a hospital with chest pain, a single doctor doesn't do everything. A triage nurse evaluates you first. Then a cardiologist examines your heart. A radiologist reads your X-ray. A pharmacist prepares your medication. Each specialist is focused and expert in their domain.\n\nMulti-agent systems work the same way. Instead of one agent with 20 tools (which gets confused and overwhelmed), you create specialized agents:\n• **Research Agent** — Has search and browsing tools\n• **Writer Agent** — Has writing and formatting tools\n• **Reviewer Agent** — Has evaluation and fact-checking tools\n\nEach agent is focused, effective, and good at ONE thing.\n\n**🏢 The Supervisor Pattern (The Boss):**\nA 'Supervisor' agent that acts as a manager, routing tasks to the right worker:\n\n1. User sends a query\n2. **Supervisor** decides: \"This needs research\"\n3. Routes to **Research Agent**\n4. Research Agent returns results → back to Supervisor\n5. **Supervisor** decides: \"Now write a report\"\n6. Routes to **Writer Agent** → back to Supervisor\n7. **Supervisor**: \"Done!\" → END\n\nWorkers always loop back to the Supervisor, who decides the next step.\n\n**🧩 Subgraphs — Graphs Inside Graphs:**\nA complete LangGraph agent can be used as a single node inside a larger graph:\n\n```python\nresearch_graph = build_research_agent()\nmain_graph.add_node('research', research_graph)\n```\n\nThis is like departments within a company — each department has its own internal workflow, but from the CEO's perspective, it's just one box on the org chart.\n\n**📋 Choosing a Pattern:**\n• Simple tool-calling → `create_react_agent`\n• Complex routing → Supervisor pattern\n• Independent subtasks → Map-Reduce pattern\n• Nested complexity → Subgraphs\n\n**🔑 The Golden Rule:** Start simple. Only add multi-agent when a single agent demonstrably struggles. Don't build a hospital when you need a first-aid kit.\n\nLet's build a supervisor system."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Building a Supervisor Router",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Create a supervisor routing function called 'supervisor_router' that reads state['next_worker']. If it's 'research', return 'research_agent'. If it's 'write', return 'writer_agent'. If it's 'done', return 'end'. Then wire it up with add_conditional_edges from the 'supervisor' node.",
            "starterCode": "from langgraph.graph import StateGraph, END\nfrom typing import TypedDict, Annotated\nfrom langgraph.graph.message import add_messages\n\nclass TeamState(TypedDict):\n    messages: Annotated[list, add_messages]\n    next_worker: str\n\ndef supervisor(state):\n    return {'next_worker': 'research'}  # placeholder\n\ndef research_agent(state):\n    return {'messages': ['Research complete']}\n\ndef writer_agent(state):\n    return {'messages': ['Draft written']}\n\n# Router function\ndef supervisor_router(state):\n    ___\n\n# Build the graph\ngraph_builder = StateGraph(TeamState)\ngraph_builder.add_node('supervisor', supervisor)\ngraph_builder.add_node('research_agent', research_agent)\ngraph_builder.add_node('writer_agent', writer_agent)\ngraph_builder.set_entry_point('supervisor')\n\n# Add conditional edges\ngraph_builder.add_conditional_edges(\n    'supervisor',\n    supervisor_router,\n    ___\n)\n\n# Workers loop back to supervisor\ngraph_builder.add_edge('research_agent', 'supervisor')\ngraph_builder.add_edge('writer_agent', 'supervisor')\n\ngraph = graph_builder.compile()",
            "solution": "from langgraph.graph import StateGraph, END\nfrom typing import TypedDict, Annotated\nfrom langgraph.graph.message import add_messages\n\nclass TeamState(TypedDict):\n    messages: Annotated[list, add_messages]\n    next_worker: str\n\ndef supervisor(state):\n    return {'next_worker': 'research'}\n\ndef research_agent(state):\n    return {'messages': ['Research complete']}\n\ndef writer_agent(state):\n    return {'messages': ['Draft written']}\n\ndef supervisor_router(state):\n    next_worker = state['next_worker']\n    if next_worker == 'research':\n        return 'research_agent'\n    elif next_worker == 'write':\n        return 'writer_agent'\n    else:\n        return 'end'\n\ngraph_builder = StateGraph(TeamState)\ngraph_builder.add_node('supervisor', supervisor)\ngraph_builder.add_node('research_agent', research_agent)\ngraph_builder.add_node('writer_agent', writer_agent)\ngraph_builder.set_entry_point('supervisor')\n\ngraph_builder.add_conditional_edges(\n    'supervisor',\n    supervisor_router,\n    {'research_agent': 'research_agent', 'writer_agent': 'writer_agent', 'end': END}\n)\n\ngraph_builder.add_edge('research_agent', 'supervisor')\ngraph_builder.add_edge('writer_agent', 'supervisor')\n\ngraph = graph_builder.compile()",
            "hint": "The router reads state['next_worker'] and returns a string. The conditional edges dictionary maps those strings to actual node names. Workers loop back to supervisor.",
            "explanation": "This is the Supervisor pattern: the supervisor decides which worker to route to, workers do their job and loop back. The supervisor then decides the next step — continuing until it returns 'done' (which maps to END).",
            "concepts": [
              "Supervisor Pattern",
              "Multi-Agent",
              "Conditional Routing",
              "Worker Loop"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "def supervisor_router.*state.*return.*research_agent.*return.*writer_agent.*return.*end"
            },
            "hints": [
              "The router reads state['next_worker'] and returns a string. The conditional edges dictionary maps those strings to actual node names. Workers loop back to supervisor.",
              "Focus on these key concepts: Supervisor Pattern, Multi-Agent, Conditional Routing, Worker Loop.",
              "Check the parameter names carefully — Python is case-sensitive and typos will cause failures."
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "drag_drop",
