          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Mini Project: Multi-Tool Research Agent with LangChain",
            "explanation": "**🎯 Your Mini Project Mission:**\n\nIt's time to put EVERYTHING together. You're going to build a multi-tool research agent using LangChain and LangGraph that can autonomously research any topic by searching the web, summarizing articles, and compiling a structured report.\n\n**What You Will Build:**\nA \"Research Assistant Agent\" that takes a topic as input and produces a comprehensive research brief. The agent will use multiple tools (web search, Wikipedia, calculator) and implement the ReAct reasoning loop to decide which tool to use at each step.\n\n**📋 Project Requirements:**\n\n1. **Agent Architecture** — Design an agent using LangGraph with:\n   - A planning node that breaks down the research question\n   - A tool-selection node that picks the right tool for each sub-task\n   - A synthesis node that compiles findings into a report\n\n2. **Tool Integration** — Connect at least 3 tools:\n   - Web search (using Tavily or SerpAPI)\n   - Wikipedia lookup for background context\n   - A text summarizer for long articles\n\n3. **Memory & State** — Implement conversation memory so the agent:\n   - Remembers what it has already researched\n   - Avoids duplicate searches\n   - Builds on previous findings\n\n4. **Output Format** — Generate a structured markdown report with:\n   - Executive summary\n   - Key findings (with sources)\n   - Areas needing further research\n\n5. **Error Handling** — Gracefully handle:\n   - Failed API calls\n   - Irrelevant search results\n   - Timeout scenarios\n\n**🌟 Stretch Goals:**\n- Add a human-in-the-loop checkpoint where the agent asks for confirmation before deep-diving\n- Implement parallel tool execution for faster research\n- Add citation tracking with proper source attribution\n\nThis project brings together everything you learned about LangChain chains, LangGraph state machines, tool binding, and agent memory!"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Project Walkthrough: Building the Agent Step by Step",
            "explanation": "**Step-by-Step Implementation Guide**\n\nLet me walk you through building this agent like we've been doing throughout this module — one clear step at a time.\n\n**📋 Step 1: Set Up the State Graph (Your Blueprint)**\n\n```python\nfrom langgraph.graph import StateGraph, END\nfrom typing import TypedDict, List, Annotated\nimport operator\n\nclass ResearchState(TypedDict):\n    topic: str\n    sub_questions: List[str]\n    findings: Annotated[List[str], operator.add]\n    sources: Annotated[List[str], operator.add]\n    final_report: str\n    iteration: int\n\n# Define the graph\nworkflow = StateGraph(ResearchState)\n```\n\nNotice how `findings` and `sources` use the `operator.add` reducer — so each node's results get APPENDED, not overwritten. This is what we learned in State Design!\n\n**📋 Step 2: Create Your Tool Arsenal**\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_community.tools import TavilySearchResults, WikipediaQueryRun\nfrom langchain_core.tools import tool\n\nsearch_tool = TavilySearchResults(max_results=3)\nwiki_tool = WikipediaQueryRun()\n\n@tool\ndef summarize_text(text: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Summarize a long piece of text into key points.\"\"\"\n    return llm.invoke(f\"Summarize this in 3 bullet points: {text}\")\n```\n\n**📋 Step 3: Wire the Graph (Connect the Dots)**\n\n```python\nworkflow.add_node(\"plan\", plan_research)\nworkflow.add_node(\"search\", execute_search)\nworkflow.add_node(\"synthesize\", compile_report)\n\nworkflow.set_entry_point(\"plan\")\nworkflow.add_edge(\"plan\", \"search\")\nworkflow.add_conditional_edges(\"search\", should_continue, {\n    \"continue\": \"search\",\n    \"done\": \"synthesize\"\n})\nworkflow.add_edge(\"synthesize\", END)\n```\n\nSee that conditional edge? The agent loops back to search MORE until it decides it has enough information. Then it moves to synthesis. This is the Think-Act-Observe loop in action!\n\n**📋 Step 4: Test Your Agent**\n\nRun it on these test topics and verify the quality:\n1. \"Latest advances in quantum computing 2024\"\n2. \"How does mRNA vaccine technology work?\"\n3. \"Compare React vs Vue vs Svelte for web development\"\n\n**📋 Evaluation Criteria:**\n- Does it find relevant, accurate information?\n- Does it cite sources properly?\n- Does the report flow logically?\n- Does it handle ambiguous queries gracefully?\n\n**🎉 Congratulations!** You have mastered LangChain AND LangGraph — from simple chains to multi-agent systems. In the next module, we'll take these agent skills even further with AI Agents architecture patterns!"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "M012-ai-agents",
      "title": "Module 12: AI Agents",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What Are AI Agents?",
            "explanation": "Welcome to Module 12 — the module that changes everything you thought you knew about AI.\n\nLet me paint a picture for you. Imagine you hire the smartest intern in the world. They've read every textbook, memorized every Wikipedia article, and can write beautiful prose in 20 languages. Impressive, right? But here's the catch — this intern is **chained to a desk**. They can only answer questions you type to them. They can't pick up the phone, open a web browser, check your calendar, or even look out the window to see if it's raining. All they can do is talk.\n\nThat's what an LLM is without agency. A brilliant mind trapped in a text box.\n\n**Now imagine you unchain them.** You give them a phone, a computer, access to your company's databases, a web browser, and permission to take actions in the real world. Suddenly, that intern doesn't just ANSWER your question about the weather — they CHECK the weather API, look at your calendar, and proactively suggest: \"It's going to rain during your 3 PM outdoor meeting. Want me to move it indoors and notify the attendees?\"\n\n**That's an AI Agent.** An LLM that can perceive its environment, reason about what to do, take actions using tools, and iterate until a goal is achieved.\n\n<img src=\"/images/agent_m12/m12_what_is_agents.png\" alt=\"What Are AI Agents?\" />\n\n**The Technical Definition:**\nAn AI Agent is a system where an LLM operates in a loop — perceiving inputs, reasoning about what to do next, taking actions (like calling APIs or running code), observing the results, and repeating until the task is complete. The key difference from a chatbot is that **the LLM decides what to do**, not the programmer.\n\n**Why This Module Will Change How You Think About AI:**\n\nHere's a secret most courses won't tell you: the future of AI is not bigger models. It's **agents**. Andrew Ng, one of the most influential figures in AI, put it perfectly: \"I think AI agent workflows will drive massive AI progress this year — perhaps even more than the next generation of foundation models.\"\n\nCompanies like Google, OpenAI, Anthropic, and Microsoft are all betting billions on agent architectures. Devin (the AI coding agent), ChatGPT's tool use, and Google's Gemini Agents are all built on the exact patterns you'll learn in this module.\n\n**What You'll Build in This Module:**\nBy the end of these 90 stages, you'll be able to:\n✅ Build agents that autonomously research, code, and analyze data\n✅ Design multi-agent systems where specialized AI workers collaborate\n✅ Implement safety guardrails that prevent your agents from going rogue\n✅ Evaluate and debug agent performance like a production engineer\n❌ You will NOT just learn theory — every pattern comes with working Python code\n\nLet's start by understanding the most fundamental concept in all of agent engineering: the agent loop."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "questions": [
            {
              "question": "What is the KEY difference between a chatbot and an AI Agent?",
              "options": [
                "An agent uses a more expensive LLM model",
                "A chatbot responds to one prompt and waits; an agent autonomously works toward a goal using tools",
                "An agent doesn't need an LLM — it's pure code",
                "A chatbot can use tools but an agent cannot"
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explanation": "The defining feature of an agent is autonomy — it pursues a goal by calling tools, making decisions, and looping until the goal is achieved, rather than just responding to a single prompt."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Agent Loop: Perceive → Think → Act",
            "explanation": "Let me tell you about the most important concept in this entire module. If you understand this ONE pattern, you understand 90% of how every AI agent works — from simple chatbots to the most sophisticated multi-agent systems powering companies like Perplexity and Devin.\n\n**The Restaurant Kitchen Analogy:**\n\nImagine you're a head chef in a busy restaurant kitchen. A ticket comes in: \"Table 7 wants a medium-rare steak with a side of garlic mashed potatoes.\" Here's what you do:\n\n1. **PERCEIVE** — You read the ticket and check what ingredients you have\n2. **THINK** — You decide: \"I'll start the steak first since it takes longer, then prep the potatoes\"\n3. **ACT** — You fire up the grill and start cooking\n\nBut you don't just do this once. After the steak hits the grill, you **loop back**: Is it the right temperature? Does it need more seasoning? Should I start the potatoes now? You keep perceiving, thinking, and acting until the dish is perfect and ready to serve.\n\n**That's exactly how an AI agent works.** It's a loop — not a single call.\n\n<img src=\"/images/agent_m12/m12_agent_loop.png\" alt=\"The Agent Loop: Perceive → Think → Act\" />\n\n**The Simplest Possible Agent:**\nHere's a minimal agent in plain Python — no frameworks, no magic:\n\n```python\nfrom openai import OpenAI\nclient = OpenAI()\n\ndef simple_agent(goal):\n    messages = [\n        {'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a helpful assistant.'},\n        {'role': 'user', 'content': goal}\n    ]\n    \n    while True:  # ← This is the agent loop!\n        response = client.chat.completions.create(\n            model='gpt-4o', messages=messages\n        )\n        reply = response.choices[0].message\n        \n        # If the LLM wants to call a tool → execute it\n        if reply.tool_calls:\n            # Execute tool, add result to messages\n            # Loop continues...\n            pass\n        else:\n            # No tool call → we have the final answer\n            return reply.content\n```\n\n**Why `while True`? — Let Me Be Very Clear About This:**\n\nThe loop is THE fundamental insight. Here's the difference:\n- A **chatbot** calls the LLM once and returns. One question, one answer. Done.\n- An **agent** calls the LLM, checks if it wants to use a tool, executes that tool, feeds the result back, and calls the LLM **again**. This loop continues until the LLM decides it has enough information to give a final answer.\n\nIt's like the difference between asking someone a question and walking away, versus having an actual back-and-forth conversation where they can research, verify, and refine their answer.\n\n**What Happens Inside Each Iteration:**\n1. **PERCEIVE** — The LLM receives all messages so far (including previous tool results)\n2. **THINK** — The LLM reasons about what to do next\n3. **ACT** — Either call a tool (loop continues) OR return a final answer (loop ends)\n\n**The Magic of This Pattern:**\nThis simple loop can solve incredibly complex problems. Need to research a topic? The agent searches, reads, searches again, reads more, and synthesizes — all from this one loop. Need to debug code? The agent reads the error, tries a fix, runs the code, reads the new error, tries another fix — same loop. The intelligence comes from the LLM's ability to decide what tool to call and when to stop.\n\n**Why This Matters in the Real World:**\nEvery agent framework you'll encounter — LangGraph, CrewAI, OpenAI Agents SDK, AutoGen — is just a fancy wrapper around this loop. If you understand Perceive → Think → Act, you can build agents from scratch and debug any framework.\n\n**Safety Note:**\nNever use a bare `while True` in production! Always add:\n• A `max_iterations` counter (typically 10-25)\n• A token/cost budget\n• A timeout\n\nWe'll cover safety guardrails in Phase 9. But first, let's test your understanding of this pattern with a hands-on exercise."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Your First Agent Loop",
            "difficulty": "Easy",
            "question": "Complete the agent loop below. The `llm_call(messages)` function returns a dictionary with:\n- `type`: either 'tool_call' or 'final_answer'\n- `content`: the response text\n\nYour loop should:\n1. Call `llm_call(messages)` in each iteration\n2. If type is 'tool_call', print 'Calling tool...' and append the result to messages\n3. If type is 'final_answer', print the content and break",
            "starterCode": "def llm_call(messages):\n    # Mock LLM that returns {'type': 'tool_call'|'final_answer', 'content': '...'}\n    pass\n\nmessages = [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'What is the weather in Tokyo?'}]\nmax_iterations = 10\niteration = 0\n\n# TODO: Write the agent loop\n# Use while iteration < max_iterations\n",
            "solution": "def llm_call(messages):\n    pass\n\nmessages = [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'What is the weather in Tokyo?'}]\nmax_iterations = 10\niteration = 0\n\nwhile iteration < max_iterations:\n    response = llm_call(messages)\n    iteration += 1\n    \n    if response['type'] == 'tool_call':\n        print('Calling tool...')\n        messages.append({'role': 'tool', 'content': 'Tool result here'})\n    elif response['type'] == 'final_answer':\n        print(response['content'])\n        break",
            "hint": "Use a while loop with a counter. Check response['type'] to decide whether to continue looping or break.",
            "explanation": "This is the core agent pattern: loop, call LLM, check if it wants a tool, execute tool, feed result back. The max_iterations guard prevents infinite loops — a critical safety measure.",
