        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Self-Correction: When Things Go Wrong",
            "explanation": "<img src=\"/images/agent_m12/m12_rag_architecture.png\" alt=\"m12 rag architecture\" />\n\nHere's a secret that separates toy demos from production agents: **real-world tools fail constantly.** APIs return errors, rate limits kick in, search queries return nothing, and arguments get formatted wrong. The question isn't IF your agent will encounter errors — it's how gracefully it handles them.\n\n**The GPS Analogy:**\n\nThink about what happens when you miss a turn while following GPS directions. A bad GPS freezes and says \"Route unavailable.\" A good GPS says: \"Recalculating...\" and finds a new path to your destination. It doesn't crash. It doesn't give up. It adapts.\n\nA well-designed AI agent works exactly like a good GPS. When a tool returns an error, the agent doesn't crash — it **recalculates**.\n\n**How Self-Correction Works:**\nThe trick is beautifully simple: feed the error back to the LLM as an observation, and let it figure out what went wrong and try a different approach.\n\n```\nThought: I need the stock price of Apple.\nAction: get_stock_price(ticker='APPLE')\nObservation: ERROR: Invalid ticker symbol 'APPLE'. Use standard format like 'AAPL'.\n\nThought: Ah, I used the company name instead of the ticker symbol. Let me try 'AAPL' instead.\nAction: get_stock_price(ticker='AAPL')\nObservation: AAPL price: $198.50\n\nThought: Got it. Apple's stock price is $198.50.\nFinal Answer: Apple (AAPL) is currently trading at $198.50.\n```\n\n**The Five Most Common Error Recovery Patterns:**\n\n| Error Type | What Happens | How the Agent Recovers |\n|---|---|---|\n| Wrong arguments | `ERROR: Invalid ticker 'APPLE'` | Reads error, fixes the input format |\n| API rate limit | `ERROR: 429 Too Many Requests` | Waits, retries, or tries an alternative tool |\n| Empty results | `No results found for 'xyz'` | Rephrases the search query |\n| Timeout | `ERROR: Request timed out` | Simplifies the request or tries a different approach |\n| Permission denied | `ERROR: Unauthorized` | Reports to user, suggests alternative |\n\n**Implementation Pattern — This Is Production-Grade:**\n```python\ntry:\n    result = execute_tool(action, args)\n    messages.append({'role': 'tool', 'content': result})\nexcept Exception as e:\n    # Feed the error back to the LLM!\n    error_msg = f'ERROR: {str(e)}. Please try a different approach.'\n    messages.append({'role': 'tool', 'content': error_msg})\n```\n\n**Why This Matters — A Real Horror Story:**\nAn early version of a popular coding agent would crash entirely when a pip install command failed. No recovery, no retrying, no alternative approach. The fix? Simply feeding the error message back to the LLM, which then figured out it needed to install a different package version. A 2-line change turned a fragile demo into a resilient agent.\n\n**The Key Insight:**\nErrors are just observations. The LLM treats them the same as any other tool result — it reads the error, thinks about what went wrong, and tries again. This is why the Thought step in ReAct is so important — it gives the LLM space to reason about failures, not just blindly retry.\n\n**When Self-Correction Fails:**\nEven good agents can get stuck in error loops. That's why you ALWAYS need:\n• A **max retry count** per tool (typically 2-3 attempts)\n• A **max total iterations** for the entire agent (10-25)\n• A **fallback strategy** — if all retries fail, return partial results with an honest explanation\n\nNow that you've mastered ReAct and self-correction, it's time to give your agents real superpowers. Let's talk about tool calling."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Adding Error Recovery to Your Agent",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Modify the tool execution code below to handle errors gracefully. If `execute_tool()` raises an exception, catch it and append the error message to the messages list as a tool message (so the LLM can see what went wrong and retry).",
            "starterCode": "def execute_tool(name, args):\n    # May raise exceptions!\n    if name == 'search':\n        return 'Search results...'\n    raise ValueError(f'Unknown tool: {name}')\n\nmessages = []\naction = 'unknown_tool'\naction_input = 'test'\n\n# TODO: Call execute_tool and handle errors\n# On success: append {'role': 'tool', 'content': result}\n# On error: append {'role': 'tool', 'content': f'ERROR: {error_message}'}",
            "solution": "def execute_tool(name, args):\n    if name == 'search':\n        return 'Search results...'\n    raise ValueError(f'Unknown tool: {name}')\n\nmessages = []\naction = 'unknown_tool'\naction_input = 'test'\n\ntry:\n    result = execute_tool(action, action_input)\n    messages.append({'role': 'tool', 'content': result})\nexcept Exception as e:\n    messages.append({'role': 'tool', 'content': f'ERROR: {str(e)}'})",
            "hint": "Wrap execute_tool() in a try/except block. In the except clause, convert the error to a string and append it as a tool message.",
            "explanation": "By catching errors and feeding them back as tool messages, the LLM can see what went wrong and adapt. This is the foundation of self-correcting agents.",
            "concepts": [
              "Error Recovery",
              "Self-Correction",
              "try/except"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "try.*execute_tool.*except.*Exception.*ERROR.*str\\(e\\)"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
