        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Why Planning Matters — Think Before You Act",
            "explanation": "Imagine giving someone directions one step at a time without telling them the destination. \"Turn left.\" Okay. \"Go straight for 2 miles.\" Sure. \"Turn right.\" Into a lake?! \n\nThat's what happens when an agent acts without planning. It takes reasonable-looking steps that lead nowhere — or worse, into disaster. **Planning is the GPS that tells the agent where it's going before it starts driving.**\n\n**The Cost of Not Planning — A Real Story:**\n\nA developer built a research agent without planning. The user asked: \"Write a report on the impact of AI on healthcare.\" The agent searched for \"AI healthcare,\" read the first result, and wrote a paragraph. Then it searched for \"AI healthcare 2024,\" read another result, and wrote another paragraph. After 15 searches and $3 in API costs, it produced a rambling, incoherent mess with no structure. Same agent, with a planner? It first created an outline (History → Current Applications → Challenges → Future), then systematically researched each section. Better output, fewer API calls, lower cost.\n\n**When Planning Is Essential:**\n✅ Tasks with more than 3 steps\n✅ Tasks with dependencies (step 3 needs step 2's output)\n✅ Research tasks (you don't know what you'll find)\n✅ Any task where going in the wrong direction is expensive\n\n**When Planning Is Overkill:**\n❌ Simple Q&A (\"What's the capital of France?\")\n❌ Single-tool tasks (\"Check the weather\")\n❌ Time-critical tasks where planning adds too much latency\n\n**The Planning Spectrum:**\n\n| Complexity | Approach | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Simple (1-2 steps) | No planning, just ReAct | \"What's AAPL's stock price?\" |\n| Medium (3-5 steps) | Simple plan: list the steps | \"Compare AAPL and MSFT stock performance\" |\n| Complex (5-20 steps) | Full plan-and-execute | \"Write a research report on AI in healthcare\" |\n| Very complex (20+ steps) | Hierarchical planning | \"Build a complete web application\" |\n\n**The Python Decision Tree:**\n```python\ndef should_plan(task):\n    if task.steps_known and task.steps <= 2:\n        return 'NO_PLAN'       # Just execute with ReAct\n    elif task.steps <= 5:\n        return 'SIMPLE_PLAN'   # Quick list of steps\n    elif task.steps <= 20:\n        return 'FULL_PLAN'     # Plan-and-execute pattern\n    else:\n        return 'HIERARCHICAL'  # Multi-level planning\n```\n\n**Why This Matters For Your Career:**\nEvery production agent system — Devin, GPT-Researcher, AutoGen — uses planning. Without it, agents are expensive, unreliable, and unpredictable. With it, they're systematic, efficient, and traceable. Understanding planning is what separates demo-builders from production engineers.\n\nNow let's learn the first planning technique: task decomposition — the art of breaking big, scary problems into small, manageable steps."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Task Decomposition: Breaking Big Problems Down",
            "explanation": "Have you ever tried to build IKEA furniture without reading the manual? You open the box, see 200 parts and 47 screws, and your brain just... freezes. But open that manual, and suddenly it's manageable: \"Step 1: Attach side panel A to base panel B using 4 screws.\" One small step at a time.\n\n**Task decomposition is the IKEA manual for your AI agent.** It takes a big, vague goal and breaks it into small, concrete steps that the agent can execute one at a time.\n\n**Why Decomposition Is Critical:**\nLLMs are brilliant at small, well-defined tasks. Ask GPT-4 to \"write a function that validates an email address\" — it nails it. Ask it to \"build me a complete CRM system\" — it produces garbage. The secret? Break \"build a CRM\" into 50 small, well-defined tasks, and GPT-4 can handle each one beautifully.\n\n**The Three Decomposition Strategies:**\n\n**Strategy 1: LLM-Generated Plan** (most common)\nAsk the LLM itself to break down the task:\n```python\nplan_prompt = f\"\"\"\nBreak this task into 3-7 concrete, actionable steps.\nEach step should be specific enough that a junior developer could execute it.\nTask: {user_goal}\nReturn as a numbered list.\n\"\"\"\nsteps = llm(plan_prompt)\n```\n\n**Strategy 2: Template-Based** (most reliable)\nPre-define step templates for common task types:\n```python\nRESEARCH_TEMPLATE = [\n    'Define 3-5 specific research questions',\n    'Search for each question using web_search tool',\n    'Read and extract key findings from top 3 results per question',\n    'Synthesize findings into a structured outline',\n    'Write the final report with citations'\n]\n```\n\n**Strategy 3: Recursive Decomposition** (most powerful)\nIf a step is still too complex, decompose it further:\n```python\ndef decompose(task, depth=0, max_depth=3):\n    steps = llm(f'Break this into 3-5 steps: {task}')\n    for step in steps:\n        if is_too_complex(step) and depth < max_depth:\n            step.substeps = decompose(step, depth + 1)\n    return steps\n```\n\n**Good Decomposition vs. Bad Decomposition:**\n\n| ❌ Bad (too vague) | ✅ Good (specific & actionable) |\n|---|---|\n| \"Research the topic\" | \"Search for 'AI healthcare applications 2024' and extract 3 key findings\" |\n| \"Write the report\" | \"Write a 200-word introduction covering the history of AI in healthcare\" |\n| \"Analyze the data\" | \"Calculate the mean, median, and std deviation of the 'revenue' column\" |\n\n**The Golden Rule of Decomposition:**\nEach step should be **atomic** — small enough that an LLM can complete it in a single tool call or generation. If a step requires multiple tool calls, it's too big. Break it down further.\n\n**Real-World Application:**\nThis is exactly how Devin (the AI coding agent) works. When you say \"add user authentication to my app,\" Devin decomposes it into: install dependencies → create user model → add login route → add signup route → add middleware → write tests → update documentation. Each step is atomic and executable.\n\nNow let's see how to execute these plans systematically with the Plan-and-Execute pattern."
          }
        },
