        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Plan-and-Execute Pattern",
            "explanation": "Think of a military general commanding troops. The general doesn't personally fight on the front lines — they study the map, create a battle plan, and then hand each objective to different units for execution. If a unit encounters unexpected resistance, the general doesn't throw out the entire plan — they adjust the relevant section and keep going.\n\n**Plan-and-Execute separates the 'thinking' from the 'doing' into two distinct roles.** This is the most important agent architecture pattern for complex tasks.\n\n**The Two Roles:**\n\n🧠 **The Planner** (the general) — A powerful LLM (like GPT-4o) that creates and updates the plan\n⚡ **The Executor** (the soldiers) — A simpler agent (or even GPT-4o-mini) that executes each step\n\n**Why Separate Them?**\n• The planner needs to be smart (expensive model) but only runs a few times\n• The executor needs to be fast and cheap, running many times\n• This division cuts costs by 60-80% compared to using GPT-4o for everything\n\n**The Complete Pattern:**\n```python\ndef plan_and_execute(goal, tools):\n    # Phase 1: PLAN (expensive model, runs once)\n    planner = ChatOpenAI(model='gpt-4o')\n    plan = planner.invoke(f\"\"\"\n        Create a step-by-step plan to achieve this goal: {goal}\n        Available tools: {[t.name for t in tools]}\n        Return as a numbered list of specific, actionable steps.\n    \"\"\")\n    \n    # Phase 2: EXECUTE (cheap model, runs many times)\n    executor = ChatOpenAI(model='gpt-4o-mini')\n    results = []\n    \n    for step in plan:\n        result = executor.invoke(f\"\"\"\n            Execute this step: {step}\n            Previous results: {results}\n        \"\"\")\n        results.append(result)\n        \n        # Phase 2.5: CHECK (should we replan?)\n        if needs_replanning(result, plan):\n            plan = planner.invoke(f\"\"\"\n                Original goal: {goal}\n                Completed: {results}\n                Current plan isn't working because: {result.error}\n                Create an updated plan for the remaining work.\n            \"\"\")\n    \n    return results\n```\n\n**Plan-and-Execute vs. Pure ReAct — When to Use Which:**\n\n| Feature | ReAct | Plan-and-Execute |\n|---|---|---|\n| # of steps | 1-5 | 5-50+ |\n| Planning overhead | None | 1-2 LLM calls |\n| Cost efficiency | Lower for simple tasks | Higher for complex tasks |\n| Traceability | Moderate | Excellent (plan is auditable) |\n| Error recovery | Implicit | Explicit replanning |\n\n**Real-World Example — GPT-Researcher:**\nWhen you ask GPT-Researcher to \"write a report on quantum computing,\" it doesn't just start searching randomly. It:\n1. Creates a plan: 5 research questions\n2. Executes each question: searches, reads articles, extracts findings\n3. Checks progress: \"Do I have enough for a comprehensive report?\"\n4. Replans if needed: \"I'm missing information about challenges — adding a new search\"\n5. Synthesizes: combines all findings into a structured report\n\n**Dynamic Replanning:**\nThe real world is messy. What if Step 3 reveals that Steps 4-5 are now unnecessary? A smart plan-and-execute agent checks after each step whether the remaining plan still makes sense. If not, it asks the planner LLM to create an updated plan. We'll dive deep into replanning in the next stage."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Building a Plan-and-Execute Agent",
            "difficulty": "Hard",
            "question": "Complete the plan-and-execute agent. The function should:\n1. Call `create_plan(goal)` to get a list of steps\n2. Loop through each step, calling `execute_step(step)` \n3. Update the step status to 'completed' with the result\n4. Return all completed steps",
            "starterCode": "def create_plan(goal):\n    return [\n        {'task': 'Research the topic', 'status': 'pending'},\n        {'task': 'Write an outline', 'status': 'pending'},\n        {'task': 'Write the draft', 'status': 'pending'}\n    ]\n\ndef execute_step(step):\n    return f'Completed: {step[\"task\"]}'\n\ndef plan_and_execute(goal):\n    # Step 1: Create the plan\n    plan = ___\n    \n    # Step 2: Execute each step\n    for step in plan:\n        print(f'Executing: {step[\"task\"]}')\n        result = ___\n        step['status'] = '___'\n        step['result'] = result\n    \n    return plan\n\ncompleted = plan_and_execute('Write a blog post about AI agents')\nfor step in completed:\n    print(f'[{step[\"status\"]}] {step[\"task\"]}')",
            "solution": "def create_plan(goal):\n    return [\n        {'task': 'Research the topic', 'status': 'pending'},\n        {'task': 'Write an outline', 'status': 'pending'},\n        {'task': 'Write the draft', 'status': 'pending'}\n    ]\n\ndef execute_step(step):\n    return f'Completed: {step[\"task\"]}'\n\ndef plan_and_execute(goal):\n    plan = create_plan(goal)\n    \n    for step in plan:\n        print(f'Executing: {step[\"task\"]}')\n        result = execute_step(step)\n        step['status'] = 'completed'\n        step['result'] = result\n    \n    return plan\n\ncompleted = plan_and_execute('Write a blog post about AI agents')\nfor step in completed:\n    print(f'[{step[\"status\"]}] {step[\"task\"]}')",
            "hint": "Call create_plan(goal), execute_step(step), set status to 'completed', and store the result.",
            "explanation": "Plan-and-Execute is the most practical planning pattern. The planner creates structured steps, the executor works through them one by one, and status tracking lets you monitor progress.",
            "concepts": [
              "Plan-and-Execute",
              "Task Tracking",
              "Agent Orchestration"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "create_plan.*execute_step.*completed.*result"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Dynamic Replanning: Adapting When Plans Fail",
            "explanation": "Here's a truth that every experienced engineer knows: **plans rarely survive contact with reality.** And a good agent doesn't stubbornly follow a broken plan — it adapts.\n\n**The Chess Player Analogy:**\n\nThink about how a chess grandmaster plays. They start with an opening strategy, but they don't plan all 40 moves in advance. After every move, they reassess: \"My opponent did something unexpected. Does my strategy still work? Should I switch to a different approach?\"\n\nAn agent with dynamic replanning works the same way. It has a plan, but after every step it asks: \"Given what I just learned, does the rest of my plan still make sense?\"\n\n**The Four Triggers for Replanning:**\n1. 🚫 **Tool Error** — A tool returns an unexpected error (API down, access denied)\n2. 🆕 **New Information** — An intermediate result changes the best approach\n3. 🗑️ **Steps Become Irrelevant** — You discover Steps 4-5 are unnecessary\n4. 📝 **User Changes Requirements** — The user provides additional constraints mid-execution\n\n**The Replanning Pattern:**\n```python\nfor i, step in enumerate(plan):\n    result = execute_step(step)\n    \n    # After each step, ask: does the plan still make sense?\n    remaining_steps = plan[i+1:]\n    if should_replan(result, remaining_steps):\n        # Ask the LLM to create a new plan given what we've learned\n        new_plan = replan(\n            original_goal=goal,\n            completed_steps=plan[:i+1],\n            completed_results=results,\n            new_information=result\n        )\n        plan[i+1:] = new_plan  # Replace remaining steps\n```\n\n**A Concrete Example:**\n\nOriginal plan: Search for competitors → Scrape their websites → Analyze pricing\nStep 2 fails: Website blocks scraping!\n\n❌ **Dumb agent:** Crashes and returns \"Error: access denied\"\n✅ **Smart agent:** Replans: Search for pricing reviews instead → Extract data from reviews → Analyze pricing\n\n**Hierarchical Planning — The Production Secret:**\nFor very complex tasks (20+ steps), use a hierarchy:\n\n```\n📋 Macro Plan (5 high-level phases)\n   ├── Phase 1: Research (3-5 steps)\n   ├── Phase 2: Analysis (3-5 steps)\n   ├── Phase 3: Writing (3-5 steps)\n   ├── Phase 4: Review (2-3 steps)\n   └── Phase 5: Finalize (2-3 steps)\n```\n\n• Only replan at the level where the failure occurred\n• If Step 2 of Phase 1 fails, only replan Phase 1 — don't throw out the entire macro plan\n• This prevents catastrophic replanning where one small failure causes the agent to rethink everything\n\n**Cost of Replanning:**\nEach replan costs one LLM call to the planner model. For a typical 10-step task, you might replan 1-2 times — that's 2 extra GPT-4o calls (~$0.02). Compare that to the cost of a broken agent spinning its wheels for 50 iterations ($2+). Replanning is cheap insurance.\n\n**This Is How Production Agents Work:**\nDevin (the AI coding agent), GPT-Researcher, and enterprise automation agents all use hierarchical planning with dynamic replanning. It's the difference between a fragile demo and a system you can actually deploy.\n\nWith planning mastered, you're ready to level up again. Let's explore the frameworks that make building agents faster and more reliable."
          }
        },
        {
