          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "OpenAI Agents SDK: The Simplest Path to Agents",
            "explanation": "OpenAI released their own agent framework — the **Agents SDK** (formerly Swarm) — and its philosophy is simple: **do less, but do it well.**\n\nIf LangGraph is a fully-loaded workshop with every tool imaginable, and CrewAI is a movie production studio, then the OpenAI Agents SDK is a **perfectly organized toolbox** — everything you need, nothing you don't, and every tool is exactly where you'd expect it to be.\n\n**Core Concepts — Just Four Things:**\n• **Agent** — Has instructions (system prompt) and tools. That's it.\n• **Handoff** — An agent can transfer control to another agent (like a phone transfer)\n• **Guardrails** — Built-in safety checks on inputs and outputs\n• **Tracing** — Automatic logging of every step (no setup required)\n\n**The Simplest Agent You'll Ever Write:**\n```python\nfrom agents import Agent, Runner\n\nagent = Agent(\n    name='Assistant',\n    instructions='You are a helpful assistant.',\n)\n\nresult = Runner.run_sync(agent, 'What is the capital of France?')\nprint(result.final_output)  # 'Paris'\n```\n\nThat's a complete, working agent. Three lines of meaningful code.\n\n**Adding Tools:**\n```python\nfrom agents import Agent, Runner, function_tool\n\n@function_tool\ndef get_weather(city: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Get current weather for a city.\"\"\"\n    return f'Weather in {city}: 22°C, Sunny'\n\nagent = Agent(\n    name='Weather Bot',\n    instructions='Help users check the weather.',\n    tools=[get_weather]\n)\n\nresult = Runner.run_sync(agent, 'Weather in Tokyo?')\n```\n\n**Agent Handoffs — The Killer Feature:**\nThis is where the SDK really shines. Handoffs let agents transfer control to each other seamlessly:\n```python\nbilling_agent = Agent(name='Billing', instructions='Handle billing questions.')\ntech_agent = Agent(name='Tech Support', instructions='Handle technical issues.')\n\nsupport_agent = Agent(\n    name='Triage',\n    instructions='Route customers to the right department.',\n    handoffs=[billing_agent, tech_agent]  # Can transfer to either!\n)\n```\n\nWhen a user asks a billing question, the triage agent automatically hands off to the billing agent. This is how real customer support systems work.\n\n**Strengths and Limitations — Being Honest:**\n\n| ✅ Strengths | ❌ Limitations |\n|---|---|\n| Simplest API of the three | OpenAI models only (no Claude, Gemini, Llama) |\n| Built-in tracing (zero config) | No checkpointing or state persistence |\n| Built-in guardrails | Less community support than LangGraph |\n| Handoffs are first-class | Fewer production deployments to learn from |\n| Great documentation | Less flexible for complex workflows |\n\n**When to Choose OpenAI Agents SDK:**\n✅ You're already using OpenAI models exclusively\n✅ You want the simplest possible agent code\n✅ You need handoffs between agents (customer support, routing)\n✅ You value built-in tracing and guardrails\n\n**When NOT to Choose It:**\n❌ You need to support multiple LLM providers\n❌ You need complex state machines or conditional branching\n❌ You need checkpointing and state persistence\n\n**My Honest Recommendation:**\nStart with the OpenAI Agents SDK if you're using OpenAI and want to move fast. Switch to LangGraph when you outgrow it. Skip CrewAI unless you specifically need role-based agents for content workflows.\n\nNow let's level up from single agents to multi-agent systems — because the real power of agents comes when they work together."
          }
        },
        {
