        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Multi-Agent Systems — Why One Agent Isn't Enough",
            "explanation": "<img src=\"/images/agent_m12/m12_multi_agent.png\" alt=\"m12 multi agent\" />\n\nLet me tell you a story about a developer named Sarah. She built a single agent to handle her company's customer support. This one agent needed to: answer billing questions, troubleshoot technical issues, process refunds, escalate urgent cases, and update the CRM. It worked... for about a week. Then the prompt grew to 4,000 words, the agent started confusing billing procedures with technical troubleshooting, and refunds were being processed with the wrong amounts. Sarah's agent wasn't stupid — it was **overwhelmed**.\n\n**Here's the lesson: a single agent trying to do everything is like a single person trying to be the CEO, accountant, developer, and receptionist at the same time.** They'll do each job poorly.\n\n**The Solution: Specialization**\n\nInstead of one overwhelmed agent, Sarah built four specialized agents:\n• **Triage Agent** — Classifies incoming requests and routes them\n• **Billing Agent** — Handles invoices, payments, and refunds (with access to billing tools)\n• **Tech Support Agent** — Troubleshoots issues (with access to logs and documentation)\n• **Escalation Agent** — Handles angry customers and urgent cases (with human-in-the-loop)\n\nEach agent had a focused system prompt, specific tools, and clear boundaries. The result? 90% fewer errors, faster response times, and happy customers.\n\n**Why Multi-Agent > Single Agent:**\n\n| Dimension | Single Agent | Multi-Agent |\n|---|---|---|\n| Prompt complexity | 4,000+ words (confusion) | 200-500 words each (clarity) |\n| Tool conflicts | 20+ tools (wrong selection) | 3-5 tools each (precision) |\n| Error blast radius | One error affects everything | Errors are isolated to one agent |\n| Debugging | \"Which of 20 things went wrong?\" | \"The billing agent failed at step 3\" |\n| Scaling | Rewrite the whole agent | Add/modify individual agents |\n\n**The Three Multi-Agent Architectures:**\n1. **Supervisor** — One manager agent delegates to worker agents (most common)\n2. **Peer-to-Peer** — Agents collaborate as equals, passing work to each other\n3. **Debate** — Agents argue and a judge picks the best answer (for accuracy)\n\n**When to Use Multi-Agent:**\n✅ Task requires expertise in multiple domains (billing + tech + legal)\n✅ Single agent prompt exceeds ~1,000 words\n✅ You need to add new capabilities without breaking existing ones\n✅ Different parts of the task need different tools\n\n**When a Single Agent Is Fine:**\n❌ Task is simple and well-defined (research, Q&A, summarization)\n❌ All steps use the same tools and expertise\n❌ You're prototyping and need speed over reliability\n\n**Real-World Multi-Agent Systems:**\n• **Devin** (AI coder) — Planner + Coder + Tester + Debugger agents\n• **ChatGPT** — Routing agent + Code interpreter + DALL·E + Browse agents\n• **Customer support platforms** — Triage + Billing + Technical + Escalation agents\n\nLet's start with the most common pattern: the Supervisor."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Building a Sequential Multi-Agent Pipeline",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Build a sequential 3-agent pipeline where:\n1. Agent 1 (Researcher) processes the user query\n2. Agent 2 (Writer) takes the research and writes content\n3. Agent 3 (Editor) polishes the final output\n\nComplete the `run_pipeline()` function.",
            "starterCode": "def run_agent(system_prompt, user_message):\n    # Mock LLM call\n    return f'[{system_prompt[:20]}...] processed: {user_message[:50]}'\n\ndef run_pipeline(user_query):\n    # Agent 1: Researcher\n    research = run_agent(\n        '___',\n        f'Research this topic: {user_query}'\n    )\n    print(f'Research: {research}')\n    \n    # Agent 2: Writer — uses research as input\n    draft = run_agent(\n        '___',\n        f'Write content based on: {___}'\n    )\n    print(f'Draft: {draft}')\n    \n    # Agent 3: Editor — polishes the draft\n    final = run_agent(\n        '___',\n        f'Edit and polish: {___}'\n    )\n    print(f'Final: {final}')\n    \n    return final\n\nrun_pipeline('AI agents in healthcare')",
            "solution": "def run_agent(system_prompt, user_message):\n    return f'[{system_prompt[:20]}...] processed: {user_message[:50]}'\n\ndef run_pipeline(user_query):\n    research = run_agent(\n        'You are a research analyst. Find key facts and data.',\n        f'Research this topic: {user_query}'\n    )\n    print(f'Research: {research}')\n    \n    draft = run_agent(\n        'You are a content writer. Write clear, engaging content.',\n        f'Write content based on: {research}'\n    )\n    print(f'Draft: {draft}')\n    \n    final = run_agent(\n        'You are an editor. Fix errors and improve clarity.',\n        f'Edit and polish: {draft}'\n    )\n    print(f'Final: {final}')\n    \n    return final\n\nrun_pipeline('AI agents in healthcare')",
            "hint": "Give each agent a descriptive system prompt. Pass 'research' to the writer and 'draft' to the editor.",
            "explanation": "This is the simplest multi-agent pattern: sequential handoff. Each agent has a focused role and passes its output to the next. The key is that each agent's system prompt is specialized.",
            "concepts": [
              "Sequential Pipeline",
              "Multi-Agent",
              "Agent Handoff"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "run_agent.*research.*run_agent.*research.*run_agent.*draft"
            }
          }
