        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Debate Pattern: Adversarial Verification",
            "explanation": "Imagine you're in a courtroom. The prosecution presents their case. The defense presents theirs. Each side pokes holes in the other's argument. And the judge — who heard both sides — makes the final decision. This adversarial process is why courts produce better verdicts than any single person could alone.\n\n**The Debate pattern brings this courtroom dynamic to AI agents.** It's the secret weapon for **reducing hallucinations** in high-stakes applications.\n\n**How the Debate Pattern Works — Five Steps:**\n1. **Agent A** generates an answer (the \"prosecution\")\n2. **Agent B** generates a different answer independently (the \"defense\")\n3. **Agent A** critiques Agent B's answer — looking for errors, weak arguments, unsupported claims\n4. **Agent B** critiques Agent A's answer — same thing, opposite direction\n5. **Judge Agent** reads both answers and both critiques, then picks the best answer or synthesizes a superior one\n\n**Why Debate Crushes Hallucinations:**\nHere's the key insight: if Agent A hallucinates a fake statistic (\"AI agents reduce costs by 73%\"), Agent B is unlikely to hallucinate the **exact same** fake statistic. When Agent B critiques Agent A's answer, it flags: \"I cannot verify the 73% cost reduction claim. This needs a citation.\" The adversarial process catches lies that neither agent would catch alone.\n\n**Implementation Sketch:**\n```python\ndef debate(question, rounds=2):\n    # Two agents with different system prompts\n    agent_a = Agent(instructions='Answer thoroughly. Cite evidence.')\n    agent_b = Agent(instructions='Answer independently. Challenge assumptions.')\n    \n    answer_a = agent_a.generate(question)\n    answer_b = agent_b.generate(question)\n    \n    for round in range(rounds):\n        critique_of_b = agent_a.critique(answer_b)  # A attacks B\n        critique_of_a = agent_b.critique(answer_a)  # B attacks A\n        \n        # Agents improve based on critiques\n        answer_a = agent_a.improve(answer_a, critique_of_a)\n        answer_b = agent_b.improve(answer_b, critique_of_b)\n    \n    # Judge picks the winner or synthesizes\n    judge = Agent(instructions='Evaluate both answers. Pick the most accurate one or combine the best parts of each.')\n    return judge.decide(answer_a, answer_b)\n```\n\n**When to Use Debate — The High-Stakes Test:**\n✅ Medical AI — where a wrong diagnosis could harm patients\n✅ Legal AI — where a wrong interpretation could lose cases\n✅ Financial analysis — where wrong numbers could cost millions\n✅ Any task where a wrong answer is MORE dangerous than a slow answer\n\n**When NOT to Use Debate:**\n❌ Simple factual questions (overkill for \"What's the capital of France?\")\n❌ Creative tasks (no single \"correct\" answer)\n❌ Time-critical applications (debate adds significant latency)\n\n**The Cost-Accuracy Tradeoff:**\n\n| Approach | API Calls | Cost Multiplier | Accuracy |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Single agent | 1 | 1x | Baseline |\n| Agent + Reflection | 2-3 | 2-3x | +15-25% |\n| Full Debate (2 rounds) | 5-7 | 5-7x | +30-50% |\n\nDebate costs 5-7x a single agent call. Only use it when accuracy is worth the price. In medical and legal AI, it absolutely is.\n\n**Pro Tip:** You don't need full debate for everything. A lightweight version — one generator + one critic — gives you 80% of the accuracy benefit at 2-3x the cost.\n\nNow let's talk about what happens when one agent needs to pass its work to another — the critical moment of the handoff."
          }
        },
