        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Agent Handoffs & Communication Protocols",
            "explanation": "Think of a relay race. Four runners, one baton. The race isn't won by the fastest individual — it's won by the team with the **smoothest baton passes**. Drop the baton during a handoff, and it doesn't matter how fast everyone runs. The race is lost.\n\n**Agent handoffs work the same way.** When Agent A finishes its work and passes control to Agent B, the quality of that handoff determines whether Agent B succeeds or fails. And in my experience, **bad handoffs are the #1 cause of multi-agent system failures.**\n\n**What Goes Wrong With Bad Handoffs:**\n\nAgent A (researcher) produces: \"I found some stuff about AI agents. There are frameworks and things. Also memory is important.\"\n\nAgent B (writer) receives this and thinks: \"What frameworks? What things? What about memory?\" It doesn't have enough context to do its job, so it either hallucinates details or produces vague, useless output.\n\n**The Structured Handoff Pattern:**\nAlways use a structured handoff message with these fields:\n```python\nhandoff = {\n    'from_agent': 'researcher',\n    'to_agent': 'writer',\n    'summary': 'Found 5 key findings about AI agent frameworks in 2024',\n    'data': {\n        'finding_1': {'topic': 'LangGraph', 'key_point': '...', 'source': '...'},\n        'finding_2': {'topic': 'CrewAI', 'key_point': '...', 'source': '...'},\n        # ...\n    },\n    'instructions': 'Write a 500-word blog post covering all 5 findings. Use a conversational tone.',\n    'constraints': 'Use simple language, include code examples, cite all sources'\n}\n```\n\n**The Three Communication Protocols:**\n\n| Protocol | How It Works | Best For |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Shared State** | All agents read/write to a common dictionary | LangGraph (default) |\n| **Message Passing** | Agents send structured messages to specific recipients | OpenAI SDK handoffs |\n| **Blackboard** | Agents post findings to a shared board that others can read | Research workflows |\n\n**Shared State Example (LangGraph):**\n```python\n# All agents read and write to the same state dict\nstate = {\n    'messages': [...],         # Conversation history\n    'research_data': {...},    # Researcher writes, Writer reads\n    'draft': '...',            # Writer writes, Editor reads\n    'feedback': '...',         # Editor writes, Writer reads\n}\n```\n\n**Validation at Handoff Points — Don't Skip This:**\nAlways validate what an agent produces before passing it to the next agent:\n```python\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\n\nclass ResearchHandoff(BaseModel):\n    findings: list[str]        # Must have at least 1 finding\n    sources: list[str]         # Must have citations\n    summary: str               # Must be non-empty\n\n# Validate before passing to the writer\ntry:\n    validated = ResearchHandoff(**researcher_output)\n    writer.execute(validated)\nexcept ValidationError as e:\n    # Send the researcher back to fix their output\n    researcher.retry(f'Your output was invalid: {e}')\n```\n\n**The Three Rules of Good Handoffs:**\n1. **Be explicit** — Don't assume the next agent knows what you did. State it clearly.\n2. **Be structured** — Use Pydantic models or typed dictionaries, not free text.\n3. **Validate** — Check the output before passing it. Catch errors at the handoff, not three agents later.\n\n**Why This Matters:**\nAt production scale, a multi-agent system might process thousands of requests per hour. A 5% handoff failure rate means 50 broken requests per hour. Structured, validated handoffs bring that to near zero.\n\nNow let's zoom out and look at the four fundamental design patterns that underpin everything we've learned so far."
          }
        },
