          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Four Agentic Design Patterns",
            "explanation": "In early 2024, Andrew Ng — one of the most influential figures in AI and co-founder of Google Brain — gave a talk that changed how the industry thinks about AI agents. He identified four foundational design patterns that every AI engineer must know. These aren't theoretical — they're the building blocks used by every production agent system in the world.\n\n**Think of these patterns as the four elements of agent engineering:**\n\n**🪞 Pattern 1: Reflection**\nThe agent generates output, then critically reviews its own work.\n→ \"Write code, then check if it compiles, then fix bugs\"\n→ Like a writer who re-reads their own draft with fresh eyes\n\n**🔧 Pattern 2: Tool Use**\nThe agent calls external tools to interact with the real world.\n→ \"Search the web, run code, query databases\"\n→ Like giving the agent hands, eyes, and ears\n\n**📋 Pattern 3: Planning**\nThe agent creates a step-by-step plan before executing.\n→ \"Break this research into 5 steps, then execute each\"\n→ Like a project manager creating a Gantt chart before the team starts building\n\n**👥 Pattern 4: Multi-Agent**\nMultiple specialized agents collaborate on a task.\n→ \"Researcher finds data, Writer creates report, Editor polishes\"\n→ Like a company with departments instead of one person doing everything\n\n**The Power Matrix — When to Use Each:**\n\n| Pattern | Best For | Typical Improvement | Cost Multiplier |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Reflection | Code generation, writing, data extraction | +15-30% quality | 2-3x |\n| Tool Use | Real-world data, computation, external APIs | Enables new capabilities | 1.5-2x |\n| Planning | Complex multi-step tasks, research | +40-60% task completion | 1.2-1.5x |\n| Multi-Agent | Domain-specific expertise, scaling | +25-40% on complex tasks | 2-5x |\n\n**How They Combine — The Real Magic:**\nMost production systems use ALL FOUR simultaneously:\n• A **Planner** decomposes the goal (Planning)\n• A **Supervisor** delegates to Workers (Multi-Agent)\n• Each Worker calls **Tools** (Tool Use)\n• Each Worker **Reflects** on its output before returning (Reflection)\n\n```\nUser Goal → Planner (Planning)\n  → Supervisor (Multi-Agent)\n    → Worker A: uses tools (Tool Use) → reflects (Reflection) → returns\n    → Worker B: uses tools (Tool Use) → reflects (Reflection) → returns\n  → Supervisor combines results → Final Answer\n```\n\n**The Key Insight:**\nThese patterns aren't mutually exclusive — they're **composable building blocks**. The best agents layer them together: plan → delegate → execute with tools → reflect → iterate. Understanding these four patterns gives you a mental framework to analyze and design ANY agent system.\n\nWe've already covered each pattern in depth in previous phases. In the next few stages, we'll see how each one works as a standalone design pattern, with practical guidelines for when to apply each one."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Reflection Pattern: Self-Improvement Loop",
            "explanation": "Let me tell you why Reflection is the highest-ROI pattern you'll ever implement.\n\n**The Code Review Analogy:**\n\nImagine a developer who writes code and immediately ships it to production without ever reviewing it. No tests, no self-review, no nothing. Terrifying, right? Now imagine they write code, then spend 5 minutes re-reading it as if they were a code reviewer seeing it for the first time. They catch bugs, improve variable names, add edge case handling. Same developer, dramatically better code — just from taking a second look.\n\n**That's Reflection.** The agent generates something, then asks itself: \"Is this good? How can I improve it?\" It's the simplest pattern but often the most impactful.\n\n**The Reflection Loop:**\n```python\ndef reflect_and_improve(task, max_rounds=3):\n    # Generate initial output\n    output = generate(task)\n    \n    for round in range(max_rounds):\n        # Critique the output (be specific!)\n        feedback = critique(output)\n        # → 'The code has a bug on line 5: undefined variable \"result\"'\n        \n        # Check if it's good enough\n        if 'no issues found' in feedback.lower():\n            break\n        \n        # Improve based on feedback\n        output = improve(output, feedback)\n    \n    return output\n```\n\n**Real-World Impact — Numbers That Matter:**\n\n| Task | Without Reflection | With Reflection (2 rounds) | Improvement |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Code generation | 65% pass rate | 85% pass rate | +31% |\n| Essay writing | 3.2/5 human rating | 4.1/5 human rating | +28% |\n| Data extraction | 72% accuracy | 91% accuracy | +26% |\n| SQL generation | 70% correct queries | 88% correct queries | +26% |\n\nThese numbers come from real benchmarks. Two extra LLM calls can improve quality by 25-30%.\n\n**When Reflection Works Best:**\n✅ Code generation (does it compile? pass tests? handle edge cases?)\n✅ Writing (is the tone consistent? are there grammar errors? is the structure logical?)\n✅ Data extraction (does the JSON match the expected schema? are all fields filled?)\n✅ SQL queries (does the query return the right results? is it optimized?)\n\n**When Reflection Doesn't Help:**\n❌ Subjective creative tasks (no clear \"correct\" answer to check against)\n❌ Tasks where the LLM can't verify its own output (it can't check live data)\n❌ Simple one-shot tasks where the first answer is almost always right (reflection adds unnecessary latency)\n\n**Pro Tips That Will Save You Money:**\n\n1. **Use a cheaper model for the critic.** GPT-4o-mini is a fantastic critic — fast, cheap, and surprisingly good at catching bugs. Use GPT-4o for generation, GPT-4o-mini for critique.\n\n2. **Be specific in the critique prompt.** Don't say \"review this code.\" Say \"check this code for: (1) syntax errors, (2) undefined variables, (3) missing edge cases, (4) security vulnerabilities.\"\n\n3. **Set a max rounds limit.** Most improvements happen in rounds 1-2. By round 3, you're getting diminishing returns. Don't waste money on rounds 4+.\n\n4. **Use concrete verification when possible.** Instead of asking the LLM \"is this code correct?\", actually RUN the code and check if it passes tests. Concrete feedback beats LLM opinions every time.\n\nNow let's look at the Tool Use pattern — the one that gives agents their superpowers."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Building a Reflection Agent",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Build a reflection loop that:\n1. Generates initial content with `generate(task)`\n2. Critiques it with `critique(content)`\n3. If the critique finds issues, improves with `improve(content, feedback)`\n4. Repeats up to `max_rounds` times",
            "starterCode": "def generate(task):\n    return f'Draft about: {task}'\n\ndef critique(content):\n    if 'improved' in content:\n        return 'LGTM - no issues found'\n    return 'Needs more detail and examples'\n\ndef improve(content, feedback):\n    return f'{content} (improved based on: {feedback})'\n\ndef reflection_agent(task, max_rounds=3):\n    # Step 1: Generate\n    output = ___\n    print(f'Initial: {output}')\n    \n    for round in range(max_rounds):\n        # Step 2: Critique\n        feedback = ___\n        print(f'Feedback: {feedback}')\n        \n        # Step 3: Check if good enough\n        if 'no issues' in feedback.lower():\n            break\n        \n        # Step 4: Improve\n        output = ___\n        print(f'Improved: {output}')\n    \n    return output\n\nresult = reflection_agent('Write about AI agents')\nprint(f'Final: {result}')",
            "solution": "def generate(task):\n    return f'Draft about: {task}'\n\ndef critique(content):\n    if 'improved' in content:\n        return 'LGTM - no issues found'\n    return 'Needs more detail and examples'\n\ndef improve(content, feedback):\n    return f'{content} (improved based on: {feedback})'\n\ndef reflection_agent(task, max_rounds=3):\n    output = generate(task)\n    print(f'Initial: {output}')\n    \n    for round in range(max_rounds):\n        feedback = critique(output)\n        print(f'Feedback: {feedback}')\n        \n        if 'no issues' in feedback.lower():\n            break\n        \n        output = improve(output, feedback)\n        print(f'Improved: {output}')\n    \n    return output\n\nresult = reflection_agent('Write about AI agents')\nprint(f'Final: {result}')",
            "hint": "Call generate(task), critique(output), and improve(output, feedback). Break when the critique says 'no issues'.",
            "explanation": "The Reflection pattern creates a self-improvement loop. The agent generates → critiques → improves until the output meets quality standards. This typically takes 2-3 rounds.",
            "concepts": [
              "Reflection",
              "Self-Improvement",
              "Iterative Refinement"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "generate.*critique.*no issues.*improve.*output.*feedback"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Tool Use Pattern: Connecting to the World",
            "explanation": "We've been building with tools throughout Phase 3, so you already know the mechanics. But let me reframe Tool Use as a design pattern, because understanding WHEN and HOW to design your tool set is the difference between a capable agent and a frustrating one.\n\n**The Hands of a Craftsman Analogy:**\n\nThink of an expert craftsman — say, a master woodworker. Their skill is in their brain (knowing what to build and how), but their capability comes from their **tools** (saws, chisels, sanders, drills). Give them a butter knife and ask them to build a cabinet — they'll fail. Give them the right tools and they'll create a masterpiece.\n\nYour agent's tools determine what it CAN do. Choose them poorly, and even GPT-4o will struggle. Choose them well, and even a smaller model can accomplish impressive tasks.\n\n**The Four Categories of Agent Tools:**\n\n| Category | Examples | When to Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| 🔍 **Information Retrieval** | web_search, db_query, file_read, api_fetch | Agent needs external data |\n| 🔢 **Computation** | code_executor, calculator, data_analyzer | Agent needs precise calculations |\n| 📤 **Communication** | send_email, post_slack, update_crm | Agent needs to notify or update |\n| ✏️ **CRUD Operations** | create_record, update_status, delete_file | Agent needs to modify state |\n\n**Tool Design Best Practices — The Five Rules:**\n\n1. **Name it clearly** — `search_products` beats `do_search`. The LLM uses the name to decide when to call it.\n\n2. **Describe it thoroughly** — Include what it does, what it returns, and examples of valid inputs. This is the #1 factor in tool selection accuracy.\n\n3. **Use specific types** — `enum: ['celsius', 'fahrenheit']` prevents the LLM from inventing `'kelvin'` when you don't support it.\n\n4. **Return structured data** — Return JSON objects with clear field names, not free text paragraphs. The LLM can reason about structured data much better.\n\n5. **Include error messages that help** — `\"ERROR: Ticker 'APPLE' not found. Did you mean 'AAPL'?\"` is infinitely better than `\"ERROR: 404\"`.\n\n**The Tool Design Anti-Patterns — Don't Do These:**\n\n❌ **Too many tools** — More than 10-15 tools confuses the LLM. Group related operations.\n❌ **Overlapping tools** — If `search_web` and `google_search` both exist, the LLM won't know which to pick.\n❌ **God tools** — A single `do_everything(action, params)` tool defeats the purpose. Keep tools atomic.\n❌ **No error context** — Returning bare `\"Error\"` gives the LLM nothing to work with for self-correction.\n\n**Why This Matters:**\nAccording to benchmarks, agents with well-designed tools (clear names, thorough descriptions, specific types) achieve 30-40% higher task completion rates than identical agents with poorly-designed tools — same model, same architecture, just better tool definitions.\n\nLet's look at the next design pattern: Planning — the one that makes agents strategic."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Planning Pattern: Strategic Execution",
            "explanation": "We covered Planning in depth in Phase 4, but let me reframe it as a design pattern with a fresh analogy — because understanding when to apply planning is as important as understanding how.\n\n**The Architect's Blueprint Analogy:**\n\nWould you build a house by just... starting? Laying bricks wherever feels right, hoping the walls line up, figuring out where the bathroom goes after the roof is on? Of course not. You'd hire an architect to create a blueprint FIRST, then build according to the plan.\n\n**The Planning pattern is the blueprint for your agent.** It ensures the agent thinks strategically before it starts executing, avoiding the \"random walk\" problem where the agent takes action after action without a coherent strategy.\n\n**Planning + Reflection = The Power Combo:**\nAfter executing a plan, the agent reflects on the results and updates the plan if needed:\n\n```\nPlan → Execute Step 1 → Reflect: \"Did that work?\"\n  → Yes → Execute Step 2 → Reflect → ...\n  → No  → Update Plan → Execute revised Step 1 → ...\n```\n\nThis create a robust feedback loop that's both strategic (planning) and adaptive (reflection).\n\n**When Planning Is Essential:**\n✅ Tasks with more than 3 steps\n✅ Tasks where steps have dependencies (step 3 needs step 2's output)\n✅ Research tasks where you don't know what you'll find\n✅ Any task where the cost of going in the wrong direction is high\n✅ Tasks with a budget constraint (planning helps optimize resource allocation)\n\n**When Planning is NOT Needed — Save the Overhead:**\n❌ Simple Q&A — just use ReAct\n❌ Single-tool tasks — just call the tool\n❌ Time-critical tasks where planning adds too much latency\n❌ Tasks where the steps are already known (use a pipeline instead)\n\n**The Planning Decision Tree:**\n```python\ndef should_use_planning(task):\n    if task.num_steps <= 2:\n        return False            # Too simple, just execute\n    if task.steps_are_fixed:\n        return False            # Use a pipeline instead\n    if task.latency_critical and task.budget_tight:\n        return False            # Planning overhead not worth it\n    return True                 # Plan first, execute second\n```\n\n**The Cost of Planning vs. Not Planning:**\n\n| Scenario | Without Planning | With Planning |\n|---|---|---|\n| 10-step research task | ~15 LLM calls (wandering) | ~12 LLM calls (directed) |\n| Avg cost | $0.45 | $0.35 + $0.02 (plan) = $0.37 |\n| Task completion rate | ~65% | ~90% |\n| Output quality | Scattered, incomplete | Structured, comprehensive |\n\nPlanning actually SAVES money on complex tasks by reducing wasted LLM calls.\n\nNow let's look at the final pattern — the one that ties everything together: Multi-Agent collaboration."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Multi-Agent Pattern: Divide and Conquer",
            "explanation": "We covered Multi-Agent Systems in depth in Phase 6. Now let me reframe it as a design pattern and show you how all four patterns compose into a production-grade system.\n\n**The Surgical Team Analogy:**\n\nA complex surgery isn't performed by one person. There's a team:\n• **Lead Surgeon** — Makes the critical decisions, performs the main procedure\n• **Anesthesiologist** — Manages sedation and monitors vital signs\n• **Surgical Nurse** — Hands instruments, manages equipment\n• **Radiologist** — Interprets imaging during the procedure\n\nEach person is deeply specialized. The lead surgeon doesn't fiddle with the anesthesia machine. The anesthesiologist doesn't pick up a scalpel. **Specialization + collaboration = better outcomes than any one person could achieve alone.**\n\n**The Multi-Agent Design Pattern — Key Insight:**\n**Specialization beats generalization.** An agent trying to research, write, code, and review will do each task poorly. Four specialized agents will each do their task excellently.\n\n**The Four Multi-Agent Topologies:**\n\n| Topology | Structure | Best For |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Supervisor** | One manager, many workers | Customer support, task routing |\n| **Pipeline** | Agent A → Agent B → Agent C | Content creation, data processing |\n| **Debate** | Agents argue, judge decides | High-stakes accuracy |\n| **Swarm** | Agents self-organize dynamically | Research, exploration |\n\n**How ALL FOUR Patterns Compose in Production:**\n\nThis is what a real production agent system looks like:\n\n```\nSupervisor (Multi-Agent)\n  ├── Planner Agent (Planning)\n  │     └── Creates step-by-step plan\n  ├── Research Worker (Tool Use + Reflection)\n  │     ├── Searches web, reads papers (Tool Use)\n  │     └── Reviews research quality (Reflection)\n  ├── Writer Worker (Tool Use + Reflection)\n  │     ├── Writes content using templates (Tool Use)\n  │     └── Self-edits for clarity and accuracy (Reflection)\n  └── Editor Worker (Reflection)\n        └── Final quality check, catches errors\n```\n\nEach pattern handles a different aspect of the problem, and they compose naturally:\n- **Planning** answers: \"WHAT should we do?\"\n- **Multi-Agent** answers: \"WHO should do it?\"\n- **Tool Use** answers: \"HOW do they do it?\"\n- **Reflection** answers: \"Is it GOOD ENOUGH?\"\n\n**Real-World Production Systems Using All Four:**\n\n| System | Planning | Multi-Agent | Tool Use | Reflection |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Devin (coding) | ✅ Task decomposition | ✅ Coder + Tester + Debugger | ✅ Code executor, terminal | ✅ Run tests, fix bugs |\n| GPT-Researcher | ✅ Research plan | ✅ Searcher + Reader + Writer | ✅ Web search, scraping | ✅ Fact-checking |\n| Enterprise support | ✅ Triage flow | ✅ Router + Specialists | ✅ CRM, KB search | ✅ Answer verification |\n\n**The Key Takeaway:**\nThese four patterns are the Lego bricks of agent engineering. Master them individually, then learn to snap them together. Any agent system you encounter in the wild — no matter how complex — is built from these four patterns.\n\nNow let's dive into something that makes agents truly intelligent over time: memory."
          }
