        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Agent Evaluation — Is Your Agent Actually Good?",
            "explanation": "Building an agent is only half the battle. The other half — the half most people skip — is figuring out if it actually works. And I don't mean \"it ran without crashing.\" I mean: does it produce correct, complete, reliable results at a reasonable cost?\n\n**The Quality Control in Manufacturing Analogy:**\n\nImagine you run a car factory. You wouldn't ship cars without testing them, right? You'd have:\n- **Unit tests** — Does each component work? (brakes, engine, lights)\n- **Integration tests** — Do components work together? (brakes + ABS + traction control)\n- **End-to-end tests** — Does the car drive safely from A to B?\n- **Performance benchmarks** — How fast? How fuel-efficient? How safe in crashes?\n\nAgent evaluation follows the exact same hierarchy:\n\n**Level 1: Component Testing** — Does each tool work correctly?\n```python\n# Test individual tools\nassert search('Python documentation') returns relevant results\nassert calculate('2 + 2') == 4\nassert format_response(data) matches expected schema\n```\n\n**Level 2: Step Testing** — Does the agent make good decisions at each step?\n```python\n# Give the agent a scenario, check its reasoning\nscenario = 'User asks for AAPL stock price'\nstep = agent.step(scenario)\nassert step.tool_called == 'get_stock_price'\nassert step.arguments == {'ticker': 'AAPL'}\n```\n\n**Level 3: End-to-End Testing** — Does the agent complete tasks correctly?\n```python\n# Full task execution\nresult = agent.run('Compare AAPL and MSFT stock performance')\nassert 'AAPL' in result and 'MSFT' in result\nassert result contains actual price data, not hallucinated numbers\nassert result completed in < 30 seconds\n```\n\n**Level 4: Benchmarking** — How does the agent perform across many tasks?\n```python\n# Run 100 test cases, measure aggregate metrics\nresults = [agent.run(test) for test in test_suite]\naccuracy = sum(r.correct for r in results) / len(results)\navg_cost = sum(r.cost for r in results) / len(results)\navg_latency = sum(r.latency for r in results) / len(results)\n```\n\n**The Five Metrics That Matter:**\n\n| Metric | What It Measures | Target |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Task Completion Rate** | % of tasks fully completed | > 85% |\n| **Accuracy** | % of correct final answers | > 90% |\n| **Avg Cost Per Task** | API costs per task | < $0.50 |\n| **Avg Latency** | Time from request to answer | < 30 seconds |\n| **Error Rate** | % of tasks that crash or loop | < 5% |\n\n**Why Most People Skip Evaluation (And Why That's Dangerous):**\nIt's tempting to test your agent on 3-4 examples and call it done. But agents are non-deterministic — they might work 9 times and fail spectacularly on the 10th. You need systematic evaluation with diverse test cases to find the edge cases that will bite you in production.\n\n**The Minimum Viable Test Suite:**\nCreate at least 20 test cases covering:\n- 5 happy-path cases (typical requests)\n- 5 edge cases (ambiguous queries, missing data)\n- 5 adversarial cases (prompt injection, out-of-scope requests)\n- 5 stress cases (complex multi-step tasks, large data)\n\nNow let's build a practical evaluation framework you can use today."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Agent Evaluation Toolkit",
            "explanation": "If you're a software engineer, you already know how to test code. Agent evaluation is the same idea — but with a few twists that make it unique.\n\n**The Software Testing Comparison:**\n\n| Software Testing | Agent Testing |\n|---|---|\n| Unit tests: test each function | Tool tests: test each tool |\n| Integration tests: test modules together | Step tests: test agent decisions |\n| E2E tests: test the full app | Task tests: test full task completion |\n| Deterministic: same input → same output | Non-deterministic: same input → different output! |\n| Pass/fail binary | Quality spectrum (partially correct counts) |\n\nThat last row is crucial. Software tests are binary — the function returns 4 or it doesn't. Agent tests exist on a spectrum — the agent might find 3 out of 5 competitors. Is that a pass or a fail? You need scoring rubrics.\n\n**Building Your Evaluation Framework:**\n\n**Step 1: Create a Test Suite**\n```python\ntest_cases = [\n    {\n        'input': 'What is the current price of AAPL?',\n        'expected_tools': ['get_stock_price'],\n        'expected_in_output': ['AAPL', '$'],\n        'max_steps': 5,\n        'max_cost': 0.05,\n        'difficulty': 'easy'\n    },\n    {\n        'input': 'Compare the market cap of AAPL, MSFT, and GOOGL',\n        'expected_tools': ['get_stock_price'],  # Called 3 times\n        'expected_in_output': ['AAPL', 'MSFT', 'GOOGL', 'trillion'],\n        'max_steps': 10,\n        'max_cost': 0.15,\n        'difficulty': 'medium'\n    },\n    {\n        'input': 'Analyze whether AAPL is overvalued based on PE ratio vs industry average',\n        'expected_tools': ['get_stock_price', 'search'],\n        'expected_in_output': ['P/E', 'ratio', 'industry'],\n        'max_steps': 15,\n        'max_cost': 0.30,\n        'difficulty': 'hard'\n    }\n]\n```\n\n**Step 2: Run and Score**\n```python\ndef evaluate_agent(agent, test_cases):\n    results = []\n    \n    for test in test_cases:\n        # Run the agent\n        start = time.time()\n        output = agent.run(test['input'])\n        latency = time.time() - start\n        \n        # Score the result\n        score = {\n            'test': test['input'],\n            'passed': all(keyword in output.text for keyword in test['expected_in_output']),\n            'correct_tools': set(output.tools_used) == set(test['expected_tools']),\n            'within_steps': output.steps <= test['max_steps'],\n            'within_budget': output.cost <= test['max_cost'],\n            'latency': latency,\n            'cost': output.cost\n        }\n        results.append(score)\n    \n    return results\n```\n\n**Step 3: Track Over Time**\nRun your test suite after every change. Watch for regressions.\n```python\n# Compare before and after a change\nbaseline = evaluate_agent(agent_v1, test_cases)\nupdated = evaluate_agent(agent_v2, test_cases)\n\nfor b, u in zip(baseline, updated):\n    if b['passed'] and not u['passed']:\n        print(f\"⚠️ REGRESSION: {b['test']}\")\n    if not b['passed'] and u['passed']:\n        print(f\"✅ FIXED: {b['test']}\")\n```\n\n**LLM-as-Judge for Complex Evaluation:**\nFor tasks where correctness is subjective (summaries, reports, creative content), use an LLM to evaluate:\n```python\ndef llm_judge(question, agent_answer, reference_answer):\n    prompt = f\"\"\"Rate the agent's answer on a scale of 1-5 for:\n    1. Accuracy (factually correct?)\n    2. Completeness (covers all aspects?)\n    3. Clarity (easy to understand?)\n    \n    Question: {question}\n    Agent Answer: {agent_answer}\n    Reference Answer: {reference_answer}\n    \"\"\"\n    return llm(prompt)  # Returns scores for each dimension\n```\n\n**The Evaluation Cadence:**\n- **During development:** Run after every significant change\n- **Before deployment:** Run the full suite + adversarial tests\n- **In production:** Sample 5-10% of real requests for quality monitoring\n- **After incidents:** Add the failing case to your test suite (never repeat the same failure)\n\nNow let's learn how to see inside your agent while it's running — observability, the X-ray vision for agents."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Building an Agent Evaluation Harness",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Build a simple evaluation function that tests an agent against a list of test cases. For each test, check if the agent's answer contains the expected keywords and count the score.",
            "starterCode": "def mock_agent(query):\n    if 'weather' in query.lower():\n        return 'The weather in Tokyo is 22°C and sunny.'\n    return 'I do not know.'\n\ntest_cases = [\n    {'query': 'Weather in Tokyo?', 'expected': ['Tokyo', '22']},\n    {'query': 'Weather in London?', 'expected': ['London']},\n    {'query': 'What is 2+2?', 'expected': ['4']}\n]\n\ndef evaluate(agent, tests):\n    passed = 0\n    for test in tests:\n        answer = agent(test['query'])\n        # Check if ALL expected keywords are in the answer\n        correct = all(___ in ___ for kw in test['expected'])\n        if correct:\n            passed += ___\n        print(f\"{'✅' if correct else '❌'} {test['query']}\")\n    \n    print(f'Score: {passed}/{len(tests)}')\n    return passed / len(tests)\n\nevaluate(mock_agent, test_cases)",
            "solution": "def mock_agent(query):\n    if 'weather' in query.lower():\n        return 'The weather in Tokyo is 22°C and sunny.'\n    return 'I do not know.'\n\ntest_cases = [\n    {'query': 'Weather in Tokyo?', 'expected': ['Tokyo', '22']},\n    {'query': 'Weather in London?', 'expected': ['London']},\n    {'query': 'What is 2+2?', 'expected': ['4']}\n]\n\ndef evaluate(agent, tests):\n    passed = 0\n    for test in tests:\n        answer = agent(test['query'])\n        correct = all(kw in answer for kw in test['expected'])\n        if correct:\n            passed += 1\n        print(f\"{'✅' if correct else '❌'} {test['query']}\")\n    \n    print(f'Score: {passed}/{len(tests)}')\n    return passed / len(tests)\n\nevaluate(mock_agent, test_cases)",
            "hint": "Use `all(kw in answer for kw in test['expected'])` to check all keywords. Increment passed by 1 for correct answers.",
            "explanation": "This is a minimal but functional evaluation harness. It tests each query, checks if the answer contains expected keywords, and reports a pass/fail score. In production, you'd add latency, cost, and LLM-as-a-judge scoring.",
            "concepts": [
              "Agent Evaluation",
              "Test Suite",
              "Metrics"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "all.*kw in answer.*expected.*passed.*len\\(tests\\)"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Observability: Seeing Inside Your Agent",
            "explanation": "Imagine trying to debug a car that won't start — but the hood is welded shut. You can hear the engine sputtering, but you can't see what's happening inside. That's what debugging an agent is like without observability.\n\n**The Flight Recorder Analogy:**\n\nEvery commercial airplane has a \"black box\" — a flight data recorder that captures every instrument reading, every pilot input, and every radio transmission. When something goes wrong, investigators can replay the entire flight and find exactly where things went off track.\n\n**Observability is your agent's black box.** It records everything the agent does so you can replay and analyze any run.\n\n**What to Log at Every Step — The Complete Record:**\n```python\nlog_entry = {\n    'run_id': run_id,              # Unique ID for this agent run\n    'step': step_number,           # Which iteration\n    'thought': llm_reasoning,      # What the agent was thinking\n    'tool_called': tool_name,      # What tool it chose\n    'tool_args': arguments,        # What arguments it passed\n    'tool_result': result[:500],   # What the tool returned (truncated)\n    'tokens_used': token_count,    # Token consumption this step\n    'latency_ms': duration_ms,     # How long this step took\n    'cost_usd': cost,              # How much this step cost\n    'cumulative_cost': total_cost, # Running total cost\n    'timestamp': datetime.now().isoformat()\n}\n```\n\n**The Trace Tree — Your Agent's X-Ray:**\nEvery agent run produces a tree structure:\n```\nAgent Run #4827 [Total: $0.12, 8.3s, 15 steps]\n├── LLM Call #1 [150 tokens, 0.8s, $0.002]\n│   └── Decision: Call search('AI agents 2024')\n├── Tool: search('AI agents 2024') [1.2s]\n│   └── Result: 3 articles found\n├── LLM Call #2 [200 tokens, 1.1s, $0.003]\n│   └── Decision: Call read_page(url='...')\n├── Tool: read_page(url='...') [2.1s]\n│   └── Result: 1,500 words extracted\n├── LLM Call #3 [350 tokens, 1.5s, $0.005]\n│   └── Decision: Enough info, generate answer\n└── Final Answer [500 tokens, 1.6s, $0.008]\n    └── 'AI agents are systems that...'\n```\n\nWith this tree, you can pinpoint exactly where things went wrong. Was it a bad search query? A misinterpreted tool result? An incorrect reasoning step?\n\n**The Observability Tool Landscape:**\n\n| Tool | Type | Best For |\n|---|---|---|\n| **LangSmith** | Managed SaaS | LangGraph/LangChain agents (most popular) |\n| **Arize Phoenix** | Open-source | Self-hosted, privacy-conscious teams |\n| **OpenTelemetry** | Standard protocol | Enterprise integrations, custom dashboards |\n| **Weights & Biases** | Experiment tracking | Comparing agent versions, A/B testing |\n| **Custom Logging** | DIY | Quick start, full control |\n\n**The Three Observability Dashboards You Need:**\n\n1. **Real-time Monitor** — Active agent runs, current step, cost so far\n2. **Historical Analysis** — Search past runs by user, task type, error code\n3. **Aggregate Metrics** — Daily task completion rate, average cost, p95 latency\n\n**Setting Up Minimal Observability (5 Minutes):**\n```python\nimport json, datetime\n\nclass AgentObserver:\n    def __init__(self, run_id):\n        self.run_id = run_id\n        self.log_file = f'agent_logs/{run_id}.jsonl'\n        self.steps = []\n    \n    def log_step(self, step_data):\n        step_data['run_id'] = self.run_id\n        step_data['timestamp'] = datetime.datetime.now().isoformat()\n        self.steps.append(step_data)\n        \n        with open(self.log_file, 'a') as f:\n            f.write(json.dumps(step_data) + '\\n')\n    \n    def summary(self):\n        total_cost = sum(s.get('cost_usd', 0) for s in self.steps)\n        total_tokens = sum(s.get('tokens_used', 0) for s in self.steps)\n        return f'Run {self.run_id}: {len(self.steps)} steps, {total_tokens} tokens, cost: {total_cost:.4f}'\n```\n\n**Why This Matters:**\nWithout observability, debugging agents is like finding a needle in a haystack — blindfolded. With it, you can replay any run, identify bottlenecks, catch regressions, and optimize costs. In production, observability isn't a nice-to-have — it's mandatory.\n\nNow let's put everything together with real-world projects. Time to build production-grade agents from scratch."
          }
        },
