        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "MCP vs Traditional APIs",
            "explanation": "Both MCP and traditional REST APIs allow systems to communicate, but they serve fundamentally different purposes. Understanding when to use each is critical for making the right architectural decision.\n\n**Traditional REST API Integration:**\nWhen you connect an AI agent to a REST API, you write a custom LangChain Tool that hardcodes the endpoint, authentication, request format, and response parsing:\n\n```python\n# Traditional approach: Custom tool per API\nimport requests\nfrom langchain_core.tools import tool\n\n@tool\ndef get_stock_price(ticker: str) -> str:\n    '''Get the current stock price.'''\n    headers = {'Authorization': 'Bearer sk-xxx'}\n    response = requests.get(\n        f'https://api.stocks.com/v1/price/{ticker}',\n        headers=headers\n    )\n    data = response.json()\n    return f'{ticker}: ${data[\"price\"]}'\n```\n\nThis tool is permanently welded to this specific API. If you want another AI app to use the same stock API, you copy-paste the entire integration.\n\n**MCP Approach:**\nWith MCP, you wrap the API in a server ONCE. Any MCP client discovers and uses it automatically:\n\n```python\n# MCP approach: Build the server once\nfrom mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\nimport requests\n\nmcp = FastMCP('StockServer')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef get_stock_price(ticker: str) -> str:\n    '''Get the current stock price for a ticker symbol.'''\n    headers = {'Authorization': 'Bearer sk-xxx'}\n    response = requests.get(\n        f'https://api.stocks.com/v1/price/{ticker}',\n        headers=headers\n    )\n    data = response.json()\n    return f'{ticker}: ${data[\"price\"]}'\n```\n\nThe code is nearly identical — but now ANY MCP-compatible client (Claude Desktop, Cursor, your custom agent) can discover and call `get_stock_price` without writing any integration code.\n\n**Key Differences:**\n\n| Feature | REST API Tool | MCP Server |\n|---|---|---|\n| Discovery | Hardcoded in agent code | Dynamic via capability negotiation |\n| Reusability | One agent only | Any MCP client |\n| Protocol | Custom HTTP | Standardized JSON-RPC |\n| Schema | Manual documentation | Auto-generated from type hints |\n| Adding to new app | Copy-paste integration | Just connect to the server |\n\n**The Key Insight:**\nMCP doesn't replace REST APIs — it wraps them. Your MCP server still calls REST APIs, databases, or any other backend service under the hood. MCP just standardizes HOW AI clients discover and invoke those capabilities."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
