        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "MCP Architecture Deep Dive",
            "explanation": "Now that you understand the WHY of MCP, let's dive into HOW it works. MCP follows a strict client-server model with three well-defined components.\n\n**The Three Components:**\n\n**1. MCP Host (The Application)**\nThe top-level application the user interacts with — Claude Desktop, Cursor IDE, VS Code, or YOUR custom AI app. The Host:\n• Creates and manages MCP Client instances\n• Handles user authorization and consent\n• Enforces security policies\n• Provides the LLM that processes tool results\n\n**2. MCP Client (The Connector)**\nLives inside the Host and maintains a 1:1 connection to a single MCP Server. It:\n• Handles JSON-RPC protocol communication\n• Performs capability negotiation on connect\n• Converts server tool schemas to the LLM's native format\n• Routes the LLM's tool calls to the correct server\n\nEach server connection gets its own dedicated client. If your Host connects to 3 servers (GitHub, Slack, PostgreSQL), it creates 3 MCP Client instances.\n\n**3. MCP Server (The Tool Wrapper)**\nA lightweight program that wraps a specific external service. It:\n• Implements the MCP protocol\n• Exposes tools, resources, and prompts\n• Handles tool execution and returns results\n• Is designed to be small, focused, and composable\n\n**The Communication Flow:**\n```\nUser → Host → Client ←JSON-RPC→ Server → External Service\n                                            (GitHub API,\n                                             Database,\n                                             File System)\n```\n\n**Transport Layers:**\nMCP supports two transport mechanisms:\n\n1. **stdio** — For local servers. The client spawns the server as a subprocess and communicates via stdin/stdout. Zero network latency, highly secure.\n\n2. **SSE/HTTP (Streamable HTTP)** — For remote servers. Client-to-server uses HTTP POST, server-to-client uses Server-Sent Events for streaming. Ideal for cloud-hosted servers.\n\n```python\n# stdio transport: local server as subprocess\nserver_params = StdioServerParameters(\n    command='python',\n    args=['my_mcp_server.py']\n)\n\n# HTTP transport: remote server\n# Client connects to: http://my-server.com:8000/mcp\n```\n\nUse **stdio** for development and local tools (file system, local databases). Use **HTTP/SSE** for production servers that need to be shared across machines or teams."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "visual",
          "content": {
            "title": "MCP Architecture Stack",
            "description": "The MCP architecture follows a layered model. The Host application contains Clients, each Client connects to one Server, and each Server wraps an external service.",
            "type": "architecture_stack",
            "layers": [
