          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "JSON-RPC 2.0: The Wire Protocol",
            "explanation": "All MCP communication uses JSON-RPC 2.0 — a simple, lightweight remote procedure call protocol encoded in JSON. Understanding these messages helps you debug issues and build custom integrations.\n\n**A JSON-RPC Request:**\nWhen the client wants to call a tool on the server:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n  \"id\": 1,\n  \"method\": \"tools/call\",\n  \"params\": {\n    \"name\": \"get_weather\",\n    \"arguments\": {\n      \"city\": \"San Francisco\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**A JSON-RPC Response:**\nThe server processes the request and responds:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n  \"id\": 1,\n  \"result\": {\n    \"content\": [\n      {\n        \"type\": \"text\",\n        \"text\": \"Weather in San Francisco: Sunny, 18°C\"\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Key JSON-RPC Fields:**\n• `jsonrpc` — Always \"2.0\" (protocol version)\n• `id` — Request identifier for matching responses to requests\n• `method` — The MCP method being called (e.g., `tools/call`, `tools/list`, `initialize`)\n• `params` — Arguments for the method\n• `result` — Success response data\n• `error` — Error response with code and message\n\n**The Initialization Handshake:**\nWhen a client first connects to a server, they exchange `initialize` messages:\n\n```python\n# Client sends:\n# {\"method\": \"initialize\", \"params\": {\"capabilities\": {...}}}\n\n# Server responds:\n# {\"result\": {\"capabilities\": {\"tools\": {}, \"resources\": {}}}}\n\n# Client confirms:\n# {\"method\": \"notifications/initialized\"}\n```\n\nThis capability negotiation tells each side what the other supports. If the server only supports tools (not resources), the client won't try to read resources."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Parsing a JSON-RPC MCP Response",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Write a function called 'parse_mcp_response' that takes a JSON-RPC response dictionary and extracts the tool result text. If the response contains an 'error' key, return the error message instead. If the result has 'content', return the 'text' field of the first content item.",
            "starterCode": "def parse_mcp_response(response: dict) -> str:\n    '''Parse an MCP JSON-RPC response and extract the result text.'''\n    # Check for errors first\n    ___\n    # Extract the text from the result content\n    ___",
            "solution": "def parse_mcp_response(response: dict) -> str:\n    '''Parse an MCP JSON-RPC response and extract the result text.'''\n    if 'error' in response:\n        return f'Error: {response[\"error\"][\"message\"]}'\n    result = response.get('result', {})\n    content = result.get('content', [])\n    if content:\n        return content[0].get('text', 'No text in response')\n    return 'Empty response'",
            "hint": "Check for 'error' key first. Then navigate: response['result']['content'][0]['text'].",
            "explanation": "MCP responses follow JSON-RPC 2.0 format. The tool result is nested inside result.content[0].text. Error responses have an 'error' object with 'code' and 'message'. Understanding this structure helps you debug MCP integrations.",
            "concepts": [
              "JSON-RPC",
              "MCP Response Format",
              "Error Handling"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "def parse_mcp_response.*response.*dict.*error.*result.*content"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The MCP Lifecycle: From Connection to Shutdown",
            "explanation": "Every MCP session follows a strict lifecycle. Understanding this sequence is critical for debugging connection issues.\n\n[IMAGE:/images/m13_mcp_lifecycle.jpg]\n\n**Phase 1 — Initialization:**\n1. Host starts and reads its MCP server configuration\n2. Host creates an MCP Client for each configured server\n3. Each Client connects to its Server via the configured transport (stdio or HTTP)\n4. Client sends `initialize` request with its capabilities\n5. Server responds with ITS capabilities (which tools, resources, prompts it supports)\n6. Client sends `notifications/initialized` to confirm\n\n**Phase 2 — Discovery:**\n7. Client calls `tools/list` to get all available tools with their JSON schemas\n8. Client calls `resources/list` to get available resources (if supported)\n9. Client calls `prompts/list` to get available prompts (if supported)\n10. Client converts tool schemas to the LLM's native function-calling format\n\n**Phase 3 — Operation:**\n11. User asks a question\n12. LLM sees available tools and decides to call one\n13. Client sends `tools/call` request to the server\n14. Server executes the tool and returns the result\n15. Client passes the result back to the LLM\n16. LLM generates a response using the tool result\n17. Steps 11-16 repeat as needed\n\n**Phase 4 — Shutdown:**\n18. Host sends shutdown signal\n19. Clients close connections gracefully\n20. Servers clean up resources (close DB connections, etc.)"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "reorder",
          "challenge": {
            "instruction": "Order the MCP connection lifecycle from start to finish:",
            "correctOrder": [
              "Host application starts",
              "Host creates MCP Client instances",
              "Clients connect to Servers via transport (stdio or HTTP)",
              "Client sends initialize request with capabilities",
              "Server responds with its capabilities",
              "Client calls tools/list to discover available tools",
              "Normal operation: tool calls and resource reads",
              "Graceful shutdown with resource cleanup"
            ],
            "question": "Write the code here:",
            "solution": "# Solution",
            "starterCode": "# Write code here"
          }
        },
