          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Building an MCP Server",
            "explanation": "Time to build! An MCP Server is a program that wraps an external tool or data source and makes it available to any MCP client. The official Python SDK (`mcp`) provides **FastMCP** — a high-level API that handles all the protocol boilerplate so you focus only on your tool logic.\n\n**Installation:**\n```bash\npip install mcp\n```\n\n**The Minimal MCP Server:**\nEvery MCP server starts with just 3 lines:\n\n```python\nfrom mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nmcp = FastMCP('MyServer')\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    mcp.run()\n```\n\nThat's it — a working MCP server. It doesn't expose any tools yet, but it handles the JSON-RPC protocol, capability negotiation, and transport layer automatically.\n\n**What FastMCP Does For You:**\n• Handles all JSON-RPC 2.0 message parsing and routing\n• Auto-generates tool schemas from Python type hints\n• Manages the initialization handshake\n• Provides decorators for tools, resources, and prompts\n• Supports both stdio and HTTP transports\n\n**Server Name Matters:**\nThe name you pass to `FastMCP('MyServer')` is what clients see during capability negotiation. Use a descriptive name like `'WeatherServer'` or `'GitHubIntegration'` — not `'test'` or `'server1'`."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Creating a Minimal MCP Server",
            "difficulty": "Easy",
            "question": "Create a minimal MCP server using FastMCP. Name it 'CalculatorServer'. Include the if __name__ == '__main__' guard to run it.",
            "starterCode": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\n# Create the server\nmcp = ___\n\n# Run the server\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    ___",
            "solution": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nmcp = FastMCP('CalculatorServer')\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    mcp.run()",
            "hint": "Initialize FastMCP with the name 'CalculatorServer', then call mcp.run() inside the __main__ guard.",
            "explanation": "This is the minimal MCP server. FastMCP('CalculatorServer') creates a server named 'CalculatorServer'. mcp.run() starts listening for client connections. The server handles all JSON-RPC protocol details automatically.",
            "concepts": [
              "FastMCP",
              "Server Initialization",
              "mcp.run()"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "FastMCP\\('CalculatorServer'\\)"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Adding Tools to Your Server",
            "explanation": "A server without tools is like a restaurant without a menu. Tools are functions that the AI model can call. Adding one is as simple as decorating a Python function.\n\n**The @mcp.tool() Decorator:**\nTurn any Python function into an MCP tool with one decorator:\n\n```python\n@mcp.tool()\ndef add(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    '''Add two numbers together.'''\n    return a + b\n```\n\n**Three Things That Matter:**\n\n1. **Type Hints are REQUIRED** — FastMCP reads `a: int, b: int` to auto-generate the tool's JSON Schema. Without type hints, the LLM won't know what parameters to pass.\n\n2. **Docstrings are Descriptions** — The LLM reads your docstring to decide WHEN to use this tool. Write clear, specific docstrings:\n   - ✅ `'''Add two numbers together and return the sum.'''`\n   - ❌ `'''Does math stuff.'''`\n\n3. **Return Values** — Tools should return strings (or types that can be serialized to text). The return value is sent back to the LLM as the tool's result.\n\n**Multiple Tools:**\nA single server can expose many tools:\n\n```python\nmcp = FastMCP('MathServer')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef add(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    '''Add two numbers.'''\n    return a + b\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef multiply(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    '''Multiply two numbers.'''\n    return a * b\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef divide(a: float, b: float) -> str:\n    '''Divide a by b. Returns error if b is zero.'''\n    if b == 0:\n        return 'Error: Cannot divide by zero'\n    return str(a / b)\n```\n\nWhen a client connects, it calls `tools/list` and receives the schemas for ALL three tools. The LLM then chooses which one to call based on the user's question."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Building a Multi-Tool MCP Server",
            "difficulty": "Easy",
            "question": "Create a FastMCP server named 'MathServer' with two tools: 'add' (takes two ints, returns their sum) and 'subtract' (takes two ints, returns their difference). Include docstrings and type hints for both.",
            "starterCode": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nmcp = FastMCP('MathServer')\n\n# Tool 1: Addition\n@mcp.tool()\ndef add(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    ___\n\n# Tool 2: Subtraction\n___\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    mcp.run()",
            "solution": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nmcp = FastMCP('MathServer')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef add(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    '''Add two numbers together and return the sum.'''\n    return a + b\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef subtract(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    '''Subtract b from a and return the difference.'''\n    return a - b\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    mcp.run()",
            "hint": "Each tool needs @mcp.tool(), type hints for all parameters, a docstring, and a return statement.",
            "explanation": "Each @mcp.tool() decorated function becomes a callable tool. FastMCP auto-generates JSON schemas from the type hints (a: int, b: int -> int) and uses the docstrings as tool descriptions for the LLM.",
            "concepts": [
              "Multiple Tools",
              "@mcp.tool()",
              "Type Hints"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "@mcp\\.tool\\(\\)\\s*\\ndef add.*@mcp\\.tool\\(\\)\\s*\\ndef subtract"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Resources: Exposing Data to the AI",
            "explanation": "Tools let the AI DO things. Resources let the AI READ things. A Resource is read-only data that the AI can access — file contents, database records, configuration, logs, etc.\n\n**Static Resources:**\nExpose data at a fixed URI:\n\n```python\n@mcp.resource('config://app/settings')\ndef get_settings() -> str:\n    '''Read the application configuration.'''\n    return json.dumps({\n        'version': '2.1.0',\n        'debug': False,\n        'max_retries': 3\n    })\n```\n\n**Dynamic Resource Templates:**\nUse URI templates for parameterized resources:\n\n```python\n@mcp.resource('users://{user_id}/profile')\ndef get_user_profile(user_id: str) -> str:\n    '''Get a user profile by ID.'''\n    return json.dumps({'id': user_id, 'name': 'Alice'})\n```\n\nThe `{user_id}` placeholder makes this a dynamic resource — the client can request `users://alice/profile` or `users://bob/profile`.\n\n**Tool vs Resource:**\n• **Tool** — Executes an ACTION. The LLM decides when to call it. Can have side effects.\n• **Resource** — Provides DATA. Read-only. No side effects. Used to give the LLM context.\n\nUse resources for data the LLM needs to reference (documentation, configs, logs). Use tools for actions (search, calculate, send email)."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Creating an MCP Resource",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Add a static resource to the MathServer that exposes the server's version info at the URI 'info://server/version'. It should return a JSON string with keys 'name', 'version', and 'tools_count'.",
            "starterCode": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\nimport json\n\nmcp = FastMCP('MathServer')\n\n# Add a resource at 'info://server/version'\n___\ndef get_version() -> str:\n    '''Get server version information.'''\n    return json.dumps(___)",
            "solution": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\nimport json\n\nmcp = FastMCP('MathServer')\n\n@mcp.resource('info://server/version')\ndef get_version() -> str:\n    '''Get server version information.'''\n    return json.dumps({'name': 'MathServer', 'version': '1.0.0', 'tools_count': 2})",
            "hint": "Use @mcp.resource('info://server/version') decorator and return json.dumps() with the required keys.",
            "explanation": "Resources use the @mcp.resource() decorator with a URI string. The function returns string data that the AI can read. Unlike tools, resources are passive data providers — they don't take action, they provide context.",
            "concepts": [
              "@mcp.resource()",
              "Static Resources",
              "URI Schemes"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "@mcp\\.resource\\(.*info://server/version.*\\)"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Prompts: Reusable Templates for the AI",
            "explanation": "The third MCP primitive is Prompts — reusable prompt templates with arguments. They let server authors provide pre-built, optimized prompts that clients can use.\n\n**Creating a Prompt:**\n```python\nfrom mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\nfrom mcp.types import TextContent\n\n@mcp.prompt()\ndef code_review(language: str, code: str) -> str:\n    '''Generate a detailed code review prompt.'''\n    return f'''You are an expert {language} code reviewer.\nReview the following code for:\n1. Bugs and logical errors\n2. Performance issues\n3. Security vulnerabilities\n4. Code style and best practices\n\nCode to review:\n```{language}\n{code}\n```'''\n```\n\n**When to Use Prompts:**\n• Your MCP server wraps a domain-specific tool, and you want to provide optimized prompts for that domain\n• You want to standardize how AI assistants interact with your tools\n• You want to version-control prompt templates alongside the tools they complement\n\n**Prompts vs Tools:**\n• A **Tool** is a function the LLM calls to get data or take action\n• A **Prompt** is a template that shapes HOW the LLM thinks about a task\n\nIn practice, Tools are used far more frequently than Prompts in MCP servers. Focus on mastering Tools first."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Complete MCP Server with Tools, Resources, and Prompts",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Build a complete MCP server named 'DevToolsServer' that has:\n1. A tool called 'count_words' that takes text (str) and returns the word count as a string\n2. A resource at 'info://help' that returns usage instructions\n3. Run the server with mcp.run()\n\nStore the server in a variable called 'mcp'.",
            "starterCode": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nmcp = FastMCP('DevToolsServer')\n\n# Tool: count words in text\n___\n\n# Resource: help info\n___\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    mcp.run()",
            "solution": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nmcp = FastMCP('DevToolsServer')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef count_words(text: str) -> str:\n    '''Count the number of words in the given text.'''\n    count = len(text.split())\n    return f'The text contains {count} words.'\n\n@mcp.resource('info://help')\ndef get_help() -> str:\n    '''Get usage instructions for the DevTools server.'''\n    return 'Available tools: count_words(text) - counts words in text.'\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    mcp.run()",
            "hint": "Use @mcp.tool() for the word counter and @mcp.resource('info://help') for the help resource. Both need docstrings and type hints.",
            "explanation": "This server exposes both a tool (count_words) and a resource (help info). When a client connects, it discovers both capabilities. The LLM can call count_words when users ask about word counts, and read the help resource for context about available features.",
            "concepts": [
              "Complete MCP Server",
              "Tools + Resources",
              "Server Composition"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "@mcp\\.tool\\(\\).*count_words.*@mcp\\.resource"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "questions": [
            {
              "question": "Why are Python type hints REQUIRED in MCP tool functions? (Select the correct option)",
              "options": [
                "They make Python code run faster",
                "FastMCP uses them to auto-generate the JSON Schema that tells the LLM what parameters to pass",
                "They are needed for Python syntax to be valid",
                "They encrypt the tool parameters for security"
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explanation": "FastMCP reads Python type hints (e.g., city: str, count: int) to automatically generate the tool's JSON Schema. This schema tells the LLM exactly what parameters the tool expects, their types, and which are required."
            },
            {
              "question": "What is the difference between an MCP Tool and an MCP Resource? (Select the correct option)",
              "options": [
                "Tools return strings, Resources return JSON",
                "Tools execute actions and can have side effects; Resources provide read-only data",
                "Tools are local, Resources are remote",
                "Tools are faster, Resources are slower"
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explanation": "Tools are action-oriented — the LLM calls them to DO something (search, calculate, send). Resources are data-oriented — they provide read-only context (files, configs, docs). Tools can have side effects; resources should not."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
