          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Building an MCP Client & Tool Integration",
            "explanation": "You've built MCP servers that expose tools. Now let's build the other side — the MCP Client that connects to those servers, discovers tools, and lets your AI agent use them.\n\n**The Client's Job:**\n1. Connect to one or more MCP servers\n2. Discover available tools via `tools/list`\n3. Convert tool schemas to the LLM's function-calling format\n4. Route the LLM's tool calls to the correct server\n5. Return results back to the LLM\n\n**Connecting via stdio:**\nFor local servers, the client spawns the server as a subprocess:\n\n```python\nfrom mcp import ClientSession, StdioServerParameters\nfrom mcp.client.stdio import stdio_client\nimport asyncio\n\nasync def main():\n    server_params = StdioServerParameters(\n        command='python',\n        args=['my_mcp_server.py']\n    )\n\n    async with stdio_client(server_params) as (read, write):\n        async with ClientSession(read, write) as session:\n            await session.initialize()\n\n            # Discover available tools\n            tools = await session.list_tools()\n            for tool in tools.tools:\n                print(f'Tool: {tool.name} — {tool.description}')\n\n            # Call a tool\n            result = await session.call_tool(\n                'add', {'a': 5, 'b': 3}\n            )\n            print(f'Result: {result.content[0].text}')\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\n\n**Key Points:**\n• `StdioServerParameters` — defines which command to run (spawns the server)\n• `stdio_client()` — context manager that starts the server process\n• `ClientSession` — the actual MCP session for communication\n• `session.initialize()` — performs capability negotiation\n• `session.list_tools()` — discovers available tools\n• `session.call_tool()` — invokes a tool with arguments\n\nEverything is async because MCP communication is I/O-bound. You'll need `asyncio.run()` to execute from a script."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Building a stdio MCP Client",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Write an async function called 'connect_and_list_tools' that connects to a local MCP server (running as 'python server.py'), initializes the session, lists all available tools, and returns a list of tool names (strings).",
            "starterCode": "from mcp import ClientSession, StdioServerParameters\nfrom mcp.client.stdio import stdio_client\n\nasync def connect_and_list_tools() -> list:\n    '''Connect to MCP server and return list of tool names.'''\n    server_params = StdioServerParameters(\n        command='python',\n        args=['server.py']\n    )\n\n    async with stdio_client(server_params) as (read, write):\n        async with ClientSession(read, write) as session:\n            # Initialize the session\n            ___\n            # List tools and return their names\n            ___",
            "solution": "from mcp import ClientSession, StdioServerParameters\nfrom mcp.client.stdio import stdio_client\n\nasync def connect_and_list_tools() -> list:\n    '''Connect to MCP server and return list of tool names.'''\n    server_params = StdioServerParameters(\n        command='python',\n        args=['server.py']\n    )\n\n    async with stdio_client(server_params) as (read, write):\n        async with ClientSession(read, write) as session:\n            await session.initialize()\n            tools = await session.list_tools()\n            return [tool.name for tool in tools.tools]",
            "hint": "Call await session.initialize() first, then await session.list_tools(). Use a list comprehension to extract tool.name from each tool.",
            "explanation": "The client flow is: connect → initialize → discover → use. session.initialize() performs the JSON-RPC handshake. session.list_tools() returns tool objects with .name and .description attributes. We extract just the names into a list.",
            "concepts": [
              "MCP Client",
              "stdio_client",
              "Tool Discovery"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "await session\\.initialize\\(\\).*await session\\.list_tools\\(\\)"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Multi-Server Orchestration & LangChain Integration",
            "explanation": "A real AI application doesn't connect to just one MCP server — it connects to many. Your chatbot might need GitHub (for code), Slack (for messages), and PostgreSQL (for data) simultaneously.\n\n**Connecting to Multiple Servers:**\nEach server gets its own client and session:\n\n```python\nasync def connect_multi_server():\n    servers = {\n        'math': StdioServerParameters(command='python', args=['math_server.py']),\n        'weather': StdioServerParameters(command='python', args=['weather_server.py']),\n    }\n\n    all_tools = []\n    for name, params in servers.items():\n        async with stdio_client(params) as (read, write):\n            async with ClientSession(read, write) as session:\n                await session.initialize()\n                tools = await session.list_tools()\n                all_tools.extend(tools.tools)\n\n    print(f'Total tools available: {len(all_tools)}')\n```\n\n**LangChain + MCP Integration:**\nThe real power comes from connecting MCP tools to a LangChain/LangGraph agent. You convert MCP tool schemas into LangChain Tool objects:\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_core.tools import StructuredTool\n\n# Convert an MCP tool to a LangChain tool\ndef mcp_to_langchain_tool(session, mcp_tool):\n    async def call_tool(**kwargs):\n        result = await session.call_tool(mcp_tool.name, kwargs)\n        return result.content[0].text\n\n    return StructuredTool(\n        name=mcp_tool.name,\n        description=mcp_tool.description,\n        func=call_tool,\n        args_schema=mcp_tool.inputSchema\n    )\n```\n\n**The langchain-mcp-adapters Package:**\nFor production, use the official adapter package that handles the conversion automatically:\n\n```bash\npip install langchain-mcp-adapters\n```\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_mcp_adapters.client import MultiServerMCPClient\nfrom langgraph.prebuilt import create_react_agent\nfrom langchain_openai import ChatOpenAI\n\nclient = MultiServerMCPClient({\n    'math': {\n        'command': 'python',\n        'args': ['math_server.py'],\n        'transport': 'stdio'\n    },\n    'weather': {\n        'url': 'http://weather-server:8000/mcp',\n        'transport': 'streamable_http'\n    }\n})\n\ntools = await client.get_tools()\nagent = create_react_agent(ChatOpenAI(model='gpt-4o-mini'), tools)\n```\n\nNow your LangGraph agent has access to ALL tools from ALL MCP servers — and it dynamically discovers them at startup."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Calling an MCP Tool from Client",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Write an async function called 'call_mcp_tool' that connects to a local MCP server ('python calc_server.py'), initializes the session, and calls a tool named 'add' with arguments a=10 and b=20. Return the text content of the result.",
            "starterCode": "from mcp import ClientSession, StdioServerParameters\nfrom mcp.client.stdio import stdio_client\n\nasync def call_mcp_tool() -> str:\n    '''Connect to server and call the add tool.'''\n    server_params = StdioServerParameters(\n        command='python',\n        args=['calc_server.py']\n    )\n\n    async with stdio_client(server_params) as (read, write):\n        async with ClientSession(read, write) as session:\n            await session.initialize()\n            # Call the 'add' tool with a=10, b=20\n            result = ___\n            # Return the text content\n            return ___",
            "solution": "from mcp import ClientSession, StdioServerParameters\nfrom mcp.client.stdio import stdio_client\n\nasync def call_mcp_tool() -> str:\n    '''Connect to server and call the add tool.'''\n    server_params = StdioServerParameters(\n        command='python',\n        args=['calc_server.py']\n    )\n\n    async with stdio_client(server_params) as (read, write):\n        async with ClientSession(read, write) as session:\n            await session.initialize()\n            result = await session.call_tool('add', {'a': 10, 'b': 20})\n            return result.content[0].text",
            "hint": "Use await session.call_tool('add', {'a': 10, 'b': 20}). The result text is at result.content[0].text.",
            "explanation": "session.call_tool() sends a JSON-RPC tools/call request to the server. The first argument is the tool name, the second is a dictionary of arguments. The result follows MCP's content format — the text is at result.content[0].text.",
            "concepts": [
              "session.call_tool()",
              "Tool Invocation",
              "Result Parsing"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "await session\\.call_tool\\('add'.*\\{.*'a'.*10.*'b'.*20.*\\}"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "LangGraph Agent with MCP Tools",
            "difficulty": "Hard",
            "question": "Create a LangGraph ReAct agent that uses MCP tools. Use MultiServerMCPClient to connect to a math server (stdio transport, command='python', args=['math_server.py']). Get the tools and create the agent with create_react_agent using ChatOpenAI('gpt-4o-mini'). Store the agent in a variable called 'agent'.",
            "starterCode": "from langchain_mcp_adapters.client import MultiServerMCPClient\nfrom langgraph.prebuilt import create_react_agent\nfrom langchain_openai import ChatOpenAI\n\n# Configure the MCP client with server details\nclient = MultiServerMCPClient({\n    'math': ___\n})\n\n# Get tools from MCP servers\ntools = ___\n\n# Create the ReAct agent\nagent = ___",
            "solution": "from langchain_mcp_adapters.client import MultiServerMCPClient\nfrom langgraph.prebuilt import create_react_agent\nfrom langchain_openai import ChatOpenAI\n\nclient = MultiServerMCPClient({\n    'math': {\n        'command': 'python',\n        'args': ['math_server.py'],\n        'transport': 'stdio'\n    }\n})\n\ntools = client.get_tools()\n\nagent = create_react_agent(ChatOpenAI(model='gpt-4o-mini'), tools)",
            "hint": "Configure the math server with 'command', 'args', and 'transport' keys. Use client.get_tools() and create_react_agent(model, tools).",
            "explanation": "MultiServerMCPClient handles connecting to MCP servers and converting their tools to LangChain format. get_tools() returns LangChain-compatible tool objects. create_react_agent builds a complete ReAct agent that can discover and call MCP tools dynamically.",
            "concepts": [
