        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "MCP Security & Authentication",
            "explanation": "MCP servers often expose access to sensitive systems — databases, internal APIs, file systems, email services. A misconfigured MCP server can turn your helpful AI assistant into an attack vector. Security isn't optional — it's foundational.\n\n**The Threat Model:**\nMCP introduces a unique security challenge: an AI model (which can hallucinate) decides which tools to call and with what arguments. You must protect against:\n\n1. **Prompt Injection** — An attacker embeds malicious instructions in data the LLM reads, causing it to call tools in unintended ways\n2. **Parameter Manipulation** — The LLM hallucinates dangerous inputs (e.g., path traversal, SQL injection)\n3. **Unauthorized Access** — An unauthenticated client connects to your server\n4. **Privilege Escalation** — A tool exposes more capabilities than intended\n\n**Authentication for Remote Servers:**\nLocal servers (stdio) are relatively safe — they run on the same machine. But remote servers (HTTP/SSE) need authentication:\n\n```python\n# Server-side: Validate API key from request headers\nfrom mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nmcp = FastMCP('SecureServer')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef get_secret_data(query: str) -> str:\n    '''Query internal data. Requires authentication.'''\n    # In production, validate the auth token\n    # from the request context\n    return f'Confidential results for: {query}'\n```\n\nThe MCP specification recommends **OAuth 2.0 with PKCE** for browser-based clients and **API keys or client credentials** for server-to-server connections.\n\n**Key Security Principles:**\n• **Validate ALL tool inputs server-side** — never trust the LLM\n• **Use allowlists, not blocklists** — restrict to known-good values\n• **Implement rate limiting** — prevent tool-calling loops from overwhelming backends\n• **Log everything** — every tool call, arguments, results, and errors\n• **Principle of Least Privilege** — each tool should have minimal permissions\n• **Use environment variables for secrets** — never hardcode API keys"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Secure Tool with Input Validation",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Create an MCP tool called 'read_file' that takes a file_path (str). Implement security validation:\n1. Check that the path doesn't contain '..' (path traversal protection)\n2. Check that the path starts with '/workspace/' (restrict to safe directory)\n3. Return appropriate error messages for violations\n4. Return mock file contents for valid paths",
            "starterCode": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nmcp = FastMCP('FileServer')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef read_file(file_path: str) -> str:\n    '''Read the contents of a file within the /workspace/ directory.\n    Only files within /workspace/ are accessible for security.'''\n    # Security validation\n    ___\n    # Mock file reading\n    return f'Contents of {file_path}: [file data here]'",
            "solution": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nmcp = FastMCP('FileServer')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef read_file(file_path: str) -> str:\n    '''Read the contents of a file within the /workspace/ directory.\n    Only files within /workspace/ are accessible for security.'''\n    if '..' in file_path:\n        return 'Error: Path traversal detected. \"..\" is not allowed in file paths.'\n    if not file_path.startswith('/workspace/'):\n        return f'Error: Access denied. Only files in /workspace/ are readable. Got: {file_path}'\n    return f'Contents of {file_path}: [file data here]'",
            "hint": "Check for '..' first (path traversal). Then check startswith('/workspace/'). Return specific error messages for each violation.",
            "explanation": "This demonstrates two critical security patterns: path traversal prevention (blocking '..') and directory restriction (only allowing /workspace/). Both checks happen server-side because the LLM might hallucinate dangerous paths.",
            "concepts": [
              "Path Traversal Prevention",
              "Directory Restriction",
              "Input Validation"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "def read_file\\(file_path:\\s*str\\).*\\.\\.'.*in.*file_path.*startswith"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Trust Boundaries & Tool Isolation",
            "explanation": "Not every tool carries the same risk. Reading a weather API is harmless. Deleting database records is dangerous. MCP security architecture requires you to separate tools by risk level.\n\n**Trust Boundary Pattern:**\nSeparate high-risk and low-risk tools into different MCP servers:\n\n```python\n# Low-risk server: read-only operations\nsafe_server = FastMCP('ReadOnlyTools')\n\n@safe_server.tool()\ndef search_docs(query: str) -> str:\n    '''Search documentation (read-only).'''\n    ...\n\n# High-risk server: write operations (separate process!)\ndanger_server = FastMCP('WriteTools')\n\n@danger_server.tool()\ndef delete_record(table: str, record_id: int) -> str:\n    '''Delete a database record. DESTRUCTIVE operation.'''\n    ...\n```\n\n**Why Separate Servers?**\n• Different authentication requirements\n• Different rate limits (write operations need stricter limits)\n• Different audit logging levels\n• You can disable the dangerous server without affecting read-only tools\n• Different deployment permissions (only senior devs can deploy write servers)\n\n**Audit Logging:**\nLog every tool invocation:\n\n```python\nimport logging\n\nlogger = logging.getLogger('mcp_audit')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef delete_user(user_id: str) -> str:\n    '''Delete a user account. This action is irreversible.'''\n    logger.warning(f'DELETE_USER called: user_id={user_id}')\n    # ... perform deletion ...\n    logger.info(f'DELETE_USER completed: user_id={user_id}')\n    return f'User {user_id} deleted successfully'\n```\n\n**Secret Management:**\nNever hardcode API keys or database passwords in your MCP server:\n\n```python\nimport os\n\n# GOOD: Load from environment\nDB_URL = os.environ['DATABASE_URL']\nAPI_KEY = os.environ['STRIPE_API_KEY']\n\n# BAD: Hardcoded secrets\n# DB_URL = 'postgresql://admin:password123@prod-db:5432/main'\n```"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Tool with Rate Limiting",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Create an MCP tool called 'search_api' that includes basic rate limiting. Use a module-level counter to track calls. If called more than 10 times, return an error asking the agent to wait. Otherwise, increment the counter and return mock results.",
            "starterCode": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nmcp = FastMCP('SearchServer')\n\n# Simple rate limit counter\ncall_count = 0\nMAX_CALLS = 10\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef search_api(query: str) -> str:\n    '''Search the external API. Rate limited to 10 calls per session.'''\n    global call_count\n    ___",
            "solution": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nmcp = FastMCP('SearchServer')\n\ncall_count = 0\nMAX_CALLS = 10\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef search_api(query: str) -> str:\n    '''Search the external API. Rate limited to 10 calls per session.'''\n    global call_count\n    if call_count >= MAX_CALLS:\n        return f'Error: Rate limit exceeded ({MAX_CALLS} calls). Please wait before searching again.'\n    call_count += 1\n    return f'Search results for \"{query}\": [result 1, result 2, result 3]'",
            "hint": "Check if call_count >= MAX_CALLS first. If so, return an error. Otherwise, increment and return results.",
            "explanation": "Rate limiting prevents an AI agent stuck in a tool-calling loop from overwhelming your backend. The error message tells the agent to stop calling, which a well-designed agent will respect. In production, use a proper rate limiter (e.g., token bucket algorithm).",
            "concepts": [
              "Rate Limiting",
              "Global State",
              "Agent Loop Prevention"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "call_count.*>=.*MAX_CALLS.*Error.*Rate limit"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "content": {
