          "type": "flashcard",
          "cards": [
            {
              "front": "Why should you never return raw stack traces from MCP tools?",
              "back": "1. The LLM can't take corrective action based on Python tracebacks.\n2. Stack traces may leak sensitive information (file paths, database schemas, internal code).\n3. Return human-readable, actionable error messages instead."
            },
            {
              "front": "What is the Trust Boundary Pattern in MCP?",
              "back": "Separate high-risk tools (database writes, deletions) and low-risk tools (searches, reads) into different MCP servers. This allows different security policies, authentication levels, rate limits, and audit logging for each risk level."
            },
            {
              "front": "How should MCP servers handle secrets (API keys, database passwords)?",
              "back": "Never hardcode secrets. Use environment variables (os.environ['SECRET_KEY']) or cloud secret managers (AWS Secrets Manager, HashiCorp Vault). Rotate credentials regularly."
            }
          ]
