        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "MCP in Production: Patterns & Best Practices",
            "explanation": "Building an MCP server that works in development is easy. Building one that's reliable, maintainable, and scalable in production requires established patterns. These have emerged from real-world enterprise MCP deployments.\n\n**1. Server Registry Pattern:**\nMaintain a central registry of all MCP servers in your organization:\n\n```python\n# server_registry.json\n{\n  \"servers\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"GitHubServer\",\n      \"transport\": \"stdio\",\n      \"command\": \"python\",\n      \"args\": [\"github_server.py\"],\n      \"description\": \"GitHub repository operations\",\n      \"auth\": \"github_token\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"SlackServer\",\n      \"transport\": \"http\",\n      \"url\": \"https://mcp.internal.company.com/slack\",\n      \"description\": \"Slack messaging operations\",\n      \"auth\": \"oauth2\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nAI hosts query the registry to discover servers dynamically. This makes it trivial to add, update, or remove servers across the organization.\n\n**2. Tool Description Engineering:**\nThe LLM decides WHICH tool to call based on the description. This is not documentation — it's AI prompt engineering:\n\n```python\n# MEDIOCRE — The LLM might confuse this with other search tools\n@mcp.tool()\ndef search(query: str) -> str:\n    '''Search for information.'''\n\n# EXCELLENT — The LLM knows exactly when to use this\n@mcp.tool()\ndef search_company_wiki(query: str, max_results: int = 5) -> str:\n    '''Search the internal company wiki for articles matching the query.\n    Use this when the user asks about company policies, onboarding\n    procedures, HR guidelines, or internal documentation.\n    Do NOT use this for general knowledge questions.\n    Returns up to max_results articles with titles and content snippets.'''\n```\n\n**3. Structured Error Responses:**\nReturn errors that help the LLM self-correct:\n\n```python\n# BAD\nreturn 'Error: Internal Server Error'\n\n# GOOD\nreturn ('Error: User not found with email \"bob@test.com\". '\n        'Please verify the email address. '\n        'Tip: Try searching by name using the search_users tool instead.')\n```\n\n**4. Versioning:**\nUse semantic versioning. Maintain backward compatibility. When breaking changes are unavoidable, run old and new versions in parallel during migration."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Health Check Endpoint",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Add a health check tool to your MCP server. Create a tool called 'health_check' that returns a JSON string with 'status' ('healthy'), 'version' ('1.0.0'), and 'tools_available' (the number 3). This lets monitoring systems verify the server is running.",
            "starterCode": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\nimport json\n\nmcp = FastMCP('ProductionServer')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef health_check() -> str:\n    '''Check if the server is healthy and responding.\n    Returns server status, version, and number of available tools.'''\n    ___",
            "solution": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\nimport json\n\nmcp = FastMCP('ProductionServer')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef health_check() -> str:\n    '''Check if the server is healthy and responding.\n    Returns server status, version, and number of available tools.'''\n    return json.dumps({\n        'status': 'healthy',\n        'version': '1.0.0',\n        'tools_available': 3\n    })",
            "hint": "Use json.dumps() to return a dictionary with status, version, and tools_available as a JSON string.",
            "explanation": "Health check tools let monitoring systems verify MCP servers are running and responsive. In production, you'd also check database connections, external API availability, and memory usage. The structured JSON response is easy for monitoring tools to parse.",
            "concepts": [
              "Health Checks",
              "Production Monitoring",
              "JSON Responses"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "def health_check.*json\\.dumps.*status.*healthy.*version"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Scaling, Monitoring & Error Design",
            "explanation": "**Scaling MCP Servers:**\n\n• **stdio servers** — The host spawns a new process per connection. Scaling happens naturally per-user.\n• **HTTP servers** — Use standard web scaling: Kubernetes, Docker, auto-scaling groups behind a load balancer.\n\n```yaml\n# docker-compose.yml for MCP server\nservices:\n  mcp-github:\n    build: ./mcp-github-server\n    ports:\n      - '8001:8000'\n    environment:\n      - GITHUB_TOKEN=${GITHUB_TOKEN}\n    deploy:\n      replicas: 3\n```\n\n**Monitoring:**\nTrack these metrics for every MCP server:\n• **Request latency** — How long tool calls take (p50, p95, p99)\n• **Error rate** — What percentage of tool calls fail\n• **Call volume** — How many tool calls per minute\n• **Active connections** — How many clients are connected\n\n**Testing MCP Servers at Three Levels:**\n\n1. **Unit tests** — Test individual tool handler functions with pytest\n2. **Integration tests** — Use the MCP Inspector to verify protocol compliance\n3. **End-to-end tests** — Connect a real LLM client and verify tools are discovered and invoked correctly\n\n```python\n# Unit test example\nimport pytest\n\ndef test_add_tool():\n    result = add(5, 3)  # Call the raw function\n    assert result == 8\n\ndef test_add_negative():\n    result = add(-5, 3)\n    assert result == -2\n\ndef test_divide_by_zero():\n    result = divide(10, 0)\n    assert 'Error' in result  # Should return error, not crash\n```"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Structured Error Response Tool",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Create an MCP tool called 'get_user_profile' that takes a user_id (str). If the user_id is empty, return an error suggesting the LLM ask the user for their ID. If user_id is 'admin', return a mock admin profile. For any other user_id, return an error suggesting the LLM try the 'search_users' tool instead.",
            "starterCode": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\nimport json\n\nmcp = FastMCP('UserService')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef get_user_profile(user_id: str) -> str:\n    '''Get a user profile by their unique ID.\n    Returns user details or a helpful error message.'''\n    ___",
            "solution": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\nimport json\n\nmcp = FastMCP('UserService')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef get_user_profile(user_id: str) -> str:\n    '''Get a user profile by their unique ID.\n    Returns user details or a helpful error message.'''\n    if not user_id.strip():\n        return 'Error: user_id is empty. Please ask the user for their ID.'\n    if user_id == 'admin':\n        return json.dumps({'id': 'admin', 'name': 'Admin User', 'role': 'administrator'})\n    return f'Error: No user found with ID \"{user_id}\". Try using the search_users tool to find users by name instead.'",
            "hint": "Check for empty user_id first, then handle the 'admin' case with json.dumps, then return a helpful error for unknown users suggesting an alternative tool.",
            "explanation": "Good error messages tell the LLM what went wrong AND what to do next. 'Try using search_users' gives the agent a concrete next step. This self-correcting behavior makes your MCP tools much more useful in agentic workflows.",
            "concepts": [
              "Structured Errors",
              "Self-Correcting Agents",
              "Alternative Tool Suggestions"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "def get_user_profile\\(user_id:\\s*str\\).*Error.*search_users"
            }
          }
        },
