          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Debugging MCP: Using the Inspector Tool",
            "explanation": "When an MCP server fails to connect, tools don't appear, or handshakes hang — you need systematic debugging tools. The MCP ecosystem provides the **MCP Inspector**, an interactive debugging tool that simulates a client and lets you test your server step by step.\n\n[IMAGE:/images/m13_mcp_inspector.png]\n\n**The Most Common MCP Failure Modes:**\n\n1. **Server Process Fails to Start**\n   - Missing dependencies (`ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'mcp'`)\n   - Wrong Python path or command in the server configuration\n   - Syntax errors in the server script\n   - Solution: Run the server manually first: `python my_server.py`\n\n2. **Capability Negotiation Fails**\n   - Version mismatch between client and server MCP SDK\n   - Server crashes during `initialize` handler\n   - Solution: Check MCP SDK versions match, review server logs\n\n3. **Tools Don't Appear After Connection**\n   - Tools defined AFTER `mcp.run()` is called (order matters!)\n   - Missing `@mcp.tool()` decorator (function exists but isn't registered)\n   - Import errors in tool handler functions\n   - Solution: Call `tools/list` manually via the Inspector\n\n4. **Tool Calls Return Errors**\n   - Type mismatch (LLM sends string, tool expects int)\n   - Missing required parameters\n   - Unhandled exceptions in tool handler\n   - Solution: Add try/except with descriptive error messages\n\n5. **Transport Hangs**\n   - stdio: Server prints to stdout (corrupts the JSON-RPC stream!)\n   - HTTP: CORS issues, wrong port, SSL certificate errors\n   - Solution: Use `stderr` for logging, never `print()` in stdio servers"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Using the MCP Inspector & Logging",
            "explanation": "**The MCP Inspector:**\nThe Inspector is a web-based debugging tool that connects to your server and lets you interactively test tools:\n\n```bash\n# Install and run the Inspector\nnpx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector python my_server.py\n```\n\nThe Inspector opens in your browser and shows:\n• Server capabilities (tools, resources, prompts)\n• Tool schemas with input fields you can fill in\n• Raw JSON-RPC messages for debugging protocol issues\n• Response inspection for verifying tool outputs\n\n**CRITICAL Rule for stdio Servers:**\nNever use `print()` in an stdio MCP server! The client reads stdout as JSON-RPC messages. Any extra output (like print statements) corrupts the protocol stream and causes mysterious hangs.\n\n```python\n# BAD — This breaks stdio communication!\n@mcp.tool()\ndef my_tool(query: str) -> str:\n    print('Processing query...')  # ← BREAKS EVERYTHING\n    return f'Result: {query}'\n\n# GOOD — Use logging to stderr instead\nimport logging\nimport sys\n\nlogging.basicConfig(stream=sys.stderr, level=logging.INFO)\nlogger = logging.getLogger('my_server')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef my_tool(query: str) -> str:\n    logger.info(f'Processing query: {query}')  # ← Goes to stderr, safe!\n    return f'Result: {query}'\n```\n\n**Adding Logging to Your Server:**\nA well-logged MCP server makes debugging 10x easier:\n\n```python\nimport logging\nimport sys\nfrom mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\n# Log to stderr (not stdout!) for stdio transport\nlogging.basicConfig(\n    stream=sys.stderr,\n    level=logging.INFO,\n    format='%(asctime)s [%(name)s] %(levelname)s: %(message)s'\n)\nlogger = logging.getLogger('mcp_server')\n\nmcp = FastMCP('DebugDemo')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef greet(name: str) -> str:\n    '''Greet someone by name.'''\n    logger.info(f'greet() called with name={name}')\n    result = f'Hello, {name}!'\n    logger.info(f'greet() returning: {result}')\n    return result\n```"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Adding Logging to an MCP Server",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Add proper logging to this MCP server. Configure logging to write to stderr (NOT stdout — this is critical for stdio transport!). Log when each tool is called and what it returns. Use the logger name 'mcp_server'.",
            "starterCode": "import logging\nimport sys\nfrom mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\n# Configure logging to stderr\n___\n\nlogger = logging.getLogger('mcp_server')\n\nmcp = FastMCP('LoggedServer')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef multiply(a: int, b: int) -> str:\n    '''Multiply two numbers together.'''\n    ___\n    result = a * b\n    ___\n    return str(result)\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    mcp.run()",
            "solution": "import logging\nimport sys\nfrom mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nlogging.basicConfig(stream=sys.stderr, level=logging.INFO)\nlogger = logging.getLogger('mcp_server')\n\nmcp = FastMCP('LoggedServer')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef multiply(a: int, b: int) -> str:\n    '''Multiply two numbers together.'''\n    logger.info(f'multiply() called with a={a}, b={b}')\n    result = a * b\n    logger.info(f'multiply() returning: {result}')\n    return str(result)\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    mcp.run()",
            "hint": "Use logging.basicConfig(stream=sys.stderr, level=logging.INFO). Then add logger.info() calls before and after the computation.",
            "explanation": "Logging to sys.stderr is CRITICAL for stdio MCP servers. stdout is the JSON-RPC communication channel — any extra output there corrupts the protocol. stderr is safe for logging because it's a separate stream that doesn't interfere with MCP messages.",
            "concepts": [
              "Logging to stderr",
              "stdio Safety",
              "Debug Logging"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "logging\\.basicConfig\\(stream=sys\\.stderr.*logger\\.info"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Debugging a Broken Tool Handler",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "This MCP tool crashes when the user passes an invalid number. Add error handling so it returns a helpful error message instead of crashing the server. Wrap the conversion in a try/except block.",
            "starterCode": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nmcp = FastMCP('ParseServer')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef parse_number(text: str) -> str:\n    '''Convert a text string to a number and double it.\n    Example: \"42\" -> 84'''\n    # This crashes if text is not a valid number!\n    number = int(text)\n    result = number * 2\n    return f'{text} doubled is {result}'",
            "solution": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nmcp = FastMCP('ParseServer')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef parse_number(text: str) -> str:\n    '''Convert a text string to a number and double it.\n    Example: \"42\" -> 84'''\n    try:\n        number = int(text)\n    except ValueError:\n        return f'Error: \"{text}\" is not a valid integer. Please provide a number like \"42\" or \"100\".'\n    result = number * 2\n    return f'{text} doubled is {result}'",
            "hint": "Wrap int(text) in a try/except ValueError block. Return an actionable error message in the except block.",
            "explanation": "Unhandled exceptions in MCP tools can crash the server or return opaque error responses. Always catch expected errors (ValueError, KeyError, ConnectionError) and return clear messages. The LLM can then ask the user to provide valid input.",
            "concepts": [
              "Error Handling",
              "try/except",
              "Graceful Degradation"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "try:.*int\\(text\\).*except ValueError.*Error"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "content": {
            "title": "Debugging: Tools Don't Appear",
            "description": "You built an MCP server with 3 tools, but when your AI client connects, it reports 0 tools available. The server process starts successfully, and the initialize handshake completes. What's the most likely cause?",
            "choices": [
              {
                "text": "The tools are defined AFTER mcp.run() is called. Move all @mcp.tool() definitions before mcp.run().",
                "score": 100,
                "outcome": "Correct! mcp.run() starts the event loop and begins listening. Any tool definitions after it are never registered. Always define tools before calling mcp.run()."
              },
              {
                "text": "The MCP protocol doesn't support tool discovery.",
                "score": 0,
                "outcome": "Incorrect. Tool discovery via tools/list is a core MCP feature. The issue is with tool registration order, not the protocol."
              },
              {
                "text": "The client needs to restart to see new tools.",
                "score": 20,
                "outcome": "While reconnecting can help, the real issue is that tools defined after mcp.run() are never registered with the server. Fix the code order first."
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
