          "type": "flashcard",
          "content": {
            "title": "Backend Basics — Quick Review",
            "cards": [
              {
                "front": "What is a Server?",
                "back": "A computer that's always on, connected to the internet, listening for requests and sending back responses."
              },
              {
                "front": "What is an API?",
                "back": "Application Programming Interface — a clean interface that defines what you can ask for, how to ask, and what you get back."
              },
              {
                "front": "What does GET do?",
                "back": "Retrieves data without changing anything on the server. Data goes in the URL."
              },
              {
                "front": "What does POST do?",
                "back": "Sends data TO the server in the request body. Used for creating resources, sending AI prompts, uploading files."
              },
              {
                "front": "What is JSON?",
                "back": "JavaScript Object Notation — the universal data format for APIs. Looks like Python dictionaries with double-quoted strings."
              },
              {
                "front": "What is REST?",
                "back": "Representational State Transfer — an API style using URLs for resources and HTTP methods for actions."
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is JSON? (How Computers Talk to Each Other)",
            "explanation": "Here's a question nobody thinks about: when your frontend sends data to your backend, what FORMAT is that data in? It's not a Word document. It's not a spreadsheet. It's not an email.\n\nIt's **JSON** — and it's the universal language of the internet.\n\n**🧒 The Universal Translator Analogy:**\n\nImagine the United Nations. Diplomats from 190 countries need to communicate, but they all speak different languages. So they use translators and a common format for documents.\n\nJSON is the internet's universal translator. Your React frontend (JavaScript), your FastAPI backend (Python), your PostgreSQL database (SQL) — they all speak different languages. But they ALL understand JSON.\n\n<img src=\"/images/module 14/m14_json_format.png\" alt=\"JSON Format Analogy\" />\n\n**What JSON Looks Like:**\n```json\n{\n    \"name\": \"Alice\",\n    \"age\": 25,\n    \"is_student\": true,\n    \"skills\": [\"Python\", \"LangChain\", \"FastAPI\"],\n    \"address\": {\n        \"city\": \"San Francisco\",\n        \"country\": \"USA\"\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**The Rules Are Dead Simple:**\n1. **Strings** use double quotes: `\"hello\"` (single quotes are INVALID in JSON!)\n2. **Numbers** are just numbers: `42`, `3.14`\n3. **Booleans** are lowercase: `true`, `false` (not `True`/`False` like Python!)\n4. **Arrays** use square brackets: `[\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]`\n5. **Objects** use curly braces: `{\"key\": \"value\"}`\n6. **Null** is `null` (not `None` like Python!)\n\n**Why This Matters for AI Applications:**\nEvery single AI API response comes back as JSON. When you call OpenAI:\n```json\n{\n    \"choices\": [\n        {\n            \"message\": {\n                \"role\": \"assistant\",\n                \"content\": \"Hello! How can I help you?\"\n            },\n            \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"\n        }\n    ],\n    \"usage\": {\n        \"prompt_tokens\": 10,\n        \"completion_tokens\": 8,\n        \"total_tokens\": 18\n    }\n}\n```\n\nUnderstanding JSON isn't optional — it's the foundation of everything you'll build. Every request your frontend sends, every response your backend returns, every configuration file you write — it's all JSON.\n\n**🔑 JSON vs Python Dictionaries:**\nThey look almost identical, but watch out for these differences:\n| Python Dict | JSON |\n|---|---|\n| `True` / `False` | `true` / `false` |\n| `None` | `null` |\n| Single or double quotes | Double quotes ONLY |\n| Trailing commas OK | Trailing commas INVALID |"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "speed_quiz",
