        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Custom Error Handling in FastAPI",
            "explanation": "Right now, when something goes wrong in your API, FastAPI just throws a generic error. But imagine you're a doctor, and every time a patient comes in, your response is just \"something is wrong.\" That would be terrifying, right? You'd want the doctor to say EXACTLY what's wrong, HOW bad it is, and WHAT to do about it.\n\n**🧒 The Hospital Triage Analogy:**\n\nThink of error handling like a hospital emergency room. When a patient arrives:\n- A **400 error** is like a patient who filled out the wrong form — \"Hey, you wrote your phone number in the date field. Please fix this.\" Easy fix.\n- A **404 error** is like a patient asking for Dr. Smith when no Dr. Smith works here — \"Sorry, that doctor doesn't exist at this hospital.\"\n- A **422 error** is like a patient bringing an X-ray when the doctor asked for a blood test — \"The data you sent isn't what we expected.\"\n- A **500 error** is like the hospital's power going out — \"Something went seriously wrong on our end. We're working on it.\"\n\n<img src=\"/images/module 14/m14_error_handling.png\" alt=\"FastAPI Error Handling Analogy\" />\n\nGood error responses are CRITICAL for AI applications. When your frontend sends a malformed prompt or the OpenAI API is down, your users need to know exactly what happened — not just see a mysterious \"Internal Server Error.\"\n\n**Custom Exception Handlers:**\n```python\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, Request\nfrom fastapi.responses import JSONResponse\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\nclass AIServiceError(Exception):\n    def __init__(self, detail: str, provider: str):\n        self.detail = detail\n        self.provider = provider\n\n@app.exception_handler(AIServiceError)\nasync def ai_service_error_handler(request: Request, exc: AIServiceError):\n    return JSONResponse(\n        status_code=503,\n        content={\n            'error': 'ai_service_unavailable',\n            'detail': exc.detail,\n            'provider': exc.provider,\n            'suggestion': 'Please retry in 30 seconds'\n        }\n    )\n\n@app.post('/chat')\nasync def chat(message: str):\n    try:\n        response = await call_openai(message)\n        return {'response': response}\n    except Exception as e:\n        raise AIServiceError(\n            detail=str(e),\n            provider='openai'\n        )\n```\n\n**🔍 The Golden Rules of Error Handling:**\n1. **Never expose internal details** — Don't send stack traces to users. Log them internally, send a clean message externally.\n2. **Always include an error code** — `'error': 'rate_limit_exceeded'` is much more useful than `'error': 'something went wrong'`\n3. **Suggest a fix** — Tell the user WHAT to do next: \"Retry in 30 seconds\" or \"Check your API key\"\n4. **Log everything server-side** — Even if you send a clean error to the user, log the full traceback on your server for debugging.\n\n**HTTP Error Response Pattern:**\n```python\n# Always structure your errors consistently\n{\n    \"error\": \"validation_error\",      # Machine-readable code\n    \"detail\": \"Temperature must be between 0 and 2\",  # Human-readable message\n    \"field\": \"temperature\",           # Which field caused the issue\n    \"suggestion\": \"Use a value like 0.7 for balanced responses\"  # How to fix it\n}\n```\n\nThis pattern is used by every major API — Stripe, OpenAI, GitHub. Your API should follow the same standard."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Status Codes — The Server's Mood",
            "explanation": "Every time your server responds to a request, it doesn't just send back data — it sends back a **mood indicator**. That's the HTTP status code. It's a three-digit number that tells the client exactly what happened.\n\n**🧒 The Mood Ring Analogy:**\n\nRemember mood rings? They change color based on how you're feeling. HTTP status codes work the same way — they tell the client the server's \"mood\" after processing a request.\n\n<img src=\"/images/module 14/m14_status_codes.png\" alt=\"HTTP Status Codes\" />\n\n**The Five Families of Status Codes:**\n\n**2xx — 😊 Success (Green):**\n- `200 OK` — Everything worked perfectly. Here's your data.\n- `201 Created` — I made the new thing you asked for. Here it is.\n- `204 No Content` — Done! But there's nothing to send back. (Used after DELETE)\n\n**4xx — 😤 Client Error (Orange) — YOU messed up:**\n- `400 Bad Request` — I can't understand what you're asking for. Check your syntax.\n- `401 Unauthorized` — Who are you? Show me your credentials first.\n- `403 Forbidden` — I know who you are, but you're not allowed to do that.\n- `404 Not Found` — That thing you're looking for? It doesn't exist.\n- `422 Unprocessable Entity` — I understand your request, but the data is invalid. (Pydantic validation errors)\n- `429 Too Many Requests` — Slow down! You're hitting me too fast. (Rate limiting)\n\n**5xx — 😱 Server Error (Red) — WE messed up:**\n- `500 Internal Server Error` — Something broke on our end. We don't know what.\n- `502 Bad Gateway` — We tried to talk to another server (like OpenAI) and it didn't respond.\n- `503 Service Unavailable` — Server is overloaded or down for maintenance.\n\n**Using Status Codes in FastAPI:**\n```python\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException\nfrom fastapi.responses import JSONResponse\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\n@app.post('/chat', status_code=200)\ndef chat(message: str):\n    if not message.strip():\n        raise HTTPException(\n            status_code=400,\n            detail='Message cannot be empty'\n        )\n    return {'response': 'Hello!'}\n\n@app.post('/users', status_code=201)  # 201 = Created\ndef create_user(email: str):\n    return {'id': 1, 'email': email}\n\n@app.delete('/users/{id}', status_code=204)  # 204 = No Content\ndef delete_user(id: int):\n    return None  # Nothing to send back\n```\n\n**💡 The Golden Rule:**\nAlways use the most SPECIFIC status code possible. Don't just return 200 for everything and put the error in the response body. Status codes are the first thing monitoring tools check — if your server returns 500s, alarms go off. If it returns 200 with an error message hidden inside, nobody notices until users start complaining."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "content": {
            "title": "Debug the API Error",
            "description": "You're building an AI chatbot API. A user reports that they keep getting an error. You check the logs and see the server returned a `429 Too Many Requests` status code. What is the most likely cause and how would you fix it?",
            "choices": [
