          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "ACID: Why You Can Trust Your Database With Money",
            "explanation": "Here's a scary scenario that will make you appreciate databases forever.\n\nA user is upgrading their subscription plan. Your code needs to do two things:\n1. Charge their credit card $20\n2. Update their `plan` column from 'free' to 'pro'\n\nBut what if the server crashes BETWEEN those two operations? The user got charged $20 but their plan never upgraded. They're furious. You have a lawsuit on your hands.\n\n**ACID properties guarantee this never happens.** Let me break them down:\n\n---\n\n**A — Atomicity (All or Nothing)**\n\nA transaction is like a deal: either EVERYTHING happens, or NOTHING happens. If you charge the card and THEN the server crashes, the database rolls back the charge too. Half-completed operations are impossible.\n\n```sql\nBEGIN TRANSACTION;\n  UPDATE billing SET charged = true WHERE user_id = 1;\n  UPDATE users SET plan = 'pro' WHERE id = 1;\nCOMMIT;  -- Both happen, or neither does\n```\n\n**C — Consistency**\nThe database never allows data that breaks your rules. For example, you can set a rule that `user_id` in `messages` MUST exist in the `users` table. Orphaned messages become impossible.\n\n**I — Isolation**\nTwo transactions happening at the same time don't interfere with each other. If Alice and Bob both try to book the last seat on a flight simultaneously, isolation ensures only ONE succeeds.\n\n**D — Durability**\nOnce a transaction COMMITS, it's permanent. Even if the server loses power 1 millisecond after saving, the data survives.\n\n---\n\n**🔑 The AI Engineer's Takeaway:**\nUse SQL databases for anything where correctness is critical: user accounts, payments, API keys, and subscription plans. The ACID guarantee is worth every bit of structure it requires.\n\nACID keeps your data safe. But what about SPEED? What if your database has 100 million rows and queries take 30 seconds?",
            "image": "/images/module_15_databases/m15_acid_properties.png"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Database Indexes: From 30 Seconds to 5 Milliseconds",
            "explanation": "Imagine you have a `messages` table with 100 million rows. A user loads your chat app and you run:\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM messages WHERE user_id = 12345;\n```\n\n**Without an index:** The database reads ALL 100 million rows one by one, checking if `user_id = 12345`. This takes **30 seconds**. Your user has already rage-quit your app.\n\n**With an index on `user_id`:** The database has a pre-sorted lookup structure. It jumps DIRECTLY to user 12345's messages in **5 milliseconds**.\n\n**🧒 The Phone Book Analogy:**\nFinding \"Smith\" in a phone book is instant because it's sorted alphabetically. But finding everyone whose phone number starts with \"555\"? You'd have to read every single entry. An index on `user_id` is like sorting the phone book by user instead of alphabetically.\n\n**Creating an Index:**\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX idx_messages_user_id ON messages(user_id);\n```\n\nThat's it. One line of SQL and your query goes from 30 seconds to 5 milliseconds.\n\n---\n\n**Common Indexes Every AI App Needs:**\n```sql\n-- Speed up user lookups by email (e.g., login)\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_users_email ON users(email);\n\n-- Speed up loading a user's chat sessions\nCREATE INDEX idx_sessions_user_id ON sessions(user_id);\n\n-- Speed up finding recent messages\nCREATE INDEX idx_messages_timestamp ON messages(timestamp DESC);\n```\n\n**The Trade-off:**\nIndexes make reads faster but writes slightly slower (because every INSERT must also update the index). The general rule: **index every column you frequently filter on**, especially foreign keys.\n\nNow, there's a critical performance issue that trips up most AI engineers. When your server is handling streaming LLM responses that take 3-5 seconds each, your database connections need special treatment. Let me explain.",
            "image": "/images/module_15_databases/m15_database_index_btree.png"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "ACID & Indexes Quiz",
            "text": "ACID stands for {{Atomicity}}, Consistency, {{Isolation}}, and {{Durability}}. Database {{indexes}} make read queries faster by creating a pre-sorted lookup structure, similar to a table of contents in a book.",
            "blanks": [
              "Atomicity",
              "Isolation",
              "Durability",
              "indexes"
            ],
            "question": "Write the code here:",
            "solution": "# Solution",
            "starterCode": "# Write code here"
          }
