        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Async Database Operations — Don't Block Your AI Server",
            "explanation": "Alright, here's a problem that doesn't get talked about enough in tutorials but will absolutely DESTROY your production server if you ignore it.\n\n**The Problem:**\n\nYour AI server calls OpenAI's API. That call takes 3-5 seconds. While your server is waiting for OpenAI to respond, what happens to the database connection?\n\nWith **synchronous** (normal) database code, the connection just... sits there. Blocked. Doing nothing. Wasting resources.\n\nNow multiply that by 500 users doing this simultaneously. That's 500 frozen database connections. Most database servers max out at 100 connections by default. Your server crashes.\n\n**🧒 The Restaurant Analogy:**\nImagine a restaurant where every waiter can only serve ONE table at a time — and they have to stand next to the table while the kitchen prepares the food. With 50 tables, you'd need 50 waiters just standing around doing nothing. Madness!\n\nA smart restaurant has waiters who take the order, give it to the kitchen, and immediately go serve another table. When the food is ready, they bring it out. That's **async** — one waiter handles many tables.\n\n**The Fix — asyncpg:**\n\n```python\nimport asyncpg\n\n# Async — the waiter serves other tables while waiting\nasync def get_user_history(user_id: int) -> list:\n    conn = await asyncpg.connect('postgresql://localhost/mydb')\n    rows = await conn.fetch(\n        'SELECT * FROM messages WHERE user_id = $1 ORDER BY timestamp',\n        user_id\n    )\n    await conn.close()\n    return rows\n```\n\nWith async, your server handles 500 simultaneous users with just 10-20 database connections instead of 500. It's not just an optimization — it's a **requirement** for any AI application that calls external APIs.\n\nBut even with async, you still need to manage those connections efficiently. Let me show you how.",
            "image": "/images/module_15_databases/m15_async_vs_sync_db.png"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Connection Pooling — Don't Open 10,000 Connections",
            "explanation": "Even with async, there's another trap waiting for you. Every time your code opens a new database connection, there's overhead — authentication, SSL handshake, memory allocation. This takes about 50-100 milliseconds.\n\nIf you open a new connection for every request and you're handling 1,000 requests per second, that's 1,000 new connections per second. Your database server will collapse.\n\n**🧒 The Highway Toll Booth Analogy:**\nOpening a new connection for every request is like building a brand-new toll booth for every car that passes. Insane! Connection pooling keeps 10-20 toll booths open permanently and routes all cars through them.\n\n**SQLAlchemy Connection Pool:**\n```python\nfrom sqlalchemy import create_engine\n\n# Good: Reuses connections from a pool\nengine = create_engine(\n    'postgresql://localhost/mydb',\n    pool_size=10,        # Keep 10 connections open permanently\n    max_overflow=20,     # Allow up to 20 extra when traffic spikes\n    pool_pre_ping=True   # Check connection is alive before reusing\n)\n```\n\n**For high-traffic deployments — PgBouncer:**\n\nPgBouncer is an external connection pooler that sits between your app and PostgreSQL. It can handle 10,000 incoming connections and funnel them through just 20 actual database connections.\n\n```\nYour App (10,000 connections) → PgBouncer (pool of 20) → PostgreSQL\n```\n\n---\n\n**🔑 The Rule of Thumb:**\n- Development: SQLAlchemy's built-in pool is enough\n- Production (< 1,000 users): SQLAlchemy pool with `pool_size=20`\n- Production (> 1,000 users): Add PgBouncer in front of PostgreSQL\n\nNow you know how to store structured data efficiently, handle async operations, and manage connections. But AI applications also deal with MESSY, unstructured data that doesn't fit neatly into SQL tables. Let's talk about that."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "questions": [
