              "question": "What is the primary purpose of a connection pool?",
              "options": [
                "To encrypt database connections",
                "To reuse a fixed set of open connections instead of creating new ones for every request",
                "To backup the database automatically",
                "To compress query results"
              ],
              "correct": 1,
              "explanation": "Connection pools keep a set of database connections open and reuse them across requests. This avoids the overhead of creating new connections (50-100ms each) and prevents the database from being overwhelmed by too many simultaneous connections.",
              "answer": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "NoSQL Databases — When Structure Gets in the Way",
            "explanation": "SQL databases are incredible, but they have one fundamental limitation: **you must define your structure before you store anything**.\n\nBefore you can store a single row, you declare every column:\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE messages (\n  id        INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n  user_id   INTEGER,\n  role      TEXT,\n  content   TEXT,\n  timestamp DATETIME\n);\n```\n\n**The Problem with AI Workloads:**\n\nLLMs return wildly different shaped outputs. An AI agent might call a weather tool (returning `{ temperature, humidity, wind_speed }`) or a stock tool (returning `{ price, volume, pe_ratio }`) or a web search (returning `{ url, title, snippet }`). These shapes are completely different.\n\nCreating a SQL table that accommodates ALL possible AI tool outputs would require dozens of nullable columns — a mess.\n\n**NoSQL databases solve this with a simple idea: just store the JSON.**\n\n```json\n// Document 1: weather tool result\n{\n  \"session_id\": \"abc123\",\n  \"tool\": \"weather\",\n  \"result\": { \"temperature\": 72, \"humidity\": 65, \"city\": \"NYC\" }\n}\n\n// Document 2: stock tool result (completely different shape!)\n{\n  \"session_id\": \"abc124\",\n  \"tool\": \"stock_lookup\",\n  \"result\": { \"ticker\": \"AAPL\", \"price\": 185.50, \"volume\": 62000000 }\n}\n```\n\nBoth documents live happily in the same collection with zero schema conflicts.\n\n**Popular NoSQL Databases:**\n- **MongoDB** — The most popular document database. JSON-native.\n- **Firestore** (Google Firebase) — Real-time sync, great for mobile AI apps\n- **DynamoDB** (AWS) — Serverless, scales automatically\n\nNoSQL handles unstructured data. But there's one database that's even more special — it stores everything in RAM and is 100,000 times faster than anything we've discussed so far."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Redis: The Ultra-Fast In-Memory Database",
            "explanation": "Most databases store data on disk (hard drive). This is durable but relatively slow. **Redis stores everything in RAM** — the same memory your CPU uses to run programs. RAM is 100,000x faster to access than a disk.\n\nThis makes Redis insanely fast:\n- Read: **under 0.1 milliseconds** (vs 5-10ms for a disk database)\n- Writes: **1 million operations per second**\n\n**How AI Engineers Use Redis:**\n\n---\n\n**1. Response Caching — Stop Paying for Duplicate LLM Calls:**\n\n```python\nimport redis, hashlib, json\n\nr = redis.Redis(host='localhost', port=6379)\n\ndef get_ai_response(prompt: str) -> str:\n    # Create a cache key from the prompt\n    cache_key = hashlib.md5(prompt.encode()).hexdigest()\n\n    # Check if we already have this answer\n    cached = r.get(cache_key)\n    if cached:\n        return json.loads(cached)  # Return instantly — $0 API cost!\n\n    # Only call the expensive LLM API if not cached\n    response = call_openai(prompt)\n\n    # Cache for 1 hour (3600 seconds)\n    r.setex(cache_key, 3600, json.dumps(response))\n    return response\n```\n\nIf 1,000 users ask \"What is RAG?\" on the same day, you only call OpenAI ONCE. The other 999 users get the cached response instantly at zero cost.\n\n---\n\n**2. Session State — Fast Chat History:**\n```python\n# Store user's chat history in Redis (auto-expires in 24 hours)\nsession_key = f'session:{user_id}:{session_id}'\nr.setex(session_key, 86400, json.dumps(messages))\n```\n\n---\n\n**3. Rate Limiting — Prevent API Abuse:**\n```python\n# Allow max 10 requests per minute per user\nrate_key = f'rate:{user_id}'\ncurrent = r.incr(rate_key)       # Increment counter atomically\nif current == 1:\n    r.expire(rate_key, 60)       # First request — set 60-second expiry\nif current > 10:\n    raise Exception('Rate limit exceeded!')\n```\n\nBasic hash caching is great, but it has a fatal flaw. Let me show you what it misses.",
            "image": "/images/module_15_databases/m15_redis_caching.png"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Semantic Caching — Stop Paying for 'Almost Identical' Questions",
            "explanation": "Alright, here's the problem with the hash-based caching we just built.\n\nHash caching only works for **EXACT matches**. The hash of \"What is RAG?\" and the hash of \"Explain RAG to me\" are completely different strings. With hash caching, you'd call OpenAI TWICE and pay TWICE — even though it's the same question!\n\nIn production, users almost NEVER ask the exact same question twice. They use different words, different phrasing, different punctuation. Hash caching misses all of these.\n\n**The Solution: Semantic Caching**\n\nInstead of hashing the text, we convert the question into an **embedding** (a vector of numbers that represents its MEANING). Then we search for similar embeddings in the cache. If we find one with 95%+ similarity, we return the cached response.\n\n**🧒 The Filing Cabinet Analogy:**\nHash caching is like a filing cabinet where every file has an EXACT label. If someone asks for \"Dog Training Guide\" but the file is labeled \"How to Train Your Dog,\" they won't find it. Semantic caching is like a smart librarian who says \"Oh, you mean THIS one\" — because they understand the MEANING, not just the label.\n\n**How It Works:**\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef semantic_cache_check(query: str, threshold: float = 0.95):\n    # Step 1: Convert query to embedding\n    query_embedding = get_embedding(query)\n\n    # Step 2: Search cache for similar embeddings\n    cached = redis_vector_search(query_embedding, top_k=1)\n\n    # Step 3: If similarity > 95%, return cached response\n    if cached and cached.score > threshold:\n        return cached.response    # FREE! No LLM call!\n\n    # Step 4: Not cached — call the LLM\n    response = call_openai(query)\n\n    # Step 5: Store embedding + response for future hits\n    cache_store(query_embedding, response)\n    return response\n```\n\n**The Cost Impact:**\n\n| Without Caching | Hash Cache Only | Semantic Cache |\n|---|---|---|\n| 50,000 LLM calls/day | 35,000 calls/day (30% hit) | 15,000 calls/day (70% hit) |\n| $1,500/day | $1,050/day | **$450/day** |\n\nCompanies using semantic caching report **40-80% cost reduction** on LLM API bills. That's thousands of dollars saved per month.\n\nLet's build one.",
            "image": "/images/module_15_databases/m15_redis_caching.png"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Build a Semantic Cache Check",
            "description": "Write a function called 'check_semantic_cache' that takes a query string and a similarity threshold (default 0.95). It should: 1) Get the embedding for the query using get_embedding(), 2) Search the cache using search_cache(embedding) which returns an object with .score and .response, 3) Return the cached response if score > threshold, otherwise return None.",
            "language": "python",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "startingCode": "def check_semantic_cache(query: str, threshold: float = 0.95):\n    '''Check if a semantically similar query was already answered.'''\n    # Step 1: Get the embedding\n    embedding = ___\n    \n    # Step 2: Search cache\n    result = ___\n    \n    # Step 3: Return cached response if similar enough\n    ___",
            "solution": "def check_semantic_cache(query: str, threshold: float = 0.95):\n    '''Check if a semantically similar query was already answered.'''\n    embedding = get_embedding(query)\n    result = search_cache(embedding)\n    if result and result.score > threshold:\n        return result.response\n    return None",
            "validation": true,
            "question": "Write the code here:",
            "starterCode": "# Write code here"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Redis as Your AI Agent's Short-Term Memory",
            "explanation": "Caching saves money. But Redis also solves another critical problem — giving your AI agent a **working memory** for the current conversation.\n\n**The Problem:**\nLLMs are stateless. Every API call is independent — the model doesn't remember what you said 5 minutes ago unless you explicitly send the entire conversation history. But conversation histories grow fast. After 20 messages, you might have 5,000 tokens of context.\n\n**The Solution: Redis Sliding Window Memory**\n\nStore the conversation in Redis with a TTL (time-to-live). When the conversation gets too long, automatically summarize the oldest messages to keep the context window manageable.\n\n```python\nimport json, redis\n\nr = redis.Redis()\n\ndef manage_memory(user_id, session_id, new_message):\n    key = f'memory:{user_id}:{session_id}'\n\n    # Load existing conversation\n    messages = json.loads(r.get(key) or '[]')\n\n    # Add new message\n    messages.append(new_message)\n\n    # If too many tokens, summarize the oldest half\n    if count_tokens(messages) > 3000:\n        old = messages[:len(messages)//2]\n        summary = summarize_with_llm(old)\n        messages = [\n            {'role': 'system', 'content': f'Context: {summary}'}\n        ] + messages[len(messages)//2:]\n\n    # Save back with 24-hour expiry\n    r.setex(key, 86400, json.dumps(messages))\n    return messages\n```\n\n**Why Redis for short-term memory?**\n- ⚡ Sub-millisecond reads — no delay loading conversation history\n- ⏰ TTL auto-expiry — inactive sessions clean themselves up\n- 🔄 Atomic operations — safe with concurrent requests\n\nShort-term memory handles the current conversation. But what about remembering things across sessions — weeks or months later? For that, we need a completely different kind of database. And that's where vector databases come in.",
            "image": "/images/module_15_databases/m15_redis_caching.png"
          }
        },
        {
