        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "pgvector — Vector Search Inside PostgreSQL",
            "explanation": "Running a separate vector database alongside your SQL database means managing two systems, two connection pools, two backups, and two bills. For many AI applications, there's a much simpler solution:\n\n**pgvector: the PostgreSQL extension that adds vector search to your existing database.**\n\nWith pgvector, you add an `embedding` column to any table and query for semantic similarity using standard SQL:\n\n**Setup:**\n```sql\n-- Enable pgvector (one time)\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS vector;\n\n-- Create a table that stores both text AND its embedding\nCREATE TABLE documents (\n  id        SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n  content   TEXT NOT NULL,\n  source    TEXT,\n  embedding vector(1536)  -- 1536-dimension OpenAI embedding\n);\n\n-- Create an HNSW index for fast search\nCREATE INDEX ON documents\nUSING hnsw (embedding vector_cosine_ops);\n```\n\n**Querying for Similar Documents:**\n```sql\n-- Find the 5 most similar documents to a query vector\nSELECT content, source,\n       1 - (embedding <=> $1::vector) AS similarity\nFROM documents\nORDER BY embedding <=> $1::vector\nLIMIT 5;\n```\n\nThe `<=>` operator is pgvector's **cosine distance** operator.\n\n**The Killer Feature — Hybrid Filtering:**\nYou can combine vector search with regular SQL filters in ONE query:\n```sql\n-- Find similar documents BUT only from the HR department\nSELECT content FROM documents\nWHERE source = 'HR'\nORDER BY embedding <=> $1::vector\nLIMIT 5;\n```\n\nThis is semantic search + metadata filtering in a single query — much harder to achieve with a pure vector database.\n\npgvector is perfect for moderate scale (up to a few million vectors). But what about 100 million+ vectors with advanced features like hybrid sparse-dense search? Let's look at the dedicated vector database engines."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "pgvector: Semantic Search Query",
            "description": "Write a SQL query using pgvector that finds the 5 most similar documents to a query vector parameter $1. Return the content, source, and a similarity score calculated as 1 - (embedding <=> $1::vector). Order by most similar first.",
            "language": "sql",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "startingCode": "SELECT \n  content,\n  source,\n  -- Calculate similarity\nFROM documents\nORDER BY \nLIMIT 5;",
            "solution": "SELECT\n  content,\n  source,\n  1 - (embedding <=> $1::vector) AS similarity\nFROM documents\nORDER BY embedding <=> $1::vector\nLIMIT 5;",
            "validation": true,
            "question": "Write the code here:",
            "starterCode": "# Write code here"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Production Vector Databases: Pinecone & Qdrant",
            "explanation": "pgvector is fantastic when you're already using PostgreSQL and have moderate scale. But dedicated vector databases offer features that pgvector can't match — serverless scaling, built-in hybrid search, advanced payload filtering, and billion-vector capacity.\n\n**🔵 Pinecone — Serverless Vector Search (The Easy Choice)**\n\nPinecone is fully managed — no servers, no infrastructure, no ops. You just send vectors and search.\n\n```python\nfrom pinecone import Pinecone\n\npc = Pinecone(api_key='your-key')\nindex = pc.Index('my-index')\n\n# Upsert vectors with metadata\nindex.upsert(vectors=[{\n    'id': 'doc_1',\n    'values': embedding,   # Your 1536-dim vector\n    'metadata': {'source': 'HR', 'dept': 'engineering'}\n}])\n\n# Search with metadata filtering\nresults = index.query(\n    vector=query_embedding,\n    top_k=5,\n    filter={'dept': {'$eq': 'engineering'}}\n)\n```\n\n✅ Zero infrastructure management — Pinecone handles everything\n✅ Automatic scaling — handles 1K to 1B vectors\n❌ Cloud-only — no local development option\n❌ Can get expensive at scale\n\n---\n\n**🟢 Qdrant — High Performance & Hybrid Search (The Power Choice)**\n\nQdrant is Rust-based (blazing fast) and supports both local AND cloud deployment. Its killer feature: built-in sparse-dense hybrid search.\n\n```python\nfrom qdrant_client import QdrantClient\n\nclient = QdrantClient(url='http://localhost:6333')\n\n# Search with payload filtering\nresults = client.search(\n    collection_name='documents',\n    query_vector=query_embedding,\n    query_filter=models.Filter(\n        must=[models.FieldCondition(\n            key='source', match=models.MatchValue(value='HR')\n        )]\n    ),\n    limit=5\n)\n```\n\n✅ Run locally during development, deploy to cloud for production\n✅ Built-in hybrid search (sparse + dense vectors)\n✅ Payload schema validation — catch data errors early\n❌ Requires infrastructure management (unless using Qdrant Cloud)\n\n---\n\n**When to use which:**\n\n| Scale | Recommendation |\n|---|---|\n| Prototyping | **ChromaDB** (embedded, zero config) |\n| < 5M vectors, already using PostgreSQL | **pgvector** (no extra DB to manage) |\n| 5M-100M vectors, want simplicity | **Pinecone** (serverless, managed) |\n| 5M-100M vectors, want control | **Qdrant** (self-hosted or cloud) |\n| 1B+ vectors, enterprise | **Milvus / Zilliz** (distributed) |\n\nNow, there's a critical limitation of pure vector search that catches people off guard in production. Let me show you.",
            "image": "/images/module_15_databases/m15_vector_search.png"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Hybrid Search — When Vectors Alone Aren't Enough",
            "explanation": "Here's a scenario that breaks pure vector search:\n\nA user searches for part number **\"A-94812\"**. Vector search returns documents about \"parts\", \"inventory management\", and \"product catalogs\" — semantically related, but NOT the specific part. The exact part number is buried in result #47.\n\n**Why does this happen?**\n\nEmbedding models are trained to capture MEANING, not exact strings. \"A-94812\" has no semantic meaning — it's just a code. The embedding model treats it like random characters.\n\n**The Solution: Hybrid Search**\n\nCombine TWO types of search and merge the results:\n1. **BM25 keyword search** — finds exact string matches like part numbers, error codes, names\n2. **Dense vector search** — finds semantically similar content\n\nThen use **Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF)** to merge both result lists:\n\n```python\ndef hybrid_search(query, k=60):\n    \"\"\"Combine keyword and vector search results.\"\"\"\n\n    # Get results from both search methods\n    keyword_results = bm25_search(query, top_k=k)\n    vector_results = vector_search(query, top_k=k)\n\n    # Reciprocal Rank Fusion\n    rrf_scores = {}\n    for rank, doc in enumerate(keyword_results):\n        rrf_scores[doc.id] = rrf_scores.get(doc.id, 0) + 1/(60 + rank)\n    for rank, doc in enumerate(vector_results):\n        rrf_scores[doc.id] = rrf_scores.get(doc.id, 0) + 1/(60 + rank)\n\n    # Sort by combined score\n    return sorted(rrf_scores.items(), key=lambda x: x[1], reverse=True)[:10]\n```\n\n**Why RRF Works:**\n- A document that ranks #1 in keyword search AND #1 in vector search gets the highest combined score\n- A document that only ranks high in one method still appears, but lower\n- Documents that are irrelevant to both methods drop off entirely\n\n**Real-World Impact:**\n| Search Type | \"Part A-94812\" | \"troubleshooting network issues\" |\n|---|---|---|\n| Keyword Only | ✅ Finds exact part | ❌ Misses related docs |\n| Vector Only | ❌ Misses exact part | ✅ Finds related docs |\n| **Hybrid (RRF)** | ✅ Finds exact part | ✅ Finds related docs |\n\nHybrid search is the industry standard for production RAG systems. Now let's talk about a critical security concern for enterprise deployments.",
            "image": "/images/module_15_databases/m15_hybrid_search_rrf.png"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Implementing RRF Hybrid Search",
            "description": "Write a function called 'reciprocal_rank_fusion' that takes two lists of document IDs (keyword_results and vector_results) and a constant k=60. For each list, calculate RRF scores as 1/(k + rank) where rank starts at 0. Sum scores for documents appearing in both lists. Return the top 10 document IDs sorted by combined score (highest first).",
            "language": "python",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "startingCode": "def reciprocal_rank_fusion(keyword_results: list, vector_results: list, k: int = 60) -> list:\n    '''Merge keyword and vector search results using RRF.'''\n    scores = {}\n    \n    # Score keyword results\n    for rank, doc_id in enumerate(keyword_results):\n        ___\n    \n    # Score vector results\n    for rank, doc_id in enumerate(vector_results):\n        ___\n    \n    # Sort and return top 10\n    ___",
            "solution": "def reciprocal_rank_fusion(keyword_results: list, vector_results: list, k: int = 60) -> list:\n    '''Merge keyword and vector search results using RRF.'''\n    scores = {}\n    \n    for rank, doc_id in enumerate(keyword_results):\n        scores[doc_id] = scores.get(doc_id, 0) + 1 / (k + rank)\n    \n    for rank, doc_id in enumerate(vector_results):\n        scores[doc_id] = scores.get(doc_id, 0) + 1 / (k + rank)\n    \n    sorted_docs = sorted(scores.items(), key=lambda x: x[1], reverse=True)\n    return [doc_id for doc_id, score in sorted_docs[:10]]",
            "validation": true,
            "question": "Write the code here:",
            "starterCode": "# Write code here"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "content": {
            "title": "Choosing Your Vector Database",
            "description": "You're building a RAG system in three phases. Phase 1: A proof-of-concept on your laptop with 500 documents. Phase 2: Production deployment with 500,000 company documents, and you're already using PostgreSQL for user accounts. Phase 3: Enterprise scale-up to 50 million documents across 200 client tenants. Which vector database strategy should you use for each phase?",
