        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Graph Databases — When Relationships ARE the Data",
            "explanation": "Vector search is brilliant at finding similar content. But it completely fails at a certain type of question:\n\n**\"What patents are held by companies that were acquired by the CEO's former employer?\"**\n\nThis requires following a CHAIN of relationships:\nCEO → Former Employer → Companies They Acquired → Patents Those Companies Hold\n\nVector search would try to find text that's semantically similar to this complex question — and return garbage. SQL could do it with JOINs, but at 5+ levels of relationships, the query becomes a nightmare.\n\n**Graph databases are built exactly for this.**\n\nIn a graph database, data is stored as:\n- **Nodes** — Entities (Person, Company, Patent)\n- **Edges** — Relationships between entities (WORKS_AT, ACQUIRED, HOLDS_PATENT)\n\n```\n(Elon Musk) --[FOUNDED]--> (Tesla)\n(Elon Musk) --[FOUNDED]--> (SpaceX)\n(Tesla)     --[HOLDS]-->   (Patent: Battery Technology)\n(Tesla)     --[ACQUIRED]--> (Maxwell Technologies)\n```\n\n**Cypher Query (Neo4j's query language):**\n```cypher\n// Find all patents held by companies Elon Musk founded\nMATCH (person:Person {name: 'Elon Musk'})\n      -[:FOUNDED]->\n      (company:Company)\n      -[:HOLDS]->\n      (patent:Patent)\nRETURN company.name, patent.title\n```\n\nThis traverses TWO relationships in one query — readable, fast, and elegant.\n\n**When to Use Graph Databases:**\n- Recommendation systems (users who liked X also liked Y)\n- Fraud detection (tracing transaction networks)\n- Enterprise knowledge graphs (org charts, product relationships)\n- Multi-hop RAG questions\n\nGraphs are powerful on their own. But combined with LLMs, they become something truly special. Let me show you.",
            "image": "/images/module_15_databases/m15_graph_rag.png"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "GraphRAG — Microsoft's Breakthrough for Complex Questions",
            "explanation": "In 2024, Microsoft Research released **GraphRAG** and it changed how we think about retrieval for AI applications.\n\n**The Problem with Standard RAG:**\nStandard RAG (vector search → retrieve chunks → send to LLM) works great for simple questions like \"What is our return policy?\" But it struggles with questions that require connecting information ACROSS multiple documents.\n\n**🧒 The Detective Analogy:**\nStandard RAG is like a detective who can only search through files one at a time. If the answer requires connecting a clue from File A with information from File B and context from File C — the detective has to get lucky and find all three.\n\nGraphRAG is like a detective with a **conspiracy board** — all the entities pinned on a board with red strings connecting the relationships. The detective sees the WHOLE picture at once.\n\n**How GraphRAG Works:**\n\n1. **Extract** — Use an LLM to read your documents and extract entities (people, companies, products, concepts) and relationships between them\n2. **Build** — Construct a knowledge graph from those extractions\n3. **Query** — When a user asks a question, traverse the graph to find relevant connected facts\n4. **Generate** — Feed the connected sub-graph to the LLM for a comprehensive answer\n\n**When to use which:**\n\n| Question Type | Best Approach |\n|---|---|\n| \"What does our return policy say?\" | Standard Vector RAG |\n| \"Who are our top 3 customers?\" | Standard Vector RAG |\n| \"Which team members worked on projects related to Client X?\" | **GraphRAG** |\n| \"What are the shared dependencies between Service A and Service B?\" | **GraphRAG** |\n| \"Summarize the themes across all Q3 reports\" | **GraphRAG** |\n\n**🔑 The Rule:** If the question can be answered from a SINGLE document chunk, use vector RAG. If the answer requires CONNECTING information across multiple documents, use GraphRAG.\n\nLet me show you how to query a knowledge graph.",
            "image": "/images/module_15_databases/m15_graph_rag.png"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Querying a Knowledge Graph with Cypher",
