          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Three Tiers of AI Agent Memory",
            "explanation": "Here's a scenario that separates amateur AI apps from professional ones:\n\nYour AI agent talks to a user today about their vacation plans. Three weeks later, the same user comes back and says \"Remember that trip we discussed?\" Your agent has no idea. It feels stupid. The user is frustrated.\n\n**The Problem:** LLMs are stateless. They don't remember anything between conversations. Redis handles the CURRENT session, but once the session expires, everything is forgotten.\n\n**The Solution: A Three-Tier Memory Architecture**\n\nJust like humans have different types of memory, your AI agent needs three layers:\n\n---\n\n**🔵 Tier 1: Short-Term Memory (Redis)**\nWhat it remembers: The current conversation\nDuration: Minutes to hours (TTL-based)\nTechnology: Redis sliding window buffer\n\n\"What did you just say 5 minutes ago?\"\n\n---\n\n**🟣 Tier 2: Long-Term Episodic Memory (Vector DB)**\nWhat it remembers: Past interactions stored as embeddings\nDuration: Weeks to months\nTechnology: pgvector / Pinecone / Qdrant\n\n\"Remember when we talked about your dog last month?\"\n\n---\n\n**🟢 Tier 3: Entity Memory (Graph DB / Structured Store)**\nWhat it remembers: Facts about the user and their world\nDuration: Permanent (until updated)\nTechnology: Neo4j / PostgreSQL JSONB\n\n\"The user's dog is named Max. Max is a golden retriever. Max is 5 years old.\"\n\n---\n\n**How They Work Together:**\n\n```\nUser says: \"Book a vet appointment for Max\"\n\nShort-Term (Redis):\n  → Current conversation: User wants a vet appointment\n\nLong-Term (Vector DB):\n  → 3 weeks ago user mentioned \"Max has been limping lately\"\n\nEntity Memory (Graph/SQL):\n  → User OWNS Max (golden retriever, 5 years old)\n  → Max's vet is Dr. Smith at Happy Paws Clinic\n  → Last vet visit: January 2024\n```\n\nThe AI combines all three memory tiers to generate a response like: \"I'll book an appointment with Dr. Smith at Happy Paws for Max. Given that he was limping a few weeks ago, should I mention that to the vet?\"\n\nTHAT is what makes an AI feel genuinely intelligent. Let me show you how to actually build this.",
            "image": "/images/module_15_databases/m15_agent_memory.png"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Building the Memory Manager — Putting All Three Together",
            "explanation": "Let's build a `MemoryManager` class that queries all three memory tiers and combines the results into a rich context for the LLM.\n\n```python\nimport json, redis\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclass MemoryManager:\n    def __init__(self, user_id, redis_client, vector_db, graph_db=None):\n        self.user_id = user_id\n        self.redis = redis_client\n        self.vector_db = vector_db\n        self.graph_db = graph_db\n        self.client = OpenAI()\n\n    def recall(self, current_message: str, session_id: str) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Recall relevant context from all memory tiers.\"\"\"\n        context_parts = []\n\n        # Tier 1: Short-term (Redis) — current conversation\n        session_key = f'memory:{self.user_id}:{session_id}'\n        recent = json.loads(self.redis.get(session_key) or '[]')\n        if recent:\n            context_parts.append(\n                f\"Current conversation:\\n\" +\n                '\\n'.join([f\"{m['role']}: {m['content']}\" for m in recent[-5:]])\n            )\n\n        # Tier 2: Long-term episodic (Vector DB) — past interactions\n        query_embedding = self._embed(current_message)\n        past_memories = self.vector_db.search(\n            query_vector=query_embedding,\n            filter={'user_id': self.user_id},\n            limit=3\n        )\n        if past_memories:\n            context_parts.append(\n                f\"Relevant past interactions:\\n\" +\n                '\\n'.join([m.content for m in past_memories])\n            )\n\n        # Tier 3: Entity memory (Graph/SQL) — facts about the user\n        if self.graph_db:\n            entities = self.graph_db.query(\n                \"MATCH (u:User {id: $uid})-[r]->(e) RETURN type(r), e\",\n                uid=self.user_id\n            )\n            if entities:\n                context_parts.append(\n                    f\"Known facts about this user:\\n\" +\n                    '\\n'.join([f\"- {e['type(r)']}: {e['e']}\" for e in entities])\n                )\n\n        return '\\n\\n'.join(context_parts)\n\n    def _embed(self, text):\n        return self.client.embeddings.create(\n            model='text-embedding-3-small', input=text\n        ).data[0].embedding\n```\n\n**How to use it:**\n```python\nmemory = MemoryManager(user_id='user_123', redis_client=r, vector_db=qdrant)\ncontext = memory.recall(\"Book a vet appointment for Max\", session_id='sess_456')\n\n# Pass the enriched context to the LLM\nresponse = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model='gpt-4o',\n    messages=[\n        {'role': 'system', 'content': f'You have access to these memories:\\n{context}'},\n        {'role': 'user', 'content': \"Book a vet appointment for Max\"}\n    ]\n)\n```\n\nNow let's design the production database schema that ties everything together.",
            "image": "/images/module_15_databases/m15_agent_memory.png"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Implementing a Memory Recall Function",
            "description": "Write a function called 'recall_relevant_history' that takes a user_id (str), current_message (str), and a db connection. It should: 1) Get the embedding of current_message using get_embedding(), 2) Query the database for the 5 most similar past messages from this user using the pgvector <=> operator, 3) Return the results.",
            "language": "python",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "startingCode": "async def recall_relevant_history(user_id: str, current_message: str, db) -> list:\n    '''Find past messages semantically related to current topic.'''\n    # Step 1: Embed the current message\n    embedding = ___\n    \n    # Step 2: Search past messages for this user\n    results = await db.fetch(\n        '''\n        SELECT content, role, created_at\n        FROM messages m\n        JOIN sessions s ON m.session_id = s.id\n        WHERE s.user_id = $1\n        ORDER BY m.embedding <=> $2::vector\n        LIMIT 5\n        ''',\n        ___\n    )\n    return results",
            "solution": "async def recall_relevant_history(user_id: str, current_message: str, db) -> list:\n    '''Find past messages semantically related to current topic.'''\n    embedding = get_embedding(current_message)\n    results = await db.fetch(\n        '''\n        SELECT content, role, created_at\n        FROM messages m\n        JOIN sessions s ON m.session_id = s.id\n        WHERE s.user_id = $1\n        ORDER BY m.embedding <=> $2::vector\n        LIMIT 5\n        ''',\n        user_id, embedding\n    )\n    return results",
            "validation": true,
            "question": "Write the code here:",
            "starterCode": "# Write code here"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Designing the Production AI Chat Schema",
            "explanation": "Let's design a production-grade database schema for an AI chat application. This is the kind of system design question you'll face in real interviews and real projects.\n\n**The Requirements:**\n- Users can have multiple chat sessions\n- Each session has multiple messages\n- Messages need vector embeddings for long-term semantic memory\n- We need to track token usage for billing\n- Sessions should auto-expire after 30 days of inactivity\n\n**The Schema:**\n```sql\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS vector;\n\n-- Users table\nCREATE TABLE users (\n  id           UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),\n  email        TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL,\n  plan         TEXT DEFAULT 'free',\n  created_at   TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW()\n);\n\n-- Sessions (one conversation thread)\nCREATE TABLE sessions (\n  id           UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),\n  user_id      UUID REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n  title        TEXT,\n  created_at   TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW(),\n  last_active  TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW()\n);\n\n-- Messages with embeddings for semantic memory\nCREATE TABLE messages (\n  id           UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),\n  session_id   UUID REFERENCES sessions(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n  role         TEXT NOT NULL,    -- 'user' or 'assistant'\n  content      TEXT NOT NULL,\n  embedding    vector(1536),     -- For semantic memory search\n  tokens_used  INTEGER,          -- For billing\n  created_at   TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW()\n);\n\n-- Performance indexes\nCREATE INDEX ON sessions(user_id);\nCREATE INDEX ON messages(session_id);\nCREATE INDEX ON messages USING hnsw (embedding vector_cosine_ops);\n```\n\n**🔑 Key Design Decisions:**\n- **UUIDs** instead of serial integers — safer for distributed systems and prevents ID guessing\n- **ON DELETE CASCADE** — deleting a user automatically deletes their sessions and messages\n- **vector(1536)** — stores OpenAI embeddings directly in the messages table\n- **tokens_used** — tracks token consumption per message for billing\n- **HNSW index** on embeddings — enables fast semantic memory search\n\nThis schema, combined with Redis for short-term memory and semantic caching, gives you a complete production-grade data layer for any AI chat application."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Choosing the Right Database — The Complete Decision Framework",
            "explanation": "You've now learned every database type used in production AI systems. Let me give you the complete decision framework so you always know which database to pick.\n\n**The Quick Decision Guide:**\n\n| Your Need | Use This |\n|---|---|\n| User accounts, payments, billing | **PostgreSQL** (ACID required) |\n| Find semantically similar documents | **pgvector** (moderate) or **Pinecone/Qdrant** (large scale) |\n| Cache expensive LLM responses | **Redis** (hash cache or semantic cache) |\n| Store current conversation memory | **Redis** (sliding window with TTL) |\n| Store dynamic JSON logs/outputs | **MongoDB** or PostgreSQL JSONB |\n| Query complex relationships | **Neo4j** (Graph DB) |\n| Long-term user memory | **pgvector** (embeddings of past interactions) |\n| Multi-tenant enterprise search | **Qdrant/Pinecone** with pre-filtering |\n\n---\n\n**🔑 For Most AI Startups, Start Here:**\n\n**PostgreSQL + pgvector + Redis** covers 90% of your needs:\n- PostgreSQL handles all structured data (users, billing, chat history)\n- pgvector adds semantic search to the same PostgreSQL instance\n- Redis handles caching and real-time session state\n\nAdd a dedicated vector DB (Pinecone/Qdrant) only when you exceed a few million vectors. Add Neo4j only when you need multi-hop relationship queries (GraphRAG).\n\n**The Database Evolution Path:**\n\n```\nStage 1 (MVP):        PostgreSQL + pgvector + Redis\nStage 2 (Growth):     + Dedicated Vector DB (Pinecone/Qdrant)\nStage 3 (Enterprise): + Neo4j for GraphRAG\n                       + Multi-tenant security (pre-filtered indexes)\n                       + Semantic caching\n                       + Full observability\n```\n\nLet's test your decision-making with a real architectural challenge.",
            "image": "/images/module_15_databases/m15_agent_memory.png"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "content": {
            "title": "Architecting a Full AI Application Stack",
            "description": "You're the lead engineer of an AI writing assistant with 50,000 users. Your system needs to: (1) Store user accounts and subscription plans with ACID guarantees, (2) Remember users' previous documents for personalized suggestions using semantic search, (3) Cache GPT-4 responses to reduce costs by 60%, (4) Log every LLM call for billing with flexible JSON schema, and (5) Give the AI long-term memory of user preferences across sessions. Design the architecture.",
            "choices": [
