          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Automated Evals in CI/CD Pipelines (GitHub Actions)",
            "explanation": "Here's something that will separate you from 95% of AI engineers out there: automated evaluation in your deployment pipeline.\n\nLet me paint the picture. You're working on a customer support chatbot. Your colleague pushes a 'small' prompt change on Friday afternoon. Over the weekend, the chatbot's accuracy drops from 92% to 67%, but nobody notices until Monday when customer complaints start flooding in.\n\n**This should NEVER happen.** And it won't, if you treat your prompts like code and test them like code.\n\n[IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: CI/CD Eval Pipeline Diagram]\n\n** The Spell-Check Analogy:**\n\nImagine Microsoft Word without spell-check. You write a document, hit 'Send', and pray there are no typos. Sounds crazy, right? But that's EXACTLY what most AI teams do with their prompts — they change them, deploy them, and pray they still work.\n\nCI/CD evaluation is the spell-check for your AI. Every time someone changes a prompt, the system automatically runs your evaluation suite BEFORE the change goes live. Red light? The change is blocked. Green light? Ship it.\n\n**How It Works — The Three-Step Pipeline:**\n\n```yaml\n# .github/workflows/eval.yml\nname: LLM Evaluation Pipeline\non:\n  pull_request:\n    paths:\n      - 'prompts/**'          # Trigger on prompt changes\n      - 'src/chains/**'       # Trigger on chain logic changes\n\njobs:\n  evaluate:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - name: Install dependencies\n        run: pip install deepeval ragas openai\n      - name: Run LLM Evaluation Suite\n        run: deepeval test run tests/eval_suite.py\n        env:\n          OPENAI_API_KEY: ${{ secrets.OPENAI_API_KEY }}\n      - name: Check Regression\n        run: python scripts/check_regression.py --threshold 0.05\n```\n\n**What This Pipeline Does:**\n1. **Trigger**: Whenever someone opens a PR that touches prompts or chain logic\n2. **Run Evals**: Execute your full DeepEval test suite against the new prompt\n3. **Check Regression**: Compare scores against the baseline. If any metric drops more than 5%, BLOCK the merge\n\n**The Regression Check Script:**\n```python\n# scripts/check_regression.py\nimport json\n\ndef check_regression(current_scores, baseline_scores, threshold=0.05):\n    for metric, score in current_scores.items():\n        baseline = baseline_scores[metric]\n        drop = baseline - score\n        if drop > threshold:\n            print(f'REGRESSION: {metric} dropped {drop:.2%}')\n            print(f'  Baseline: {baseline:.2%} -> Current: {score:.2%}')\n            return False  # Block the merge!\n    return True  # Safe to merge\n```\n\n**Real-World Example:**\nAnthropic runs thousands of automated evaluations on every model update. If Claude's helpfulness score drops even 1% on their benchmark suite, the update is rolled back automatically. You should do the same for your prompts.\n\n**The Key Insight:**\nPrompts are production code. A one-word change in your system prompt can cause a 30% accuracy drop. Without automated evals in CI/CD, you're flying blind. With them, you catch regressions before they reach users."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Building a GitHub Actions Eval Workflow",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Write a regression check function that compares current evaluation scores against baseline scores. If any metric drops by more than the threshold (default 0.05), the function should return False (blocking the merge). Otherwise return True.",
            "starterCode": "def check_regression(current_scores, baseline_scores, threshold=0.05):\n    \"\"\"\n    Compare current eval scores against baseline.\n    Returns False if any metric regressed beyond threshold.\n    \"\"\"\n    for metric, score in current_scores.items():\n        baseline = baseline_scores[metric]\n        # TODO: Calculate the drop and check against threshold\n        pass\n    return True\n\n# Test it\nbaseline = {'faithfulness': 0.92, 'relevancy': 0.88}\ncurrent = {'faithfulness': 0.85, 'relevancy': 0.87}\nprint(check_regression(current, baseline))  # Should print False",
            "solution": "def check_regression(current_scores, baseline_scores, threshold=0.05):\n    for metric, score in current_scores.items():\n        baseline = baseline_scores[metric]\n        drop = baseline - score\n        if drop > threshold:\n            print(f'REGRESSION: {metric} dropped {drop:.2%}')\n            return False\n    return True\n\nbaseline = {'faithfulness': 0.92, 'relevancy': 0.88}\ncurrent = {'faithfulness': 0.85, 'relevancy': 0.87}\nprint(check_regression(current, baseline))",
            "hint": "Calculate drop = baseline - score. If drop > threshold, return False.",
            "explanation": "This function is the heart of your CI/CD eval pipeline. It compares each metric against the baseline and blocks deployment if any metric regresses beyond the threshold. The 0.05 default means a 5% drop triggers a block.",
            "concepts": [
              "CI/CD",
              "Regression Testing",
              "Evaluation Pipeline"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "drop.*=.*baseline.*-.*score.*if.*drop.*>.*threshold.*return False"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Human-in-the-Loop (HITL) Feedback & Annotation Workflows",
            "explanation": "Automated evals are essential, but they have blind spots. There are things that only humans can catch — subtle tone issues, culturally insensitive responses, answers that are technically correct but unhelpful. That's where Human-in-the-Loop feedback comes in.\n\n** The Hotel Concierge Analogy:**\n\nThink about a hotel concierge training program. The concierge learns from a manual (pre-training), practices with mock guests (fine-tuning), but the REAL learning happens when actual hotel guests give feedback: 'That restaurant recommendation was perfect!' or 'You sent me to a place that was closed on Sundays.'\n\nHITL is how your AI learns from its real users, in production, every day.\n\n**The Three Types of Human Feedback:**\n\n**1. Implicit Feedback (Zero User Effort):**\n```python\n# Track user behavior as signals\nfeedback_signals = {\n    'regenerated': True,    # User clicked 'Regenerate' (bad sign)\n    'copied_response': True, # User copied the text (good sign)\n    'session_continued': True, # User asked a follow-up (good sign)\n    'abandoned': False,      # User left immediately (bad sign)\n}\n```\n\n**2. Explicit Feedback (Minimal User Effort):**\n```python\n# The classic thumbs up/down\nfeedback = {\n    'response_id': 'resp_abc123',\n    'rating': 'thumbs_down',\n    'category': 'incorrect_information',  # Optional dropdown\n    'comment': 'The refund policy is wrong'  # Optional text\n}\n```\n\n**3. Expert Annotation (High Effort, Highest Value):**\n```python\n# Domain experts review and correct responses\nannotation = {\n    'response_id': 'resp_abc123',\n    'original_response': 'Take 2 tablets daily...',\n    'corrected_response': 'Take 1 tablet twice daily with food...',\n    'error_type': 'dosage_error',\n    'severity': 'critical'\n}\n```\n\n**Building the Feedback Flywheel:**\n\n```\nUser interacts with AI\n    ↓\nCollect feedback (thumbs up/down, edits, behavior)\n    ↓\nFilter low-scoring responses into review queue\n    ↓\nDomain experts annotate corrections\n    ↓\nCorrected pairs become fine-tuning data\n    ↓\nRetrain/update the model\n    ↓\nDeploy improved model\n    ↓\nRepeat (the model gets better every cycle!)\n```\n\n**Tools for HITL Workflows:**\n| Tool | Best For | Open Source? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Label Studio** | General annotation platform | Yes |\n| **Argilla** | LLM-specific feedback collection | Yes |\n| **LangSmith** | Integrated with LangChain ecosystem | Freemium |\n| **Scale AI** | Enterprise annotation at scale | No |\n\n**The Key Insight:**\nEvery thumbs-down is a free training example. Every user correction is gold. The companies winning the AI race aren't just building better models — they're building better feedback loops."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
