        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Prompt Management — Treating Prompts Like Code",
            "explanation": "Here's a question that exposes whether you've worked in production AI or not: **Where is your prompt stored?**\n\nIf your answer is 'hardcoded in the Python file,' you're going to have a bad time. Let me explain why.\n\n**The Prompt Catastrophe Timeline:**\n\n- **Monday**: Developer A changes the system prompt to improve tone\n- **Tuesday**: Quality drops 20%, but nobody notices\n- **Wednesday**: Developer B changes the prompt again, trying to fix an unrelated issue\n- **Thursday**: Quality drops to 50%. Customer complaints triple.\n- **Friday**: Everyone panics. Nobody remembers what the prompt said on Sunday.\n- **Saturday**: The team spends 8 hours manually testing prompt variations\n\nThis happens because the prompt — the MOST important piece of your AI application — has zero version control, zero testing, and zero rollback capability.\n\n** The Recipe Book Analogy:**\n\nImagine a restaurant where the recipe for their signature dish isn't written down. The chef just 'knows' it. One day, the chef adds extra salt. The next day, another chef removes the garlic. Within a week, the dish is unrecognizable. Customers are furious.\n\nNow imagine the same restaurant with a proper recipe book: every change is dated, the old version is preserved, and you can always revert to 'last week's recipe.' That's prompt management.\n\n**The Prompt Registry Pattern:**\n```python\nclass PromptRegistry:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.prompts = {}\n        self.versions = {}\n    \n    def register(self, name: str, template: str, version: str):\n        \"\"\"Register a new prompt version\"\"\"\n        self.prompts[name] = {\n            'template': template,\n            'version': version,\n            'created_at': datetime.now().isoformat(),\n            'is_active': False\n        }\n        if name not in self.versions:\n            self.versions[name] = []\n        self.versions[name].append(version)\n    \n    def activate(self, name: str, version: str):\n        \"\"\"Promote a version to production\"\"\"\n        self.prompts[name]['is_active'] = True\n        \n    def rollback(self, name: str):\n        \"\"\"Revert to previous version\"\"\"\n        versions = self.versions[name]\n        if len(versions) >= 2:\n            previous = versions[-2]\n            self.activate(name, previous)\n```\n\n**Best Practices for Prompt Management:**\n\n1. **Version Every Change** — Even a one-word edit gets a new version number\n2. **Separate Prompts from Code** — Store prompts in config files or a database, not inline\n3. **Test Before Deploying** — Run your eval suite against the new prompt before going live\n4. **Enable Instant Rollback** — If quality drops, revert in seconds, not hours\n5. **Track Metrics Per Version** — Know which prompt version performs best\n\n**Tools for Prompt Management:**\n| Tool | Approach | Best For |\n|---|---|---|\n| **LangSmith Hub** | Cloud-hosted prompt registry | LangChain users |\n| **Promptfoo** | CLI-based prompt testing | CI/CD integration |\n| **Git + YAML** | Version prompts in repo | Simple, free |\n| **Humanloop** | Full prompt lifecycle platform | Enterprise |"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "hint": "In prompt management, every change to a prompt should be tracked with a version number, tested against an evaluation suite, and deployable with instant rollback.",
          "explanation": "Prompt management treats prompts as production code with versioning, testing, and deployment controls. This prevents the common disaster where an untested prompt change silently degrades quality.",
          "challenge": {
