          "type": "scenario",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Prompt Deployment Decision",
            "description": "Your team has a new system prompt that improves helpfulness by 15% in eval tests, but it also causes a 3% increase in hallucination rate. The current production prompt is stable. What should you do?",
            "choices": [
              {
                "text": "Deploy immediately — 15% helpfulness improvement is worth a small hallucination increase.",
                "score": 10,
                "outcome": "Risky! A 3% hallucination increase in production could mean hundreds of factually incorrect responses per day. In medical, legal, or financial domains, this could be catastrophic."
              },
              {
                "text": "Run an A/B test — deploy the new prompt to 10% of users and monitor both helpfulness AND hallucination rates in production.",
                "score": 100,
                "outcome": "Perfect! A/B testing lets you validate the improvement with real users while limiting the blast radius of any hallucination issues. If the hallucination rate is acceptable in production, gradually increase the rollout percentage."
              },
              {
                "text": "Reject the new prompt — any hallucination increase is unacceptable.",
                "score": 40,
                "outcome": "Conservative but potentially too rigid. A 3% increase might be acceptable depending on your domain. The better approach is to A/B test and make a data-driven decision rather than rejecting based on eval tests alone."
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "A/B Testing & Shadow Deployments for Prompts & Models",
            "explanation": "You've got a new prompt that looks great in eval tests. But eval tests are controlled environments — real users are chaotic, unpredictable, and creative in ways your test suite never imagined. How do you safely test in production?\n\n[IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: A/B Testing Architecture for LLMs]\n\n** The New Menu Item Analogy:**\n\nImagine a restaurant wants to add a new dish. They don't replace the entire menu overnight. Instead, they:\n1. **Shadow test**: Cook the new dish alongside the old one for a week. Chefs eat both, compare quality. Customers never see the new dish.\n2. **A/B test**: Offer the new dish to 10% of tables. Compare ratings and reorder rates.\n3. **Gradual rollout**: If the new dish wins, expand to 25%, then 50%, then 100%.\n\nThe same approach works for LLM prompts and models.\n\n**Strategy 1: Shadow Deployment (Zero Risk)**\n\nRun BOTH prompts simultaneously, but only serve the old one to users. Log both outputs for comparison:\n\n```python\nasync def shadow_test(query: str):\n    # Production path (served to user)\n    prod_response = await llm(query, prompt=PROD_PROMPT)\n    \n    # Shadow path (logged, never served)\n    shadow_response = await llm(query, prompt=NEW_PROMPT)\n    \n    # Log both for comparison\n    log_comparison(query, prod_response, shadow_response)\n    \n    return prod_response  # User only sees production\n```\n\n**Strategy 2: A/B Testing (Controlled Risk)**\n\nRoute a percentage of users to each variant:\n\n```python\nimport hashlib\n\ndef ab_test_router(user_id: str, test_percentage: float = 0.1):\n    \"\"\"Deterministic A/B split based on user ID\"\"\"\n    hash_val = int(hashlib.md5(user_id.encode()).hexdigest(), 16)\n    bucket = hash_val % 100\n    \n    if bucket < test_percentage * 100:\n        return 'variant_b'  # New prompt (10% of users)\n    return 'variant_a'      # Production prompt (90%)\n\ndef get_prompt(user_id: str):\n    variant = ab_test_router(user_id)\n    prompts = {\n        'variant_a': PROD_PROMPT,    # Current production\n        'variant_b': NEW_PROMPT      # Challenger\n    }\n    return prompts[variant], variant\n```\n\n**Strategy 3: Canary Deployment (Gradual Rollout)**\n```\nDay 1: 5% of traffic → new prompt\nDay 3: 10% (if metrics hold)\nDay 5: 25%\nDay 7: 50%\nDay 10: 100% (full rollout)\n```\nAt each stage, compare metrics. If ANY metric regresses, automatically roll back to the previous stage.\n\n**Key Metrics to Track During A/B Tests:**\n| Metric | What It Tells You |\n|---|---|\n| **Latency** | Is the new prompt slower? |\n| **Cost** | Does it use more tokens? |\n| **Accuracy/Faithfulness** | Is it more or less accurate? |\n| **User Satisfaction** | Thumbs up/down ratio |\n| **Error Rate** | Does it fail more often? |"
          }
        },
        {
