            "starterCode": "import hashlib\n\ndef ab_router(user_id: str, experiment_pct: float = 0.10) -> str:\n    # Hash the user_id for deterministic routing\n    hash_hex = hashlib.md5(user_id.encode()).hexdigest()\n    bucket = int(hash_hex, 16) % ___\n    \n    if bucket < ___:\n        return '___'\n    return '___'\n\n# Test\nprint(ab_router('user_001'))  # Should consistently return same group\nprint(ab_router('user_002'))",
            "solution": "import hashlib\n\ndef ab_router(user_id: str, experiment_pct: float = 0.10) -> str:\n    hash_hex = hashlib.md5(user_id.encode()).hexdigest()\n    bucket = int(hash_hex, 16) % 100\n    \n    if bucket < experiment_pct * 100:\n        return 'experiment'\n    return 'control'\n\nprint(ab_router('user_001'))\nprint(ab_router('user_002'))",
            "hint": "Use modulo 100 to get a bucket 0-99. Compare bucket to experiment_pct * 100.",
            "explanation": "MD5 hashing ensures deterministic routing — the same user_id always maps to the same bucket, so they always see the same variant. This prevents confusing UX where the model behavior changes between requests.",
