          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Production Semantic Caching with Redis for 0ms Responses",
            "explanation": "Here's a mind-blowing optimization: what if you could answer many user queries in 0 milliseconds, for $0.00, with perfect accuracy? That's what semantic caching does.\n\n[IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Semantic Caching Architecture]\n\n** The Library Reference Desk Analogy:**\n\nImagine a library reference desk. Every time someone asks 'Where is the history section?', the librarian walks through the entire library to find it (LLM call). But a smart librarian keeps a notebook. After the first person asks, they write down the answer. Next time someone asks, they just check the notebook. Instant answer!\n\nBut here's the clever part: if someone asks 'Where can I find history books?', that's essentially the SAME question with different words. A semantic cache understands this and returns the cached answer for semantically similar queries.\n\n**Traditional Cache vs. Semantic Cache:**\n\n```python\n# Traditional cache (exact match only)\ncache = {'What are your hours?': 'We are open 9am-5pm'}\nquery = 'What time do you open?'  # Cache MISS! Different words.\n\n# Semantic cache (meaning-based match)\nsemantic_cache.get('What time do you open?')  \n# Cache HIT! Semantically similar to 'What are your hours?'\n```\n\n**How Semantic Caching Works:**\n\n```\n1. User asks: 'What time do you open?'\n2. Embed the question → [0.23, -0.45, 0.12, ...] (vector)\n3. Search Redis for similar vectors (cosine similarity > 0.95)\n4. Found match? → Return cached answer (0ms, $0.00)\n5. No match? → Call LLM, cache the response for next time\n```\n\n**Implementation with Redis:**\n\n```python\nimport redis\nimport numpy as np\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()\nr = redis.Redis(host='localhost', port=6379)\n\ndef get_embedding(text: str) -> list:\n    response = client.embeddings.create(\n        model='text-embedding-3-small',\n        input=text\n    )\n    return response.data[0].embedding\n\ndef semantic_cache_lookup(query: str, threshold: float = 0.95):\n    query_embedding = get_embedding(query)\n    \n    # Search Redis for similar embeddings\n    cached_keys = r.keys('cache:*')\n    for key in cached_keys:\n        cached_data = json.loads(r.get(key))\n        similarity = cosine_similarity(\n            query_embedding, cached_data['embedding']\n        )\n        if similarity >= threshold:\n            return cached_data['response']  # Cache HIT!\n    \n    return None  # Cache MISS\n\ndef answer_with_cache(query: str) -> str:\n    # Try cache first\n    cached = semantic_cache_lookup(query)\n    if cached:\n        return cached  # 0ms, $0.00!\n    \n    # Cache miss — call LLM\n    response = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model='gpt-4o-mini',\n        messages=[{'role': 'user', 'content': query}]\n    )\n    answer = response.choices[0].message.content\n    \n    # Store in cache for next time\n    cache_data = {\n        'embedding': get_embedding(query),\n        'response': answer,\n        'created_at': datetime.now().isoformat()\n    }\n    r.setex(f'cache:{hash(query)}', 3600, json.dumps(cache_data))\n    \n    return answer\n```\n\n**Real-World Impact:**\n| Metric | Without Cache | With Semantic Cache |\n|---|---|---|\n| Avg. Latency | 1.5s | 0.3s (80% reduction) |\n| Cost per 100K queries | $150 | $45 (70% reduction) |\n| Cache Hit Rate | 0% | 35-60% (typical) |\n\n**When NOT to Cache:**\n- Real-time data queries ('What's the stock price right now?')\n- Personalized responses that depend on user context\n- Conversations where context changes with each message\n- Creative tasks where variety is desired\n\n**Production Tips:**\n1. Set TTL (time-to-live) on cache entries — stale answers are dangerous\n2. Use a similarity threshold of 0.93-0.97 (too low = wrong answers, too high = low hit rate)\n3. Monitor cache hit rates — below 20% means your queries are too diverse for caching\n4. Invalidate cache when underlying data changes"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Building a Semantic Cache with Redis",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Write a cosine_similarity function that compares two embedding vectors and returns a similarity score between -1 and 1. Then write a simple cache lookup that returns the cached response if similarity is above a threshold.",
            "starterCode": "import numpy as np\n\ndef cosine_similarity(vec_a, vec_b):\n    \"\"\"Calculate cosine similarity between two vectors.\"\"\"\n    dot_product = np.dot(vec_a, vec_b)\n    norm_a = np.linalg.norm(___)\n    norm_b = np.linalg.norm(___)\n    return ___ / (___ * ___)\n\n# Test\nv1 = [1, 0, 1]\nv2 = [1, 0, 1]\nprint(cosine_similarity(v1, v2))  # Should be 1.0 (identical)",
            "solution": "import numpy as np\n\ndef cosine_similarity(vec_a, vec_b):\n    dot_product = np.dot(vec_a, vec_b)\n    norm_a = np.linalg.norm(vec_a)\n    norm_b = np.linalg.norm(vec_b)\n    return dot_product / (norm_a * norm_b)\n\nv1 = [1, 0, 1]\nv2 = [1, 0, 1]\nprint(cosine_similarity(v1, v2))",
            "hint": "Cosine similarity = dot(A,B) / (||A|| * ||B||). Use np.linalg.norm for vector norms.",
            "explanation": "Cosine similarity is the foundation of semantic caching. Two semantically similar questions will have embeddings that point in nearly the same direction, producing a similarity score close to 1.0.",
            "concepts": [
              "Cosine Similarity",
              "Semantic Caching",
              "Embeddings"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "np.dot.*np.linalg.norm.*dot_product.*norm_a.*norm_b"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Dynamic Model Routing (Tiered Cost Optimization)",
            "explanation": "Let's put cost optimization into practice with the most powerful technique: dynamic model routing. The idea is simple but the impact is massive.\n\n[IMAGE:/images/m17/m17_model_routing.png]\n\n** The Hospital Triage Analogy:**\n\nHospitals don't send every patient to a brain surgeon. They triage:\n- **Minor cut?** → Nurse (fast, cheap)\n- **Broken bone?** → ER Doctor (moderate)\n- **Brain surgery?** → Specialist (slow, expensive)\n\nModel routing is triage for AI queries:\n\n```python\nclass ModelRouter:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.tiers = {\n            'tier1_cheap': {\n                'model': 'gpt-4o-mini',\n                'cost_per_1m': 0.15,\n                'max_complexity': 3\n            },\n            'tier2_mid': {\n                'model': 'gpt-4o',\n                'cost_per_1m': 2.50,\n                'max_complexity': 7\n            },\n            'tier3_premium': {\n                'model': 'claude-3-5-sonnet',\n                'cost_per_1m': 3.00,\n                'max_complexity': 10\n            }\n        }\n    \n    def classify_complexity(self, query: str) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Score query complexity 1-10 using a tiny classifier\"\"\"\n        # Use a small model or heuristic to classify\n        indicators = {\n            'simple_greeting': 1,\n            'faq_question': 2,\n            'multi_step_reasoning': 6,\n            'code_generation': 7,\n            'complex_analysis': 9\n        }\n        # In practice, use a fine-tuned classifier here\n        return self._classify(query)\n    \n    def route(self, query: str) -> str:\n        complexity = self.classify_complexity(query)\n        \n        if complexity <= 3:\n            return self.tiers['tier1_cheap']['model']\n        elif complexity <= 7:\n            return self.tiers['tier2_mid']['model']\n        else:\n            return self.tiers['tier3_premium']['model']\n```\n\n**The Complexity Classifier:**\n\nThe trick is making the classifier MUCH cheaper than the LLM call itself:\n\n```python\n# Option 1: Keyword heuristic (free)\ndef heuristic_classify(query: str) -> int:\n    simple_patterns = ['hello', 'thanks', 'what are your hours']\n    complex_patterns = ['analyze', 'compare', 'write code', 'debug']\n    \n    query_lower = query.lower()\n    if any(p in query_lower for p in simple_patterns):\n        return 1\n    if any(p in query_lower for p in complex_patterns):\n        return 8\n    return 4  # Default to mid-tier\n\n# Option 2: Tiny fine-tuned classifier ($0.001 per query)\ndef model_classify(query: str) -> int:\n    resp = completion(\n        model='gpt-4o-mini',\n        messages=[{\n            'role': 'system',\n            'content': 'Rate query complexity 1-10. Output ONLY the number.'\n        }, {'role': 'user', 'content': query}],\n        max_tokens=2\n    )\n    return int(resp.choices[0].message.content)\n```\n\n**Real Cost Impact:**\n\nAssume 100,000 queries/month with typical distribution:\n| Tier | % of Queries | Model | Cost/1M tokens | Monthly Cost |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Tier 1 | 60% | GPT-4o-mini | $0.15 | $9 |\n| Tier 2 | 30% | GPT-4o | $2.50 | $75 |\n| Tier 3 | 10% | Claude 3.5 | $3.00 | $30 |\n| **Total** | | | | **$114** |\n\n**Without routing (all GPT-4o):** $250/month\n**With routing:** $114/month — **54% savings!**"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Building a Smart Model Router",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Build a model router function that takes a query string and returns the appropriate model name based on keyword-based complexity classification. Simple queries (containing 'hello', 'thanks', 'hi') go to 'gpt-4o-mini'. Complex queries (containing 'analyze', 'debug', 'compare') go to 'gpt-4o'. Everything else goes to 'gpt-4o-mini' by default.",
            "starterCode": "def route_query(query: str) -> str:\n    simple_keywords = [___]\n    complex_keywords = [___]\n    \n    query_lower = query.lower()\n    \n    for kw in complex_keywords:\n        if kw in query_lower:\n            return '___'\n    \n    return '___'  # Default to cheapest\n\n# Test\nprint(route_query('Hello there!'))  # gpt-4o-mini\nprint(route_query('Analyze this dataset'))  # gpt-4o\nprint(route_query('What is AI?'))  # gpt-4o-mini",
            "solution": "def route_query(query: str) -> str:\n    simple_keywords = ['hello', 'thanks', 'hi', 'bye']\n    complex_keywords = ['analyze', 'debug', 'compare', 'explain in detail']\n    \n    query_lower = query.lower()\n    \n    for kw in complex_keywords:\n        if kw in query_lower:\n            return 'gpt-4o'\n    \n    return 'gpt-4o-mini'\n\nprint(route_query('Hello there!'))\nprint(route_query('Analyze this dataset'))\nprint(route_query('What is AI?'))",
            "hint": "Check complex keywords first (they should route to the expensive model). Default to the cheap model.",
            "explanation": "This simple keyword-based router can save 50%+ on API costs. In production, you'd use a fine-tuned classifier or embedding-based similarity instead of keywords for more accurate routing.",
