          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Speculative Decoding for Accelerated Generation",
            "explanation": "Here's a counterintuitive optimization: what if running TWO models simultaneously was FASTER than running one?\n\nThat's exactly what speculative decoding does, and it can speed up generation by 2-3x.\n\n** The Executive Assistant Analogy:**\n\nImagine a CEO who types very slowly but is brilliant. They draft important emails at 10 words per minute. Now give them an assistant who types fast but makes some mistakes. The assistant drafts the email at 100 wpm, and the CEO quickly reviews it — accepting correct parts and fixing mistakes. The result? The email is done 5x faster.\n\nSpeculative decoding works the same way:\n\n1. A **small, fast 'draft' model** generates several tokens quickly\n2. The **large, accurate 'target' model** verifies all tokens in ONE forward pass\n3. Correct tokens are accepted, wrong ones are regenerated\n\n```\nWithout Speculative Decoding:\nTarget Model: [token1] → [token2] → [token3] → [token4]\n                 1s         1s         1s         1s     = 4s total\n\nWith Speculative Decoding:\nDraft Model:  [t1, t2, t3, t4] generated in 0.2s\nTarget Model: [verify all 4]   in one pass = 1.2s\n              Accept t1✓ t2✓ t3✗ t4✓, regenerate t3 = 0.4s\nTotal: 1.8s (2.2x faster!)\n```\n\n**Why Verification is Cheap:**\nLLMs generate tokens ONE at a time (sequential). But verifying multiple tokens can be done in PARALLEL — the target model processes all draft tokens in a single forward pass. Verification of 5 tokens costs about the same as generating 1 token.\n\n**When Speculative Decoding Shines:**\n- The draft model accepts 70%+ of tokens (high acceptance rate)\n- You're serving a large model (70B+) where generation is expensive\n- Latency matters more than throughput\n\n**When to Skip It:**\n- Small models (7-8B) are already fast\n- The draft model has low acceptance rate (<50%)\n- You're optimizing for throughput, not latency\n\n```python\n# vLLM with speculative decoding\n# python -m vllm.entrypoints.openai.api_server \\\n#   --model meta-llama/Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct \\\n#   --speculative-model meta-llama/Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct \\\n#   --num-speculative-tokens 5\n```\n\nThe 1B model drafts 5 tokens at a time, and the 70B model verifies them. Result: 2-3x faster inference for the 70B model at nearly zero quality cost."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "LLM GPU Memory Estimator",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Build a function that estimates total GPU VRAM needed for serving an LLM. The formula is: VRAM = model_size_gb (from quantization) + kv_cache_gb (estimated as 10% of model size per concurrent user) + overhead_gb (fixed 2GB). Calculate for 10 concurrent users with a 70B model at INT4 quantization.",
            "starterCode": "def estimate_vram(\n    params_billions: float,\n    quantization_bits: int = 4,\n    concurrent_users: int = 10,\n    overhead_gb: float = 2.0\n) -> dict:\n    bytes_per_param = quantization_bits / 8\n    model_size_gb = params_billions * ___\n    kv_cache_per_user = model_size_gb * ___\n    total_kv_cache = kv_cache_per_user * ___\n    total_vram = model_size_gb + total_kv_cache + ___\n    \n    return {\n        'model_size_gb': model_size_gb,\n        'kv_cache_gb': total_kv_cache,\n        'total_vram_gb': total_vram\n    }\n\nresult = estimate_vram(70, 4, 10)\nprint(f\"Total VRAM needed: {result['total_vram_gb']:.1f} GB\")",
            "solution": "def estimate_vram(\n    params_billions: float,\n    quantization_bits: int = 4,\n    concurrent_users: int = 10,\n    overhead_gb: float = 2.0\n) -> dict:\n    bytes_per_param = quantization_bits / 8\n    model_size_gb = params_billions * bytes_per_param\n    kv_cache_per_user = model_size_gb * 0.10\n    total_kv_cache = kv_cache_per_user * concurrent_users\n    total_vram = model_size_gb + total_kv_cache + overhead_gb\n    \n    return {\n        'model_size_gb': model_size_gb,\n        'kv_cache_gb': total_kv_cache,\n        'total_vram_gb': total_vram\n    }\n\nresult = estimate_vram(70, 4, 10)\nprint(f\"Total VRAM needed: {result['total_vram_gb']:.1f} GB\")",
            "hint": "bytes_per_param for 4-bit = 0.5. KV cache per user = 10% of model size (0.10).",
            "explanation": "A 70B model at INT4 = 35 GB model + 35 GB KV cache (10 users × 3.5 GB each) + 2 GB overhead = 72 GB total. This fits on a single A100 80GB GPU! Without quantization (FP16), the model alone would be 140 GB.",
            "concepts": [
              "VRAM Estimation",
              "KV Cache",
              "GPU Planning"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "bytes_per_param.*model_size_gb.*kv_cache.*concurrent_users.*total_vram"
            }
          }
        },
