        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Simple Rate Limiter",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Complete the rate limiter. The check_rate_limit function should: 1) Remove timestamps older than the window, 2) Check if count >= max_requests, 3) Add current timestamp.",
            "starterCode": "from fastapi import FastAPI, Request, HTTPException\nimport time\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\napp = FastAPI()\nrequest_log = defaultdict(list)\n\ndef check_rate_limit(ip: str, max_requests: int = 10, window: int = 60):\n    now = time.time()\n    # TODO: Remove old timestamps outside the window\n    # TODO: Check if user exceeded max_requests\n    # TODO: Log the new request timestamp\n    pass\n\n@app.middleware(\"http\")\nasync def rate_limit_middleware(request: Request, call_next):\n    ip = request.client.host\n    if not check_rate_limit(ip):\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=429, detail=\"Too many requests\")\n    return await call_next(request)",
            "solution": "from fastapi import FastAPI, Request, HTTPException\nimport time\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\napp = FastAPI()\nrequest_log = defaultdict(list)\n\ndef check_rate_limit(ip: str, max_requests: int = 10, window: int = 60):\n    now = time.time()\n    request_log[ip] = [t for t in request_log[ip] if now - t < window]\n    if len(request_log[ip]) >= max_requests:\n        return False\n    request_log[ip].append(now)\n    return True\n\n@app.middleware(\"http\")\nasync def rate_limit_middleware(request: Request, call_next):\n    ip = request.client.host\n    if not check_rate_limit(ip):\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=429, detail=\"Too many requests\")\n    return await call_next(request)",
            "hint": "Filter request_log[ip] to keep only timestamps within the window (now - t < window). Then check if the list length >= max_requests.",
            "explanation": "This sliding window rate limiter keeps a list of timestamps per IP. On each request, it removes expired timestamps, checks if the count exceeds the limit, and adds the new timestamp. In production, use Redis for persistence.",
            "concepts": [
              "Rate Limiting",
              "Sliding Window",
              "Middleware"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "request_log\\[ip\\].*=.*\\[.*for.*in.*request_log"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "questions": [
            {
              "question": "What HTTP status code should a rate limiter return when a user exceeds their quota?",
              "options": [
                "401 Unauthorized",
                "403 Forbidden",
                "429 Too Many Requests",
                "503 Service Unavailable"
              ],
              "answer": 2,
              "explanation": "429 Too Many Requests is the standard HTTP status code for rate limiting. It tells the client they've sent too many requests and should wait before retrying."
            },
            {
              "question": "Why should API versioning use URL paths (/api/v1/) instead of headers?",
              "options": [
                "Headers are not supported in all browsers",
                "URL versioning is easier to test, debug, and understand at a glance",
                "URL versioning is faster",
                "Headers don't support version numbers"
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explanation": "URL versioning (/api/v1/chat) is visible in logs, easy to test with curl, and immediately tells you which version is being used."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Caching: The #1 Cost Saver",
            "explanation": "LLM API calls are expensive ($2.50-$15 per million tokens for GPT-4o) and slow (1-30 seconds). Caching lets you skip the API call entirely for queries you've already answered.\n\n**The 3 Levels of Caching:**\n\n**Level 1: Exact Match Cache (Redis)**\nHash the prompt + model + parameters. If you've seen this EXACT input before, return the cached response instantly. Cost: $0. Latency: <10ms.\n- Best for: FAQ-style questions, repeated queries\n- Limitation: 'What is Python?' and 'Tell me about Python' are different\n\n**Level 2: Semantic Cache (Vector Similarity)**\nConvert queries to embedding vectors. Find cached queries that are 95%+ similar.\n- 'What is Python?' and 'Can you explain Python?' hit the same cache\n- Cost: One embedding call (~$0.00002) instead of full LLM (~$0.01)\n\n**Level 3: Provider Prefix Cache**\nOpenAI/Anthropic automatically cache your system prompt prefix. 50-90% discount on cached tokens. No code changes needed.\n\n**Impact:** Multi-layer caching typically saves 40-70% on LLM costs. For an app spending $10,000/month, that's $4,000-$7,000/month saved."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "visual",
          "content": {
            "title": "Multi-Layer Caching Architecture",
            "description": "Production AI systems use multiple caching layers. A query checks each layer in order — if any layer has a cached answer, it returns immediately without calling the LLM.",
            "type": "architecture_stack",
            "layers": [
