    {
      "id": "M019-cloud-deployment",
      "title": "Module 19: Cloud & Deployment",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Why Deployment Matters: From Code to Users",
            "explanation": "You've built an incredible AI application — a chatbot that answers questions, a RAG pipeline that searches documents, an agent that books flights. It works perfectly on your laptop. But here's the brutal truth: **nobody can use it except you.**\n\n<img src=\"/images/m19_cloud/m19_deployment_overview.png\" alt=\"From Code to Production\" />\n\n**🧒 The Restaurant Analogy:**\n\nImagine you're an amazing cook. You can make the best pasta in the world — in YOUR kitchen. But if you want to feed 1,000 people, you can't invite them all to your apartment. You need a restaurant. You need a kitchen that can handle hundreds of orders simultaneously, waiters to serve customers, a menu system, health inspections, backup ingredients, and fire safety.\n\nThat's exactly what deployment is. Your laptop is your home kitchen. The cloud is your restaurant. Deployment is the process of taking your home recipe and making it work at restaurant scale.\n\n**Why Can't You Just Run Python on Your Laptop?**\n\nLet's count the problems:\n\n• **Availability** — Your laptop sleeps, restarts, loses WiFi. A production server runs 24/7/365.\n• **Scale** — Your laptop handles 1 user. Production handles 1,000+ concurrent users.\n• **Security** — Your laptop has your personal files. Production isolates your app from everything else.\n• **Reproducibility** — \"It works on my machine\" is the #1 deployment nightmare. You need identical environments everywhere.\n• **Updates** — How do you update your app without downtime? Users hate seeing \"Service Unavailable.\"\n\n**The Deployment Stack for AI Applications:**\n\n| Layer | What It Does | Tools |\n|-------|-------------|-------|\n| **Containerization** | Package your app + dependencies into a portable box | Docker, Podman |\n| **Orchestration** | Run multiple containers, auto-scale, self-heal | Kubernetes, Docker Swarm |\n| **LLM Serving** | Serve models with high throughput & low latency | vLLM, TGI, Ollama |\n| **Cloud Platform** | Provide the actual servers (compute, storage, networking) | AWS, GCP, Azure |\n| **CI/CD** | Automatically test & deploy on every code push | GitHub Actions, GitLab CI |\n| **Monitoring** | Track health, performance, errors, and costs | Prometheus, Grafana, Datadog |\n\n**🔑 Key Insight:** Deployment is NOT an afterthought. The best AI engineers think about deployment from Day 1. Your model architecture, your API design, your data pipeline — all of these are shaped by how you plan to deploy.\n\nIn this module, we'll go from \"it works on my laptop\" to \"it's running in production, serving thousands of users, automatically scaling, and you get alerts if anything breaks.\" Let's start with the foundation: environment variables and secrets management."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Environment Variables & Secrets Management",
            "explanation": "Before we deploy anything, we need to solve the #1 security mistake beginners make: **hardcoding API keys directly in their code.**\n\n**🧒 The House Key Analogy:**\n\nImagine writing your house key's cutting pattern on a sticky note and posting it on your front door. That's what hardcoding API keys looks like. Anyone who sees your code (on GitHub, in a Docker image, in logs) gets full access to your OpenAI account, your database, your users' data.\n\n**What Are Environment Variables?**\n\nEnvironment variables are key-value pairs that live OUTSIDE your code but are accessible TO your code at runtime. They're like a secure vault that your application can read from, but that isn't stored in your Git repository.\n\n```python\nimport os\n\n# ❌ NEVER DO THIS — hardcoded API key\nopenai_api_key = \"sk-proj-abc123...\"\n\n# ✅ ALWAYS DO THIS — read from environment\nopenai_api_key = os.environ[\"OPENAI_API_KEY\"]\n\n# ✅ OR with a default fallback\nopenai_api_key = os.getenv(\"OPENAI_API_KEY\", \"not-set\")\n```\n\n**The .env File — Local Development:**\n\nFor local development, you store secrets in a `.env` file that is NEVER committed to Git:\n\n```bash\n# .env (add this to .gitignore!)\nOPENAI_API_KEY=sk-proj-abc123...\nDATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:pass@localhost:5432/mydb\nREDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379\nENVIRONMENT=development\nLOG_LEVEL=debug\n```\n\n```python\n# Load .env file in Python\nfrom dotenv import load_dotenv\nimport os\n\nload_dotenv()  # Reads .env file into environment\n\napi_key = os.getenv(\"OPENAI_API_KEY\")\ndb_url = os.getenv(\"DATABASE_URL\")\n```\n\n**The Critical .gitignore Rule:**\n\n```bash\n# .gitignore — ALWAYS include these\n.env\n.env.local\n.env.production\n*.pem\n*.key\n```\n\n**Environment Variable Hierarchy in Production:**\n\n| Priority | Source | Use Case |\n|----------|--------|----------|\n| 1 (Highest) | Runtime injection (Docker, K8s) | Production secrets |\n| 2 | Cloud secrets manager (AWS SSM, GCP Secret Manager) | Sensitive credentials |\n| 3 | CI/CD variables (GitHub Secrets) | Build-time configuration |\n| 4 | .env file | Local development only |\n| 5 (Lowest) | Default values in code | Fallback configuration |\n\n**Pydantic Settings — The Professional Way:**\n\nIn production FastAPI apps, use Pydantic's BaseSettings for type-safe, validated configuration:\n\n```python\nfrom pydantic_settings import BaseSettings\n\nclass Settings(BaseSettings):\n    openai_api_key: str\n    database_url: str\n    redis_url: str = \"redis://localhost:6379\"\n    environment: str = \"development\"\n    log_level: str = \"info\"\n    max_tokens: int = 4096\n\n    class Config:\n        env_file = \".env\"\n\nsettings = Settings()\n# Now use settings.openai_api_key everywhere\n```\n\n**🔑 Key Takeaway:** Never hardcode secrets. Use environment variables for configuration, .env files for local development, and cloud secret managers for production. This is non-negotiable — a single leaked API key can cost you thousands of dollars.\n\nNow that your secrets are safe, let's package your entire application into something portable and reproducible — a Docker container."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "content": {
            "question": "You're deploying a FastAPI app that uses OpenAI's API. Where should you store the OPENAI_API_KEY for production deployment?",
            "options": [
              "Hardcode it directly in main.py for easy access",
              "Store it in a .env file and commit it to GitHub",
              "Use environment variables injected at runtime via Docker/Kubernetes secrets",
