            ],
            "correctAnswer": 2,
            "explanation": "Production secrets should NEVER be hardcoded or committed to version control. The correct approach is to inject them at runtime through Docker environment variables, Kubernetes Secrets, or a cloud secrets manager like AWS SSM or GCP Secret Manager."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is Docker? The Container Revolution",
            "explanation": "Docker is the single most important deployment tool you'll learn as an AI engineer. It solves the oldest, most frustrating problem in software: **\"It works on my machine!\"**\n\n<img src=\"/images/m19_cloud/m19_docker_containers.png\" alt=\"Docker Containers Explained\" />\n\n**🧒 The Shipping Container Analogy:**\n\nBefore shipping containers were invented in 1956, loading a cargo ship was chaos. Every item — furniture, fruit, machinery — had different shapes and sizes. Workers had to carefully pack each item individually. It took days to load a single ship, and items frequently got damaged or lost.\n\nThen Malcolm McLean invented the standardized shipping container. Now EVERYTHING goes inside the same-sized metal box. The ship doesn't care what's inside — it just stacks boxes. Trucks don't care what's inside — they just carry boxes. The entire global shipping industry was revolutionized by one simple idea: **standardize the packaging.**\n\nDocker does the exact same thing for software. Your AI application, with all its dependencies (Python 3.11, PyTorch, LangChain, CUDA drivers, model weights) — everything goes inside one standardized \"container.\" Any server that runs Docker can run your container, regardless of what operating system or libraries are installed on the host.\n\n**The Problem Docker Solves:**\n\nYour AI app needs:\n• Python 3.11 (not 3.10, not 3.12 — exactly 3.11)\n• PyTorch 2.1 with CUDA 12.1\n• LangChain 0.2.x\n• FastAPI + Uvicorn\n• A specific version of tokenizers compiled with Rust\n• Environment variables for API keys\n\nYour colleague has Python 3.10, your production server has Python 3.9, and the CI server has Python 3.12. Nothing works anywhere except your laptop.\n\n**With Docker:**\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM python:3.11-slim\n# Now EVERYONE has Python 3.11 — guaranteed\n```\n\n**Key Docker Concepts:**\n\n| Concept | What It Is | Analogy |\n|---------|-----------|---------|\n| **Dockerfile** | A recipe for building your container | A cooking recipe |\n| **Image** | The built, frozen snapshot of your app | A frozen meal ready to heat |\n| **Container** | A running instance of an image | The meal being eaten |\n| **Registry** | Where images are stored and shared | A grocery store for frozen meals |\n| **Volume** | Persistent storage that survives container restarts | A USB drive you plug in |\n\n**Docker vs Virtual Machines:**\n\n| | Docker Container | Virtual Machine |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Boot time** | Milliseconds | Minutes |\n| **Size** | MBs to low GBs | GBs to tens of GBs |\n| **Overhead** | Minimal (shares host kernel) | High (runs entire OS) |\n| **Isolation** | Process-level | Hardware-level |\n| **Use case** | Applications | Full OS environments |\n\n**🔑 Key Takeaway:** Docker guarantees that your application runs identically everywhere — your laptop, your colleague's laptop, CI/CD, staging, and production. No more \"works on my machine.\"\n\nLet's see the Docker workflow in action before we write our first Dockerfile."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "visual",
          "content": {
            "title": "Docker Workflow: From Code to Container",
            "description": "The end-to-end journey from source code to a running container accessible by users.",
            "type": "lcel_pipeline",
            "steps": [
              {
                "name": "Dockerfile",
                "example": "Source Code",
                "description": "Write the recipe that describes your environment and dependencies.",
                "outputType": "RECIPE"
              },
              {
                "name": "docker build",
                "example": "Build Process",
                "description": "Docker reads the recipe and builds an immutable image.",
                "outputType": "BUILDING"
              },
              {
                "name": "Image",
                "example": "Frozen Snapshot",
                "description": "A packaged environment containing your code, runtime, and libraries.",
                "outputType": "PACKAGE"
              },
              {
                "name": "docker run",
                "example": "Deployment",
                "description": "Starts an isolated container instance from your frozen image.",
                "outputType": "RUNTIME"
              },
              {
                "name": "Container",
                "example": "Running App",
                "description": "Your API is now running and ready to accept user traffic.",
                "outputType": "LIVE"
              }
            ],
            "codePanel": {
              "title": "Docker Commands",
              "code": "# 1. Build the image\ndocker build -t my-ai-api .\n\n# 2. Run the container\ndocker run -p 8000:8000 my-ai-api"
            },
            "typeFlowPanel": {
              "title": "The Core Loop",
              "flow": [
                "Code → Dockerfile",
                "Dockerfile → Image",
                "Image → Container"
              ]
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Writing Dockerfiles for AI Applications",
            "explanation": "A Dockerfile is a simple text file with step-by-step instructions for building your container image. Think of it as a recipe — each line is one step in setting up your environment.\n\n**The Essential Dockerfile Instructions:**\n\n• `FROM` — Choose your base image (the starting point)\n• `WORKDIR` — Set the working directory inside the container\n• `COPY` — Copy files from your machine into the container\n• `RUN` — Execute a command during the build process\n• `ENV` — Set environment variables\n• `EXPOSE` — Declare which port your app listens on\n• `CMD` — The command to run when the container starts\n\n**Your First AI Application Dockerfile:**\n\n```dockerfile\n# Step 1: Start with Python 3.11 slim (smaller than full image)\nFROM python:3.11-slim\n\n# Step 2: Set working directory\nWORKDIR /app\n\n# Step 3: Copy requirements first (Docker caching trick!)\nCOPY requirements.txt .\n\n# Step 4: Install Python dependencies\nRUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt\n\n# Step 5: Copy the rest of your application code\nCOPY . .\n\n# Step 6: Set environment variables\nENV PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1\nENV ENVIRONMENT=production\n\n# Step 7: Expose the port FastAPI runs on\nEXPOSE 8000\n\n# Step 8: Start the FastAPI server\nCMD [\"uvicorn\", \"main:app\", \"--host\", \"0.0.0.0\", \"--port\", \"8000\"]\n```\n\n**🧒 The Layer Caching Trick (This Saves You Hours):**\n\nNotice we copy `requirements.txt` BEFORE copying the rest of the code? This is a critical optimization. Docker caches each layer. If `requirements.txt` hasn't changed, Docker skips the `pip install` step entirely — saving 5-10 minutes on every build!\n\n**Build Order Rule:** Copy things that change LEAST first, things that change MOST last.\n\n1. Base image (almost never changes)\n2. System dependencies (rarely changes)\n3. Python requirements (changes occasionally)\n4. Application code (changes frequently)\n\n**Building and Running:**\n\n```bash\n# Build the image (the -t flag gives it a name)\ndocker build -t my-ai-app .\n\n# Run the container\ndocker run -p 8000:8000 --env-file .env my-ai-app\n\n# Run in background (detached mode)\ndocker run -d -p 8000:8000 --env-file .env --name my-app my-ai-app\n\n# Check running containers\ndocker ps\n\n# View logs\ndocker logs my-app\n\n# Stop the container\ndocker stop my-app\n```\n\n**Common Gotchas for AI Apps:**\n\n1. **Large image sizes** — PyTorch alone is 2GB+. Always use `--no-cache-dir` with pip.\n2. **CUDA dependencies** — If using GPU, start with `nvidia/cuda:12.1.0-runtime-ubuntu22.04` instead of `python:3.11-slim`.\n3. **Model weights** — Don't bake 7GB model files into your image. Download them at runtime or mount them as volumes.\n4. **.dockerignore** — Create a `.dockerignore` file to exclude `__pycache__`, `.env`, `.git`, `venv/`, and model files from the build context.\n\n**🔑 Key Takeaway:** A Dockerfile is your deployment recipe. Master the layer caching trick (copy requirements before code) and you'll save hours of build time. Now let's practice writing one."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "dockerfile",
            "title": "Write a Dockerfile for a FastAPI AI App",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Complete the Dockerfile below for a FastAPI AI application.\n1. Use `python:3.11-slim` as the base image.\n2. Set the working directory to `/app`.\n3. Copy `requirements.txt` and install dependencies with `--no-cache-dir`.\n4. Copy the rest of the application code.\n5. Set `PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1` as an environment variable.\n6. Expose port 8000.\n7. Set the startup command to run `uvicorn main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000`.",
            "starterCode": "# Step 1: Base image\\n___\\n\\n# Step 2: Working directory\\n___\\n\\n# Step 3: Copy and install requirements\\n___\\n___\\n\\n# Step 4: Copy application code\\n___\\n\\n# Step 5: Environment variable\\n___\\n\\n# Step 6: Expose port\\n___\\n\\n# Step 7: Startup command\\n___",
            "solution": "FROM python:3.11-slim\\n\\nWORKDIR /app\\n\\nCOPY requirements.txt .\\nRUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt\\n\\nCOPY . .\\n\\nENV PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1\\n\\nEXPOSE 8000\\n\\nCMD [\"uvicorn\", \"main:app\", \"--host\", \"0.0.0.0\", \"--port\", \"8000\"]",
            "hints": [
              "Use FROM to specify the base image",
              "WORKDIR sets the directory for subsequent commands",
              "COPY copies files, RUN executes commands",
              "CMD takes a JSON array of command parts"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
