        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Multi-Stage Builds & CUDA Optimization for AI",
            "explanation": "AI Docker images have a unique problem: they're **massive**. A typical PyTorch + Transformers image can be 10-15GB. That's because you need CUDA drivers, cuDNN, PyTorch, and the entire Hugging Face ecosystem. Let's learn how to shrink these images dramatically.\n\n<img src=\"/images/m19_cloud/m19_multistage_build.png\" alt=\"Multi-Stage Docker Builds\" />\n\n**🧒 The Moving Day Analogy:**\n\nWhen you move apartments, you pack EVERYTHING into boxes — tools, wrapping paper, tape, scissors, labels. But you don't unpack the tools and wrapping paper at your new apartment. You only unpack what you actually need to LIVE there.\n\nMulti-stage builds work the same way. Stage 1 is the \"packing stage\" — you install compilers, build tools, and everything needed to BUILD your application. Stage 2 is the \"living stage\" — you copy ONLY the finished, built artifacts into a clean, minimal image.\n\n**Multi-Stage Dockerfile for AI:**\n\n```dockerfile\n# ============ Stage 1: Builder ============\nFROM python:3.11-slim AS builder\n\nWORKDIR /app\n\n# Install build dependencies\nRUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y \\\n    build-essential \\\n    gcc \\\n    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*\n\nCOPY requirements.txt .\nRUN pip install --no-cache-dir --prefix=/install -r requirements.txt\n\n# ============ Stage 2: Runtime ============\nFROM python:3.11-slim AS runtime\n\nWORKDIR /app\n\n# Copy ONLY the installed packages from builder\nCOPY --from=builder /install /usr/local\n\n# Copy application code\nCOPY . .\n\nENV PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1\nEXPOSE 8000\n\nCMD [\"uvicorn\", \"main:app\", \"--host\", \"0.0.0.0\", \"--port\", \"8000\"]\n```\n\n**Result:** The builder stage might be 3GB. The runtime stage is only 800MB. All the build tools (gcc, make, etc.) are left behind.\n\n**GPU-Enabled Dockerfile (NVIDIA CUDA):**\n\nIf your AI app needs GPU access for inference (e.g., running a local LLM):\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM nvidia/cuda:12.1.0-runtime-ubuntu22.04\n\n# Install Python\nRUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y \\\n    python3.11 python3-pip \\\n    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*\n\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY requirements.txt .\nRUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt\n\nCOPY . .\n\n# Run with GPU access: docker run --gpus all ...\nCMD [\"uvicorn\", \"main:app\", \"--host\", \"0.0.0.0\", \"--port\", \"8000\"]\n```\n\n**Image Size Optimization Checklist:**\n\n| Technique | Size Reduction | Difficulty |\n|-----------|---------------|------------|\n| Use `slim` or `alpine` base images | 30-50% | Easy |\n| Multi-stage builds | 50-70% | Medium |\n| `--no-cache-dir` on pip install | 10-20% | Easy |\n| Remove apt cache (`rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*`) | 5-10% | Easy |\n| Use `.dockerignore` | Varies | Easy |\n| Pin exact dependency versions | Prevents bloat | Easy |\n\n**🔑 Key Takeaway:** Always use multi-stage builds for production AI images. Your build stage can be as messy as needed — compilers, build tools, everything. Your runtime stage should be surgical — only what's needed to RUN the application.\n\nNow let's scale up — what happens when your AI app needs multiple services working together?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "dockerfile",
            "title": "Optimize an AI Container Image",
            "difficulty": "Hard",
            "question": "Write a multi-stage Dockerfile that:\n1. Uses a builder stage based on `python:3.11-slim`\n2. Installs dependencies from requirements.txt with `--no-cache-dir` and `--prefix=/install`\n3. Uses a clean runtime stage (also `python:3.11-slim`)\n4. Copies only the installed packages from the builder\n5. Copies the app code and starts uvicorn",
            "starterCode": "# Stage 1: Builder\\n___\\n\\nWORKDIR /build\\nCOPY requirements.txt .\\n___\\n\\n# Stage 2: Runtime\\n___\\n\\nWORKDIR /app\\n___\\nCOPY . .\\n\\nCMD [\"uvicorn\", \"main:app\", \"--host\", \"0.0.0.0\", \"--port\", \"8000\"]",
            "solution": "FROM python:3.11-slim AS builder\\n\\nWORKDIR /build\\nCOPY requirements.txt .\\nRUN pip install --no-cache-dir --prefix=/install -r requirements.txt\\n\\nFROM python:3.11-slim AS runtime\\n\\nWORKDIR /app\\nCOPY --from=builder /install /usr/local\\nCOPY . .\\n\\nCMD [\"uvicorn\", \"main:app\", \"--host\", \"0.0.0.0\", \"--port\", \"8000\"]",
            "hints": [
              "Use 'AS builder' to name the first stage",
              "Use '--prefix=/install' to install packages to a specific directory",
              "Use 'COPY --from=builder' to copy from the first stage"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Docker Compose: Running Multi-Container AI Stacks",
            "explanation": "Real AI applications aren't just a single container. They're a collection of services working together: your FastAPI backend, a PostgreSQL database, a Redis cache for session management, a vector database for RAG, and maybe a Celery worker for async tasks.\n\nDocker Compose lets you define and run ALL of these services with a single command: `docker compose up`.\n\n**🧒 The Band Analogy:**\n\nA solo musician can play a song, but a band creates a symphony. Docker Compose is the conductor — it tells the drummer (database) to start on beat 1, the bassist (cache) to come in on beat 2, and the vocalist (your API) to start singing on beat 4. Everyone starts in the right order, plays on the right channels, and can hear each other.\n\n**Complete AI Stack with Docker Compose:**\n\n```yaml\n# docker-compose.yml\nversion: \"3.9\"\n\nservices:\n  # Your FastAPI AI Backend\n  api:\n    build: .\n    ports:\n      - \"8000:8000\"\n    environment:\n      - DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:pass@db:5432/mydb\n      - REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379\n      - OPENAI_API_KEY=${OPENAI_API_KEY}\n    depends_on:\n      - db\n      - redis\n    restart: unless-stopped\n\n  # PostgreSQL Database\n  db:\n    image: postgres:16-alpine\n    environment:\n      POSTGRES_USER: user\n      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: pass\n      POSTGRES_DB: mydb\n    volumes:\n      - postgres_data:/var/lib/postgresql/data\n    ports:\n      - \"5432:5432\"\n\n  # Redis Cache\n  redis:\n    image: redis:7-alpine\n    ports:\n      - \"6379:6379\"\n    volumes:\n      - redis_data:/data\n\n  # Qdrant Vector Database (for RAG)\n  qdrant:\n    image: qdrant/qdrant:latest\n    ports:\n      - \"6333:6333\"\n    volumes:\n      - qdrant_data:/qdrant/storage\n\nvolumes:\n  postgres_data:\n  redis_data:\n  qdrant_data:\n```\n\n**Essential Docker Compose Commands:**\n\n```bash\n# Start all services\ndocker compose up -d\n\n# View logs\ndocker compose logs -f api\n\n# Stop all services\ndocker compose down\n\n# Rebuild after code changes\ndocker compose up -d --build\n\n# Scale a specific service\ndocker compose up -d --scale api=3\n```\n\n**Key Features:**\n\n• **depends_on** — Controls startup order (db starts before api)\n• **volumes** — Data persists even when containers restart\n• **environment** — Inject secrets from `.env` file using `${VAR}` syntax\n• **restart: unless-stopped** — Automatically restart crashed containers\n• **networks** — Services can reach each other by name (e.g., `db`, `redis`)\n\n**🔑 Key Takeaway:** Docker Compose is your local development powerhouse. It lets you run your entire AI stack — API, database, cache, vector store — with one command. It's also the stepping stone to Kubernetes, which does the same thing at massive scale.\n\nNow let's practice writing a Docker Compose file."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "yaml",
            "title": "Write a Docker Compose for an AI Stack",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Complete the docker-compose.yml to run a FastAPI app with a PostgreSQL database.\n1. The 'api' service should build from the current directory, map port 8000, and depend on 'db'\n2. The 'db' service should use postgres:16-alpine, set POSTGRES_DB to 'aiapp', and use a volume for data persistence",
            "starterCode": "version: '3.9'\\n\\nservices:\\n  api:\\n    ___\\n    ports:\\n      - '8000:8000'\\n    depends_on:\\n      - ___\\n\\n  db:\\n    image: ___\\n    environment:\\n      POSTGRES_DB: ___\\n    volumes:\\n      - db_data:/var/lib/postgresql/data\\n\\nvolumes:\\n  db_data:",
            "solution": "version: '3.9'\\n\\nservices:\\n  api:\\n    build: .\\n    ports:\\n      - '8000:8000'\\n    depends_on:\\n      - db\\n\\n  db:\\n    image: postgres:16-alpine\\n    environment:\\n      POSTGRES_DB: aiapp\\n    volumes:\\n      - db_data:/var/lib/postgresql/data\\n\\nvolumes:\\n  db_data:",
            "hints": [
              "Use 'build: .' to build from the current directory",
              "depends_on takes a list of service names",
              "postgres:16-alpine is a lightweight PostgreSQL image"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "content": {
            "question": "Why should you copy requirements.txt BEFORE copying your application code in a Dockerfile?",
            "options": [
              "Because requirements.txt must be processed first alphabetically",
              "Because Docker caches each layer — if requirements.txt hasn't changed, pip install is skipped on subsequent builds",
              "Because Docker can only process one COPY command per build",
