        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is Kubernetes? Container Orchestration Explained",
            "explanation": "Docker runs one container on one machine. But what happens when your AI chatbot gets popular and one server can't handle all the traffic? You need more containers, on more servers, with automatic load balancing, self-healing, and zero-downtime deployments. That's what Kubernetes does.\n\n<img src=\"/images/m19_cloud/m19_kubernetes_overview.png\" alt=\"Kubernetes Architecture Overview\" />\n\n**🧒 The Air Traffic Control Analogy:**\n\nImagine an airport. Docker is like having one airplane. You can fly passengers from A to B. But what happens during holiday season when demand triples? You need MORE planes, MORE gates, MORE runways, and someone to coordinate everything — preventing collisions, rerouting flights when weather is bad, and adding extra flights when demand spikes.\n\nKubernetes is your air traffic controller. It decides:\n• How many \"planes\" (containers) to run\n• Where to land them (which servers)\n• How to reroute traffic when one plane breaks down\n• When to add more planes during peak hours\n• When to ground idle planes to save fuel (money)\n\n**Why Kubernetes for AI?**\n\nAI workloads are uniquely challenging:\n• **Variable demand** — Your chatbot might get 10 requests/minute at night and 10,000/minute during a product launch\n• **Heavy compute** — Each LLM inference request uses significant CPU/GPU\n• **Stateful components** — Vector databases, model caches, and conversation histories need persistent storage\n• **Mixed workloads** — Some services need GPUs, others just need CPUs\n\n**The Core Kubernetes Architecture:**\n\n| Component | Role | Analogy |\n|-----------|------|---------|\n| **Control Plane** | The brain that makes decisions | Airport control tower |\n| **API Server** | The single entry point for all commands | Front desk / reception |\n| **Scheduler** | Decides WHERE to run new pods | Gate assignment system |\n| **Controller Manager** | Ensures desired state matches actual state | Quality control inspector |\n| **etcd** | Stores all cluster state and configuration | Airport database |\n| **Worker Nodes** | The actual machines running your containers | Airport terminals |\n| **kubelet** | Agent on each node that manages pods | Gate agent at each terminal |\n\n**What Kubernetes Does Automatically:**\n\n1. **Self-healing** — If a container crashes, K8s restarts it immediately\n2. **Auto-scaling** — Spins up more containers when CPU/memory usage spikes\n3. **Load balancing** — Distributes traffic evenly across all containers\n4. **Rolling updates** — Deploys new versions with zero downtime\n5. **Secret management** — Securely injects API keys and credentials\n6. **Resource limits** — Prevents one service from hogging all the CPU/GPU\n\n**🔑 Key Takeaway:** Docker packages your app. Kubernetes runs it at scale. Docker is the container. Kubernetes is the orchestra conductor. You need both for production AI systems.\n\nLet's visualize how all these components connect."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "visual",
          "content": {
