        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Kubernetes Core Primitives: Pods, Deployments & Services",
            "explanation": "Kubernetes has its own vocabulary. Let's learn the three most important building blocks you'll use daily.\n\n**Pod — The Smallest Unit:**\n\nA Pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes. It wraps one or more containers that share the same network and storage. In practice, most pods run a single container.\n\n```yaml\n# pod.yaml — You rarely create pods directly\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata:\n  name: ai-api\nspec:\n  containers:\n    - name: fastapi\n      image: myregistry/ai-api:v1.2\n      ports:\n        - containerPort: 8000\n      resources:\n        requests:\n          memory: \"512Mi\"\n          cpu: \"250m\"\n        limits:\n          memory: \"1Gi\"\n          cpu: \"500m\"\n```\n\n**Deployment — The Real Workhorse:**\n\nA Deployment manages a set of identical pods. It handles:\n• Creating the right NUMBER of pods (replicas)\n• Rolling updates (deploy new version → gradually replace old pods)\n• Rollbacks (if new version breaks → instantly revert)\n\n```yaml\n# deployment.yaml — This is what you'll use 99% of the time\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: ai-api-deployment\nspec:\n  replicas: 3    # Run 3 identical pods\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: ai-api\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: ai-api\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: fastapi\n          image: myregistry/ai-api:v1.2\n          ports:\n            - containerPort: 8000\n          env:\n            - name: OPENAI_API_KEY\n              valueFrom:\n                secretKeyRef:\n                  name: ai-secrets\n                  key: openai-key\n          resources:\n            requests:\n              memory: \"512Mi\"\n              cpu: \"500m\"\n            limits:\n              memory: \"2Gi\"\n              cpu: \"1000m\"\n```\n\n**Service — The Load Balancer:**\n\nPods are ephemeral — they come and go. A Service provides a stable endpoint that routes traffic to the right pods, even as pods are created and destroyed.\n\n```yaml\n# service.yaml\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  name: ai-api-service\nspec:\n  selector:\n    app: ai-api    # Routes to pods with this label\n  ports:\n    - port: 80          # External port\n      targetPort: 8000  # Container port\n  type: LoadBalancer    # Exposes externally\n```\n\n**How They Work Together:**\n\n```\nUsers → Ingress → Service → Pod 1 (container)\n                          → Pod 2 (container)\n                          → Pod 3 (container)\n```\n\n**Essential kubectl Commands:**\n\n```bash\n# Apply a configuration\nkubectl apply -f deployment.yaml\n\n# Get running pods\nkubectl get pods\n\n# Get services\nkubectl get services\n\n# Scale up/down\nkubectl scale deployment ai-api-deployment --replicas=5\n\n# View logs\nkubectl logs -f deployment/ai-api-deployment\n\n# Rollback to previous version\nkubectl rollout undo deployment/ai-api-deployment\n```\n\n**🔑 Key Takeaway:** Pod = one container instance. Deployment = manages N replica pods with rolling updates. Service = stable endpoint that load-balances across pods. These three primitives handle 90% of your Kubernetes needs.\n\nNow let's learn how Kubernetes automatically scales your AI application based on demand."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Horizontal Pod Autoscaling: Scaling AI on Demand",
            "explanation": "Your AI chatbot gets 50 requests/minute during the day and 5,000 requests/minute when it goes viral on Twitter. Running 50 pods 24/7 is wasteful. Running 3 pods during a traffic spike crashes your service. You need automatic scaling.\n\n**🧒 The Uber Driver Analogy:**\n\nUber doesn't hire 10,000 full-time drivers and have them wait around at 3 AM. During Friday night rush, more drivers come online. At 4 AM, most go home. The system scales based on demand — exactly what HPA does for your pods.\n\n**Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA):**\n\nHPA watches your pods' metrics (CPU, memory, or custom metrics) and automatically adjusts the number of replicas.\n\n```yaml\n# hpa.yaml\napiVersion: autoscaling/v2\nkind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler\nmetadata:\n  name: ai-api-hpa\nspec:\n  scaleTargetRef:\n    apiVersion: apps/v1\n    kind: Deployment\n    name: ai-api-deployment\n  minReplicas: 2        # Never go below 2 (redundancy)\n  maxReplicas: 20       # Never exceed 20 (cost control)\n  metrics:\n    - type: Resource\n      resource:\n        name: cpu\n        target:\n          type: Utilization\n          averageUtilization: 70   # Scale up when CPU > 70%\n    - type: Resource\n      resource:\n        name: memory\n        target:\n          type: Utilization\n          averageUtilization: 80   # Scale up when memory > 80%\n```\n\n**How HPA Works:**\n\n1. HPA checks metrics every 15 seconds (configurable)\n2. If average CPU across all pods > 70%, it adds more pods\n3. If average CPU drops < 70%, it removes pods (with a cooldown period)\n4. It respects `minReplicas` and `maxReplicas` bounds\n\n**Scaling Formula:**\n\n```\ndesiredReplicas = ceil(currentReplicas × (currentMetricValue / desiredMetricValue))\n```\n\nExample: 3 pods at 90% CPU, target is 70%:\n```\ndesiredReplicas = ceil(3 × (90 / 70)) = ceil(3.86) = 4 pods\n```\n\n**AI-Specific Scaling Tips:**\n\n| Metric | When to Use | Example |\n|--------|------------|---------|\n| CPU Utilization | General API scaling | FastAPI endpoints |\n| Memory Utilization | Model loading & embeddings | Vector search services |\n| Custom Metrics (requests/sec) | Traffic-based scaling | High-throughput chat APIs |\n| GPU Utilization | GPU inference scaling | Self-hosted LLM serving |\n\n**🔑 Key Takeaway:** HPA lets your AI application automatically scale from 2 pods during quiet hours to 20 pods during traffic spikes — then scale back down to save money. Set `minReplicas` for redundancy and `maxReplicas` for cost control.\n\nLet's practice writing a Kubernetes deployment manifest."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "yaml",
            "title": "Write a Kubernetes Deployment Manifest",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Complete the Kubernetes Deployment manifest for an AI API:\n1. Set replicas to 3\n2. Use the image 'myregistry/ai-api:latest'\n3. Set containerPort to 8000\n4. Set CPU request to '500m' and limit to '1000m'\n5. Set memory request to '512Mi' and limit to '2Gi'",
            "starterCode": "apiVersion: apps/v1\\nkind: Deployment\\nmetadata:\\n  name: ai-api\\nspec:\\n  replicas: ___\\n  selector:\\n    matchLabels:\\n      app: ai-api\\n  template:\\n    metadata:\\n      labels:\\n        app: ai-api\\n    spec:\\n      containers:\\n        - name: api\\n          image: ___\\n          ports:\\n            - containerPort: ___\\n          resources:\\n            requests:\\n              cpu: ___\\n              memory: ___\\n            limits:\\n              cpu: ___\\n              memory: ___",
            "solution": "apiVersion: apps/v1\\nkind: Deployment\\nmetadata:\\n  name: ai-api\\nspec:\\n  replicas: 3\\n  selector:\\n    matchLabels:\\n      app: ai-api\\n  template:\\n    metadata:\\n      labels:\\n        app: ai-api\\n    spec:\\n      containers:\\n        - name: api\\n          image: myregistry/ai-api:latest\\n          ports:\\n            - containerPort: 8000\\n          resources:\\n            requests:\\n              cpu: '500m'\\n              memory: '512Mi'\\n            limits:\\n              cpu: '1000m'\\n              memory: '2Gi'",
            "hints": [
              "replicas controls how many pod copies run",
              "Image format is registry/name:tag",
              "CPU is measured in millicores (m), memory in Mi/Gi"
            ]
          }
        },
