          "type": "mcq",
          "content": {
            "question": "Your AI chatbot deployment has 3 pods running. Pod 2 crashes due to an out-of-memory error. What does Kubernetes do automatically?",
            "options": [
              "Shuts down all remaining pods and sends you an alert",
              "Redirects all traffic to Pod 1 and Pod 3, and restarts Pod 2 automatically",
              "Scales up to 10 pods to compensate for the lost capacity",
              "Does nothing — you need to manually restart the pod"
            ],
            "correctAnswer": 1,
            "explanation": "Kubernetes self-healing automatically detects the crashed pod, removes it from the Service's routing table (so no traffic goes to it), and restarts it. Meanwhile, Pod 1 and Pod 3 continue serving requests. This happens within seconds, usually without any user impact."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "content": {
            "title": "AI Application Scaling Decision",
            "scenario": "Your AI document analysis service runs on 3 pods. During business hours (9 AM - 6 PM), CPU usage averages 80%. At night, it drops to 10%. Each pod costs $0.15/hour. You're spending $32.40/day on compute. Your CTO asks you to optimize costs without affecting daytime performance.",
            "options": [
              {
                "text": "Manually scale to 1 pod at 6 PM and back to 3 pods at 9 AM using a cron job",
                "feedback": "This works but is fragile — what about weekends, holidays, or unexpected traffic spikes at night? Manual scaling doesn't adapt to real demand.",
                "isCorrect": false
              },
              {
                "text": "Configure HPA with minReplicas=1, maxReplicas=5, and CPU target of 70%. Let Kubernetes auto-scale based on actual demand",
                "feedback": "Perfect! HPA dynamically adjusts pods based on real-time CPU usage. During the day, it scales to 3-4 pods. At night, it drops to 1. If there's an unexpected spike, it can scale to 5. Cost drops from $32.40/day to ~$15/day while maintaining performance.",
                "isCorrect": true
              },
              {
                "text": "Keep 3 pods running 24/7 but use smaller, cheaper instances",
                "feedback": "Smaller instances might not handle peak load, and you're still wasting resources at night. Auto-scaling is the correct solution for variable workloads.",
                "isCorrect": false
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Helm Charts & GitOps for AI Deployments",
            "explanation": "As your Kubernetes setup grows, you'll have dozens of YAML files — Deployments, Services, HPAs, ConfigMaps, Secrets, Ingresses. Managing them manually becomes a nightmare. Enter Helm and GitOps.\n\n**Helm — The Package Manager for Kubernetes:**\n\nHelm is like `pip` for Kubernetes. Instead of writing 10 separate YAML files, you create a Helm \"chart\" — a template package that can be installed with one command.\n\n```bash\n# Install a complete monitoring stack with one command\nhelm install prometheus prometheus-community/kube-prometheus-stack\n\n# Install your AI app with custom values\nhelm install my-ai-app ./charts/ai-api \\\n  --set replicas=3 \\\n  --set image.tag=v1.5 \\\n  --set env.OPENAI_API_KEY=$OPENAI_API_KEY\n\n# Upgrade to a new version\nhelm upgrade my-ai-app ./charts/ai-api --set image.tag=v1.6\n\n# Rollback if something breaks\nhelm rollback my-ai-app 1\n```\n\n**Helm Chart Structure:**\n\n```\nmy-ai-chart/\n├── Chart.yaml          # Chart metadata (name, version)\n├── values.yaml         # Default configuration values\n├── templates/\n│   ├── deployment.yaml # Kubernetes Deployment template\n│   ├── service.yaml    # Service template\n│   ├── hpa.yaml        # Autoscaler template\n│   └── ingress.yaml    # Ingress template\n```\n\n**GitOps — Git as the Source of Truth:**\n\nGitOps is a methodology where your Git repository is the single source of truth for your infrastructure. Tools like ArgoCD or Flux watch your Git repo and automatically apply changes to your Kubernetes cluster.\n\n**The GitOps Workflow:**\n\n1. Developer pushes code changes to GitHub\n2. CI pipeline builds a new Docker image and pushes to registry\n3. CI updates the Helm chart's `values.yaml` with the new image tag\n4. ArgoCD detects the Git change and automatically deploys to Kubernetes\n5. If something breaks, `git revert` rolls back the infrastructure\n\n**Why GitOps for AI Teams?**\n\n• **Audit trail** — Every deployment is a Git commit with author, timestamp, and diff\n• **Rollback** — `git revert` instantly rolls back infrastructure\n• **Review process** — Deployments go through pull request reviews\n• **Reproducibility** — Any team member can recreate the entire infrastructure from Git\n\n**🔑 Key Takeaway:** Helm packages your Kubernetes configs into reusable charts. GitOps ensures your infrastructure is always in sync with your Git repository. Together, they make Kubernetes manageable at scale.\n\nNow let's test your Kubernetes knowledge before moving on to LLM-specific serving infrastructure."
          }
        },
