        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Production LLM Serving: vLLM & Continuous Batching",
            "explanation": "You've built an AI app that calls OpenAI's API. That's fine for prototyping. But what happens when you want to run your OWN model — Llama 3, Mistral, or a fine-tuned model? You can't just load it in Python and call `model.generate()`. That approach handles one request at a time and wastes 90% of your GPU.\n\n<img src=\"/images/m19_cloud/m19_vllm_architecture.png\" alt=\"vLLM Architecture\" />\n\n**🧒 The Restaurant Kitchen Analogy:**\n\nImagine a restaurant where the chef makes one dish at a time. Customer 1 orders pasta — chef makes pasta while 49 other customers wait. Customer 2 orders salad — chef starts only after pasta is done. This is how naive LLM inference works.\n\nNow imagine the chef prepares multiple dishes simultaneously — pasta boils while salad is being chopped while soup simmers. That's **continuous batching** — the core innovation that makes production LLM serving practical.\n\n**Why You Need a Serving Engine:**\n\n| Approach | Throughput | Latency | GPU Utilization |\n|----------|-----------|---------|-----------------|\n| Raw Python (`model.generate()`) | 1-5 req/sec | High | 10-20% |\n| vLLM | 50-500 req/sec | Low | 80-95% |\n| TGI (Text Generation Inference) | 30-300 req/sec | Low | 70-90% |\n\n**vLLM — The Gold Standard:**\n\nvLLM (Virtual Large Language Model) is the most popular open-source LLM serving engine. Its killer feature is **PagedAttention**, which manages GPU memory like an operating system manages RAM.\n\n```bash\n# Install vLLM\npip install vllm\n\n# Start a vLLM server (OpenAI-compatible API!)\npython -m vllm.entrypoints.openai.api_server \\\n    --model meta-llama/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct \\\n    --port 8000 \\\n    --max-model-len 4096\n\n# Now use it exactly like OpenAI's API:\ncurl http://localhost:8000/v1/chat/completions \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\n    \"model\": \"meta-llama/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct\",\n    \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Hello!\"}],\n    \"max_tokens\": 256\n  }'\n```\n\n**The Magic of Continuous Batching:**\n\nTraditional batching waits until a batch is full, then processes everything together. If Request 1 needs 10 tokens and Request 2 needs 500 tokens, Request 1 waits for Request 2 to finish.\n\nContinuous batching is smarter: as soon as Request 1 finishes generating its 10 tokens, a NEW request immediately takes its slot in the batch. The GPU is never idle.\n\n**TGI — Hugging Face's Serving Engine:**\n\n```bash\n# Run TGI with Docker (the easiest way)\ndocker run --gpus all -p 8080:80 \\\n  ghcr.io/huggingface/text-generation-inference:latest \\\n  --model-id meta-llama/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct \\\n  --max-input-length 2048 \\\n  --max-total-tokens 4096\n```\n\n**vLLM vs TGI Comparison:**\n\n| Feature | vLLM | TGI |\n|---------|------|-----|\n| PagedAttention | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes (v2) |\n| OpenAI-compatible API | ✅ Native | ❌ Custom API |\n| Speculative decoding | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes |\n| Multi-GPU (Tensor Parallel) | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes |\n| Production readiness | ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ | ⭐⭐⭐⭐ |\n| Community & ecosystem | Larger | Hugging Face backed |\n\n**🔑 Key Takeaway:** Never serve LLMs with raw Python in production. Use vLLM for its OpenAI-compatible API and superior PagedAttention memory management. It can increase your throughput 10-50x with the same GPU hardware.\n\nLet's dive deeper into the GPU memory math that makes this work."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "VRAM Math, KV Cache & PagedAttention",
            "explanation": "Understanding GPU memory (VRAM) is the most important skill for deploying AI models in production. If you don't understand VRAM math, you'll either run out of memory (crash) or waste expensive GPU resources.\n\n**How Much VRAM Does a Model Need?**\n\nThe formula is simple:\n\n```\nVRAM (GB) ≈ Parameters (B) × Bytes per Parameter\n```\n\n| Model | Parameters | FP16 (2 bytes) | INT8 (1 byte) | INT4 (0.5 bytes) |\n|-------|-----------|---------------|--------------|-----------------|\n| Llama 3.2 1B | 1B | 2 GB | 1 GB | 0.5 GB |\n| Llama 3.2 3B | 3B | 6 GB | 3 GB | 1.5 GB |\n| Mistral 7B | 7B | 14 GB | 7 GB | 3.5 GB |\n| Llama 3.1 70B | 70B | 140 GB | 70 GB | 35 GB |\n\n**But wait — that's just the MODEL weights!** You also need VRAM for:\n\n1. **KV Cache** — Stores attention keys and values for each token in each layer\n2. **Activations** — Intermediate computation results\n3. **CUDA overhead** — GPU driver and framework memory\n\n**The KV Cache Problem:**\n\nEvery token your model processes creates a Key and Value vector that must be stored for future attention calculations. For a 7B model with 32 layers and 4096 hidden dimensions:\n\n```\nKV Cache per token ≈ 2 × num_layers × hidden_dim × 2 bytes (FP16)\n                   ≈ 2 × 32 × 4096 × 2\n                   ≈ 512 KB per token\n```\n\nFor a context window of 4096 tokens × 10 concurrent users:\n```\nKV Cache = 512 KB × 4096 tokens × 10 users ≈ 20 GB!\n```\n\nThat's 20 GB of VRAM JUST for the KV cache, on top of 14 GB for model weights. Your 24 GB GPU can only handle ~5 concurrent users!\n\n**PagedAttention — vLLM's Breakthrough:**\n\nTraditional KV cache allocates memory contiguously — like assigning each person a fixed-size desk, even if they only use half of it. Wasted space everywhere.\n\nPagedAttention splits KV cache into small \"pages\" (like OS virtual memory). Pages are allocated on demand and can be non-contiguous. This reduces memory waste from 60-80% to less than 4%.\n\n**Practical VRAM Planning Table:**\n\n| GPU | VRAM | Max Model (FP16) | Max Model (INT4) | Concurrent Users (4K ctx) |\n|-----|------|------------------|-------------------|--------------------------|\n| RTX 4060 | 8 GB | 3B | 7B | 2-5 |\n| RTX 4090 | 24 GB | 7B | 13B | 10-30 |\n| A10G | 24 GB | 7B | 13B | 15-40 |\n| A100 40GB | 40 GB | 13B | 30B | 30-80 |\n| A100 80GB | 80 GB | 30B | 70B | 50-150 |\n| H100 | 80 GB | 30B | 70B | 100-300 |\n\n**🔑 Key Takeaway:** VRAM = Model Weights + KV Cache + Overhead. The KV cache scales with context length × concurrent users, making it the biggest memory consumer at scale. PagedAttention (vLLM) reduces KV cache waste by 90%+, dramatically increasing concurrent capacity.\n\nNow let's look at a more cost-effective approach — serverless GPUs."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Configure vLLM for Production Serving",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Write a Python script that starts a vLLM OpenAI-compatible server with:\n1. Model: 'meta-llama/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct'\n2. Tensor parallel size of 1 (single GPU)\n3. Max model length of 4096 tokens\n4. GPU memory utilization of 0.9 (90%)\n5. Then make a test request using the OpenAI client",
            "starterCode": "from vllm import LLM, SamplingParams\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\n# Configure the LLM engine\nllm = LLM(\n    model=___,\n    tensor_parallel_size=___,\n    max_model_len=___,\n    gpu_memory_utilization=___,\n)\n\n# Test with a simple generation\nsampling_params = SamplingParams(temperature=0.7, max_tokens=256)\nprompts = [\"Explain Docker in one sentence.\"]\noutputs = llm.generate(prompts, sampling_params)\n\nfor output in outputs:\n    print(output.outputs[0].text)",
            "solution": "from vllm import LLM, SamplingParams\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\n# Configure the LLM engine\nllm = LLM(\n    model=\"meta-llama/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct\",\n    tensor_parallel_size=1,\n    max_model_len=4096,\n    gpu_memory_utilization=0.9,\n)\n\n# Test with a simple generation\nsampling_params = SamplingParams(temperature=0.7, max_tokens=256)\nprompts = [\"Explain Docker in one sentence.\"]\noutputs = llm.generate(prompts, sampling_params)\n\nfor output in outputs:\n    print(output.outputs[0].text)",
