          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Serverless GPU Endpoints: Modal Labs & RunPod",
            "explanation": "Running a GPU server 24/7 costs $1,000-$5,000/month. But what if your AI app only gets 100 requests per day? That's 99.9% waste. Serverless GPU platforms solve this by charging you ONLY when your model is actually processing a request.\n\n<img src=\"/images/m19_cloud/m19_serverless_gpu.png\" alt=\"Serverless GPU Deployment\" />\n\n**🧒 The Taxi vs Car Ownership Analogy:**\n\nOwning a car (always-on GPU) means paying insurance, maintenance, and parking 24/7 — even if you only drive 30 minutes a day. Taking a taxi (serverless GPU) means you pay ONLY for the ride. For occasional use, taxis are dramatically cheaper.\n\n**The Cost Math:**\n\n| Approach | Monthly Cost | When It Makes Sense |\n|----------|-------------|-------------------|\n| Always-on A10G GPU | ~$750/month | 1,000+ requests/hour consistently |\n| Serverless GPU (Modal) | ~$30-150/month | Variable traffic, < 500 req/hour |\n| OpenAI API | Pay per token | Prototyping, low volume |\n\n**Modal Labs — The Python-Native Platform:**\n\nModal is the most developer-friendly serverless GPU platform. You write normal Python functions with a decorator, and Modal handles everything else — container building, GPU allocation, auto-scaling, and cold start optimization.\n\n```python\nimport modal\n\n# Define your app\napp = modal.App(\"my-ai-service\")\n\n# Create a container image with dependencies\nimage = modal.Image.debian_slim().pip_install(\n    \"vllm\", \"torch\", \"transformers\"\n)\n\n@app.function(\n    image=image,\n    gpu=\"A10G\",                    # GPU type\n    timeout=300,                    # Max 5 min per request\n    container_idle_timeout=120,     # Keep warm for 2 min\n    allow_concurrent_inputs=10,     # Handle 10 requests at once\n)\ndef generate(prompt: str) -> str:\n    from vllm import LLM, SamplingParams\n    \n    llm = LLM(model=\"meta-llama/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct\")\n    params = SamplingParams(temperature=0.7, max_tokens=256)\n    output = llm.generate([prompt], params)\n    return output[0].outputs[0].text\n\n# Deploy as a web endpoint\n@app.function(image=image, gpu=\"A10G\")\n@modal.web_endpoint()\ndef chat(prompt: str):\n    return {\"response\": generate.remote(prompt)}\n```\n\n```bash\n# Deploy with one command\nmodal deploy my_app.py\n\n# Your API is live at:\n# https://your-name--my-ai-service-chat.modal.run\n```\n\n**RunPod Serverless — The GPU Cloud:**\n\nRunPod offers serverless GPU endpoints with more GPU options (A100, H100) and lower per-second pricing:\n\n```python\nimport runpod\n\ndef handler(event):\n    prompt = event[\"input\"][\"prompt\"]\n    # Your inference logic here\n    return {\"output\": \"Generated response...\"}\n\nrunpod.serverless.start({\"handler\": handler})\n```\n\n**Serverless GPU Platform Comparison:**\n\n| Feature | Modal Labs | RunPod | Baseten |\n|---------|-----------|--------|---------|\n| Cold start | 2-5 sec | 5-15 sec | 3-8 sec |\n| GPU options | T4, A10G, A100, H100 | T4 to H100 | T4, A10G, A100 |\n| Pricing model | Per-second | Per-second | Per-second |\n| Developer experience | ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ | ⭐⭐⭐ | ⭐⭐⭐⭐ |\n| Custom containers | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |\n| Web endpoints | ✅ Built-in | ✅ | ✅ |\n\n**🔑 Key Takeaway:** If your AI service doesn't need 24/7 GPU capacity, serverless GPUs can cut costs by 80-90%. Modal Labs is the best developer experience — write Python, deploy with one command, and pay per second of actual GPU usage.\n\nNow let's compare self-hosting vs managed inference to help you choose the right approach."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Deploy an AI Model on Modal Labs",
            "difficulty": "Hard",
            "question": "Complete the Modal Labs deployment:\n1. Create a modal App named 'llm-service'\n2. Build an image with vllm and transformers installed\n3. Create a function with A10G GPU and 5-minute timeout\n4. Load the model and generate a response",
            "starterCode": "import modal\n\napp = modal.App(___)\n\nimage = modal.Image.debian_slim().pip_install(\n    ___, ___\n)\n\n@app.function(\n    image=image,\n    gpu=___,\n    timeout=___,\n)\ndef generate(prompt: str) -> str:\n    from vllm import LLM, SamplingParams\n    llm = LLM(model=\"meta-llama/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct\")\n    params = SamplingParams(temperature=0.7, max_tokens=256)\n    output = llm.generate([prompt], params)\n    return output[0].outputs[0].text",
            "solution": "import modal\n\napp = modal.App(\"llm-service\")\n\nimage = modal.Image.debian_slim().pip_install(\n    \"vllm\", \"transformers\"\n)\n\n@app.function(\n    image=image,\n    gpu=\"A10G\",\n    timeout=300,\n)\ndef generate(prompt: str) -> str:\n    from vllm import LLM, SamplingParams\n    llm = LLM(model=\"meta-llama/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct\")\n    params = SamplingParams(temperature=0.7, max_tokens=256)\n    output = llm.generate([prompt], params)\n    return output[0].outputs[0].text",
            "hints": [
              "App name is a string",
              "pip_install takes package names as strings",
              "GPU type is a string like 'A10G'",
              "Timeout is in seconds"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Self-Hosting vs Managed Cloud Inference",
            "explanation": "One of the most important decisions you'll make as an AI engineer: should you self-host your models or use managed services? Let's break it down.\n\n**The Decision Framework:**\n\n| Factor | Self-Hosted (vLLM on your GPU) | Managed API (OpenAI, Anthropic) | Serverless GPU (Modal, RunPod) |\n|--------|-------------------------------|--------------------------------|-------------------------------|\n| **Cost at low volume** | High (GPU runs 24/7) | Low (pay per token) | Low (pay per second) |\n| **Cost at high volume** | Low (amortized) | Very High ($$$) | Medium |\n| **Latency** | Lowest (your hardware) | Medium (network hop) | Medium (cold starts) |\n| **Data privacy** | Full control | Data leaves your network | Depends on provider |\n| **Model flexibility** | Any model, any size | Provider's models only | Any model |\n| **Ops complexity** | High (you manage everything) | Zero | Low |\n| **Scaling** | Manual/K8s | Automatic | Automatic |\n\n**When to Self-Host:**\n\n✅ You need data privacy (healthcare, finance, legal)\n✅ You're processing 10,000+ requests/hour consistently\n✅ You need custom fine-tuned models not available via APIs\n✅ You need the absolute lowest latency\n✅ You have a DevOps team to manage infrastructure\n\n**When to Use Managed APIs:**\n\n✅ You're a startup with < 1,000 requests/day\n✅ You need the best frontier models (GPT-4, Claude)\n✅ You don't have GPU infrastructure expertise\n✅ You need to ship fast and iterate quickly\n✅ Your data isn't sensitive\n\n**When to Use Serverless GPUs:**\n\n✅ Variable, unpredictable traffic patterns\n✅ You want to run open-source models but don't want to manage servers\n✅ You need GPU access for batch jobs (fine-tuning, embeddings)\n✅ Cost optimization is critical\n\n**The Hybrid Approach (Most Common in Production):**\n\nThe smartest teams use a combination:\n\n```\nFast, simple queries → OpenAI GPT-4o-mini (cheap, fast)\nComplex reasoning   → Anthropic Claude (quality)\nSensitive data      → Self-hosted Llama 3 via vLLM (privacy)\nBatch embeddings    → Modal Labs serverless (cost-effective)\n```\n\nThis is called **model routing** — and it's exactly what AI gateways like LiteLLM handle (we'll cover this in the next phase).\n\n**🔑 Key Takeaway:** There's no single right answer. Most production AI systems use a hybrid approach — managed APIs for convenience, self-hosted models for privacy-sensitive workloads, and serverless GPUs for variable batch jobs. Choose based on your volume, privacy needs, and team capability.\n\nLet's practice making these infrastructure decisions."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
