        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Model Quantization for Deployment: GGUF, GPTQ & AWQ",
            "explanation": "You want to run Llama 3.1 70B, but it needs 140 GB of VRAM in FP16. Your best GPU has 24 GB. Quantization is the technique that makes this possible by reducing the precision of model weights.\n\n**🧒 The Music Quality Analogy:**\n\nThink of model precision like audio quality. A FLAC file (lossless) is perfect but huge. An MP3 at 320kbps is almost indistinguishable to most ears but 5x smaller. An MP3 at 128kbps is noticeably worse but 10x smaller. Quantization does the same trade-off for model weights — slightly reduced quality for dramatically smaller size.\n\n**Quantization Formats:**\n\n| Format | Precision | Size Reduction | Quality Loss | Speed | Use Case |\n|--------|----------|---------------|-------------|-------|----------|\n| FP16 | 16-bit float | 1x (baseline) | None | Normal | Training, high-quality inference |\n| INT8 | 8-bit integer | 2x smaller | Minimal | Faster | Production servers |\n| INT4 (GPTQ) | 4-bit integer | 4x smaller | Small | Fast | GPU inference |\n| INT4 (AWQ) | 4-bit integer | 4x smaller | Very small | Fast | GPU inference (newer) |\n| GGUF (Q4_K_M) | 4-bit mixed | 4x smaller | Small | Fast | CPU + GPU inference |\n\n**GGUF — The Format for Local & Edge AI:**\n\nGGUF (GPT-Generated Unified Format) is the standard format for running models with Ollama, llama.cpp, and other local inference tools. It supports mixed-precision quantization and CPU offloading.\n\n```bash\n# Run a GGUF model with Ollama\nollama pull llama3.2:3b\nollama run llama3.2:3b \"What is Docker?\"\n\n# Or serve it as an API\nollama serve  # Starts OpenAI-compatible API on :11434\n\n# Use from Python\nfrom openai import OpenAI\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:11434/v1\", api_key=\"ollama\")\nresponse = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=\"llama3.2:3b\",\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"What is Docker?\"}]\n)\n```\n\n**GPTQ & AWQ — GPU-Optimized Quantization:**\n\n```python\n# Load a GPTQ-quantized model with vLLM\nfrom vllm import LLM\n\nllm = LLM(\n    model=\"TheBloke/Llama-2-7B-Chat-GPTQ\",\n    quantization=\"gptq\",\n    dtype=\"float16\"\n)\n\n# Load an AWQ-quantized model\nllm = LLM(\n    model=\"TheBloke/Llama-2-7B-Chat-AWQ\",\n    quantization=\"awq\"\n)\n```\n\n**Choosing the Right Quantization:**\n\n```\nNeed highest quality?     → FP16 (no quantization)\nProduction GPU server?    → AWQ or GPTQ (4-bit GPU)\nLocal/Edge deployment?    → GGUF with Ollama\nCPU-only server?          → GGUF (Q4_K_M or Q5_K_M)\n```\n\n**🔑 Key Takeaway:** Quantization lets you run models 2-4x larger on the same hardware with minimal quality loss. GGUF + Ollama is the easiest path for local deployment. GPTQ/AWQ + vLLM is best for production GPU servers. Always benchmark quantized vs full-precision on YOUR specific use case.\n\nLet's practice configuring quantized model deployment."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Serve a Quantized Model with Ollama",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Write a Python script that:\n1. Creates an OpenAI client pointing to Ollama's local API (base_url='http://localhost:11434/v1')\n2. Makes a chat completion request using 'llama3.2:3b' model\n3. Sends a system message: 'You are a DevOps expert.'\n4. Sends a user message: 'Explain Docker containers in 3 bullet points.'\n5. Prints the response content",
            "starterCode": "from openai import OpenAI\n\n# Connect to Ollama's local API\nclient = OpenAI(\n    base_url=___,\n    api_key=___,\n)\n\nresponse = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=___,\n    messages=[\n        {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": ___},\n        {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": ___}\n    ],\n    temperature=0.7,\n    max_tokens=256\n)\n\nprint(response.choices[0].message.content)",
            "solution": "from openai import OpenAI\n\n# Connect to Ollama's local API\nclient = OpenAI(\n    base_url=\"http://localhost:11434/v1\",\n    api_key=\"ollama\",\n)\n\nresponse = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=\"llama3.2:3b\",\n    messages=[\n        {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are a DevOps expert.\"},\n        {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Explain Docker containers in 3 bullet points.\"}\n    ],\n    temperature=0.7,\n    max_tokens=256\n)\n\nprint(response.choices[0].message.content)",
            "hints": [
              "Ollama serves on port 11434",
              "api_key can be any string for Ollama",
              "Model name matches what you pulled with ollama pull"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "content": {
            "question": "A 7B parameter model in FP16 precision requires approximately how much GPU VRAM just for the model weights?",
            "options": [
              "3.5 GB",
              "7 GB",
              "14 GB",
              "28 GB"
            ],
            "correctAnswer": 2,
            "explanation": "VRAM ≈ Parameters × Bytes per Parameter. A 7B model in FP16 (2 bytes per parameter) = 7 × 10^9 × 2 bytes = 14 GB. This is just for the weights — you need additional VRAM for KV cache, activations, and CUDA overhead."
          }
        },
