          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Cloud AI Infrastructure: AWS, GCP & Azure",
            "explanation": "Now that you understand Docker, Kubernetes, and LLM serving, the next question is: WHERE do your containers actually run? The answer is cloud providers. The three major providers each offer specialized services for AI deployment.\n\n**🧒 The Shopping Mall Analogy:**\n\nThink of cloud providers as three competing shopping malls — each with the same basic stores (compute, storage, networking) but different specialty shops. AWS has the biggest mall with the most variety. GCP has the best AI research lab inside. Azure has the best corporate office space.\n\n**The Big Three for AI:**\n\n| Provider | AI Strengths | Best For |\n|----------|-------------|----------|\n| **AWS** | Bedrock (managed LLMs), SageMaker (ML platform), ECS/EKS (containers) | Enterprise teams, widest service selection |\n| **GCP** | Vertex AI, TPUs, Cloud Run (serverless containers), GKE | AI/ML-focused teams, best Kubernetes experience |\n| **Azure** | Azure OpenAI Service, AKS, Container Apps | Microsoft ecosystem, enterprise compliance |\n\n**AWS for AI Engineers:**\n\n```\nYour AI App Deployment Options on AWS:\n\n1. ECS (Elastic Container Service)\n   → Run Docker containers without managing Kubernetes\n   → Best for: Teams new to container orchestration\n\n2. EKS (Elastic Kubernetes Service)\n   → Managed Kubernetes cluster\n   → Best for: Teams that need full K8s flexibility\n\n3. AWS Bedrock\n   → Managed API for Claude, Llama, Titan models\n   → Best for: Using foundation models without managing GPUs\n\n4. SageMaker Endpoints\n   → Deploy custom models with auto-scaling\n   → Best for: Custom fine-tuned models in production\n```\n\n**GCP for AI Engineers:**\n\n```\n1. Cloud Run\n   → Serverless containers with auto-scaling to zero\n   → Best for: CPU-based AI APIs (no GPU needed)\n\n2. GKE (Google Kubernetes Engine)\n   → Best managed Kubernetes experience\n   → Best for: GPU workloads with K8s orchestration\n\n3. Vertex AI\n   → End-to-end ML platform (training + serving)\n   → Best for: Full ML lifecycle management\n```\n\n**Azure for AI Engineers:**\n\n```\n1. Azure Container Apps\n   → Serverless containers (similar to Cloud Run)\n   → Best for: Simple container deployments\n\n2. AKS (Azure Kubernetes Service)\n   → Managed Kubernetes with GPU support\n   → Best for: Enterprise K8s deployments\n\n3. Azure OpenAI Service\n   → GPT-4, GPT-4o with enterprise compliance\n   → Best for: Enterprise teams needing OpenAI models with SLAs\n```\n\n**Quick Decision Guide:**\n\n```\nNeed GPT-4 with enterprise SLAs?       → Azure OpenAI\nNeed managed LLM API (multi-model)?    → AWS Bedrock\nNeed cheapest GPU Kubernetes?          → GCP GKE\nNeed serverless containers (no GPU)?   → GCP Cloud Run or AWS ECS\nNeed full Kubernetes control?          → Any (EKS/GKE/AKS)\n```\n\n**🔑 Key Takeaway:** All three clouds can run your AI application. Choose based on your team's existing expertise, compliance requirements, and whether you need managed LLM APIs (Bedrock/Azure OpenAI) or raw GPU compute (EKS/GKE with GPU nodes).\n\nLet's practice choosing the right cloud strategy."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
