        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "AWS for AI: ECS, Bedrock & SageMaker Deep Dive",
            "explanation": "Let's go deeper into AWS — the most popular cloud for AI deployments. We'll focus on the three services you'll use most.\n\n**AWS ECS — The Simplest Container Deployment:**\n\nECS (Elastic Container Service) runs Docker containers without Kubernetes complexity. It's perfect for deploying your FastAPI AI backend.\n\n```bash\n# 1. Build and push your Docker image to ECR (AWS Container Registry)\naws ecr get-login-password | docker login --username AWS --password-stdin 123456.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com\ndocker build -t my-ai-api .\ndocker tag my-ai-api:latest 123456.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/my-ai-api:latest\ndocker push 123456.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/my-ai-api:latest\n\n# 2. Create an ECS service (via AWS Console or CLI)\n# ECS handles load balancing, auto-scaling, and health checks\n```\n\n**AWS Bedrock — Managed Foundation Models:**\n\nBedrock gives you API access to Claude, Llama, Titan, and other models WITHOUT managing any GPU infrastructure.\n\n```python\nimport boto3\nimport json\n\nbedrock = boto3.client(\"bedrock-runtime\", region_name=\"us-east-1\")\n\nresponse = bedrock.invoke_model(\n    modelId=\"anthropic.claude-3-sonnet-20240229-v1:0\",\n    body=json.dumps({\n        \"anthropic_version\": \"bedrock-2023-05-31\",\n        \"messages\": [\n            {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"What is Docker?\"}\n        ],\n        \"max_tokens\": 256\n    })\n)\n\nresult = json.loads(response[\"body\"].read())\nprint(result[\"content\"][0][\"text\"])\n```\n\n**AWS SageMaker — Custom Model Deployment:**\n\nFor deploying your own fine-tuned models with auto-scaling:\n\n```python\nimport sagemaker\nfrom sagemaker.huggingface import HuggingFaceModel\n\nrole = sagemaker.get_execution_role()\n\nmodel = HuggingFaceModel(\n    model_data=\"s3://my-bucket/my-fine-tuned-model.tar.gz\",\n    role=role,\n    transformers_version=\"4.37\",\n    pytorch_version=\"2.1\",\n    py_version=\"py310\",\n)\n\npredictor = model.deploy(\n    initial_instance_count=1,\n    instance_type=\"ml.g5.xlarge\",  # GPU instance\n)\n```\n\n**Cost Comparison (approximate):**\n\n| Service | Use Case | Monthly Cost (estimate) |\n|---------|----------|----------------------|\n| ECS (2 tasks, t3.medium) | FastAPI backend | $60-100 |\n| Bedrock (Claude Sonnet) | 100K requests/month | $300-500 |\n| SageMaker (ml.g5.xlarge) | Custom model endpoint | $800-1,200 |\n\n**🔑 Key Takeaway:** Use ECS for your API containers, Bedrock for managed LLM access (no GPUs to manage), and SageMaker only when you need custom model deployment with auto-scaling. Most AI startups start with ECS + Bedrock.\n\nNow let's talk about deploying the data layer — vector databases for RAG."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Deploying Production Vector Databases",
            "explanation": "Your RAG pipeline needs a vector database to store and search document embeddings. In development, you used an in-memory FAISS index. In production, you need something that persists data, handles concurrent users, and scales.\n\n**Production Vector Database Options:**\n\n| Database | Type | Best For | Hosting |\n|----------|------|----------|---------|\n| **pgvector** | PostgreSQL extension | Teams already using PostgreSQL | Self-hosted or managed (Supabase, RDS) |\n| **Qdrant** | Purpose-built vector DB | High-performance similarity search | Self-hosted (Docker) or Qdrant Cloud |\n| **Pinecone** | Fully managed | Zero-ops, fastest setup | Pinecone Cloud only |\n| **Weaviate** | Multi-modal vector DB | Complex search with filters | Self-hosted or Weaviate Cloud |\n| **ChromaDB** | Lightweight | Prototyping, small datasets | Self-hosted only |\n\n**pgvector — The Pragmatic Choice:**\n\nIf you already use PostgreSQL (and most apps do), pgvector adds vector search without a separate database:\n\n```sql\n-- Enable the extension\nCREATE EXTENSION vector;\n\n-- Create a table with vector column\nCREATE TABLE documents (\n    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n    content TEXT,\n    embedding vector(1536),  -- OpenAI embedding dimension\n    metadata JSONB\n);\n\n-- Create an index for fast similarity search\nCREATE INDEX ON documents USING ivfflat (embedding vector_cosine_ops)\n    WITH (lists = 100);\n\n-- Search for similar documents\nSELECT content, 1 - (embedding <=> query_embedding) AS similarity\nFROM documents\nORDER BY embedding <=> query_embedding\nLIMIT 5;\n```\n\n**Qdrant — The Performance Choice:**\n\n```python\nfrom qdrant_client import QdrantClient\nfrom qdrant_client.models import Distance, VectorParams, PointStruct\n\nclient = QdrantClient(host=\"localhost\", port=6333)\n\n# Create a collection\nclient.create_collection(\n    collection_name=\"documents\",\n    vectors_config=VectorParams(\n        size=1536,\n        distance=Distance.COSINE\n    )\n)\n\n# Insert vectors\nclient.upsert(\n    collection_name=\"documents\",\n    points=[\n        PointStruct(\n            id=1,\n            vector=[0.1, 0.2, ...],  # 1536-dim embedding\n            payload={\"title\": \"Docker Guide\", \"content\": \"...\"}\n        )\n    ]\n)\n\n# Search\nresults = client.search(\n    collection_name=\"documents\",\n    query_vector=[0.1, 0.2, ...],\n    limit=5\n)\n```\n\n**Docker Compose for Vector DB:**\n\n```yaml\nservices:\n  qdrant:\n    image: qdrant/qdrant:latest\n    ports:\n      - \"6333:6333\"\n    volumes:\n      - qdrant_data:/qdrant/storage\n    environment:\n      QDRANT__SERVICE__API_KEY: \"your-secret-key\"\n\nvolumes:\n  qdrant_data:\n```\n\n**Decision Guide:**\n\n```\nAlready using PostgreSQL?        → pgvector (add extension)\nNeed maximum performance?        → Qdrant (purpose-built)\nWant zero infrastructure?        → Pinecone (fully managed)\nSmall dataset (< 100K docs)?     → ChromaDB (simplest)\n```\n\n**🔑 Key Takeaway:** For most AI applications, pgvector is the pragmatic choice — it adds vector search to your existing PostgreSQL database. If you need higher performance at scale (millions of vectors), use Qdrant. Pinecone is best for zero-ops teams.\n\nNow let's learn how to route requests across multiple LLM providers."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "yaml",
            "title": "Deploy Qdrant with Docker Compose",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Complete the docker-compose.yml to deploy a Qdrant vector database alongside your FastAPI app:\n1. Add a 'qdrant' service using 'qdrant/qdrant:latest' image\n2. Map port 6333\n3. Add persistent volume for '/qdrant/storage'\n4. Set QDRANT__SERVICE__API_KEY environment variable\n5. Make the 'api' service depend on 'qdrant'",
            "starterCode": "version: '3.9'\\nservices:\\n  api:\\n    build: .\\n    ports:\\n      - '8000:8000'\\n    depends_on:\\n      - ___\\n\\n  qdrant:\\n    image: ___\\n    ports:\\n      - '___:6333'\\n    volumes:\\n      - qdrant_data:___\\n    environment:\\n      ___: 'my-secret-key'\\n\\nvolumes:\\n  qdrant_data:",
            "solution": "version: '3.9'\\nservices:\\n  api:\\n    build: .\\n    ports:\\n      - '8000:8000'\\n    depends_on:\\n      - qdrant\\n\\n  qdrant:\\n    image: qdrant/qdrant:latest\\n    ports:\\n      - '6333:6333'\\n    volumes:\\n      - qdrant_data:/qdrant/storage\\n    environment:\\n      QDRANT__SERVICE__API_KEY: 'my-secret-key'\\n\\nvolumes:\\n  qdrant_data:",
            "hints": [
              "Qdrant's default port is 6333",
              "Storage path is /qdrant/storage",
              "Double underscores in env var name"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "content": {
            "question": "Your AI application already uses PostgreSQL for user data and needs to add RAG-based document search with 50,000 documents. What's the most pragmatic vector database choice?",
            "options": [
              "Deploy a separate Qdrant cluster for maximum performance",
              "Use pgvector — add the vector extension to your existing PostgreSQL database",
              "Use Pinecone's managed service for zero ops",
              "Store vectors as JSON arrays in a MongoDB collection"
