        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "AI Gateways: LiteLLM, Portkey & Model Routing",
            "explanation": "In production, you rarely use just one LLM provider. You might use GPT-4o for complex reasoning, Claude for long documents, Llama 3 for privacy-sensitive data, and GPT-4o-mini for simple tasks. Managing multiple API keys, different request formats, fallback logic, and cost tracking across providers is a nightmare.\n\nAI Gateways solve this by providing a single, unified API that routes requests to any LLM provider.\n\n**🧒 The Travel Agent Analogy:**\n\nInstead of booking flights on 5 different airline websites (each with different interfaces, login systems, and payment methods), you go to ONE travel agent who handles all airlines. They find the best price, rebook you automatically if a flight is cancelled, and give you one bill at the end. That's what an AI Gateway does for LLM APIs.\n\n**LiteLLM — The Open-Source AI Gateway:**\n\nLiteLLM provides a unified OpenAI-compatible API for 100+ LLM providers:\n\n```python\nfrom litellm import completion\n\n# Call OpenAI\nresponse = completion(\n    model=\"gpt-4o-mini\",\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Hello!\"}]\n)\n\n# Call Anthropic (same interface!)\nresponse = completion(\n    model=\"claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022\",\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Hello!\"}]\n)\n\n# Call self-hosted vLLM (same interface!)\nresponse = completion(\n    model=\"openai/meta-llama/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct\",\n    api_base=\"http://localhost:8000\",\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Hello!\"}]\n)\n```\n\n**LiteLLM Proxy — Production Gateway Server:**\n\n```yaml\n# litellm_config.yaml\nmodel_list:\n  - model_name: gpt-4o\n    litellm_params:\n      model: openai/gpt-4o\n      api_key: sk-...\n\n  - model_name: claude-sonnet\n    litellm_params:\n      model: anthropic/claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022\n      api_key: sk-ant-...\n\n  - model_name: local-llama\n    litellm_params:\n      model: openai/meta-llama/Llama-3.2-3B\n      api_base: http://vllm-server:8000\n\n# Fallback chain: try gpt-4o first, then claude, then local\nrouter_settings:\n  routing_strategy: \"simple-shuffle\"\n  num_retries: 2\n  fallbacks:\n    - gpt-4o: [\"claude-sonnet\", \"local-llama\"]\n```\n\n```bash\n# Start LiteLLM Proxy\nlitellm --config litellm_config.yaml --port 4000\n\n# Now ALL your apps use one endpoint:\ncurl http://localhost:4000/v1/chat/completions \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer sk-your-litellm-key\" \\\n  -d '{\"model\": \"gpt-4o\", \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Hello\"}]}'\n```\n\n**Key Gateway Features:**\n\n| Feature | What It Does |\n|---------|-------------|\n| **Unified API** | One OpenAI-compatible API for all providers |\n| **Fallbacks** | Automatically retry with a different provider on failure |\n| **Load balancing** | Distribute requests across multiple API keys/endpoints |\n| **Rate limiting** | Prevent exceeding provider rate limits |\n| **Cost tracking** | Track spending per model, per user, per team |\n| **Caching** | Cache identical requests to save money |\n\n**🔑 Key Takeaway:** An AI Gateway is essential for production AI systems. LiteLLM Proxy gives you a unified API, automatic fallbacks, cost tracking, and rate limiting — all in one open-source tool. Your application code calls ONE endpoint; the gateway handles the rest.\n\nNext, let's cover the security layer that protects your deployed AI services."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Configure LiteLLM for Multi-Provider Routing",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Write a Python script using LiteLLM that:\n1. Imports the 'completion' function from litellm\n2. Makes a request to 'gpt-4o-mini' with a user message\n3. Wraps it in a try/except that falls back to 'claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022' on any error\n4. Prints the response content",
            "starterCode": "from litellm import ___\n\ntry:\n    response = ___(\n        model=___,\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Explain AI gateways.\"}]\n    )\n    print(response.choices[0].message.content)\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(f\"Primary failed: {e}, trying fallback...\")\n    response = ___(\n        model=___,\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Explain AI gateways.\"}]\n    )\n    print(response.choices[0].message.content)",
            "solution": "from litellm import completion\n\ntry:\n    response = completion(\n        model=\"gpt-4o-mini\",\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Explain AI gateways.\"}]\n    )\n    print(response.choices[0].message.content)\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(f\"Primary failed: {e}, trying fallback...\")\n    response = completion(\n        model=\"claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022\",\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Explain AI gateways.\"}]\n    )\n    print(response.choices[0].message.content)",
            "hints": [
              "Import 'completion' from litellm",
              "Model names follow provider naming conventions",
              "The fallback uses the same completion() function"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Security: API Keys, TLS & Rate Limiting in Production",
            "explanation": "Your AI API is deployed and serving users. But without proper security, anyone can abuse it — running up your OpenAI bill, scraping your data, or DDoS-ing your service. Let's build a security fortress.\n\n**The Three Pillars of API Security:**\n\n**1. Authentication — Who are you?**\n\nEvery request to your API must include a valid API key or JWT token:\n\n```python\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, Depends, Header\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\nVALID_API_KEYS = {\n    \"sk-user-abc123\": {\"user\": \"alice\", \"tier\": \"pro\"},\n    \"sk-user-def456\": {\"user\": \"bob\", \"tier\": \"free\"},\n}\n\nasync def verify_api_key(x_api_key: str = Header(...)):\n    if x_api_key not in VALID_API_KEYS:\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=401, detail=\"Invalid API key\")\n    return VALID_API_KEYS[x_api_key]\n\n@app.post(\"/v1/chat\")\nasync def chat(request: dict, user=Depends(verify_api_key)):\n    # user is now authenticated\n    return {\"response\": \"...\", \"user\": user[\"user\"]}\n```\n\n**2. Rate Limiting — How much can you use?**\n\nPrevent abuse by limiting requests per user per time window:\n\n```python\nfrom slowapi import Limiter\nfrom slowapi.util import get_remote_address\n\nlimiter = Limiter(key_func=get_remote_address)\napp.state.limiter = limiter\n\n@app.post(\"/v1/chat\")\n@limiter.limit(\"60/minute\")  # Max 60 requests per minute\nasync def chat(request: Request):\n    return {\"response\": \"...\"}\n```\n\n**3. HTTPS/TLS — Is the connection encrypted?**\n\nNEVER serve an AI API over plain HTTP in production. All traffic must be encrypted with TLS. In cloud deployments, this is typically handled by the load balancer or ingress controller:\n\n```yaml\n# Kubernetes Ingress with TLS\napiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: Ingress\nmetadata:\n  name: ai-api-ingress\n  annotations:\n    cert-manager.io/cluster-issuer: letsencrypt\nspec:\n  tls:\n    - hosts:\n        - api.myaiapp.com\n      secretName: tls-secret\n  rules:\n    - host: api.myaiapp.com\n      http:\n        paths:\n          - path: /\n            pathType: Prefix\n            backend:\n              service:\n                name: ai-api-service\n                port:\n                  number: 80\n```\n\n**Security Checklist for AI APIs:**\n\n| Security Layer | Implementation | Priority |\n|---------------|---------------|----------|\n| API key authentication | FastAPI middleware + Header validation | 🔴 Critical |\n| Rate limiting | slowapi or Redis-based limiter | 🔴 Critical |\n| HTTPS/TLS | Let's Encrypt + cloud load balancer | 🔴 Critical |\n| CORS policy | FastAPI CORSMiddleware (restrict origins) | 🟡 High |\n| Input validation | Pydantic models for all request bodies | 🟡 High |\n| Secrets management | Environment variables + cloud secrets manager | 🔴 Critical |\n| Request logging | Log request metadata (not sensitive content) | 🟡 High |\n| IP allowlisting | Cloud firewall rules | 🟢 Optional |\n\n**🔑 Key Takeaway:** Every production AI API needs authentication (API keys), rate limiting (prevent abuse), and TLS encryption (protect data in transit). These are non-negotiable — a single unprotected endpoint can lead to thousands of dollars in API abuse.\n\nLet's practice implementing API key authentication."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Implement API Key Authentication",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Complete the FastAPI endpoint with API key authentication:\n1. Create a dependency function 'verify_api_key' that reads 'x-api-key' from the header\n2. Check if the key exists in VALID_KEYS dictionary\n3. Raise HTTPException with 401 if invalid\n4. Return the user info if valid\n5. Use the dependency in the chat endpoint",
            "starterCode": "from fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, Depends, Header\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\nVALID_KEYS = {\"sk-abc123\": {\"user\": \"alice\", \"tier\": \"pro\"}}\n\nasync def verify_api_key(x_api_key: str = Header(...)):\n    if ___:\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=___, detail=\"Invalid API key\")\n    return ___\n\n@app.post(\"/v1/chat\")\nasync def chat(user=Depends(___)):\n    return {\"message\": f\"Hello {user['user']}!\"}",
            "solution": "from fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, Depends, Header\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\nVALID_KEYS = {\"sk-abc123\": {\"user\": \"alice\", \"tier\": \"pro\"}}\n\nasync def verify_api_key(x_api_key: str = Header(...)):\n    if x_api_key not in VALID_KEYS:\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=401, detail=\"Invalid API key\")\n    return VALID_KEYS[x_api_key]\n\n@app.post(\"/v1/chat\")\nasync def chat(user=Depends(verify_api_key)):\n    return {\"message\": f\"Hello {user['user']}!\"}",
            "hints": [
              "Check if key is 'not in' the dictionary",
              "401 is the HTTP status code for Unauthorized",
              "Return the value from the dictionary for the key"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
