          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "CI/CD for AI Systems: Automating Everything",
            "explanation": "CI/CD (Continuous Integration / Continuous Deployment) automates the process of testing and deploying your code. Instead of manually building Docker images, running tests, and deploying — a CI/CD pipeline does it automatically every time you push code to GitHub.\n\n**🧒 The Factory Assembly Line Analogy:**\n\nWithout CI/CD, deploying is like handcrafting each product individually — slow, error-prone, and inconsistent. CI/CD is your automated factory assembly line. Raw materials (code) go in one end, and finished products (deployed containers) come out the other end — tested, validated, and ready for customers.\n\n**The CI/CD Pipeline for AI Applications:**\n\n```\nPush Code → Run Tests → Run AI Evals → Build Docker → Push to Registry → Deploy\n```\n\n**GitHub Actions — The Most Popular CI/CD Tool:**\n\n```yaml\n# .github/workflows/deploy.yml\nname: Deploy AI API\n\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\n\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n\n      - name: Set up Python\n        uses: actions/setup-python@v5\n        with:\n          python-version: '3.11'\n\n      - name: Install dependencies\n        run: pip install -r requirements.txt\n\n      - name: Run unit tests\n        run: pytest tests/ -v\n\n      - name: Run linting\n        run: ruff check .\n\n  build-and-deploy:\n    needs: test  # Only runs if tests pass\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n\n      - name: Build Docker image\n        run: docker build -t myapp:$\\{{ github.sha }} .\n\n      - name: Push to container registry\n        run: |\n          echo $\\{{ secrets.REGISTRY_PASSWORD }} | docker login -u $\\{{ secrets.REGISTRY_USER }} --password-stdin\n          docker push myapp:$\\{{ github.sha }}\n\n      - name: Deploy to production\n        run: |\n          kubectl set image deployment/ai-api api=myapp:$\\{{ github.sha }}\n```\n\n**Key CI/CD Concepts:**\n\n| Concept | What It Means |\n|---------|-------------|\n| **CI (Continuous Integration)** | Automatically test every code push |\n| **CD (Continuous Deployment)** | Automatically deploy after tests pass |\n| **Pipeline** | The sequence of steps (test → build → deploy) |\n| **Job** | A group of steps that run on the same machine |\n| **Secret** | Encrypted credentials stored in GitHub Settings |\n| **Artifact** | A file produced during the pipeline (Docker image, test report) |\n\n**🔑 Key Takeaway:** CI/CD turns deployment from a scary manual process into a boring automated one. Every push to `main` triggers tests → build → deploy. If tests fail, deployment is blocked. This catches bugs before they reach production.\n\nLet's practice writing a GitHub Actions workflow."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "yaml",
            "title": "Write a GitHub Actions Workflow",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Complete the GitHub Actions workflow YAML that:\n1. Triggers on push to the 'main' branch\n2. Sets up Python 3.11\n3. Installs dependencies from requirements.txt\n4. Runs pytest on the tests/ directory\n5. Runs ruff for linting",
            "starterCode": "name: CI Pipeline\\n\\non:\\n  push:\\n    branches: [___]\\n\\njobs:\\n  test:\\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\\n    steps:\\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\\n\\n      - name: Set up Python\\n        uses: actions/setup-python@v5\\n        with:\\n          python-version: ___\\n\\n      - name: Install dependencies\\n        run: ___\\n\\n      - name: Run tests\\n        run: ___\\n\\n      - name: Lint code\\n        run: ___",
            "solution": "name: CI Pipeline\\n\\non:\\n  push:\\n    branches: [main]\\n\\njobs:\\n  test:\\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\\n    steps:\\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\\n\\n      - name: Set up Python\\n        uses: actions/setup-python@v5\\n        with:\\n          python-version: '3.11'\\n\\n      - name: Install dependencies\\n        run: pip install -r requirements.txt\\n\\n      - name: Run tests\\n        run: pytest tests/ -v\\n\\n      - name: Lint code\\n        run: ruff check .",
            "hints": [
              "Branch name is 'main'",
              "Python version is a string '3.11'",
              "Use pip install -r to install from requirements file",
              "pytest runs tests, ruff checks linting"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Automated AI Evaluations in CI/CD",
            "explanation": "Traditional software tests check if code works correctly: \"does this function return the right value?\" AI tests are different — they check if your LLM pipeline produces GOOD responses. This is called AI evaluation (evals).\n\n**Why Evals in CI/CD?**\n\nImagine you update a prompt template. Unit tests pass — the code runs without errors. But the LLM's responses are now 30% worse. Without automated evals, you'd only discover this when users complain.\n\n**What to Evaluate:**\n\n| Eval Type | What It Measures | Example |\n|-----------|-----------------|---------|\n| **Relevance** | Does the response answer the question? | \"What is Docker?\" → Response should mention containers |\n| **Faithfulness** | Does the RAG response match the source documents? | No hallucinated facts |\n| **Correctness** | Is the factual content accurate? | Dates, numbers, definitions |\n| **Toxicity** | Does the response contain harmful content? | No hate speech, bias |\n| **Latency** | How fast is the response? | P95 < 2 seconds |\n\n**DeepEval — The Testing Framework for LLMs:**\n\n```python\n# tests/test_evals.py\nfrom deepeval import assert_test\nfrom deepeval.test_case import LLMTestCase\nfrom deepeval.metrics import AnswerRelevancyMetric, FaithfulnessMetric\n\ndef test_chatbot_relevance():\n    test_case = LLMTestCase(\n        input=\"What is Docker?\",\n        actual_output=get_chatbot_response(\"What is Docker?\"),\n        expected_output=\"Docker is a containerization platform...\"\n    )\n    \n    relevancy = AnswerRelevancyMetric(threshold=0.7)\n    assert_test(test_case, [relevancy])\n\ndef test_rag_faithfulness():\n    context = [\"Docker is a platform for building containers...\"]\n    test_case = LLMTestCase(\n        input=\"What is Docker?\",\n        actual_output=get_rag_response(\"What is Docker?\"),\n        retrieval_context=context\n    )\n    \n    faithfulness = FaithfulnessMetric(threshold=0.8)\n    assert_test(test_case, [faithfulness])\n```\n\n```bash\n# Run evals in CI\ndeepeval test run tests/test_evals.py\n```\n\n**Adding Evals to GitHub Actions:**\n\n```yaml\n  eval:\n    needs: test\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - name: Install dependencies\n        run: pip install -r requirements.txt deepeval\n      - name: Run AI evaluations\n        env:\n          OPENAI_API_KEY: ${{ secrets.OPENAI_API_KEY }}\n        run: deepeval test run tests/test_evals.py\n```\n\n**🔑 Key Takeaway:** Add AI evals to your CI/CD pipeline. They catch prompt regressions, RAG quality drops, and response degradation BEFORE code reaches production. DeepEval is the easiest framework to get started with.\n\nNow let's learn how to keep your deployed AI system healthy with monitoring and observability."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
