        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Monitoring & Observability: Keeping Your AI Alive",
            "explanation": "Running AI services in production without monitoring is like driving blind at 100 mph. You need real-time visibility into your system's health, performance, and AI-specific metrics.\n\n**The Three Pillars of Observability:**\n\n1. **Metrics** — Numbers over time (CPU usage, request latency, error rate, token costs)\n2. **Logs** — Detailed event records (request/response pairs, errors, warnings)\n3. **Traces** — End-to-end request journey (API call → embedding → vector search → LLM → response)\n\n**The Monitoring Stack:**\n\n```\nPrometheus (collect metrics) → Grafana (visualize dashboards)\n                              ↕\nYour FastAPI App (expose metrics) → AlertManager (send alerts)\n```\n\n**Adding Prometheus Metrics to FastAPI:**\n\n```python\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI\nfrom prometheus_client import Counter, Histogram, generate_latest\nfrom starlette.responses import Response\nimport time\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\n# Define metrics\nREQUEST_COUNT = Counter(\n    \"ai_api_requests_total\",\n    \"Total API requests\",\n    [\"method\", \"endpoint\", \"status\"]\n)\n\nREQUEST_LATENCY = Histogram(\n    \"ai_api_request_duration_seconds\",\n    \"Request latency in seconds\",\n    [\"endpoint\"],\n    buckets=[0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 1.0, 2.5, 5.0, 10.0]\n)\n\nTOKEN_USAGE = Counter(\n    \"ai_api_tokens_total\",\n    \"Total tokens consumed\",\n    [\"model\", \"type\"]  # type: prompt or completion\n)\n\n@app.middleware(\"http\")\nasync def track_metrics(request, call_next):\n    start = time.time()\n    response = await call_next(request)\n    duration = time.time() - start\n    \n    REQUEST_COUNT.labels(\n        method=request.method,\n        endpoint=request.url.path,\n        status=response.status_code\n    ).inc()\n    \n    REQUEST_LATENCY.labels(\n        endpoint=request.url.path\n    ).observe(duration)\n    \n    return response\n\n@app.get(\"/metrics\")\ndef metrics():\n    return Response(generate_latest(), media_type=\"text/plain\")\n```\n\n**AI-Specific Metrics You Must Track:**\n\n| Metric | Why It Matters | Alert Threshold |\n|--------|---------------|-----------------|\n| **Request latency (P95)** | User experience | > 3 seconds |\n| **Error rate** | Service reliability | > 1% |\n| **Token usage/hour** | Cost control | > budget limit |\n| **Model response quality** | AI effectiveness | Eval score < 0.7 |\n| **Cache hit rate** | Cost optimization | < 50% |\n| **Queue depth** | Capacity planning | > 100 pending |\n| **GPU utilization** | Resource efficiency | < 30% (wasting money) |\n| **Daily cost** | Budget management | > daily budget |\n\n**🔑 Key Takeaway:** Monitor everything — latency, errors, token costs, and AI quality metrics. Use Prometheus + Grafana for visualization and set up alerts for critical thresholds. The goal is to know about problems BEFORE your users do.\n\nLet's look at the specific metrics that matter most for AI applications."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "AI-Specific Metrics: Latency, Token Costs & Quality",
            "explanation": "AI applications have unique metrics that traditional web apps don't need. Let's break down each one and how to track them.\n\n**1. Latency Breakdown (Where Time Goes):**\n\nA typical AI API request has multiple stages:\n\n```\nTotal Latency = Network + Embedding + Vector Search + LLM Generation + Post-processing\n\nExample breakdown:\n  Network:        50ms\n  Embedding:     100ms  (convert query to vector)\n  Vector Search:  50ms  (find relevant documents)\n  LLM Generation: 800ms (generate response)\n  Post-processing: 20ms (format, filter)\n  ─────────────────────\n  Total:         1020ms\n```\n\nTrack each component separately to identify bottlenecks:\n\n```python\nimport time\n\nasync def process_request(query: str):\n    timings = {}\n    \n    t0 = time.time()\n    embedding = await get_embedding(query)\n    timings[\"embedding_ms\"] = (time.time() - t0) * 1000\n    \n    t0 = time.time()\n    documents = await vector_search(embedding)\n    timings[\"search_ms\"] = (time.time() - t0) * 1000\n    \n    t0 = time.time()\n    response = await llm_generate(query, documents)\n    timings[\"generation_ms\"] = (time.time() - t0) * 1000\n    \n    timings[\"total_ms\"] = sum(timings.values())\n    return response, timings\n```\n\n**2. Token Cost Tracking:**\n\n```python\n# Track costs per request\ndef calculate_cost(model: str, input_tokens: int, output_tokens: int) -> float:\n    pricing = {\n        \"gpt-4o\": {\"input\": 2.50 / 1_000_000, \"output\": 10.00 / 1_000_000},\n        \"gpt-4o-mini\": {\"input\": 0.15 / 1_000_000, \"output\": 0.60 / 1_000_000},\n        \"claude-3-5-sonnet\": {\"input\": 3.00 / 1_000_000, \"output\": 15.00 / 1_000_000},\n    }\n    \n    p = pricing.get(model, {\"input\": 0, \"output\": 0})\n    cost = (input_tokens * p[\"input\"]) + (output_tokens * p[\"output\"])\n    \n    # Track in Prometheus\n    TOKEN_USAGE.labels(model=model, type=\"prompt\").inc(input_tokens)\n    TOKEN_USAGE.labels(model=model, type=\"completion\").inc(output_tokens)\n    COST_COUNTER.labels(model=model).inc(cost)\n    \n    return cost\n```\n\n**3. Quality Monitoring (Production Evals):**\n\nRun lightweight quality checks on a sample of production responses:\n\n```python\nimport random\n\nasync def monitor_quality(query, response, context):\n    # Sample 5% of requests for quality monitoring\n    if random.random() > 0.05:\n        return\n    \n    # Check response length (too short = probably bad)\n    if len(response) < 50:\n        QUALITY_ALERTS.labels(type=\"too_short\").inc()\n    \n    # Check for refusal patterns\n    refusal_patterns = [\"I cannot\", \"I'm unable\", \"As an AI\"]\n    if any(p in response for p in refusal_patterns):\n        QUALITY_ALERTS.labels(type=\"refusal\").inc()\n    \n    # Log for async batch evaluation\n    await log_for_eval(query, response, context)\n```\n\n**🔑 Key Takeaway:** Track latency breakdown (embedding, search, generation separately), token costs per model, and sample production responses for quality monitoring. These AI-specific metrics are just as important as traditional uptime and error rate metrics.\n\nNow let's cover the final piece — making your AI system resilient to failures."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Set Up Prometheus Metrics for an AI API",
            "difficulty": "Hard",
            "question": "Create Prometheus metrics for an AI API:\n1. A Counter called 'ai_requests_total' with labels ['model', 'status']\n2. A Histogram called 'ai_latency_seconds' with labels ['endpoint']\n3. A Counter called 'ai_tokens_total' with labels ['model', 'type']\n4. Create a '/metrics' endpoint that returns Prometheus metrics",
            "starterCode": "from fastapi import FastAPI\nfrom prometheus_client import Counter, Histogram, generate_latest\nfrom starlette.responses import Response\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\n# Define metrics\nREQUEST_COUNT = ___(\n    \"ai_requests_total\",\n    \"Total requests\",\n    [\"model\", \"status\"]\n)\n\nLATENCY = ___(\n    \"ai_latency_seconds\",\n    \"Request latency\",\n    [\"endpoint\"]\n)\n\nTOKEN_COUNT = ___(\n    \"ai_tokens_total\",\n    \"Token usage\",\n    [\"model\", \"type\"]\n)\n\n@app.get(\"/metrics\")\ndef metrics():\n    return Response(___, media_type=\"text/plain\")",
            "solution": "from fastapi import FastAPI\nfrom prometheus_client import Counter, Histogram, generate_latest\nfrom starlette.responses import Response\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\n# Define metrics\nREQUEST_COUNT = Counter(\n    \"ai_requests_total\",\n    \"Total requests\",\n    [\"model\", \"status\"]\n)\n\nLATENCY = Histogram(\n    \"ai_latency_seconds\",\n    \"Request latency\",\n    [\"endpoint\"]\n)\n\nTOKEN_COUNT = Counter(\n    \"ai_tokens_total\",\n    \"Token usage\",\n    [\"model\", \"type\"]\n)\n\n@app.get(\"/metrics\")\ndef metrics():\n    return Response(generate_latest(), media_type=\"text/plain\")",
            "hints": [
              "Counter counts events, Histogram measures distributions",
              "generate_latest() returns all metrics as text"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Production Resilience: Circuit Breakers & Fallbacks",
            "explanation": "In production, things fail. OpenAI goes down for 2 hours. Your vector database runs out of memory. The embedding service times out. Your AI application needs to handle these failures gracefully — without crashing and without giving users error pages.\n\n**🧒 The Electrical Circuit Breaker Analogy:**\n\nYour home has circuit breakers. When a short circuit happens, the breaker TRIPS — cutting power to that circuit before it starts a fire. Once the problem is fixed, you flip the breaker back on.\n\nSoftware circuit breakers work the same way. When a service starts failing, the circuit breaker \"opens\" — immediately returning a fallback response instead of waiting for the failing service to timeout. This prevents cascade failures.\n\n**Circuit Breaker States:**\n\n```\nCLOSED (normal)  →  too many failures  →  OPEN (blocking)\n                                              ↓ (after timeout)\n                                         HALF-OPEN (testing)\n                                              ↓ (success)\n                                         CLOSED (normal)\n```\n\n**Implementing Circuit Breakers in Python:**\n\n```python\nimport time\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\n@dataclass\nclass CircuitBreaker:\n    failure_threshold: int = 5\n    recovery_timeout: int = 30\n    failures: int = 0\n    state: str = \"CLOSED\"\n    last_failure_time: float = 0\n    \n    def call(self, func, *args, fallback=None, **kwargs):\n        if self.state == \"OPEN\":\n            if time.time() - self.last_failure_time > self.recovery_timeout:\n                self.state = \"HALF_OPEN\"\n            else:\n                return fallback() if fallback else {\"error\": \"Service unavailable\"}\n        \n        try:\n            result = func(*args, **kwargs)\n            if self.state == \"HALF_OPEN\":\n                self.state = \"CLOSED\"\n                self.failures = 0\n            return result\n        except Exception as e:\n            self.failures += 1\n            self.last_failure_time = time.time()\n            if self.failures >= self.failure_threshold:\n                self.state = \"OPEN\"\n            return fallback() if fallback else {\"error\": str(e)}\n\n# Usage\nopenai_breaker = CircuitBreaker(failure_threshold=3, recovery_timeout=60)\n\ndef get_ai_response(prompt):\n    return openai_breaker.call(\n        call_openai,\n        prompt,\n        fallback=lambda: call_local_llm(prompt)  # Fallback to local model\n    )\n```\n\n**The Fallback Chain Pattern:**\n\n```python\nasync def get_response(prompt: str) -> str:\n    # Try primary: OpenAI GPT-4o\n    try:\n        return await call_openai(prompt, model=\"gpt-4o\")\n    except Exception:\n        pass\n    \n    # Fallback 1: Anthropic Claude\n    try:\n        return await call_anthropic(prompt)\n    except Exception:\n        pass\n    \n    # Fallback 2: Local Ollama\n    try:\n        return await call_ollama(prompt)\n    except Exception:\n        pass\n    \n    # Final fallback: cached or static response\n    return \"I'm currently experiencing issues. Please try again shortly.\"\n```\n\n**Production Resilience Checklist:**\n\n| Pattern | What It Does | When to Use |\n|---------|-------------|-------------|\n| **Circuit Breaker** | Stops calling a failing service | External API dependencies |\n| **Fallback Chain** | Try alternative providers | Multi-model routing |\n| **Retry with Backoff** | Retry with increasing delays | Transient failures |\n| **Timeout** | Kill slow requests | Preventing request pile-up |\n| **Bulkhead** | Isolate failure domains | Multi-service architectures |\n| **Health Checks** | Report service health | Load balancer routing |\n\n**🔑 Key Takeaway:** Production AI systems MUST handle failures gracefully. Use circuit breakers to prevent cascade failures, fallback chains for multi-provider resilience, and retries with exponential backoff for transient errors. Your users should never see a raw error page.\n\nLet's test your knowledge before moving to the practice project."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "content": {
            "title": "Incident Response Decision",
            "scenario": "Your production AI chatbot's response latency suddenly spikes from 500ms to 8 seconds. Monitoring shows OpenAI's API is responding with 429 (rate limit) errors for 60% of requests. Users are complaining. Your team has a local Llama 3 model running on Ollama as a backup. What do you do?",
            "options": [
              {
                "text": "Wait for OpenAI to fix their rate limiting — it's their problem, not ours",
                "feedback": "Waiting passively while 60% of your users get slow or failed responses is unacceptable. You have a backup system — use it!",
                "isCorrect": false
