          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Practice Project: AI-Powered Code Review Assistant",
            "explanation": "**Your Deployment Mission:**\n\nBuild and deploy an AI-powered code review tool that analyzes pull requests, identifies bugs, suggests improvements, and ensures code style consistency — deployed to production with Docker, CI/CD, monitoring, and resilience.\n\n**What You Will Build:**\n\nA \"CodeReview AI\" system with:\n1. **FastAPI backend** — Receives code snippets via API and returns AI-powered reviews\n2. **OpenAI GPT-4o-mini integration** — With LiteLLM for multi-provider fallback\n3. **Docker containerization** — Multi-stage build for optimized image\n4. **GitHub Actions CI/CD** — Automated testing and deployment\n5. **Prometheus metrics** — Track latency, token costs, and error rates\n6. **Circuit breaker** — Fallback to a simpler review when OpenAI is down\n\n**Architecture Overview:**\n\n```\nGitHub PR → Webhook → FastAPI API → LiteLLM Gateway → GPT-4o-mini\n                          ↓                               ↓ (fallback)\n                     PostgreSQL                      Local Ollama\n                     (review history)\n                          ↓\n                     Prometheus → Grafana Dashboard\n```\n\n**Skills You'll Practice:**\n\n| Skill | Module Connection |\n|-------|------------------|\n| Docker & Docker Compose | Phase 1 |\n| Kubernetes manifests | Phase 2 |\n| LLM serving configuration | Phase 3 |\n| Cloud deployment strategy | Phase 4 |\n| CI/CD pipeline | Phase 5 |\n| Monitoring & resilience | Phase 5 |\n\n**Project Structure:**\n\n```\ncodereview-ai/\n├── app/\n│   ├── main.py              # FastAPI application\n│   ├── review.py             # Code review logic\n│   ├── metrics.py            # Prometheus metrics\n│   └── circuit_breaker.py    # Resilience patterns\n├── tests/\n│   ├── test_api.py           # Unit tests\n│   └── test_evals.py         # AI evaluation tests\n├── Dockerfile                # Multi-stage build\n├── docker-compose.yml        # Full stack (API + DB + monitoring)\n├── .github/workflows/\n│   └── deploy.yml            # CI/CD pipeline\n├── requirements.txt\n└── README.md\n```\n\nLet's build this step by step."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Architecture & System Design",
            "explanation": "Before writing code, let's design the system architecture. Good architecture decisions at this stage prevent painful rewrites later.\n\n**API Design:**\n\n```python\n# POST /v1/review\n{\n    \"code\": \"def add(a, b):\\n    return a + b\",\n    \"language\": \"python\",\n    \"review_type\": \"full\"  # \"full\", \"security\", \"performance\"\n}\n\n# Response\n{\n    \"review\": {\n        \"score\": 8.5,\n        \"issues\": [\n            {\n                \"severity\": \"info\",\n                \"line\": 1,\n                \"message\": \"Consider adding type hints: def add(a: int, b: int) -> int\"\n            }\n        ],\n        \"suggestions\": [\"Add docstring\", \"Add input validation\"],\n        \"summary\": \"Clean, functional code. Minor improvements suggested.\"\n    },\n    \"metadata\": {\n        \"model\": \"gpt-4o-mini\",\n        \"tokens_used\": 450,\n        \"latency_ms\": 1200,\n        \"cost_usd\": 0.0003\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Prompt Engineering for Code Review:**\n\n```python\nSYSTEM_PROMPT = \"\"\"You are an expert code reviewer. Analyze the provided code and return a JSON response with:\n1. score (0-10): Overall code quality\n2. issues: Array of {severity, line, message} objects\n   - severity: \"critical\", \"warning\", \"info\"\n3. suggestions: Array of improvement recommendations\n4. summary: One-paragraph review summary\n\nFocus on:\n- Bugs and logic errors (critical)\n- Security vulnerabilities (critical)  \n- Performance issues (warning)\n- Code style and best practices (info)\n- Missing error handling (warning)\n\nBe specific — reference exact line numbers and provide actionable fixes.\"\"\"\n```\n\n**Technology Choices:**\n\n| Component | Choice | Why |\n|-----------|--------|-----|\n| API Framework | FastAPI | Async, fast, auto-docs |\n| LLM | GPT-4o-mini via LiteLLM | Cheap, fast, good at code |\n| Database | PostgreSQL | Store review history |\n| Metrics | Prometheus + custom counters | Industry standard |\n| Fallback | Ollama + Llama 3.2 | Free, local, private |\n| CI/CD | GitHub Actions | Native GitHub integration |\n\n**🔑 Architecture Principle:** Design for failure. Every external dependency (OpenAI, database) should have a fallback or graceful degradation path. Your API should ALWAYS return a response — even if it's a simplified one.\n\nNow let's start building."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Step 1: Build the FastAPI Backend",
            "explanation": "**The Core Application:**\n\n```python\n# app/main.py\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, Depends, Header\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\nfrom litellm import completion\nimport time\nfrom app.metrics import REQUEST_COUNT, LATENCY, TOKEN_USAGE\n\napp = FastAPI(title=\"CodeReview AI\", version=\"1.0\")\n\nclass ReviewRequest(BaseModel):\n    code: str\n    language: str = \"python\"\n    review_type: str = \"full\"\n\nclass ReviewResponse(BaseModel):\n    review: dict\n    metadata: dict\n\nSYSTEM_PROMPT = \"\"\"You are an expert code reviewer. Analyze the code and return a JSON response with: score (0-10), issues (array of {severity, line, message}), suggestions (array of strings), and summary (string).\"\"\"\n\n@app.post(\"/v1/review\", response_model=ReviewResponse)\nasync def review_code(request: ReviewRequest):\n    start = time.time()\n    \n    try:\n        response = completion(\n            model=\"gpt-4o-mini\",\n            messages=[\n                {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": SYSTEM_PROMPT},\n                {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": f\"Language: {request.language}\\nReview type: {request.review_type}\\n\\n{request.code}\"}\n            ],\n            temperature=0.3,\n            max_tokens=1024,\n            response_format={\"type\": \"json_object\"}\n        )\n        \n        result = response.choices[0].message.content\n        tokens_in = response.usage.prompt_tokens\n        tokens_out = response.usage.completion_tokens\n        latency = (time.time() - start) * 1000\n        \n        # Track metrics\n        REQUEST_COUNT.labels(model=\"gpt-4o-mini\", status=\"success\").inc()\n        LATENCY.labels(endpoint=\"/v1/review\").observe(latency / 1000)\n        TOKEN_USAGE.labels(model=\"gpt-4o-mini\", type=\"prompt\").inc(tokens_in)\n        TOKEN_USAGE.labels(model=\"gpt-4o-mini\", type=\"completion\").inc(tokens_out)\n        \n        import json\n        return ReviewResponse(\n            review=json.loads(result),\n            metadata={\n                \"model\": \"gpt-4o-mini\",\n                \"tokens_used\": tokens_in + tokens_out,\n                \"latency_ms\": round(latency),\n                \"cost_usd\": round((tokens_in * 0.15 + tokens_out * 0.6) / 1_000_000, 6)\n            }\n        )\n    except Exception as e:\n        REQUEST_COUNT.labels(model=\"gpt-4o-mini\", status=\"error\").inc()\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=500, detail=str(e))\n\n@app.get(\"/health\")\ndef health_check():\n    return {\"status\": \"healthy\", \"version\": \"1.0\"}\n```\n\n**requirements.txt:**\n\n```\nfastapi==0.115.0\nuvicorn==0.30.0\nlitellm==1.48.0\npydantic==2.9.0\nprometheus-client==0.21.0\npython-dotenv==1.0.0\n```\n\n**Test it locally:**\n\n```bash\npip install -r requirements.txt\nuvicorn app.main:app --reload --port 8000\n\n# Test with curl\ncurl -X POST http://localhost:8000/v1/review \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"code\": \"def hello():\\n    print(\\\"hello\\\")\", \"language\": \"python\"}'\n```\n\nNow let's containerize it."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Step 2: Dockerize & Deploy",
            "explanation": "**Multi-Stage Dockerfile:**\n\n```dockerfile\n# ============ Stage 1: Builder ============\nFROM python:3.11-slim AS builder\n\nWORKDIR /build\nCOPY requirements.txt .\nRUN pip install --no-cache-dir --prefix=/install -r requirements.txt\n\n# ============ Stage 2: Runtime ============\nFROM python:3.11-slim AS runtime\n\nWORKDIR /app\n\n# Copy installed packages from builder\nCOPY --from=builder /install /usr/local\n\n# Copy application code\nCOPY app/ ./app/\n\n# Environment\nENV PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1\nENV ENVIRONMENT=production\n\nEXPOSE 8000\n\nHEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=5s \\\n  CMD curl -f http://localhost:8000/health || exit 1\n\nCMD [\"uvicorn\", \"app.main:app\", \"--host\", \"0.0.0.0\", \"--port\", \"8000\"]\n```\n\n**Docker Compose with Full Stack:**\n\n```yaml\n# docker-compose.yml\nversion: \"3.9\"\n\nservices:\n  api:\n    build: .\n    ports:\n      - \"8000:8000\"\n    environment:\n      - OPENAI_API_KEY=${OPENAI_API_KEY}\n      - DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:pass@db:5432/reviews\n    depends_on:\n      - db\n    restart: unless-stopped\n\n  db:\n    image: postgres:16-alpine\n    environment:\n      POSTGRES_USER: user\n      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: pass\n      POSTGRES_DB: reviews\n    volumes:\n      - pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data\n\n  prometheus:\n    image: prom/prometheus:latest\n    ports:\n      - \"9090:9090\"\n    volumes:\n      - ./prometheus.yml:/etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml\n\nvolumes:\n  pgdata:\n```\n\n**prometheus.yml:**\n\n```yaml\nglobal:\n  scrape_interval: 15s\n\nscrape_configs:\n  - job_name: \"codereview-api\"\n    static_configs:\n      - targets: [\"api:8000\"]\n```\n\n**Build and Run:**\n\n```bash\n# Build and start everything\ndocker compose up -d --build\n\n# Check it's working\ncurl http://localhost:8000/health\ncurl http://localhost:9090  # Prometheus UI\n```\n\nNow let's automate the testing and deployment."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Step 3: Add CI/CD & Monitoring",
            "explanation": "**GitHub Actions CI/CD Pipeline:**\n\n```yaml\n# .github/workflows/deploy.yml\nname: CodeReview AI - CI/CD\n\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\n  pull_request:\n    branches: [main]\n\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n\n      - name: Set up Python 3.11\n        uses: actions/setup-python@v5\n        with:\n          python-version: \"3.11\"\n\n      - name: Install dependencies\n        run: pip install -r requirements.txt pytest ruff deepeval\n\n      - name: Lint with ruff\n        run: ruff check app/\n\n      - name: Run unit tests\n        run: pytest tests/test_api.py -v\n\n      - name: Run AI evaluations\n        env:\n          OPENAI_API_KEY: ${{ secrets.OPENAI_API_KEY }}\n        run: deepeval test run tests/test_evals.py\n        continue-on-error: true  # Don't block deploy on eval failures (log them)\n\n  build:\n    needs: test\n    if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n\n      - name: Build Docker image\n        run: docker build -t codereview-ai:${{ github.sha }} .\n\n      - name: Run container smoke test\n        run: |\n          docker run -d --name test -p 8000:8000 codereview-ai:${{ github.sha }}\n          sleep 5\n          curl -f http://localhost:8000/health\n          docker stop test\n```\n\n**Unit Test Example:**\n\n```python\n# tests/test_api.py\nfrom fastapi.testclient import TestClient\nfrom app.main import app\n\nclient = TestClient(app)\n\ndef test_health_endpoint():\n    response = client.get(\"/health\")\n    assert response.status_code == 200\n    assert response.json()[\"status\"] == \"healthy\"\n\ndef test_review_endpoint_validation():\n    response = client.post(\"/v1/review\", json={})\n    assert response.status_code == 422  # Validation error\n```\n\n**AI Evaluation Test:**\n\n```python\n# tests/test_evals.py\nfrom deepeval import assert_test\nfrom deepeval.test_case import LLMTestCase\nfrom deepeval.metrics import AnswerRelevancyMetric\n\ndef test_review_relevance():\n    # Call your actual review endpoint\n    test_case = LLMTestCase(\n        input=\"def divide(a, b): return a / b\",\n        actual_output='{\"score\": 6, \"issues\": [{\"severity\": \"critical\", \"line\": 1, \"message\": \"No zero division check\"}]}',\n        expected_output=\"Should identify missing zero division error handling\"\n    )\n    \n    metric = AnswerRelevancyMetric(threshold=0.6)\n    assert_test(test_case, [metric])\n```\n\n**🔑 The Complete Pipeline Flow:**\n\n```\nPush to main → Lint code → Run unit tests → Run AI evals → Build Docker image → Smoke test → Deploy ✅\n```\n\nYour CodeReview AI is now production-ready with automated testing, containerization, and deployment."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Complete Architecture & Next Steps",
            "explanation": "**Congratulations!** You've learned the entire deployment lifecycle for AI applications — from containerizing your code to running it in production with monitoring, CI/CD, and resilience.\n\n**What You've Learned in This Module:**\n\n| Phase | Key Skills |\n|-------|-----------|\n| **Phase 1: Docker** | Dockerfiles, multi-stage builds, Docker Compose, image optimization |\n| **Phase 2: Kubernetes** | Pods, Deployments, Services, HPA, Helm, GitOps |\n| **Phase 3: LLM Serving** | vLLM, PagedAttention, VRAM math, Ollama, serverless GPUs (Modal) |\n| **Phase 4: Cloud & Gateways** | AWS/GCP/Azure, vector DBs (pgvector, Qdrant), LiteLLM AI gateway |\n| **Phase 5: CI/CD & Monitoring** | GitHub Actions, DeepEval, Prometheus, circuit breakers |\n| **Phase 6: Project** | End-to-end deployment of a real AI application |\n\n**The Production AI Deployment Checklist:**\n\n```\n□ Application containerized with Docker (multi-stage build)\n□ Environment variables for all secrets (.env + cloud secrets manager)\n□ Docker Compose for local development stack\n□ CI/CD pipeline with unit tests + AI evals\n□ Prometheus metrics (latency, tokens, costs, errors)\n□ Circuit breaker with fallback chain\n□ Rate limiting and API key authentication\n□ HTTPS/TLS encryption\n□ Health check endpoint (/health)\n□ Structured logging\n□ Auto-scaling configured (HPA or serverless)\n□ Alerting on critical thresholds\n```\n\n**Next Steps After This Module:**\n\n1. **Deploy your own project** — Take your Module 14 FastAPI app or Module 12 AI Agent and deploy it using everything you've learned here.\n2. **Get cloud certified** — AWS Solutions Architect Associate or GCP Professional Cloud Architect.\n3. **Learn Infrastructure as Code** — Terraform or Pulumi for managing cloud resources declaratively.\n4. **Explore advanced Kubernetes** — Service meshes (Istio), custom operators, GPU scheduling.\n5. **Study SRE practices** — Site Reliability Engineering principles for maintaining AI systems at scale.\n\n**🔑 Final Key Takeaway:** Deployment is not a one-time event — it's a continuous process. The best AI engineers treat deployment as a first-class concern from Day 1, not an afterthought. Your code is only valuable when it's running in production, serving real users, and being monitored for quality.\n\nYou're now equipped to take any AI application from localhost to production. Go build something amazing! 🚀"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "M020-phase-1",
      "title": "Module 20: Phase 1 — Project Vision & Development Environment",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "title": "Welcome to the Capstone: What We're Building",
          "content": {
            "title": "Welcome to the Practice Project: What We're Building",
            "explanation": "Welcome to Module 20 — the culmination of everything you've learned. Over the next phases, you'll build a **production-grade AI-Powered Customer Intelligence Platform** from scratch.\n\n**What is this project?**\nImagine you work at a SaaS company. Customers send emails, chat messages, and support tickets every day. Your job is to build an AI system that:\n\n1. **Understands** every customer message using LLMs\n2. **Searches** a knowledge base using RAG to find relevant answers\n3. **Routes** complex queries to specialized AI agents\n4. **Generates** professional responses automatically\n5. **Learns** from feedback to improve over time\n6. **Deploys** to production with monitoring and CI/CD\n\n**Why this project?**\nThis is exactly what companies like Intercom, Zendesk, and Freshdesk are building — and charging $50-200/seat/month for. You'll build the core engine.\n\n**Technologies you'll use (from previous modules):**\n- **Python & FastAPI** (Module 1, 14)\n- **Machine Learning concepts** (Module 2-3)\n- **NLP & Embeddings** (Module 4)\n- **LLM APIs** (Module 5, 9)\n- **Prompt Engineering** (Module 6)\n- **Vector Databases** (Module 7)\n- **RAG Pipeline** (Module 8)\n- **Fine-tuning** (Module 10)\n- **LangChain & LangGraph** (Module 11)\n- **AI Agents** (Module 12)\n- **MCP** (Module 13)\n- **Backend & Databases** (Module 14, 15)\n- **Frontend** (Module 16)\n- **LLMOps** (Module 17)\n- **System Design** (Module 18)\n- **Docker & Deployment** (Module 19)\n\nEvery single module comes together here. Let's begin.",
            "sections": [
              {
                "title": "The Architecture at a Glance",
                "body": "Here's the high-level architecture of what you'll build:\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                  Frontend (Next.js)              │\n│   Dashboard │ Chat Widget │ Analytics View       │\n└──────────────────────┬──────────────────────────┘\n                       │ REST API / WebSocket\n┌──────────────────────▼──────────────────────────┐\n│              FastAPI Backend                     │\n│  ┌─────────┐  ┌──────────┐  ┌───────────────┐   │\n│  │ Auth    │  │ Ingestion│  │ Query Engine  │   │\n│  │ Service │  │ Pipeline │  │ (RAG + Agents)│   │\n│  └─────────┘  └──────────┘  └───────────────┘   │\n│  ┌─────────┐  ┌──────────┐  ┌───────────────┐   │\n│  │ Feedback│  │ Analytics│  │ Response Gen  │   │\n│  │ Loop    │  │ Engine   │  │ Service       │   │\n│  └─────────┘  └──────────┘  └───────────────┘   │\n└──────┬────────────┬────────────┬────────────────┘\n       │            │            │\n┌──────▼───┐  ┌─────▼────┐  ┌───▼────────────┐\n│PostgreSQL│  │ChromaDB  │  │ Redis Cache    │\n│(Users,   │  │(Knowledge│  │ (Sessions,     │\n│ Tickets) │  │ Vectors) │  │  Rate Limits)  │\n│          │  │          │  │                │\n└──────────┘  └──────────┘  └────────────────┘\n```\n\nDon't worry if this looks complex — we'll build every piece step by step."
              }
            ],
            "example": "# What the finished product looks like:\n# A REST API that handles customer support intelligently\n\n# POST /api/v1/tickets\n# Body: { \"customer_email\": \"jane@acme.com\", \"message\": \"How do I reset my password?\" }\n#\n# Response: {\n#   \"ticket_id\": \"TKT-2024-001\",\n#   \"classification\": \"account_access\",\n#   \"confidence\": 0.94,\n#   \"ai_response\": \"Hi Jane! To reset your password: 1) Go to login page...\",\n#   \"sources\": [\"docs/password-reset.md\"],\n#   \"requires_human\": false\n# }"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "title": "Step 1: Opening VS Code & Creating the Project",
          "content": {
            "title": "Step 1: Opening VS Code & Creating the Project",
            "explanation": "Every great project starts with a single step. Let's open VS Code and set up our workspace.\n\n**Step-by-step instructions:**\n\n**1. Open your terminal and create the project directory:**\n```bash\nmkdir customer-intelligence-platform\ncd customer-intelligence-platform\ncode .   # Opens VS Code in this directory\n```\n\n**2. Create the project structure:**\nEnterprise projects follow a modular architecture. Each feature lives in its own directory. Here's what we'll build:\n\n```\ncustomer-intelligence-platform/\n├── app/                      # FastAPI application\n│   ├── __init__.py\n│   ├── main.py               # Application entry point\n│   ├── config.py             # Configuration management\n│   ├── api/                  # API routes\n│   │   ├── __init__.py\n│   │   ├── v1/\n│   │   │   ├── __init__.py\n│   │   │   ├── tickets.py    # Ticket endpoints\n│   │   │   ├── knowledge.py  # Knowledge base endpoints\n│   │   │   ├── analytics.py  # Analytics endpoints\n│   │   │   └── auth.py       # Authentication\n│   ├── services/             # Business logic\n│   │   ├── __init__.py\n│   │   ├── classifier.py     # Ticket classification\n│   │   ├── rag_engine.py     # RAG pipeline\n│   │   ├── response_gen.py   # Response generation\n│   │   └── feedback.py       # Feedback processing\n│   ├── agents/               # LangGraph agents\n│   │   ├── __init__.py\n│   │   ├── router_agent.py   # Routes to specialist agents\n│   │   ├── support_agent.py  # General support\n│   │   ├── billing_agent.py  # Billing queries\n│   │   └── escalation.py     # Human escalation\n│   ├── models/               # Database models\n│   │   ├── __init__.py\n│   │   ├── ticket.py\n│   │   ├── customer.py\n│   │   └── knowledge.py\n│   ├── db/                   # Database connections\n│   │   ├── __init__.py\n│   │   ├── postgres.py\n│   │   ├── chromadb.py\n│   │   └── redis.py\n│   └── middleware/           # Middleware\n│       ├── __init__.py\n│       ├── auth.py\n│       ├── rate_limit.py\n│       └── logging.py\n├── tests/                    # Test suite\n│   ├── __init__.py\n│   ├── test_tickets.py\n│   ├── test_rag.py\n│   └── test_agents.py\n├── frontend/                 # Next.js frontend (Phase 18)\n├── scripts/                  # Utility scripts\n│   └── seed_knowledge.py\n├── .env.example              # Environment template\n├── .gitignore\n├── docker-compose.yml        # Infrastructure\n├── Dockerfile                # Application container\n├── requirements.txt          # Python dependencies\n├── pyproject.toml            # Project metadata\n└── README.md                 # Documentation\n```\n\n**Why this structure?**\n- **Separation of concerns**: Each directory has ONE job\n- **Scalability**: New features = new files, not bigger files\n- **Testability**: Every service can be tested independently\n- **Industry standard**: This is how companies like Stripe and Shopify structure backend services",
            "example": "# Run these commands in your terminal to create the structure:\n\n# Create main app directories\nmkdir -p app/api/v1 app/services app/agents app/models app/db app/middleware\nmkdir -p tests scripts frontend\n\n# Create __init__.py files (makes Python treat directories as packages)\ntouch app/__init__.py app/api/__init__.py app/api/v1/__init__.py\ntouch app/services/__init__.py app/agents/__init__.py\ntouch app/models/__init__.py app/db/__init__.py app/middleware/__init__.py\ntouch tests/__init__.py\n\n# Create core files\ntouch app/main.py app/config.py\ntouch .env.example .gitignore README.md\ntouch requirements.txt pyproject.toml\ntouch docker-compose.yml Dockerfile\n\n# Verify the structure\nfind . -type f | head -30\n\necho \"✅ Project structure created successfully!\""
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "questions": [
            {
              "question": "Why do we separate the project into directories like 'services/', 'agents/', and 'models/' instead of putting everything in one file?",
              "options": [
                "It makes the code run faster",
                "Separation of concerns — each module has one responsibility, making it easier to test, debug, and scale",
                "Python requires this structure to work",
                "It's just for aesthetics and doesn't matter functionally"
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explanation": "Separation of concerns is a core software engineering principle. When each module has one job, you can: test it independently, change it without breaking other parts, and have multiple developers work on different modules simultaneously."
            },
            {
              "question": "What command opens VS Code in the current directory?",
              "options": [
                "vscode .",
                "open-vscode",
                "code .",
                "editor ."
              ],
              "answer": 2,
              "explanation": "'code .' opens VS Code with the current directory as the workspace. The dot (.) means 'current directory'. Make sure VS Code is installed and the 'code' command is in your PATH."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "title": "Step 2: Python Virtual Environment & Dependencies",
          "content": {
            "title": "Step 2: Python Virtual Environment & Dependencies",
            "explanation": "Before writing any code, we need to set up an isolated Python environment. This prevents dependency conflicts between projects.\n\n**What is a virtual environment?**\nThink of it like a clean room for your project. Every project gets its own set of installed packages. If Project A needs `langchain==0.2` and Project B needs `langchain==0.3`, virtual environments keep them separate.\n\n**Step-by-step:**\n\n```bash\n# 1. Create the virtual environment\npython -m venv venv\n\n# 2. Activate it\n# On macOS/Linux:\nsource venv/bin/activate\n# On Windows:\nvenv\\Scripts\\activate\n\n# 3. You'll see (venv) in your terminal prompt — this means it's active!\n# (venv) $\n\n# 4. Upgrade pip first (always do this)\npip install --upgrade pip\n```\n\n**Now install our dependencies:**\nWe need packages from almost every module you've studied. Here's what each one does:",
            "sections": [
              {
                "title": "Core Dependencies Explained",
                "body": "```\n# requirements.txt\n\n# === Web Framework (Module 14) ===\nfastapi==0.115.0          # Our API framework\nuvicorn[standard]==0.30.0 # ASGI server to run FastAPI\npython-multipart==0.0.9   # File upload support\n\n# === LLM & AI (Modules 5, 9, 11) ===\nopenai==1.40.0            # OpenAI API client\nlangchain==0.2.14         # LLM framework\nlangchain-openai==0.1.22  # LangChain + OpenAI\nlangchain-community==0.2.12\nlanggraph==0.2.3          # Agent orchestration\n\n# === Embeddings & Vector DB (Modules 4, 7) ===\nchromadb==0.5.5           # Vector database\nsentence-transformers==3.0.1  # Local embeddings\n\n# === Databases (Module 15) ===\nsqlalchemy==2.0.32        # SQL ORM\nasyncpg==0.29.0           # Async PostgreSQL driver\nalembic==1.13.2           # Database migrations\nredis==5.0.8              # Redis client\n\n# === Document Processing (Module 8) ===\nPyMuPDF==1.24.9           # PDF parsing\npython-docx==1.1.2        # Word documents\nbeautifulsoup4==4.12.3    # HTML parsing\n\n# === Configuration & Validation ===\npydantic==2.8.2           # Data validation\npydantic-settings==2.4.0  # Environment config\npython-dotenv==1.0.1      # .env file loading\npython-jose[cryptography]==3.3.0  # JWT tokens\npasslib[bcrypt]==1.7.4    # Password hashing\n\n# === Monitoring & Logging (Module 17, 19) ===\nstructlog==24.4.0         # Structured logging\nprometheus-client==0.21.0 # Metrics\n\n# === Testing ===\npytest==8.3.2\npytest-asyncio==0.23.8\nhttpx==0.27.0             # Async HTTP test client\n```"
              }
            ],
            "example": "# Install all dependencies at once:\npip install -r requirements.txt\n\n# Verify key packages installed correctly:\npython -c \"import fastapi; print(f'FastAPI {fastapi.__version__}')\"  \npython -c \"import langchain; print(f'LangChain {langchain.__version__}')\"\npython -c \"import chromadb; print(f'ChromaDB {chromadb.__version__}')\"\npython -c \"import sqlalchemy; print(f'SQLAlchemy {sqlalchemy.__version__}')\"\n\n# Expected output:\n# FastAPI 0.115.0\n# LangChain 0.2.14\n# ChromaDB 0.5.5\n# SQLAlchemy 2.0.32\n\nprint(\"\\n✅ All dependencies installed successfully!\")"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Create the .env.example file with all required environment variables for the project. Include: OPENAI_API_KEY, DATABASE_URL (PostgreSQL), REDIS_URL, CHROMA_HOST, CHROMA_PORT, JWT_SECRET_KEY, LOG_LEVEL, and APP_ENV. Provide sensible default values for local development.",
            "starterCode": "# .env.example\n# Copy this file to .env and fill in your values\n# cp .env.example .env\n\n# === AI Configuration ===\n# TODO: Add OPENAI_API_KEY\n\n# === Database Configuration ===  \n# TODO: Add DATABASE_URL, REDIS_URL\n\n# === Vector Database ===\n# TODO: Add CHROMA_HOST, CHROMA_PORT\n\n# === Security ===\n# TODO: Add JWT_SECRET_KEY\n\n# === Application ===\n# TODO: Add LOG_LEVEL, APP_ENV",
            "solution": "# .env.example\n# Copy this file to .env and fill in your values\n# cp .env.example .env\n\n# === AI Configuration ===\nOPENAI_API_KEY=sk-your-api-key-here\nOPENAI_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini\nEMBEDDING_MODEL=text-embedding-3-small\n\n# === Database Configuration ===\nDATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/customer_intel\nREDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379/0\n\n# === Vector Database ===\nCHROMA_HOST=localhost\nCHROMA_PORT=8000\nCHROMA_COLLECTION=knowledge_base\n\n# === Security ===\nJWT_SECRET_KEY=your-super-secret-key-change-in-production\nJWT_ALGORITHM=HS256\nACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES=30\n\n# === Application ===\nLOG_LEVEL=INFO\nAPP_ENV=development\nAPP_PORT=8080\nCORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000",
            "language": "python"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Create the config.py file using Pydantic Settings. It should load all environment variables from .env, validate their types, and provide a cached singleton via a get_settings() function with @lru_cache.",
            "starterCode": "from pydantic_settings import BaseSettings\nfrom functools import lru_cache\n\nclass Settings(BaseSettings):\n    # TODO: Define all settings fields\n    # AI Config\n    # Database Config\n    # Vector DB Config\n    # Security Config\n    # App Config\n    pass\n\n@lru_cache()\ndef get_settings():\n    # TODO: Return Settings instance\n    pass",
            "solution": "from pydantic_settings import BaseSettings\nfrom pydantic import Field\nfrom functools import lru_cache\n\nclass Settings(BaseSettings):\n    # AI Configuration\n    OPENAI_API_KEY: str = Field(..., description=\"OpenAI API key\")\n    OPENAI_MODEL: str = Field(default=\"gpt-4o-mini\")\n    EMBEDDING_MODEL: str = Field(default=\"text-embedding-3-small\")\n\n    # Database\n    DATABASE_URL: str = Field(default=\"postgresql+asyncpg://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/customer_intel\")\n    REDIS_URL: str = Field(default=\"redis://localhost:6379/0\")\n\n    # Vector Database\n    CHROMA_HOST: str = Field(default=\"localhost\")\n    CHROMA_PORT: int = Field(default=8000)\n    CHROMA_COLLECTION: str = Field(default=\"knowledge_base\")\n\n    # Security\n    JWT_SECRET_KEY: str = Field(default=\"change-me-in-production\")\n    JWT_ALGORITHM: str = Field(default=\"HS256\")\n    ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES: int = Field(default=30)\n\n    # Application\n    LOG_LEVEL: str = Field(default=\"INFO\")\n    APP_ENV: str = Field(default=\"development\")\n    APP_PORT: int = Field(default=8080)\n    CORS_ORIGINS: str = Field(default=\"http://localhost:3000\")\n\n    @property\n    def is_production(self) -> bool:\n        return self.APP_ENV == \"production\"\n\n    @property\n    def cors_origin_list(self) -> list[str]:\n        return [origin.strip() for origin in self.CORS_ORIGINS.split(\",\")]\n\n    class Config:\n        env_file = \".env\"\n        env_file_encoding = \"utf-8\"\n\n@lru_cache()\ndef get_settings() -> Settings:\n    return Settings()",
            "language": "python"
          }
        },
