    {
      "id": "M020-phase-2",
      "title": "Module 20: Phase 2 — Docker Infrastructure & Database Setup",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "title": "Step 3: Docker Compose — Spinning Up Your Infrastructure",
          "content": {
            "title": "Step 3: Docker Compose — Spinning Up Your Infrastructure",
            "explanation": "Before writing application code, we need databases running locally. In Module 19, you learned Docker and Docker Compose. Now we'll use that knowledge for real.\n\n**What services do we need?**\n\n| Service | Purpose | Port |\n|---------|---------|------|\n| PostgreSQL | Store customers, tickets, feedback | 5432 |\n| Redis | Cache, sessions, rate limiting | 6379 |\n| ChromaDB | Vector database for RAG | 8000 |\n\n**Why Docker Compose?**\nInstead of installing PostgreSQL, Redis, and ChromaDB separately on your machine (which is error-prone and platform-specific), Docker Compose lets you define all services in one file and start everything with a single command:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose up -d\n```\n\nThat's it. All three databases start in isolated containers, with data persisted in Docker volumes.",
            "sections": [
              {
                "title": "Understanding the docker-compose.yml",
                "body": "Each service in docker-compose.yml defines:\n- **image**: Which Docker image to use (pre-built by the database vendor)\n- **ports**: Which port to expose to your machine\n- **volumes**: Where to store data so it survives container restarts\n- **environment**: Configuration variables for the database\n- **healthcheck**: A command Docker runs to verify the service is ready\n\nThe healthcheck is crucial — it prevents your app from crashing because it tried to connect to a database that hasn't finished starting yet."
              }
            ],
            "example": "# docker-compose.yml\n# Run with: docker compose up -d\n\nversion: '3.8'\n\nservices:\n  postgres:\n    image: postgres:16-alpine\n    container_name: cip-postgres\n    ports:\n      - \"5432:5432\"\n    environment:\n      POSTGRES_USER: postgres\n      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: postgres\n      POSTGRES_DB: customer_intel\n    volumes:\n      - postgres_data:/var/lib/postgresql/data\n    healthcheck:\n      test: [\"CMD-SHELL\", \"pg_isready -U postgres\"]\n      interval: 5s\n      timeout: 5s\n      retries: 5\n\n  redis:\n    image: redis:7-alpine\n    container_name: cip-redis\n    ports:\n      - \"6379:6379\"\n    volumes:\n      - redis_data:/data\n    healthcheck:\n      test: [\"CMD\", \"redis-cli\", \"ping\"]\n      interval: 5s\n      timeout: 5s\n      retries: 5\n\n  chromadb:\n    image: chromadb/chroma:latest\n    container_name: cip-chromadb\n    ports:\n      - \"8000:8000\"\n    volumes:\n      - chroma_data:/chroma/chroma\n    environment:\n      ANONYMIZED_TELEMETRY: \"false\"\n\nvolumes:\n  postgres_data:\n  redis_data:\n  chroma_data:"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Write the FastAPI main.py entry point with: a lifespan context manager that connects to PostgreSQL and Redis on startup and disconnects on shutdown, CORS middleware, and a /health endpoint that returns the status of all services.",
            "starterCode": "from fastapi import FastAPI\nfrom contextlib import asynccontextmanager\n\n# TODO: Import settings\n# TODO: Create lifespan manager\n# TODO: Create FastAPI app with lifespan\n# TODO: Add CORS middleware\n# TODO: Create /health endpoint\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    import uvicorn\n    uvicorn.run(\"app.main:app\", host=\"0.0.0.0\", port=8080, reload=True)",
            "solution": "from fastapi import FastAPI\nfrom fastapi.middleware.cors import CORSMiddleware\nfrom contextlib import asynccontextmanager\nimport redis.asyncio as aioredis\nimport structlog\n\nfrom app.config import get_settings\n\nlogger = structlog.get_logger()\nsettings = get_settings()\n\n@asynccontextmanager\nasync def lifespan(app: FastAPI):\n    # === STARTUP ===\n    logger.info(\"Starting Customer Intelligence Platform\", env=settings.APP_ENV)\n    \n    # Connect to Redis\n    app.state.redis = aioredis.from_url(\n        settings.REDIS_URL,\n        decode_responses=True\n    )\n    logger.info(\"Redis connected\")\n    \n    yield  # App runs here\n    \n    # === SHUTDOWN ===\n    await app.state.redis.close()\n    logger.info(\"Shutdown complete\")\n\napp = FastAPI(\n    title=\"Customer Intelligence Platform\",\n    description=\"AI-Powered Customer Support API\",\n    version=\"1.0.0\",\n    lifespan=lifespan\n)\n\n# CORS Middleware\napp.add_middleware(\n    CORSMiddleware,\n    allow_origins=settings.cors_origin_list,\n    allow_credentials=True,\n    allow_methods=[\"*\"],\n    allow_headers=[\"*\"],\n)\n\n@app.get(\"/health\")\nasync def health_check():\n    checks = {\"api\": \"healthy\"}\n    try:\n        await app.state.redis.ping()\n        checks[\"redis\"] = \"healthy\"\n    except Exception:\n        checks[\"redis\"] = \"unhealthy\"\n    \n    overall = \"healthy\" if all(v == \"healthy\" for v in checks.values()) else \"degraded\"\n    return {\"status\": overall, \"services\": checks}\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    import uvicorn\n    uvicorn.run(\"app.main:app\", host=\"0.0.0.0\", port=8080, reload=True)",
            "language": "python"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "questions": [
            {
              "question": "What does 'docker compose up -d' do?",
              "options": [
                "Downloads Docker Desktop",
                "Starts all services defined in docker-compose.yml in detached (background) mode",
                "Builds a Docker image",
                "Stops all running containers"
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explanation": "The '-d' flag means 'detached mode' — containers run in the background so you get your terminal back. Without '-d', logs from all containers would flood your terminal."
            },
            {
              "question": "Why do we use Docker volumes (like postgres_data:/var/lib/postgresql/data)?",
              "options": [
                "To make containers run faster",
                "To persist data across container restarts — without volumes, all data is lost when the container stops",
                "To share code between containers",
                "Docker requires volumes to work"
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explanation": "Containers are ephemeral by design — when they stop, everything inside is gone. Volumes mount a persistent storage location that survives container lifecycle events."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "drag_drop",
