    {
      "id": "M020-phase-3",
      "title": "Module 20: Phase 3 — Database Models & Migrations",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "title": "Step 4: Designing the Database Schema",
          "content": {
            "title": "Step 4: Designing the Database Schema",
            "explanation": "Every SaaS application needs a well-designed database. In Module 15, you learned SQLAlchemy and database design. Now we'll apply that knowledge to design the schema for our Customer Intelligence Platform.\n\n**What data do we need to store?**\n\n1. **Customers** — Who's contacting us?\n2. **Tickets** — What are they asking?\n3. **Messages** — The conversation thread\n4. **Knowledge Articles** — Our knowledge base documents\n5. **Feedback** — Was the AI response helpful?\n\n**Database Design Principles:**\n- **Normalize** — Don't repeat data across tables\n- **Use UUIDs** — Better than auto-increment IDs for distributed systems\n- **Add timestamps** — Always track created_at and updated_at\n- **Index wisely** — Speed up queries you'll run frequently\n- **Use enums** — For fields with fixed options (ticket status, priority)",
            "sections": [
              {
                "title": "The Entity Relationship Diagram",
                "body": "```\n┌──────────────┐     ┌──────────────────┐     ┌─────────────┐\n│  customers   │     │     tickets      │     │  messages   │\n├──────────────┤     ├──────────────────┤     ├─────────────┤\n│ id (UUID)    │──┐  │ id (UUID)        │──┐  │ id (UUID)   │\n│ email        │  └─>│ customer_id (FK) │  └─>│ ticket_id   │\n│ name         │     │ subject          │     │ role        │\n│ company      │     │ status           │     │ content     │\n│ tier         │     │ priority         │     │ sources     │\n│ created_at   │     │ category         │     │ confidence  │\n│ updated_at   │     │ ai_confidence    │     │ created_at  │\n└──────────────┘     │ assigned_to      │     └─────────────┘\n                     │ created_at       │\n                     │ resolved_at      │     ┌─────────────┐\n                     └──────────────────┘     │  feedback   │\n                                              ├─────────────┤\n┌──────────────────┐                          │ id (UUID)   │\n│ knowledge_docs   │                          │ message_id  │\n├──────────────────┤                          │ rating      │\n│ id (UUID)        │                          │ comment     │\n│ title            │                          │ created_at  │\n│ content          │                          └─────────────┘\n│ category         │\n│ source_url       │\n│ chunk_ids        │\n│ created_at       │\n│ updated_at       │\n└──────────────────┘\n```"
              }
            ],
            "example": "# app/models/ticket.py\nimport uuid\nfrom datetime import datetime\nfrom enum import Enum as PyEnum\nfrom sqlalchemy import Column, String, DateTime, ForeignKey, Float, Enum, Text\nfrom sqlalchemy.dialects.postgresql import UUID\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import relationship\nfrom app.db.postgres import Base\n\nclass TicketStatus(str, PyEnum):\n    OPEN = \"open\"\n    IN_PROGRESS = \"in_progress\"\n    WAITING_CUSTOMER = \"waiting_customer\"\n    RESOLVED = \"resolved\"\n    ESCALATED = \"escalated\"\n\nclass TicketPriority(str, PyEnum):\n    LOW = \"low\"\n    MEDIUM = \"medium\"\n    HIGH = \"high\"\n    URGENT = \"urgent\"\n\nclass TicketCategory(str, PyEnum):\n    ACCOUNT = \"account\"\n    BILLING = \"billing\"\n    TECHNICAL = \"technical\"\n    FEATURE_REQUEST = \"feature_request\"\n    BUG_REPORT = \"bug_report\"\n    GENERAL = \"general\"\n\nclass Ticket(Base):\n    __tablename__ = \"tickets\"\n\n    id = Column(UUID(as_uuid=True), primary_key=True, default=uuid.uuid4)\n    customer_id = Column(UUID(as_uuid=True), ForeignKey(\"customers.id\"), nullable=False)\n    subject = Column(String(500), nullable=False)\n    status = Column(Enum(TicketStatus), default=TicketStatus.OPEN, index=True)\n    priority = Column(Enum(TicketPriority), default=TicketPriority.MEDIUM)\n    category = Column(Enum(TicketCategory), nullable=True)\n    ai_confidence = Column(Float, nullable=True)  # How confident AI was\n    assigned_to = Column(String(255), nullable=True)  # Human agent if escalated\n    created_at = Column(DateTime, default=datetime.utcnow, index=True)\n    updated_at = Column(DateTime, default=datetime.utcnow, onupdate=datetime.utcnow)\n    resolved_at = Column(DateTime, nullable=True)\n\n    # Relationships\n    customer = relationship(\"Customer\", back_populates=\"tickets\")\n    messages = relationship(\"Message\", back_populates=\"ticket\", order_by=\"Message.created_at\")"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Create the Customer SQLAlchemy model with fields: id (UUID primary key), email (unique, indexed), name, company (nullable), tier (enum: free, pro, enterprise, default: free), created_at, and updated_at. Include a relationship to tickets.",
            "starterCode": "import uuid\nfrom datetime import datetime\nfrom enum import Enum as PyEnum\nfrom sqlalchemy import Column, String, DateTime, Enum\nfrom sqlalchemy.dialects.postgresql import UUID\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import relationship\nfrom app.db.postgres import Base\n\n# TODO: Define CustomerTier enum\n\n# TODO: Define Customer model",
            "solution": "import uuid\nfrom datetime import datetime\nfrom enum import Enum as PyEnum\nfrom sqlalchemy import Column, String, DateTime, Enum\nfrom sqlalchemy.dialects.postgresql import UUID\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import relationship\nfrom app.db.postgres import Base\n\nclass CustomerTier(str, PyEnum):\n    FREE = \"free\"\n    PRO = \"pro\"\n    ENTERPRISE = \"enterprise\"\n\nclass Customer(Base):\n    __tablename__ = \"customers\"\n\n    id = Column(UUID(as_uuid=True), primary_key=True, default=uuid.uuid4)\n    email = Column(String(255), unique=True, nullable=False, index=True)\n    name = Column(String(255), nullable=False)\n    company = Column(String(255), nullable=True)\n    tier = Column(Enum(CustomerTier), default=CustomerTier.FREE)\n    created_at = Column(DateTime, default=datetime.utcnow)\n    updated_at = Column(DateTime, default=datetime.utcnow, onupdate=datetime.utcnow)\n\n    # Relationships\n    tickets = relationship(\"Ticket\", back_populates=\"customer\", cascade=\"all, delete-orphan\")\n\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return f\"<Customer {self.email} ({self.tier.value})>\"",
            "language": "python"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Create the async PostgreSQL database connection module (app/db/postgres.py) using SQLAlchemy 2.0 async. Include: create_async_engine, async_sessionmaker, Base declarative class, a get_db async generator for FastAPI dependency injection, and an init_db function that creates all tables.",
            "starterCode": "from sqlalchemy.ext.asyncio import create_async_engine, AsyncSession, async_sessionmaker\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import DeclarativeBase\nfrom app.config import get_settings\n\n# TODO: Create Base class\n# TODO: Create engine and session factory\n# TODO: Create get_db dependency\n# TODO: Create init_db function",
            "solution": "from sqlalchemy.ext.asyncio import create_async_engine, AsyncSession, async_sessionmaker\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import DeclarativeBase\nfrom typing import AsyncGenerator\nfrom app.config import get_settings\n\nsettings = get_settings()\n\nclass Base(DeclarativeBase):\n    pass\n\nengine = create_async_engine(\n    settings.DATABASE_URL,\n    echo=settings.LOG_LEVEL == \"DEBUG\",\n    pool_size=20,\n    max_overflow=10,\n    pool_pre_ping=True,  # Verify connections are alive\n)\n\nasync_session = async_sessionmaker(\n    engine,\n    class_=AsyncSession,\n    expire_on_commit=False\n)\n\nasync def get_db() -> AsyncGenerator[AsyncSession, None]:\n    \"\"\"FastAPI dependency that provides a database session.\"\"\"\n    async with async_session() as session:\n        try:\n            yield session\n            await session.commit()\n        except Exception:\n            await session.rollback()\n            raise\n\nasync def init_db():\n    \"\"\"Create all tables. Use Alembic for production migrations.\"\"\"\n    async with engine.begin() as conn:\n        await conn.run_sync(Base.metadata.create_all)",
            "language": "python"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "questions": [
            {
              "question": "Why do we use UUIDs instead of auto-incrementing integers for primary keys?",
              "options": [
                "UUIDs are shorter and easier to read",
                "UUIDs can be generated client-side, prevent ID enumeration attacks, and work across distributed databases",
                "PostgreSQL doesn't support auto-incrementing IDs",
                "UUIDs use less storage space"
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explanation": "UUIDs solve several problems: they can be generated anywhere (no need to ask the database), they prevent attackers from guessing valid IDs (like /users/1, /users/2), and they work in distributed systems where multiple databases might create records simultaneously."
            },
            {
              "question": "What does 'expire_on_commit=False' do in the async session factory?",
              "options": [
                "It makes the session never expire",
                "It prevents SQLAlchemy from clearing cached attributes after commit, so you can still access object attributes without re-querying",
                "It makes commits faster",
                "It disables transaction support"
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explanation": "By default, after a commit, SQLAlchemy marks all loaded objects as 'expired' and will re-query the database when you access their attributes. In async code, this causes issues because the session might be closed. Setting expire_on_commit=False lets you safely access attributes after commit."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "M020-phase-4",
      "title": "Module 20: Phase 4 — Knowledge Base & Document Ingestion",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "title": "Step 5: Building the Knowledge Base Ingestion Pipeline",
          "content": {
            "title": "Step 5: Building the Knowledge Base Ingestion Pipeline",
            "explanation": "For our AI to answer customer questions, it needs a **knowledge base** — a collection of documents containing your product's documentation, FAQs, troubleshooting guides, and policies.\n\nIn Modules 7 and 8, you learned about vector databases and RAG. Now we'll build a production ingestion pipeline that:\n\n1. **Accepts** documents (PDF, Markdown, plain text)\n2. **Chunks** them intelligently (not just by character count)\n3. **Embeds** each chunk into a vector\n4. **Stores** vectors in ChromaDB with metadata\n5. **Tracks** what's been ingested in PostgreSQL\n\n**Why is chunking important?**\nIf you embed an entire 50-page document as one vector, the search will be too vague. If you chunk every sentence, you lose context. The sweet spot is **semantic chunking** — splitting on paragraph/section boundaries with overlap.\n\n**Our chunking strategy:**\n- Split on markdown headers (##, ###) first\n- Then split on double newlines (paragraphs)\n- Target chunk size: 500-800 tokens\n- Overlap: 100 tokens (so context isn't lost at boundaries)\n- Preserve metadata: source file, section title, chunk index",
            "sections": [
              {
                "title": "The Ingestion Flow",
                "body": "```\nDocument Upload  →  Parse (PDF/MD/TXT)  →  Clean Text\n       ↓                                       ↓\n  Save metadata     ←──────────────────   Semantic Chunk\n  to PostgreSQL                               ↓\n                                         Embed chunks\n                                         (OpenAI API)\n                                              ↓\n                                         Store in ChromaDB\n                                         with metadata\n```\n\nEach chunk stored in ChromaDB includes:\n- **document**: The text content\n- **embedding**: The vector representation\n- **metadata**: { source, title, section, chunk_index, ingested_at }"
              }
            ],
            "example": "# app/services/ingestion.py\nfrom langchain.text_splitter import RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter\nfrom langchain_openai import OpenAIEmbeddings\nimport chromadb\nfrom app.config import get_settings\nimport structlog\n\nlogger = structlog.get_logger()\nsettings = get_settings()\n\nclass IngestionService:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.embeddings = OpenAIEmbeddings(\n            model=settings.EMBEDDING_MODEL,\n            openai_api_key=settings.OPENAI_API_KEY\n        )\n        self.text_splitter = RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter(\n            chunk_size=800,\n            chunk_overlap=100,\n            separators=[\"\\n## \", \"\\n### \", \"\\n\\n\", \"\\n\", \". \", \" \"],\n            length_function=len,\n        )\n        self.chroma_client = chromadb.HttpClient(\n            host=settings.CHROMA_HOST,\n            port=settings.CHROMA_PORT\n        )\n        self.collection = self.chroma_client.get_or_create_collection(\n            name=settings.CHROMA_COLLECTION,\n            metadata={\"hnsw:space\": \"cosine\"}\n        )\n\n    async def ingest_document(self, content: str, metadata: dict) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Ingest a document into the knowledge base.\"\"\"\n        # 1. Chunk the document\n        chunks = self.text_splitter.split_text(content)\n        logger.info(\"Document chunked\", chunk_count=len(chunks), source=metadata.get(\"source\"))\n\n        # 2. Generate embeddings\n        vectors = self.embeddings.embed_documents(chunks)\n\n        # 3. Store in ChromaDB\n        ids = [f\"{metadata.get('source', 'doc')}_{i}\" for i in range(len(chunks))]\n        metadatas = [\n            {**metadata, \"chunk_index\": i, \"chunk_total\": len(chunks)}\n            for i in range(len(chunks))\n        ]\n\n        self.collection.add(\n            ids=ids,\n            documents=chunks,\n            embeddings=vectors,\n            metadatas=metadatas\n        )\n\n        logger.info(\"Document ingested successfully\", vectors_stored=len(chunks))\n        return len(chunks)"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Create the knowledge base API endpoint (app/api/v1/knowledge.py) with: POST /api/v1/knowledge/ingest that accepts a file upload (PDF or text), extracts content, calls the IngestionService, and returns the number of chunks created. Include proper error handling.",
            "starterCode": "from fastapi import APIRouter, UploadFile, File, HTTPException\nfrom app.services.ingestion import IngestionService\nimport structlog\n\nrouter = APIRouter(prefix=\"/api/v1/knowledge\", tags=[\"knowledge\"])\nlogger = structlog.get_logger()\ningestion_service = IngestionService()\n\n# TODO: Create POST /ingest endpoint\n# Accept file upload, validate type, extract text, ingest, return result",
            "solution": "from fastapi import APIRouter, UploadFile, File, HTTPException\nfrom app.services.ingestion import IngestionService\nimport fitz  # PyMuPDF\nimport structlog\nfrom datetime import datetime\n\nrouter = APIRouter(prefix=\"/api/v1/knowledge\", tags=[\"knowledge\"])\nlogger = structlog.get_logger()\ningestion_service = IngestionService()\n\nALLOWED_TYPES = {\"application/pdf\", \"text/plain\", \"text/markdown\"}\n\n@router.post(\"/ingest\")\nasync def ingest_document(file: UploadFile = File(...)):\n    \"\"\"Ingest a document into the knowledge base.\"\"\"\n    # Validate file type\n    if file.content_type not in ALLOWED_TYPES:\n        raise HTTPException(\n            status_code=400,\n            detail=f\"Unsupported file type: {file.content_type}. Allowed: {ALLOWED_TYPES}\"\n        )\n\n    try:\n        content = \"\"\n        raw_bytes = await file.read()\n\n        if file.content_type == \"application/pdf\":\n            doc = fitz.open(stream=raw_bytes, filetype=\"pdf\")\n            content = \"\\n\".join(page.get_text() for page in doc)\n            doc.close()\n        else:\n            content = raw_bytes.decode(\"utf-8\")\n\n        if not content.strip():\n            raise HTTPException(status_code=400, detail=\"Document is empty\")\n\n        metadata = {\n            \"source\": file.filename,\n            \"content_type\": file.content_type,\n            \"ingested_at\": datetime.utcnow().isoformat()\n        }\n\n        chunks_created = await ingestion_service.ingest_document(content, metadata)\n\n        return {\n            \"status\": \"success\",\n            \"filename\": file.filename,\n            \"chunks_created\": chunks_created,\n            \"message\": f\"Successfully ingested {file.filename} into knowledge base\"\n        }\n\n    except HTTPException:\n        raise\n    except Exception as e:\n        logger.error(\"Ingestion failed\", error=str(e), filename=file.filename)\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=500, detail=f\"Ingestion failed: {str(e)}\")",
            "language": "python"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "questions": [
            {
              "beforeBlank": "When chunking documents for RAG, we use chunk_overlap to prevent losing",
              "afterBlank": "at the boundaries between chunks",
              "answer": 0,
              "hint": "Without overlap, a sentence split across two chunks would be incomplete in both",
              "options": [
                "True",
